window.WORDS={"adults":[["herald","HER-uhld","verb","To announce, or to be a sign of something coming.","The smell of coffee heralds the start of my day.","","Herald sounds like Harold, the man who announces the news."],["melancholy","MEL-uhn-kol-ee","noun","A quiet, gentle sadness.","Rainy Sundays fill him with a quiet melancholy.","From Greek melas, “black,” and khole, “bile.”","Melancholy sounds like “melon-collie”: a sad dog with a melon."],["vagabond","VAG-uh-bond","noun","A person who wanders from place to place.","After college, he lived like a vagabond, sleeping on friends’ couches in different cities.","From Latin vagari, “to wander.”","Vagabond sounds like “vague bond”: no ties to any one place."],["odious","OH-dee-uhs","adjective","Very unpleasant.","Cleaning the oven is an odious chore.","From Latin odium, “hatred.”","Odious sounds like odor: as unpleasant as a bad smell."],["nestle","NES-uhl","verb","To settle in snugly and comfortably.","The kitten nestled into the warm blanket.","","Nestle has nest in it: snug like a bird in a nest."],["audacious","aw-DAY-shuhs","adjective","Bold and daring, sometimes surprisingly so.","The kid made an audacious move and asked the boss for a raise on day one.","From Latin audax, “bold.”","Audacious sounds like “a daring us”: bold and daring."],["dour","DOOR","adjective","Stern and gloomy.","The dour clerk at the store finally cracked a smile.","","Dour sounds like sour: a sour, gloomy face."],["disparate","DIS-per-it","adjective","Very different from each other.","The book club brings together disparate people who all love reading.","","Disparate: not on par, very different."],["abeyance","uh-BAY-uhns","noun","A state of being paused or put on hold for a while.","Our plan to paint the kitchen is in abeyance until we save more money.","","Abeyance sounds like “a bay”: a boat waiting in the bay, on hold."],["humdrum","HUM-drum","adjective","Dull and boring.","Folding laundry is a humdrum chore.","","Humdrum: hum, hum, drum, drum, the same dull beat."],["gourmand","goor-MAHND","noun","A person who loves good food, and lots of it.","My uncle is a gourmand who always asks for seconds and thirds.","From French gourmand.","Gourmand: a gourmet who also wants a mountain of food."],["extemporize","ik-STEM-puh-ryz","verb","To speak or perform without planning.","When the power went out, the teacher had to extemporize the lesson.","From Latin ex tempore, “out of the time.”","Ex tempore means “out of the time”: you make it up right then."],["lenient","LEE-nee-uhnt","adjective","Gentle and not strict.","Grandma is more lenient than Mom about bedtime.","From Latin lenis, “soft, mild.”","Lenient sounds like “leaning”: bending the rules gently."],["wayward","WAY-werd","adjective","Going off in an unexpected or unruly way.","A wayward shopping cart rolled across the parking lot.","","Wayward: going away from the right way."],["bulwark","BUL-werk","noun","A strong wall or a strong source of protection.","Good friends are a bulwark against hard times.","","Bulwark sounds like “bull walk”: a strong wall even a bull can’t break."],["cahoots","kuh-HOOTS","noun","Secret teamwork, usually for mischief.","The cousins were in cahoots to throw Grandma a surprise party.","","Cahoots sounds like two owls hooting secrets together."],["onomatopoeia","on-uh-mat-uh-PEE-uh","noun","A word that sounds like the thing it names, like buzz or pop.","“Sizzle” is a tasty bit of onomatopoeia.","From Greek onoma, “name,” and poiein, “to make.”","Onomatopoeia sounds like “on a mat, a pea” going plop."],["muse","MYOOZ","noun","Someone or something that inspires you to create.","The ocean is the muse for many of his songs.","","A muse makes you “amused” and ready to create."],["acclimate","AK-luh-mayt","verb","To get used to a new place or situation.","It took our family a week to acclimate to the new time zone.","","Acclimate has “climate” inside: getting used to new weather and places."],["orator","OR-uh-ter","noun","A skilled public speaker.","The young orator held the crowd’s attention for an hour.","From Latin orare, “to speak.”","Orator sounds like “oral”: someone who speaks out loud."],["cacophony","kuh-KOF-uh-nee","noun","A loud mix of harsh, clashing sounds.","With the TV on, the dog barking and the baby crying, our house was a cacophony.","From Greek kakophonia, “bad sound.”","Phone means sound, like a telephone. Cacophony is bad sound."],["prowess","PROU-is","noun","Great skill at something.","Her prowess at chess surprised everyone.","","Prowess sounds like “pro-wins”: winning like a pro."],["woolgathering","WOOL-gath-er-ing","noun","Idle daydreaming.","Stop woolgathering and help me set the table.","","Woolgathering: drifting around picking up wool bits, not working."],["pique","PEEK","verb","To stir up interest or curiosity.","The mystery box piqued the children’s curiosity.","From French piquer, “to prick.”","Pique (PEEK) sounds like peek: it makes you want to peek."],["valiant","VAL-yuhnt","adjective","Brave and bold.","The valiant firefighter carried the family’s dog out of the house.","","Valiant sounds like “valor”: brave and bold."],["fastidious","fa-STID-ee-uhs","adjective","Very careful about small details and cleanliness.","My fastidious uncle folds his socks into neat little squares.","From Latin fastidium, “loathing.”","Fastidious: fussy about every tiny detail, fast to spot a crumb."],["tinker","TING-ker","verb","To try fixing or improving something in small ways.","Dad loves to tinker with the old radio in the garage.","","Tinker sounds like “tinkle”: little taps and tweaks with tools."],["wily","WY-lee","adjective","Clever and sneaky.","The wily raccoon opened the trash can lid.","","Wily sounds like “wile”: full of sneaky tricks."],["capricious","kuh-PRISH-uhs","adjective","Likely to change suddenly for no clear reason.","The capricious toddler wanted the red cup, then the blue cup, then no cup.","","Capricious sounds like “caprice”: changing on a whim."],["perseverance","pur-suh-VEER-uhns","noun","Sticking with something even when it is hard.","With perseverance, she finished the thousand-piece puzzle.","","Perseverance has “severe” in it: keep going through severe times."],["upheaval","uhp-HEE-vuhl","noun","A big, sudden change or disturbance.","Moving to a new city was an upheaval, but a happy one.","","Upheaval: your whole life heaved up and turned over."],["idiosyncrasy","id-ee-uh-SING-kruh-see","noun","An odd habit special to one person.","Dad’s idiosyncrasy is eating his pizza crust first.","From Greek idiosynkrasia, “one's own mixture.”","Idio means one’s own, like idiom: your own odd habit."],["wheedle","WEED-l","verb","To coax someone with sweet talk.","The kids wheedled an extra cookie out of Grandma.","","Wheedle sounds like “wheel”: sweet-talking your way around someone."],["drab","DRAB","adjective","Dull and lacking color.","A few bright pillows cheered up the drab room.","","Drab sounds like “drab gray”: dull, no color."],["hamlet","HAM-lit","noun","A very small village.","My grandparents live in a tiny hamlet with one store and one gas station.","","Hamlet: a little “ham” of a town, smaller than a village."],["whimsical","WIM-zi-kuhl","adjective","Playful and fanciful in an unusual way.","The kids’ room had whimsical wallpaper with flying pigs.","","Whimsical has “whim”: a playful, fun idea."],["epitome","ih-PIT-uh-mee","noun","A perfect example of something.","My grandma’s warm kitchen is the epitome of comfort.","From Greek epitome, “a cutting short.”","Epitome rhymes with “a-pit-of-me”: the perfect example of something."],["pellucid","puh-LOO-sid","adjective","Very clear, like clean water or simple writing.","The pool water was pellucid, and we could see every coin on the bottom.","From Latin pellucidus, “transparent.”","Pellucid sounds like “pale lucid”: so clear it’s see-through."],["demarcate","dih-MAR-kayt","verb","To mark the limits of something.","Orange cones demarcate the parking spots at the school fair.","","Demarcate has mark in it: you mark the edges."],["mosey","MOH-zee","verb","To walk in a slow, easy way.","Let’s mosey down to the ice cream shop after dinner.","","Mosey sounds like a slow, lazy cow named Mosey."],["preen","PREEN","verb","To clean feathers, or to fuss over how you look.","My teen preens in the mirror before school.","","Preen rhymes with clean: birds preen to keep feathers clean."],["candelabra","kan-dl-AH-bruh","noun","A fancy holder for several candles.","A silver candelabra held five candles on the dinner table.","From Latin candela, “candle.”","Candelabra has candle in it: a stand for many candles."],["glean","GLEEN","verb","To gather bits of information slowly.","I gleaned a few tips about the new job from my friend’s chat.","","Glean sounds like “glean the clean bits”: picking up useful pieces."],["shambolic","sham-BOL-ik","adjective","Messy and badly organized.","Our first try at a family garage sale was shambolic but fun.","","Shambolic sounds like “shambles”: a total mess."],["whiff","WIF","noun","A quick, light smell.","I caught a whiff of fresh cookies from the kitchen.","","Whiff sounds like a quick little sniff of something in the air."],["bedraggled","bih-DRAG-uhld","adjective","Wet, dirty and messy.","The bedraggled puppy came in from the rain, wet and muddy.","","Bedraggled has “dragged”: like you were dragged through the mud."],["vicissitude","vi-SIS-i-tood","noun","A change in luck or life, often unwelcome.","Their friendship lasted through all of life’s ups, downs and vicissitudes.","From Latin vicissitudo, “change.”","Vicissitude sounds like “vice-versa”: things flip back and forth."],["palimpsest","PAL-imp-sest","noun","Something with old layers still showing under new ones.","The old wall was a palimpsest of posters, each one pasted over the last.","From Greek palin, “again,” and psestos, “scraped.”","Palin means again: written again on top of old layers."],["laconic","luh-KON-ik","adjective","Using very few words.","My laconic teen answered, “Fine,” when I asked about school.","From Greek Lakonikos, “Spartan.”","Laconic sounds like “lack-onic”: lacking words."],["impresario","im-pruh-SAR-ee-oh","noun","A person who organizes shows or concerts.","The school impresario organized the whole talent show.","From Italian impresario, “one who undertakes.”","Impresario sounds like “impress-ario”: the person who puts on the show."],["mannerism","MAN-uh-riz-uhm","noun","A small habit in how someone moves or talks.","Tapping her pen when she thinks is one of my sister’s mannerisms.","","It is part of your manners, the little ways you act."],["vista","VIS-tuh","noun","A wide, beautiful view.","The vista from the top of the hill took our breath away.","From Italian vista, “view.”","Vista is Italian for “view”: a big, wide view."],["placate","PLAY-kayt","verb","To calm someone and make them less upset.","A hug and a juice box placated the crying child.","From Latin placare, “to calm.”","Placate sounds like “play-cake”: a cake calms everyone down."],["jiffy","JIF-ee","noun","A very short time.","I’ll be ready in a jiffy, just let me grab my keys.","","Jiffy sounds like “quickie”: done in a flash."],["honorific","on-uh-RIF-ik","noun","A title or word that shows respect.","“Doctor” is an honorific she earned after many years of school.","","Honorific has “honor”: a title that shows respect."],["bayou","BY-oo","noun","A slow-moving, marshy stream or creek.","On our trip to the South, we took a boat ride on a slow, swampy bayou.","From Choctaw bayuk, “a small stream.”","Bayou sounds like “bye, you”: slow water drifting away."],["silhouette","sil-oo-ET","noun","A dark outline seen against a light background.","We saw Dad’s silhouette in the doorway with the hall light behind him.","","Silhouette sounds like “silly wet”: a dark shadow shape, no details."],["cumulative","KYOO-myuh-luh-tiv","adjective","Growing by adding up over time.","Saving five dollars a week has a cumulative effect over a year.","From Latin cumulus, “heap.”","Cumulative: it accumulates, piling up bit by bit."],["nigh","NY","adverb","Near, or almost.","The holidays are nigh, so we are baking cookies.","","Nigh sounds like nearby."],["abacus","AB-uh-kuhs","noun","A frame with beads on rods, used for counting.","My grandma still adds up the grocery bill by sliding beads on her old abacus.","From Greek abax, “a counting board.”","Picture a bus full of beads: “a-bus” full of counting beads."],["forte","FORT","noun","Something a person is very good at.","Cooking is Dad’s forte, but cleaning up is not.","From French fort, “strong.”","Forte sounds like fort: your strong spot."],["akimbo","uh-KIM-boh","adverb","With hands on hips and elbows pointing out.","Mom stood with arms akimbo, waiting for us to explain the mess.","","Akimbo sounds like “a kimbo”: arms bent like a jug’s two handles."],["perennial","puh-REN-ee-uhl","adjective","Lasting for a very long time, or coming back each year.","Pumpkin pie is a perennial favorite in our house every fall.","From Latin perennis, “lasting through the years.”","Per-annual: it comes back every year."],["adulation","aj-uh-LAY-shuhn","noun","Very great praise or admiration, often more than is deserved.","The young singer loved the adulation of her screaming fans.","","Adulation sounds like “adore-lation”: being adored by a crowd."],["pedestrian","puh-DES-tree-uhn","adjective","Dull and ordinary.","The movie plot was pedestrian, but the songs were great.","From Latin pedester, “on foot.”","Pedestrian means on foot: plain and walking, not flying."],["cleave","KLEEV","verb","To split apart, or to cling closely to.","Dad used a big knife to cleave the watermelon in two.","","A meat cleaver splits things apart."],["vintage","VIN-tij","adjective","Old but of high quality.","She found a vintage dress at the thrift store.","","Vintage: think “vintage car,” old but still great."],["defray","dih-FRAY","verb","To help pay for costs.","The bake sale helped defray the cost of the school trip.","","Defray sounds like “the fee away”: it helps pay the fee."],["natter","NAT-er","verb","To chat in a light, easy way for a long time.","The two friends nattered over tea all afternoon.","","Natter sounds like chatter."],["kinetic","ki-NET-ik","adjective","Having to do with motion.","The kids had so much kinetic energy after the candy.","From Greek kinein, “to move.”","Kinetic sounds like “kids in action”: all motion."],["deliberate","dih-LIB-er-it","adjective","Done on purpose and with care.","She took slow, deliberate steps across the icy driveway.","From Latin librare, “to weigh.”","Deliberate: you did it on purpose, after thinking it through."],["trepidation","trep-i-DAY-shuhn","noun","A nervous feeling about what might happen.","I opened the envelope from the bank with some trepidation.","From Latin trepidare, “to tremble.”","Trepidation sounds like “trip-idation”: nervous you might trip up."],["blanch","BLANCH","verb","To turn pale; also to dip food quickly in boiling water.","She blanched when she saw the size of the repair bill.","From French blanc, “white.”","Blanch sounds like “blank”: your face goes blank and white."],["strident","STRY-duhnt","adjective","Loud, harsh and hard to ignore.","The strident car alarm woke the whole street.","From Latin stridere, “to creak.”","Strident sounds like “strike”: a sound that strikes your ears."],["gobsmacked","GOB-smakt","adjective","Totally amazed.","I was gobsmacked when my teen offered to do the dishes.","","Gobsmacked: so shocked it’s like your gob, your mouth, got smacked."],["robust","roh-BUST","adjective","Strong, healthy and hard to break.","This robust phone case has survived lots of drops.","From Latin robustus, “strong as oak.”","Robust sounds like “robot”: strong and hard to break."],["orb","ORB","noun","A round ball or sphere.","The full moon hung in the sky like a silver orb.","From Latin orbis, “circle.”","Orb rhymes with “orbit,” the round path planets take."],["apex","AY-peks","noun","The very top or highest point.","We cheered when we reached the apex of the steep hill.","From Latin apex, “a peak.”","Apex sounds like “ape-x”: an ape at the very top of a tree."],["dormant","DOR-muhnt","adjective","Asleep or inactive for a time.","My old phone sat dormant in a drawer for a year.","From French dormir, “to sleep.”","Dorm: where you sleep. Dormant: sleeping."],["philanthropy","fih-LAN-thruh-pee","noun","Giving money or time to help others.","Her philanthropy paid for a new playground at the school.","From Greek philos, “loving,” and anthropos, “human.”","Phil means love, and anthro means people: love of people."],["torpor","TOR-per","noun","A slow, heavy, lazy feeling.","After the big holiday meal, a lazy torpor settled over us.","From Latin torpere, “to be numb.”","Torpor sounds like “tired-por”: too tired to move."],["bevy","BEV-ee","noun","A large group of people or things.","A bevy of kids ran up to the ice cream truck.","","Bevy sounds like “bunch of many”: a big group."],["irascible","ih-RAS-uh-buhl","adjective","Easily made angry.","Our irascible neighbor yells when balls land in his yard.","From Latin ira, “anger.”","Irascible has “ira,” Latin for anger, and sounds like irritable."],["conflate","kuhn-FLAYT","verb","To mix two different ideas up as if they were one.","Don’t conflate being quiet with being unfriendly.","","Conflate: two things are “flattened” together like one pancake."],["aghast","uh-GAST","adjective","Shocked and upset.","She was aghast to find the dog had eaten the whole cake.","","Aghast sounds like “a ghost”: shocked like you just saw one."],["resolute","REZ-uh-loot","adjective","Firm and set on doing something.","She was resolute about finishing her homework before dinner.","","Resolute has “resolve”: your mind is made up."],["innate","ih-NAYT","adjective","Something you are born with.","She has an innate talent for making people laugh.","From Latin innatus, “inborn.”","Innate: in + natal, birth: you were born with it."],["delve","DELV","verb","To dig deep into a subject.","We delved into old photo albums all afternoon.","","Delve sounds like dig and shovel: you dig deep."],["annotate","AN-oh-tayt","verb","To add notes to a book or text to explain it.","She annotated her cookbook with notes in the margins.","From Latin nota, “a mark.”","Annotate has “note” inside: add notes."],["utopia","yoo-TOH-pee-uh","noun","An imagined perfect place.","To my son, a utopia is a world where homework doesn’t exist.","Coined in 1516 from Greek ou, “not,” and topos, “place.”","Utopia comes from Greek for “no place”: a perfect place that isn’t real."],["relish","REL-ish","verb","To enjoy something very much.","I relish quiet Sunday mornings with a cup of tea.","","Relish is also a tasty topping: you enjoy it with gusto."],["caustic","KAW-stik","adjective","Sharp and biting, like harsh words.","Her caustic comment about my cooking stung all evening.","From Greek kaustikos, “burning.”","Caustic sounds like “cost stick”: words that sting and burn."],["befitting","bih-FIT-ing","adjective","Right and suitable for a person or moment.","They gave the retiring teacher a party befitting her thirty years of work.","","Befitting has “fitting”: something that fits just right."],["umbrage","UHM-brij","noun","A feeling of being hurt or offended.","The cat took umbrage when we moved her bed.","From Latin umbra, “shadow.”","Umbrage sounds like “umbrella rage”: hiding under a hurt mood."],["punctilious","punk-TIL-ee-uhs","adjective","Very careful about rules and good manners.","The punctilious host wrote thank-you notes to every guest.","","Punctilious sounds like punctual: careful about every little thing."],["dub","DUHB","verb","To give someone a nickname.","We dubbed our new kitten “Captain.”","","A king dubs a knight with a sword tap and gives a new name."],["latent","LAY-tuhnt","adjective","Hidden but able to grow later.","My daughter has a latent talent for singing we just discovered.","From Latin latere, “to lie hidden.”","Latent sounds like “late-ent”: hidden now, shows up later."],["decadent","DEK-uh-duhnt","adjective","Richly indulgent, often in a fun way.","We shared a decadent chocolate cake with three layers of frosting.","","Decadent sounds like “deca-dent”: so rich it makes a dent in your diet."],["nomenclature","NOH-muhn-klay-cher","noun","A system of names used in a subject.","Every sport has its own nomenclature, like “love” meaning zero in tennis.","From Latin nomen, “name.”","Nomen means name: a whole set of names."],["quotidian","kwoh-TID-ee-uhn","adjective","Ordinary and part of every day.","She found joy in quotidian things like folding warm laundry.","From Latin quotidie, “every day.”","Quotidian sounds like “quota daily”: the stuff you do daily."],["apogee","AP-uh-jee","noun","The highest or best point of something.","Her career reached its apogee when she opened her own bakery.","From Greek apogaion, “away from the earth.”","Apogee sounds like “up to gee-whiz”: the highest point."],["taciturn","TAS-i-turn","adjective","Quiet and not inclined to talk much.","The taciturn plumber just nodded and fixed the sink.","From Latin tacitus, “silent.”","Taciturn sounds like “tacit turn”: he takes his turn but stays silent."],["skedaddle","ski-DAD-l","verb","To run away fast.","The kids skedaddled when Mom asked who made the mess.","","Skedaddle sounds like “scat, dash”: run off fast."],["ordeal","or-DEEL","noun","A hard and painful experience.","Getting the cat into the carrier was an ordeal.","","Ordeal sounds like “or deal”: a tough deal to get through."],["arboreal","ar-BOR-ee-uhl","adjective","Living in trees, or having to do with trees.","Monkeys are arboreal; they spend most of their lives in trees.","From Latin arbor, “tree.”","Arbor is Latin for tree: arboreal animals live in trees."],["assuage","uh-SWAYJ","verb","To ease or soothe a feeling or need.","A cup of hot cocoa assuaged our chill after playing in the snow.","","Assuage sounds like “a sweet wage”: something sweet to ease the pain."],["adorn","uh-DORN","verb","To decorate something to make it prettier.","We adorned the birthday table with balloons and streamers.","From Latin adornare, “to furnish.”","Adorn sounds like “adore”: you decorate what you adore."],["glade","GLAYD","noun","An open, grassy space in a forest.","We had a picnic in a sunny glade in the park’s woods.","","Glade sounds like glad: a happy, sunny clearing in the trees."],["homage","OM-ij","noun","Something done to show respect or honor.","The bakery’s pie is a homage to the owner’s grandmother.","From French hommage.","Homage sounds like “home-age”: honoring someone you look up to."],["convoluted","KON-vuh-loo-tid","adjective","Twisted and hard to follow.","The phone company’s billing was so convoluted we gave up reading it.","From Latin convolvere, “to roll together.”","Convoluted: rolled up and twisted, like a curly straw."],["saunter","SAWN-ter","verb","To walk in a slow, relaxed way.","We sauntered through the mall eating ice cream.","","Saunter sounds like “saint stroll”: a slow, easy walk."],["apparition","ap-uh-RISH-uhn","noun","A ghostly figure, or something that appears suddenly.","In the fog, the man in the white coat looked like an apparition.","From Latin apparere, “to appear.”","Apparition sounds like “appear”: a ghost that suddenly appears."],["posit","POZ-it","verb","To put forward an idea as true.","My sister posited that the dog ate the missing sandwich.","","Posit sounds like “post it”: you post an idea up as true."],["capacious","kuh-PAY-shuhs","adjective","Roomy and able to hold a lot.","Her capacious purse held snacks, wipes, toys and a spare shirt.","From Latin capax, “able to hold much.”","Capacious sounds like capacity: room for a lot."],["fallow","FAL-oh","adjective","Left unplanted for a while to rest.","We let the backyard garden lie fallow this year, with nothing planted.","","Fallow sounds like “follow” nothing: the field rests and waits."],["mishmash","MISH-mash","noun","A messy mix of things.","Dinner was a mishmash of leftovers, and it was great.","","Mish and mash: everything mashed together."],["xenial","ZEE-nee-uhl","adjective","Friendly and welcoming to guests.","Our xenial hosts set out snacks the moment we arrived.","From Greek xenos, “guest, stranger.”","Xenos is Greek for guest: kind to guests."],["remorse","ri-MORS","noun","A deep feeling of being sorry for something you did.","He felt remorse for snapping at his little brother.","From Latin remordere, “to bite again.”","Remorse sounds like “re-mourn”: you feel sad about what you did."],["journeyman","JUR-nee-muhn","noun","A skilled worker who has fully learned a trade.","The journeyman plumber fixed our leak in an hour.","","Journeyman: someone whose long journey of learning is done."],["enchant","en-CHANT","verb","To fill with delight, as if by magic.","The puppet show enchanted the little ones.","From Latin incantare, “to chant a spell over.”","Enchant has “chant”: like a magic spell that delights."],["apprentice","uh-PREN-tis","noun","A person learning a trade by working with a skilled worker.","He spent a year as an apprentice to a plumber, learning the job.","","Apprentice comes from an old French word meaning to learn."],["billow","BIL-oh","verb","To swell and move in large waves.","The sheets billowed on the clothesline in the wind.","","Billow sounds like “pillow”: puffing up big and soft."],["devotee","dev-uh-TEE","noun","A big fan of something.","He is a devotee of morning walks.","","Devotee sounds like devoted: a devoted fan."],["bagatelle","bag-uh-TEL","noun","Something small and unimportant; a trifle.","To her, the extra chore was a mere bagatelle.","From Italian bagatella, “a trifle.”","Bagatelle sounds like “bag of tell”: a little bag of nothing."],["owlish","OW-lish","adjective","Looking wise or serious, like an owl, often with glasses.","With his big round glasses, my owlish uncle looked like a professor.","","Owlish has owl in it: wide-eyed and wise-looking."],["doldrums","DOHL-druhmz","noun","A state of dullness or low spirits.","A new puzzle pulled us out of the winter doldrums.","","Doldrums sounds like “dull drums”: dull and low."],["neophyte","NEE-uh-fyt","noun","A beginner at something.","As a neophyte baker, I burned my first batch of cookies.","From Greek neophytos, “newly planted.”","Neo means new, like a newbie."],["candor","KAN-der","noun","The quality of being open and honest.","I liked my doctor’s candor when she told me I needed more sleep.","From Latin candor, “whiteness, brightness.”","Candor sounds like “candid”: open and honest."],["xeriscape","ZEER-uh-skayp","noun","A garden designed to need very little water.","Our neighbors made a xeriscape of rocks and cactus so they never water the lawn.","From Greek xēros, “dry.”","Xeros is Greek for dry: a dry garden."],["estuary","ES-choo-er-ee","noun","The wide mouth of a river where it meets the sea.","We watched boats sail out of the estuary where the river meets the ocean.","From Latin aestuarium, “tidal place.”","Estuary sounds like “a stew” of river water and sea water."],["pathos","PAY-thos","noun","A quality that makes people feel pity or sadness.","The movie’s ending had real pathos, and we all cried.","From Greek pathos, “suffering.”","Pathos sounds like “pathetic,” in the old sense: it makes you feel pity."],["amicable","AM-i-kuh-buhl","adjective","Friendly and peaceful, without arguing.","The brothers reached an amicable deal about the last slice of pizza.","From Latin amicus, “friend.”","Ami is French for friend: settling things as friends."],["offhand","OF-hand","adjective","Said casually, without much thought.","Her offhand comment gave me a great idea.","","Said right off the hand, without stopping to think."],["adept","uh-DEPT","adjective","Very skilled at something.","My sister is adept at fixing anything on her phone.","From Latin adeptus, “having attained.”","Adept sounds like “a pro, deep in skill”: very good at it."],["unkempt","uhn-KEMPT","adjective","Messy and not cared for.","After the long road trip, our hair was wild and unkempt.","From Old English cemban, “to comb.”","Unkempt: “un” means not, and kempt means combed."],["affable","AF-uh-buhl","adjective","Friendly and easy to talk to.","Our affable mail carrier always stops to chat and smile.","From Latin affabilis, “easy to speak to.”","Affable sounds like “laughable”: easy to laugh and talk with."],["belittle","bih-LIT-l","verb","To make someone or something seem unimportant.","Never belittle a small act of kindness.","","Belittle: make it little."],["acrimony","AK-ruh-moh-nee","noun","Bitter and angry feelings between people.","The divorce was full of acrimony, with lots of yelling and blame.","","Acrimony sounds like “acid money”: bitter fights, often over money."],["aroma","uh-ROH-muh","noun","A pleasant smell.","The aroma of fresh bread filled the whole house.","From Greek aroma, “a spice.”","Aroma sounds like “a roam”: a smell that roams through the house."],["brook","BRUK","noun","A small stream.","We skipped stones across the little brook behind the house.","","Brook sounds like “book” of water: a small stream."],["daydream","DAY-dreem","verb","To let your mind drift to happy thoughts.","She daydreams about the beach during long meetings.","","Daydream: a dream you have while you’re awake in the day."],["keen","KEEN","adjective","Eager, or sharp.","My daughter is keen to learn how to ride a bike.","","Keen sounds like “keen bean”: eager and sharp."],["denizen","DEN-uh-zuhn","noun","A creature or person who lives in a certain place.","Raccoons are the noisy denizens of our garbage cans at night.","","Denizen sounds like citizen: someone who lives there."],["bramble","BRAM-buhl","noun","A prickly bush, like a blackberry bush.","I scratched my arm on a bramble while picking blackberries.","","Bramble sounds like “ramble”: a prickly bush that rambles everywhere."],["microcosm","MY-kruh-koz-uhm","noun","A small thing that shows what a bigger thing is like.","The school fair is a microcosm of our whole town: everyone shows up.","From Greek mikros, “small,” and kosmos, “world.”","Micro means small, and cosmos means world: a small world."],["vespertine","VES-per-tin","adjective","Having to do with the evening.","Our dog gets vespertine energy and runs around every evening.","From Latin vesper, “evening.”","Vesper is the evening star: anything of the evening."],["percolate","PUR-kuh-layt","verb","To slowly spread or filter through.","The idea percolated in her mind for days.","From Latin percolare, “to strain through.”","Like coffee slowly dripping through a coffee maker."],["paucity","PAW-sih-tee","noun","A shortage of something.","There was a paucity of chairs, so we sat on the floor.","From Latin paucus, “few.”","Paucity sounds like “poor city”: not enough to go around."],["thwart","THWORT","verb","To stop someone from doing what they planned.","The fence thwarted the dog’s plan to run into the street.","","Thwart sounds like “thwack”: a blow that stops a plan cold."],["scintilla","sin-TIL-uh","noun","A tiny trace or spark of something.","There wasn’t a scintilla of doubt that he would help.","From Latin scintilla, “a spark.”","Scintilla sounds like “sparkle”: just one tiny spark."],["heft","HEFT","noun","Weight or heaviness.","I felt the heft of the bag of dog food when I lifted it.","","Heft sounds like “hefty”: you feel how heavy it is."],["brio","BREE-oh","noun","Lively energy and spirit.","The band at the wedding played with great brio, and everyone danced.","From Italian brio, “liveliness.”","Brio sounds like “bright-o”: full of life and spark."],["divest","dy-VEST","verb","To get rid of something.","They divested themselves of clutter before the move.","","Divest: take off your vest, get rid of things."],["allot","uh-LOT","verb","To give out a share of something.","Each child was allotted one hour of screen time a day.","","Allot has “lot” inside: everyone gets their lot, their share."],["octave","OK-tiv","noun","The gap between one musical note and the next note of the same name.","She can sing two octaves higher than me.","From Latin octavus, “eighth.”","Oct means eight, like an octopus: eight notes up."],["belie","bih-LY","verb","To give a false picture of something; to hide what is true.","His calm face belied how nervous he felt about the job interview.","","Belie has “lie” inside: it tells a lie about what is true."],["colossal","kuh-LOS-uhl","adjective","Very, very large.","We ate a colossal plate of pancakes at the diner.","From Greek kolossos, “giant statue.”","Colossal sounds like colossus: a huge giant statue."],["maverick","MAV-er-ik","noun","A person who thinks and acts on their own, not like the crowd.","The maverick cook put fruit on the pizza, and everyone loved it.","","A maverick does it their own way, not the crowd’s way."],["baleful","BAYL-fuhl","adjective","Looking threatening or unfriendly.","The cat gave the dog a baleful stare from the couch.","","Baleful has “bale,” an old word for harm: full of harm."],["acerbic","uh-SUR-bik","adjective","Sharp and biting in a way that can sting.","Her acerbic comments about my cooking stung a little.","From Latin acerbus, “sour.”","Acerbic sounds like “acid”: words that sting like lemon juice."],["ponder","PON-der","verb","To think about something carefully.","I pondered which movie to watch next.","From Latin ponderare, “to weigh.”","Ponder sounds like pond: sit by a pond and think."],["benign","bih-NYN","adjective","Gentle, kind and harmless.","The big dog looks scary, but he is benign and loves kids.","From Latin benignus, “kindly.”","Bene means good, like a benefit: gentle and harmless."],["scrupulous","SKROO-pyuh-luhs","adjective","Very careful to do things right and honestly.","She was scrupulous about returning every borrowed dish.","","Scrupulous sounds like “scrub”: scrubbing every detail clean."],["willowy","WIL-oh-ee","adjective","Tall, slim and graceful.","The willowy dancer seemed to float across the stage.","","Willowy: tall and slim like a willow tree."],["fortuitous","for-TOO-i-tuhs","adjective","Happening by lucky chance.","Finding a twenty in my old coat was fortuitous.","From Latin fortuitus, “by chance.”","Fortuitous sounds like fortune: a lucky chance."],["bevel","BEV-uhl","noun","A slanted edge on wood, glass or stone.","The glass tabletop had a smooth beveled edge so no one got cut.","","Bevel sounds like “level” on a slant: a sloped edge."],["unbeknownst","uhn-bi-NOHNST","adjective","Without someone knowing.","Unbeknownst to Dad, we had planned a surprise party.","","Unbeknownst has “un” and “known”: not known to him."],["azure","AZH-er","adjective","Bright blue, like a clear sky.","We swam in the clear azure water on our beach vacation.","From Persian lazhward, “lapis lazuli.”","Azure sounds like “a sure blue sky”: bright, clear blue."],["elegy","EL-uh-jee","noun","A sad poem or song about someone or something lost.","At the funeral, my uncle read an elegy about Grandma’s kind heart.","From Greek elegeia, “a song of mourning.”","Elegy sounds like “L.E.G.,” a sad song for someone gone."],["serenade","ser-uh-NAYD","verb","To sing or play music for someone you care about.","The kids serenaded Mom with a song on her birthday.","","Serenade sounds like “serene song”: a sweet song for someone."],["sobriquet","SOH-bri-kay","noun","A nickname.","Her nickname in the family was “Sunny,” a sobriquet she loved.","From French sobriquet, “a nickname.”","Sobriquet sounds like “so bracket”: a name put in brackets next to yours."],["biophilia","by-oh-FIL-ee-uh","noun","A natural love of living things and nature.","Her biophilia shows in the dozens of plants in her apartment.","From Greek bios, “life,” and philia, “love.”","Bio means life, philia means love: love of living things."],["agape","uh-GAYP","adjective","Wide open, often in surprise.","The kids stood with mouths agape at the giant birthday cake.","","Agape has “gape”: a mouth wide open like a gap."],["contrarian","kuhn-TRAIR-ee-uhn","noun","A person who likes to take the opposite view.","My contrarian brother says winter is the best season just to argue.","","Contrarian has contrary in it: always the other way."],["bastion","BAS-chuhn","noun","A place or group that strongly protects an idea or way of life.","The family dinner table is a bastion of no-phone time.","","Bastion sounds like “bastille,” a fort: a strong place that protects."],["polyglot","POL-ee-glot","noun","A person who speaks many languages.","Our tour guide was a polyglot who spoke six languages.","From Greek polys, “many,” and glotta, “tongue.”","Poly means many, and glot means tongue: many tongues."],["malleable","MAL-ee-uh-buhl","adjective","Easy to shape or change.","Warm clay is malleable, so kids can shape it into anything.","From Latin malleus, “hammer.”","Malleable sounds like “mold-able,” easy to mold."],["heuristic","hyoo-RIS-tik","noun","A simple rule of thumb that helps you decide.","My heuristic for picking melons is to buy the heaviest one.","From Greek heuriskein, “to find.”","Heuristic comes from Greek “to find,” like “Eureka!”: a quick rule."],["stymie","STY-mee","verb","To block or get in the way of progress.","A missing puzzle piece stymied our plan to finish tonight.","","Stymie sounds like “stuck me”: something that gets you stuck."],["invigorate","in-VIG-uh-rayt","verb","To fill with energy.","A cold glass of water in the morning invigorates me.","From Latin vigor, “liveliness.”","Invigorate has “vigor” inside: it fills you with energy."],["nincompoop","NIN-kuhm-poop","noun","A silly, foolish person (said kindly).","I felt like a nincompoop when I wore two different shoes to work.","","Nincompoop is just fun to say, like a silly clown’s name."],["bemoan","bih-MOHN","verb","To complain or express sadness about something.","We bemoaned the end of summer vacation on the first school morning.","","Bemoan has “moan” inside: moaning about something sad."],["bleak","BLEEK","adjective","Cold, bare and gloomy.","The beach looked bleak in the cold winter fog.","","Bleak sounds like “weak” and dark: gray and gloomy."],["verve","VURV","noun","Lively energy and spirit.","She danced with great verve at the wedding.","","Verve sounds like “nerve” and “swerve”: bold, lively energy."],["chronicle","KRON-i-kuhl","noun","A record of events in the order they happened.","My mom’s photo albums are a chronicle of every birthday we had.","From Greek chronos, “time.”","Chron means time, like a chronic cough that lasts a long time."],["bizarre","bih-ZAR","adjective","Very strange and unusual.","We saw a bizarre cloud shaped exactly like a teapot.","From French bizarre, “odd.”","Bizarre sounds like “bazaar”: a market full of strange things."],["apropos","ap-ruh-POH","adjective","Fitting for the moment; related to what is being said.","Her joke about umbrellas was apropos, since it had just started pouring.","From French à propos, “to the purpose.”","Apropos sounds like “a proper pose”: just right for the moment."],["bolster","BOHL-ster","verb","To support or make stronger.","The coach’s pep talk bolstered the team’s spirits.","","A bolster is a long pillow that props you up."],["proximity","prok-SIM-ih-tee","noun","Being near something.","We love our home’s proximity to the park.","From Latin proximus, “nearest.”","Proximity sounds like approximate: close by."],["fettle","FET-l","noun","Condition or shape, as in good health.","After a good night’s sleep, Grandpa was in fine fettle.","","Fettle rhymes with kettle: a kettle in good shape whistles happily."],["offbeat","OF-beet","adjective","Unusual in a fun way.","He has an offbeat sense of humor that makes everyone laugh.","","Off the beat: not quite like the normal rhythm."],["mirth","MURTH","noun","Joy and laughter.","The room filled with mirth when the puppy sneezed.","","Mirth rhymes with birth: happy news brings laughter."],["baffle","BAF-uhl","verb","To confuse someone completely.","The math homework baffled both me and my son.","","Baffle sounds like “waffle”: you waffle back and forth, confused."],["wrought","RAWT","adjective","Made or shaped with care, often from metal.","The house had a beautiful wrought iron gate.","","Wrought is an old form of “worked”: worked by hand."],["culinary","KUHL-uh-ner-ee","adjective","Having to do with cooking or the kitchen.","His culinary skills turned leftovers into a feast.","From Latin culina, “kitchen.”","Culinary sounds like “kitchen-ary”: all about the kitchen."],["abscond","ab-SKOND","verb","To leave in a hurry and in secret, often with something not yours.","The puppy absconded with my sock and hid under the bed.","From Latin abscondere, “to hide away.”","Abscond sounds like “absent, gone”: the thief is absent in a second."],["dross","DRAWS","noun","Something worthless; junk.","We sorted the treasures from the dross at the yard sale.","","Dross sounds like gross: the junk nobody wants."],["modulate","MOJ-uh-layt","verb","To adjust or change the level of something.","She modulated her voice to a whisper in the library.","From Latin modulari, “to measure.”","Like the volume knob on a radio: turn it up or down."],["behest","bih-HEST","noun","A person's order or request.","At my mother’s behest, I wrote thank-you notes for every gift.","","Behest sounds like “request”: done because someone asked."],["verbose","ver-BOHS","adjective","Using more words than needed.","His verbose email could have been one line.","From Latin verbum, “word.”","Verbose has “verb”: way too many words."],["aglow","uh-GLOH","adjective","Glowing with light, warmth or happiness.","Her face was aglow when she held her new baby.","","Aglow has “glow” inside: shining with light or joy."],["halcyon","HAL-see-uhn","adjective","Calm, happy and peaceful, often about the past.","Mom talks about the halcyon summers she spent at the lake as a kid.","From Greek halkyon, “kingfisher.”","Halcyon sounds like “Hal, so calm”: peaceful happy days."],["complicit","kuhm-PLIS-it","adjective","Helping with something wrong.","By staying quiet, the twins were complicit in hiding the cookie jar.","","Complicit sounds like “accomplice”: a partner in the wrong."],["culminate","KUHL-muh-nayt","verb","To reach the high point or end.","The school year culminated in a big graduation party.","From Latin culmen, “summit.”","Culminate: you climb to the summit, the very top."],["bibliophile","BIB-lee-uh-fyl","noun","A person who loves books.","A true bibliophile, she has books in every room of her house.","From Greek biblion, “book,” and philos, “loving.”","Biblio means book, phile means lover: book lover."],["opulent","OP-yuh-luhnt","adjective","Rich, fancy and full of costly things.","The hotel lobby was opulent, with gold lamps and velvet chairs.","From Latin opulentus, “wealthy.”","Opulent sounds like “opal-lent”: full of jewels."],["banal","buh-NAL","adjective","So ordinary that it is boring.","The movie had a banal plot; we guessed the ending in five minutes.","From French banal, “common.”","Banal sounds like “bland-al”: boring and ordinary."],["maestro","MY-stroh","noun","A great master of an art, especially music.","Grandpa is a maestro at the grill, and every burger comes out perfect.","From Italian maestro, “master.”","Maestro sounds like “master-o,” and it means a master."],["fulcrum","FUL-kruhm","noun","The point a lever rests on, or the key part of something.","Mom is the fulcrum of our family; everything turns around her.","From Latin fulcire, “to prop up.”","Fulcrum: the spot a seesaw balances on in the middle."],["waft","WAFT","verb","To float gently through the air.","The smell of fresh bread wafted out of the bakery.","","Waft sounds like “wave”: a smell waving through the air."],["solitude","SOL-i-tood","noun","Being alone in a peaceful way.","He enjoyed the solitude of an empty house on Sunday morning.","From Latin solus, “alone.”","Solitude starts with “sol,” from Latin solus, meaning alone."],["repose","ri-POHZ","noun","A state of rest and calm.","After a busy day, Grandma sat in repose in her big chair.","","Repose has “pose”: you strike a resting pose."],["coterie","KOH-tuh-ree","noun","A small, close group of people with shared interests.","A coterie of moms meets at the coffee shop after school drop-off.","","Coterie sounds like “coat-tree”: a small group that hangs together."],["cajole","kuh-JOHL","verb","To gently talk someone into doing something.","She cajoled her little brother into eating his peas by making airplane noises.","From French cajoler, “to coax.”","Cajole sounds like “cage-a-goal”: sweet talk that gets you there."],["cavort","kuh-VORT","verb","To jump and play in a lively way.","The puppies cavorted in the backyard all afternoon.","","Cavort sounds like “cave-romp”: romping around in play."],["sojourn","SOH-jern","noun","A short stay somewhere.","Our sojourn at Grandma’s house lasted a happy week.","","Sojourn has “journ,” like journey: a short stop on a journey."],["kilter","KIL-ter","noun","Good balance or working order.","When I miss my morning coffee, my whole day is out of kilter.","","Kilter sounds like “tilt”: out of kilter is tilted, off balance."],["constituent","kuhn-STICH-oo-uhnt","noun","One of the parts that make up a whole.","Flour is a key constituent of bread.","","Constituent: the things that constitute, or build, the whole."],["plume","PLOOM","noun","A big feather, or a tall cloud of smoke or steam.","A plume of steam rose from the teapot.","From Latin pluma, “feather.”","Plume sounds like bloom: steam blooming up into the air."],["plaintive","PLAYN-tiv","adjective","Sounding sad.","We heard the plaintive cry of a lost kitten.","","Plaintive has “plain” and “complaint”: a sad complaining sound."],["meridian","muh-RID-ee-uhn","noun","The highest point, or a line on a map from pole to pole.","At the meridian of her career, she was training dozens of new nurses.","From Latin meridies, “midday.”","Meridian comes from midday, when the sun is at its highest."],["leeway","LEE-way","noun","Extra room or freedom.","The teacher gave us some leeway on the due date.","","Leeway: a little extra way, a little extra room."],["nutshell","NUT-shel","noun","A very short summary of something.","In a nutshell, the trip was wonderful.","","Small enough to fit inside a nutshell."],["scamper","SKAM-per","verb","To run with quick, light steps.","The kittens scampered after the ball of yarn.","","Scamper sounds like “scram”: quick little steps running off."],["genial","JEEN-yuhl","adjective","Friendly and cheerful.","Our genial mail carrier always waves and says hello.","","Genial sounds like “genie-al”: a friendly genie with a big smile."],["uncanny","uhn-KAN-ee","adjective","Strange in a surprising, hard-to-explain way.","She has an uncanny knack for knowing when I’m sad.","","Uncanny: you “can’t” explain it; strange and spooky."],["bungle","BUHNG-guhl","verb","To do something clumsily and badly.","I bungled the directions, and we ended up at the wrong store.","","Bungle sounds like “bungee tangle”: a clumsy mess."],["jitters","JIT-erz","noun","Nervous, shaky feelings.","I always get the jitters before a job interview.","","Jitters sounds like jittery jelly: shaking all over."],["archipelago","ar-kuh-PEL-uh-goh","noun","A group of islands close together.","On vacation, we took a ferry through an archipelago of small islands.","","Archipelago: picture many arches of islands dotting the sea."],["jape","JAYP","noun","A playful trick or joke.","Hiding Dad’s slippers was the kids’ favorite jape.","","Jape sounds like “ape”: a silly monkey trick."],["aloof","uh-LOOF","adjective","Distant and not friendly or involved.","The new kid stayed aloof at first, eating lunch alone.","","Aloof sounds like “a roof”: staying up and away from everyone."],["complement","KOM-pluh-ment","verb","To go well with something and make it better.","A cold drink complements a hot slice of pizza.","From Latin complere, “to fill up.”","Complement with an E completes something. Compliment with an I is praise."],["meld","MELD","verb","To blend together into one.","The flavors meld nicely if the soup sits in the fridge overnight.","","Meld sounds like melt: things melt together into one."],["perambulate","per-AM-byuh-layt","verb","To walk around in a slow, easy way.","We perambulated around the block after supper.","From Latin per, “through,” and ambulare, “to walk.”","Ambulate means walk, like an ambulance that comes to you."],["conversant","kuhn-VUR-suhnt","adjective","Familiar with and knowing about something.","She is conversant in three languages.","","Conversant sounds like conversation: you know enough to talk about it."],["diffident","DIF-i-duhnt","adjective","Shy and lacking confidence.","The diffident new student smiled when we waved her over.","","Diffident is the opposite of confident."],["fortnight","FORT-nyt","noun","Two weeks.","We’re going to Grandma’s for a fortnight this summer.","From Old English feowertiene niht, “fourteen nights.”","Fortnight is short for “fourteen nights”: two weeks."],["lethargy","LETH-er-jee","noun","A feeling of being slow and sleepy.","After Thanksgiving dinner, lethargy hit the whole family.","From Greek lethargia, “forgetfulness.”","Lethargy sounds like “lead-argy”: legs heavy as lead."],["hermit","HUR-mit","noun","A person who lives alone, away from others.","Our neighbor is a hermit who rarely leaves his house.","","Hermit sounds like “her mitt”: alone, hands tucked away at home."],["euphemism","YOO-fuh-miz-uhm","noun","A gentle word used in place of a harsh one.","“Passed away” is a gentle euphemism for “died.”","From Greek euphemismos, “use of good words.”","Eu means good, as in euphony: a good, soft way to say it."],["vehement","VEE-uh-muhnt","adjective","Showing very strong feeling.","She made a vehement case for getting a dog.","From Latin vehemens, “eager, violent.”","Vehement sounds like “vehicle”: full speed ahead with feeling."],["matriarch","MAY-tree-ark","noun","A woman who leads a family or group.","Our grandmother is the matriarch who hosts every holiday dinner.","From Latin mater, “mother,” and Greek archein, “to rule.”","Matri means mother, like maternity: the mother in charge."],["warble","WOR-buhl","verb","To sing with trills, like a bird.","My daughter warbled a song in the shower.","","Warble sounds like “wobble”: a wobbly, trilling song."],["pithy","PITH-ee","adjective","Short but full of meaning.","Her pithy advice was “Be kind, be brief.”","","Pithy sounds like “pith”: the core of the fruit, nothing extra."],["fraught","FRAWT","adjective","Filled with something, usually worry or trouble.","The drive to the airport was fraught with traffic and worry.","","Fraught sounds like “fought”: full of struggle and stress."],["tidings","TY-dingz","noun","News or messages.","The phone call brought glad tidings of a new baby.","From Old English tīdung, “news.”","Tidings sounds like “tide”: news washing in like the tide."],["nautical","NAW-tih-kuhl","adjective","Having to do with ships, sailors or the sea.","The beach house had a nautical theme with ropes and anchors.","From Greek nautes, “sailor.”","Nautical sounds like naughty sailor on a boat."],["analogy","uh-NAL-uh-jee","noun","A comparison that explains one thing by showing how it is like another.","She used an analogy: the heart works like a pump.","From Greek analogia, “proportion.”","Analogy sounds like “a la logic”: explaining by comparing."],["panorama","pan-uh-RAM-uh","noun","A wide view of a whole area.","From the top of the parking garage, we saw a panorama of the whole city.","From Greek pan, “all,” and horama, “view.”","Pan means all: a view of it all, like a phone panorama photo."],["acumen","AK-yuh-muhn","noun","The skill of making good, quick judgments.","Her business acumen helped her lemonade stand make real money.","From Latin acumen, “a point, sharpness.”","Acumen sounds like “a cue, man”: she always reads the cues right."],["moniker","MON-ih-ker","noun","A name or nickname.","His moniker at the bakery is “Mister Muffin.”","","Moniker sounds like “my nicker”: my nickname."],["congruent","KONG-groo-uhnt","adjective","In agreement or matching.","His actions were congruent with his promises, so we trusted him.","","Congruent shapes match exactly, like twin slices of bread."],["unctuous","UHNGK-choo-uhs","adjective","Too smooth and flattering to be sincere.","The unctuous car salesman praised every choice I made.","From Latin unctus, “oiled.”","Unctuous sounds like “oily”; it comes from a word for ointment."],["forbearance","for-BAIR-uhns","noun","Patience and self-control.","Mom showed great forbearance while the toddler spilled juice again.","","Forbearance: you forbear, you bear it, without getting mad."],["drizzle","DRIZ-uhl","noun","Light, fine rain.","We walked through the drizzle without umbrellas.","","Drizzle sounds like dribble: just a little rain."],["ardent","AR-duhnt","adjective","Full of strong, warm feeling; very eager.","She is an ardent fan of her son’s soccer team and never misses a game.","From Latin ardere, “to burn.”","Ardent sounds like “burnin’”: and it comes from Latin for burning."],["workaday","WURK-uh-day","adjective","Ordinary and everyday.","Our workaday dinner of rice and beans turned into a fun picnic.","","Workaday: like any work day, plain and normal."],["newfangled","NOO-fang-guhld","adjective","New and a bit strange or fussy.","Grandma laughed at the newfangled toaster with ten buttons.","","New and fangled: brand new and a little too fancy."],["knead","NEED","verb","To press and fold dough with your hands.","Mom kneaded the pizza dough on the counter.","","Knead sounds like need: you need to knead dough."],["negligible","NEG-lih-juh-buhl","adjective","So small it hardly matters.","The cost of the extra stamp was negligible.","","Negligible sounds like neglect: so small you can ignore it."],["mendacious","men-DAY-shuhs","adjective","Not telling the truth.","The mendacious note said the dog ate the homework, but we have no dog.","From Latin mendax, “lying.”","Mendacious sounds like “mend the truth”: patching it with lies."],["solicitous","suh-LIS-i-tuhs","adjective","Showing caring concern for someone.","The solicitous nurse made sure I had an extra blanket.","","Solicitous sounds like “so-lots-of-care”: very caring."],["adage","AD-ij","noun","An old, well-known saying that holds some truth.","As the old adage goes, many hands make light work.","","Adage sounds like “old age”: a saying that has lived a long time."],["formidable","FOR-mi-duh-buhl","adjective","Impressive and hard to beat.","The other team was formidable; they had not lost a game all year.","From Latin formido, “fear.”","Formidable comes from Latin for “fear”: a foe you respect."],["aperture","AP-er-cher","noun","An opening, hole or gap.","Light came in through a small aperture in the curtains.","From Latin apertura, “an opening.”","Aperture sounds like “open-ture”: an opening."],["clamor","KLAM-er","noun","A loud, steady noise from many voices.","The clamor of kids at recess could be heard down the street.","From Latin clamare, “to cry out.”","Clamor sounds like “clam roar”: a whole beach of loud clams."],["chasm","KAZ-uhm","noun","A deep crack in the earth, or a big gap between people.","A wide chasm opened between the two brothers after the fight over money.","From Greek chasma, “gaping hollow.”","Chasm sounds like “cast them apart”: a deep gap."],["tenable","TEN-uh-buhl","adjective","Able to be defended or believed.","Her plan to save for a car by June seemed tenable.","From Latin tenere, “to hold.”","Tenable sounds like “ten-able”: it holds up, a ten out of ten."],["dichotomy","dy-KOT-uh-mee","noun","A split between two opposite things.","There’s a dichotomy between what we want to buy and what we need.","From Greek dichotomia, “a cutting in two.”","Dicho means in two, in Greek. Tomy means cut. Cut in two."],["unassuming","uhn-uh-SOO-ming","adjective","Modest and not showy.","The unassuming little diner makes the best soup in town.","","Unassuming: it doesn’t assume it’s special, but it is."],["mutable","MYOO-tuh-buhl","adjective","Able to change.","Plans are mutable when you travel with small kids.","From Latin mutare, “to change.”","Mutable sounds like mutate: able to change."],["bouquet","boo-KAY","noun","A bunch of flowers; also a pleasant smell.","He brought his wife a bouquet of roses on their anniversary.","From French bouquet, “a little wood.”","Bouquet sounds like “boo-KAY”: a bunch of flowers says “okay, I love you.”"],["embellish","em-BEL-ish","verb","To add details or decoration to make something nicer.","Grandma likes to embellish her stories, so the fish gets bigger every time.","From Old French embellir, “to make beautiful.”","Embellish has “bell”: you add bells and bows to make it pretty."],["metamorphosis","met-uh-MOR-fuh-sis","noun","A big change in form, like a caterpillar to a butterfly.","The old garage went through a metamorphosis into a cozy art room.","From Greek meta, “change,” and morphe, “form.”","Morph means change shape: a total change of form."],["jot","JOT","verb","To write something down quickly.","I jotted the grocery list on a sticky note.","","Jot is short and quick, just like jotting a note."],["catapult","KAT-uh-puhlt","verb","To throw or launch something with great force.","The good news catapulted him off the couch.","","A catapult throws rocks far and fast."],["heyday","HAY-day","noun","The time of greatest success.","In its heyday, the old mall was packed every weekend.","","Heyday: the “hey, hey!” days when everything was great."],["marvel","MAR-vuhl","verb","To be filled with wonder at something.","We marveled at how fast the baby learned to walk.","From Latin mirabilia, “wonderful things.”","Marvel sounds like marble: stare at it like a shiny marble."],["meticulous","muh-TIK-yuh-luhs","adjective","Very careful about every small detail.","She is meticulous when she wraps gifts, with every corner perfectly folded.","","Meticulous sounds like “meh, tickle us”: fussy about every tiny bit."],["kestrel","KES-truhl","noun","A small falcon that hovers in the air.","A kestrel hovered over the field next to the highway.","","Kestrel sounds like “quest roll”: a small falcon hovering on a quest."],["needle","NEED-l","verb","To tease or annoy someone again and again.","My sister needles me about my messy room every day.","","Like poking someone with a needle again and again."],["didactic","dy-DAK-tik","adjective","Meant to teach a lesson.","The kids’ TV show is didactic, teaching a lesson in every episode.","From Greek didaskein, “to teach.”","Didactic comes from the Greek word for teach: always a lesson."],["truculent","TRUK-yuh-luhnt","adjective","Eager to argue or fight.","The truculent driver honked and yelled at everyone.","From Latin trux, “fierce.”","Truculent sounds like “truck-ulent”: rough and pushy as a truck."],["kindle","KIN-duhl","verb","To start a fire, or to stir up a feeling.","Reading to my son kindled his love of books.","","Kindle: like kindling, the small sticks that start a fire."],["languid","LANG-gwid","adjective","Slow and relaxed, without energy.","We spent a languid Sunday lying on the couch.","From Latin languere, “to be faint.”","Languid sounds like “lounging”: slow and lazy."],["cursory","KUR-suh-ree","adjective","Quick and not very careful.","He gave the room a cursory look and missed his keys on the couch.","","Cursory sounds like cursor: a quick swipe across, not a close look."],["engender","en-JEN-der","verb","To cause or bring about a feeling or situation.","Saying thank you to the bus driver engenders kindness in return.","From Latin ingenerare, “to beget.”","Engender sounds like “generate”: it causes something to grow."],["mammoth","MAM-uhth","adjective","Huge in size or amount.","Cleaning out the whole garage was a mammoth job.","","A woolly mammoth was huge, so a mammoth job is huge too."],["abhor","ab-HOR","verb","To hate something very much.","My son abhors broccoli and pushes it to the side of his plate.","From Latin abhorrere, “to shrink back from.”","Abhor sounds like “a horror”: something you hate like a scary movie."],["jejune","ji-JOON","adjective","Dull, or naive and childish.","His jejune jokes made the grown-ups roll their eyes.","From Latin jejunus, “fasting, dry.”","Jejune sounds like “just June”: young and childish."],["abound","uh-BOUND","verb","To be found in large numbers.","Toys abound in our living room, on the floor, the couch and the stairs.","From Latin abundare, “to overflow.”","Abound sounds like “a bounce”: things bouncing up everywhere."],["winsome","WIN-suhm","adjective","Charming in a sweet, innocent way.","The toddler gave a winsome smile to everyone in line.","From Old English wynsum, “pleasant.”","Winsome sounds like “win some”: a smile that wins hearts."],["buffoon","buh-FOON","noun","A person who acts silly to make others laugh.","Dad played the buffoon to cheer up the kids after a bad day.","From Italian buffone, “a jester.”","Buffoon sounds like “baboon”: acting silly for laughs."],["susurrus","soo-SUR-uhs","noun","A soft whispering or rustling sound.","The susurrus of the ceiling fan helped me fall asleep.","From Latin susurrus, “a whisper.”","Susurrus sounds like what it means: “sss-sss,” a soft whisper."],["gratuitous","gruh-TOO-i-tuhs","adjective","Not needed or not called for.","The movie had too much gratuitous yelling.","From Latin gratuitus, “free, done without pay.”","Gratuitous sounds like gratis, free: extra and not needed."],["vanguard","VAN-gard","noun","The front part of a group, leading the way.","Our school is in the vanguard of using tablets in class.","","Vanguard: the van at the front, guarding the way."],["adrift","uh-DRIFT","adjective","Floating without being steered, or feeling lost and without aim.","After he lost his job, he felt adrift and unsure what to do next.","","Adrift has “drift”: floating along with no one steering."],["omniscient","om-NISH-uhnt","adjective","Knowing everything.","Mom seems omniscient; she always knows when I skip my vegetables.","From Latin omnis, “all,” and sciens, “knowing.”","Omni means all, and sci means know, like science."],["hearth","HARTH","noun","The floor of a fireplace, or the home itself.","We hung the stockings over the hearth.","","Hearth has “heart” inside: the warm heart of a home."],["din","DIN","noun","A loud, constant noise.","We could barely talk over the din of the busy restaurant.","","Din sounds like dinner: the noisy clatter of a big family dinner."],["devolve","dih-VOLV","verb","To pass down to someone, or to slowly get worse.","The pillow fight devolved into a fit of giggles.","","Devolve is the opposite of evolve: things slide downhill."],["rotund","roh-TUND","adjective","Round and plump.","Our rotund cat waddles to his food bowl.","From Latin rotundus, “round.”","Rotund sounds like “round”: nice and round."],["blasé","blah-ZAY","adjective","Not excited or impressed because you have seen it before.","The teenager was blasé about the roller coaster; “I’ve done it before.”","From French blasé, “tired of pleasure.”","Blasé sounds like “bla, eh”: so what, not impressed."],["neighborly","NAY-ber-lee","adjective","Friendly and helpful, like a good neighbor.","It was neighborly of them to shovel our sidewalk.","","It has the word neighbor in it."],["facetious","fuh-SEE-shuhs","adjective","Joking at a time when you should be serious.","He was being facetious when he said he would eat the whole cake.","From Latin facetia, “a jest.”","Facetious sounds like “face-tious”: a joking face when you should be serious."],["diplomacy","dih-PLOH-muh-see","noun","Skill in handling people kindly and wisely.","It took diplomacy to settle who got the window seat.","","A diplomat keeps peace between countries, kindly and wisely."],["lattice","LAT-is","noun","A crisscross pattern of strips.","Mom made a lattice crust on top of the apple pie.","","Lattice sounds like “let us crisscross”: crisscross strips."],["jumble","JUM-buhl","noun","A messy mix.","The laundry came out of the dryer in one big jumble.","","Jumble sounds like tumble: everything tossed together."],["indelible","in-DEL-uh-buhl","adjective","Impossible to remove or forget.","Her kindness left an indelible mark on our family.","From Latin indelebilis, “not able to be wiped out.”","Indelible: in means not, so it can’t be deleted."],["kvetch","KVECH","verb","To complain a lot.","My uncle loves to kvetch about the price of gas.","From Yiddish kvetshn, “to squeeze.”","Kvetch sounds like “kvetch-kvetch”: a squeaky wheel of complaints."],["zinger","ZING-er","noun","A quick, clever, funny line.","Mom ended the argument with a friendly zinger.","","Zinger: a line that zings, quick and sharp."],["tacit","TAS-it","adjective","Understood without being said out loud.","We had a tacit agreement that Dad gets the last cookie.","From Latin tacitus, “silent.”","Tacit sounds like “tacked it”: tacked down without a word."],["ablaze","uh-BLAYZ","adjective","Burning brightly or glowing with light or color.","At Christmas, our street is ablaze with bright lights on every house.","","Ablaze has “blaze” inside it, a big bright fire."],["bustle","BUHS-uhl","noun","Busy, noisy activity.","We loved the bustle of the grocery store on a Saturday morning.","","Bustle sounds like “hustle”: busy and on the move."],["conundrum","kuh-NUHN-druhm","noun","A hard problem or puzzle.","Which bill to pay first was a real conundrum this month.","","Conundrum sounds like “a puzzle drum”: it keeps banging in your head."],["nocturnal","nok-TUR-nl","adjective","Active at night.","Our cat is nocturnal and races around the house at night.","From Latin nox, “night.”","Noct means night, like a night light."],["fervent","FUR-vuhnt","adjective","Showing strong and warm feeling.","She is a fervent fan who never misses her team’s games.","From Latin fervere, “to boil.”","Fervent sounds like “fever”: hot with strong feeling."],["desist","dih-ZIST","verb","To stop doing something.","Please desist from tapping your pencil during the test.","","Desist sounds like “de-sit”: stop it and sit still."],["disquisition","dis-kwuh-ZISH-uhn","noun","A long, detailed talk or essay on a topic.","Dad gave a long disquisition on how to make the perfect grilled cheese.","","Disquisition sounds like inquisition: a long, deep look at one topic."],["judder","JUD-er","verb","To shake hard and fast.","The old washing machine judders loudly on spin.","","Judder sounds like shudder: shaking hard and fast."],["quell","KWEL","verb","To calm something down or put an end to it.","Grandpa’s soft song quelled the baby’s crying.","From Old English cwellan, “to kill.”","Quell sounds like “quiet well”: you make things quiet."],["conjure","KON-jer","verb","To bring something to mind, as if by magic.","The smell of cinnamon rolls conjures memories of Grandma’s kitchen.","","A magician conjures a rabbit out of a hat."],["effusive","ih-FYOO-siv","adjective","Showing feelings in a big, gushing way.","She was effusive in her thanks, hugging us three times for the cake.","From Latin effundere, “to pour out.”","Effusive pours out, like a spilled jug of sweet words."],["bent","BENT","noun","A natural talent or liking for something.","She has an artistic bent and doodles on every napkin.","","A bent is the way you naturally lean, like a bent tree."],["kiosk","KEE-osk","noun","A small stand that sells things or gives information.","I bought a phone charger at a kiosk in the mall.","From Turkish köşk, “pavilion.”","Kiosk sounds like “key ask”: a little booth where you ask for help."],["defunct","dih-FUHNGKT","adjective","No longer existing or working.","The defunct video rental store is now a coffee shop.","From Latin defunctus, “finished.”","Defunct: no longer functioning."],["woebegone","WOH-bi-gon","adjective","Looking very sad.","The puppy gave a woebegone look when we left.","","Woebegone has “woe,” which means sadness."],["disseminate","dih-SEM-uh-nayt","verb","To spread information widely.","The school disseminates news to parents through emails.","From Latin semen, “seed.”","Disseminate comes from the Latin for seed: you scatter news like seeds."],["toothsome","TOOTH-suhm","adjective","Tasty and pleasing to eat.","The bakery sells toothsome cinnamon rolls.","","Toothsome: something your teeth love."],["jostle","JOS-uhl","verb","To push and bump in a crowd.","Shoppers jostled each other at the big holiday sale.","","Jostle sounds like “joust”: bumping each other in a crowd."],["headlong","HED-lawng","adverb","Head first, or in a rush without thinking.","The toddler ran headlong into the pile of pillows.","","Headlong: head first, long before you think."],["flit","FLIT","verb","To move quickly and lightly.","My little girl flits from toy to toy, never staying long.","","Flit rhymes with quick little hit: here, there, gone."],["dudgeon","DUHJ-uhn","noun","A feeling of being offended, often called “high dudgeon” when strong.","He left in high dudgeon when we ate his fries.","","Dudgeon sounds like grudge on: you storm off holding a grudge."],["hummock","HUM-uhk","noun","A small, rounded hill or mound.","The kids rolled down the little grassy hummock in the park.","","Hummock sounds like “hump”: a little bump of a hill."],["latitude","LAT-i-tood","noun","Freedom to choose how to act.","Our boss gives us latitude to work from home on Fridays.","From Latin latitudo, “breadth.”","Latitude: wide room to move, like wide lines on a map."],["mnemonic","nih-MON-ik","noun","A trick that helps you remember something.","My teacher gave us a mnemonic to remember the order of the colors.","From Greek mneme, “memory.”","Mnemonic has a silent M, and it helps memory."],["blurt","BLURT","verb","To say something suddenly without thinking.","My little brother blurted out the birthday surprise.","","Blurt sounds like “burp”: it just pops out."],["raffish","RAF-ish","adjective","Carefree and a bit unconventional in a charming way.","Uncle Joe has a raffish smile and wears his hat tipped to the side.","","Raffish sounds like “raff fish”: a cool, carefree fish."],["minuscule","MIN-uh-skyool","adjective","Very, very small.","There was a minuscule crumb left on the plate.","","Minuscule starts like “mini”: very tiny."],["supercilious","soo-per-SIL-ee-uhs","adjective","Acting as if you are better than others.","The supercilious waiter rolled his eyes at our simple order.","From Latin supercilium, “eyebrow.”","Supercilious starts with “super”: acting super, better than everyone."],["befuddle","bih-FUHD-l","verb","To confuse someone.","The new TV remote befuddled Grandpa.","","Befuddle sounds like “be muddled”: all mixed up."],["comely","KUHM-lee","adjective","Pleasant to look at.","The comely little house had a red door and fresh white paint.","","Comely sounds like “come-lee”: so pretty you want to come look."],["outlier","OUT-ly-er","noun","Something very different from the rest of its group.","The one sunny day was an outlier in a rainy month.","","An outlier lies out, far from the rest of the group."],["atone","uh-TOHN","verb","To make up for a mistake or wrong.","He atoned for forgetting her birthday by baking her a cake.","From the phrase at one, “in harmony.”","Atone is “at one”: making up so you are at one again."],["bonny","BON-ee","adjective","Pretty, healthy and pleasing to see.","“What a bonny baby,” the neighbor said with a smile.","","Bonny comes from “bon,” French for good: good to look at."],["covert","KOH-vert","adjective","Secret or hidden.","The kids ran a covert mission to bake Dad a birthday cake.","","Covert sounds like “covered”: kept under cover."],["tangential","tan-JEN-shuhl","adjective","Only loosely connected to the main topic.","His tangential story about his cat made us forget the question.","From Latin tangere, “to touch.”","Tangential sounds like “tangent”: going off to the side."],["banter","BAN-ter","noun","Friendly, playful joking talk.","The cooks kept up a cheerful banter in the kitchen.","","Banter sounds like “bounce chatter”: playful back-and-forth talk."],["smitten","SMIT-n","adjective","Completely charmed by someone or something.","The whole family was smitten with the new puppy.","","Smitten sounds like “mitten”: warm and wrapped up in love."],["prudent","PROOD-nt","adjective","Wise and careful about the future.","It’s prudent to bring a jacket in case it gets cold.","From Latin prudens, “foreseeing.”","Prudent sounds like “prude dent”: careful not to dent the car."],["morass","muh-RAS","noun","A messy situation that is hard to get out of.","We got stuck in a morass of paperwork when we bought the house.","","Morass sounds like “more mess”: a mess you sink into."],["daub","DAWB","verb","To smear paint or something soft roughly on a surface.","The toddler daubed paint all over the paper.","","Daub sounds like “dab”: dab, dab, dab of paint."],["pragmatic","prag-MAT-ik","adjective","Practical and focused on what works.","A pragmatic packer brings only what she really needs.","From Greek pragma, “a deed.”","Pragmatic sounds like practical."],["discord","DIS-kord","noun","Disagreement or lack of harmony.","Discord over the TV remote ended with a coin toss.","From Latin discors, “not of one heart.”","Discord: the chords are out of tune."],["noggin","NOG-in","noun","A playful word for your head.","Use your noggin and wear a hat in the cold.","","Noggin sounds like “knock in”: knock on your head."],["vernacular","ver-NAK-yuh-ler","noun","The everyday language people speak in a place.","The tour guide spoke in the local vernacular, so we got a few words wrong.","From Latin vernaculus, “native.”","Vernacular sounds like “very regular”: the regular way people talk."],["demystify","dee-MIS-tuh-fy","verb","To make something easier to understand.","Her video demystified how to change a car tire.","","Demystify: take the mystery out."],["passé","pa-SAY","adjective","Out of date and no longer in fashion.","My teen says my jeans are passé.","From French passé, “past.”","Passé (pa-SAY) means past: its time has passed."],["quaint","KWAYNT","adjective","Charming in an old-fashioned way.","The little coffee shop with old teacups on the wall felt quaint.","From Old French cointe, “clever, pretty.”","Quaint sounds like “paint”: an old cottage with fresh, cute paint."],["citadel","SIT-uh-dl","noun","A fortress on high ground protecting a city.","From the old citadel on the hill, we could see the whole town.","","Citadel sounds like city: the fort that guards the city."],["ragamuffin","RAG-uh-muf-in","noun","A messy, scruffy child in old clothes.","The kids came home from the muddy park looking like ragamuffins.","","Ragamuffin starts with “rag”: a kid dressed in rags and mud."],["debilitate","dih-BIL-i-tayt","verb","To make someone or something weak.","The flu debilitated him for a whole week.","From Latin debilis, “weak.”","Debilitate: takes away your ability."],["zest","ZEST","noun","Great enjoyment and energy, or the grated peel of a lemon or orange.","Grandpa still has a real zest for life.","From French zeste, “citrus peel.”","Zest: like lemon zest, it adds zing and flavor."],["digress","dy-GRES","verb","To wander off the main topic.","Grandpa often digresses into stories about his old job.","From Latin digredi, “to step aside.”","Digress: you go off the path, away from the point."],["spellbound","SPEL-bownd","adjective","So amazed you cannot look away.","The kids sat spellbound during the puppet show.","","Spellbound: as if bound by a magic spell."],["avuncular","uh-VUHNG-kyuh-ler","adjective","Kind and friendly, like a favorite uncle.","The avuncular shopkeeper always slipped us a free candy.","From Latin avunculus, “mother's brother.”","Avunculus is Latin for uncle: kind like an uncle."],["paradigm","PAIR-uh-dym","noun","A model or a usual way of thinking about something.","Working from home changed the whole paradigm of office life.","From Greek paradeigma, “pattern.”","Paradigm sounds like “pair of dimes”: the usual way to count things."],["blithe","BLYTH","adjective","Cheerful and carefree, sometimes too much so.","She hummed with blithe cheer as she cooked dinner.","","Blithe sounds like “bright”: cheerful and carefree."],["bookish","BOOK-ish","adjective","Fond of reading and study.","The bookish boy always had a novel in his backpack.","","Bookish: book-ish, always with a book."],["paradox","PAIR-uh-doks","noun","Something that seems to go against itself but may be true.","It’s a paradox that the more I rest, the more energy I have.","From Greek para, “beyond,” and doxa, “opinion.”","Para-dox sounds like “pair of docs” who disagree but are both right."],["deduce","dih-DOOS","verb","To figure something out from clues.","From the crumbs on his shirt, we deduced who ate the cookies.","From Latin deducere, “to lead down.”","A detective deduces who did it from the clues."],["amiable","AY-mee-uh-buhl","adjective","Friendly and pleasant.","Our amiable neighbor waves and says hello every morning.","","Ami is French for friend: friendly and pleasant."],["chrysalis","KRIS-uh-lis","noun","The hard case a caterpillar makes before it turns into a butterfly.","The kids watched the class butterfly break out of its chrysalis.","From Greek chrysos, “gold.”","Chrysalis comes from Greek for gold: some are shiny gold."],["prolific","pruh-LIF-ik","adjective","Making a lot of something.","The prolific writer finished a new book every year.","","Prolific sounds like “pro-lots”: making lots and lots."],["berate","bih-RAYT","verb","To scold someone angrily.","The manager berated the worker in front of the customers.","","Berate has “rate”: rating someone badly, loudly."],["pseudonym","SOOD-n-im","noun","A made-up name an author uses.","She wrote mystery novels under a pseudonym.","From Greek pseudes, “false,” and onoma, “name.”","Pseudo means fake, and nym means name."],["refulgent","ri-FUL-juhnt","adjective","Shining very brightly.","The refulgent glow of the Christmas lights lit up the whole street.","From Latin refulgere, “to shine back.”","Refulgent sounds like “full gent”: a man who shines full bright."],["placid","PLAS-id","adjective","Calm and peaceful.","The pool was placid early in the morning before anyone jumped in.","From Latin placidus, “gentle.”","Placid sounds like “plastic”: smooth and still."],["gilded","GIL-did","adjective","Covered with a thin layer of gold.","The old picture frame was gilded and shone in the light.","","Gilded sounds like “gold-ed”: covered in a thin coat of gold."],["besotted","bih-SOT-id","adjective","Completely charmed or in love.","The whole family is besotted with the new kitten.","","Besotted sounds like “be-spotted”: you only spot your new love."],["makeshift","MAYK-shift","adjective","Made quickly from whatever is around, for now.","We made a makeshift tent from blankets and chairs in the living room.","","Make it, then shift it: built fast for now."],["annals","AN-uhlz","noun","Records of events year by year; history.","That big snowstorm went down in the annals of our town.","From Latin annus, “year.”","Annals sounds like “annual”: records kept year by year."],["garner","GAR-ner","verb","To gather or collect.","The bake sale garnered enough money for new playground swings.","","Garner sounds like “gather”: collect it up."],["commensurate","kuh-MEN-ser-it","adjective","Matching in size or amount.","Her pay raise was commensurate with all her extra hours.","","Commensurate has measure in it: they measure up the same."],["belabor","bih-LAY-ber","verb","To talk about something far too long.","Let’s not belabor the point; we all agree on pizza.","","Belabor has “labor,” hard work: working a point too hard."],["frugal","FROO-guhl","adjective","Careful not to waste money or things.","My frugal grandma reuses every plastic bag and jar.","From Latin frugalis, “thrifty.”","Frugal sounds like “frog-al”: a little frog who saves every penny."],["ludicrous","LOO-di-kruhs","adjective","So silly it is laughable.","Paying ten dollars for a bottle of water is ludicrous.","From Latin ludus, “play.”","Ludicrous sounds like “loony-crous”: so silly you laugh."],["durable","DOOR-uh-buhl","adjective","Able to last a long time.","These durable boots have lasted ten winters.","From Latin durare, “to last.”","Durable sounds like endure: it lasts and lasts."],["voluble","VOL-yuh-buhl","adjective","Talking easily and a lot.","My voluble aunt can chat for hours.","From Latin volvere, “to roll.”","Voluble sounds like “volume-ble”: talking at full volume, nonstop."],["stalwart","STAWL-wert","adjective","Loyal, strong and reliable.","He has been a stalwart helper at the food bank for years.","","Stalwart has “stal,” like stall: a strong post that always stays."],["deride","dih-RYD","verb","To make fun of in a mean way.","Nobody should deride a beginner for trying.","From Latin deridere, “to laugh at.”","Deride has ride in it: they ride on you with mean jokes."],["nadir","NAY-der","noun","The lowest point of something.","Getting the flu on vacation was the nadir of our year.","From Arabic nazir, “opposite.”","Nadir sounds like “nada”: down at nothing, the very bottom."],["urbane","er-BAYN","adjective","Polished, polite and at ease with people.","The urbane host made every guest feel welcome.","From Latin urbanus, “of the city.”","Urbane sounds like “urban”: polished like a city gentleman."],["vicarious","vy-KAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Felt through someone else’s experience.","I got vicarious joy from watching my son learn to ride a bike.","From Latin vicarius, “substitute.”","Vicarious sounds like “via carrier”: you feel it via someone else."],["mischievous","MIS-chuh-vuhs","adjective","Playful in a way that causes small trouble.","The mischievous kitten hid all my socks under the couch.","","Mischief means small trouble, so mischievous kids cause it."],["dogmatic","dawg-MAT-ik","adjective","Sure your opinions are right and unwilling to listen.","Don’t be so dogmatic about how to fold towels.","","Dogmatic: like a stubborn dog that won’t budge."],["embolden","em-BOHL-duhn","verb","To give someone courage.","Her mom’s cheering emboldened her to jump off the diving board.","","Embolden puts “bold” inside you: it makes you brave."],["alacrity","uh-LAK-ri-tee","noun","Cheerful eagerness and quickness.","The kids cleaned their rooms with alacrity when they heard about ice cream.","From Latin alacer, “lively.”","Alacrity sounds like “alert-city”: quick and eager."],["dewy","DOO-ee","adjective","Wet with dew; fresh.","We walked barefoot across the dewy grass in the morning.","","Dewy has dew in it: little water drops in the morning."],["cognizant","KOG-nuh-zuhnt","adjective","Aware of something.","Be cognizant of the time so we don’t miss the bus.","","Cognizant is related to recognize: you know it’s there."],["puckish","PUK-ish","adjective","Playful and full of harmless mischief.","He gave a puckish grin before the prank.","","Picture a hockey puck bouncing around, causing playful trouble."],["yonder","YON-der","adverb","Over there, at a distance.","Look at the ice cream truck parked yonder by the park.","","Yonder sounds like “yon, over there”: way over there."],["affluent","AF-loo-uhnt","adjective","Having a lot of money and comfort.","The affluent family had three cars and a pool.","From Latin affluere, “to flow toward.”","Affluent has “fluent” like flowing: money flows in."],["distill","dih-STIL","verb","To boil something down to its most important part.","She distilled the long email into three points.","From Latin destillare, “to drip down.”","Distill: like boiling water down to only the best part."],["iridescent","ir-i-DES-uhnt","adjective","Showing shifting rainbow colors.","The soap bubbles were iridescent in the sunlight.","From Greek iris, “rainbow.”","Iridescent comes from Greek “iris,” rainbow: rainbow colors."],["exhilarate","ig-ZIL-uh-rayt","verb","To make someone feel very happy and full of energy.","The roller coaster ride exhilarated the kids.","From Latin exhilarare, “to make cheerful.”","Exhilarate sounds like “hilarious”: it fills you with happy energy."],["attune","uh-TOON","verb","To make aware of and in tune with something.","Over time, she became attuned to her baby’s different cries.","","Attune has “tune”: getting in tune with something."],["hubris","HYOO-bris","noun","Too much pride.","His hubris made him skip the map, and we got lost.","From Greek hybris, “excess pride.”","Hubris sounds like “huge brows”: raised high with too much pride."],["disenchant","dis-en-CHANT","verb","To make someone lose a good feeling about something.","The long lines disenchanted us with the theme park.","","Disenchant: the magic spell is broken."],["abode","uh-BOHD","noun","The place where someone lives; a home.","“Welcome to my humble abode,” Dad joked as he opened our front door.","","Abode sounds like “a boat”: some people make a boat their home."],["collegial","kuh-LEE-jee-uhl","adjective","Friendly and cooperative among coworkers.","The office had a collegial mood, with everyone helping each other.","","Collegial sounds like colleague: friendly with coworkers."],["aria","AR-ee-uh","noun","A song sung by one voice, often in an opera.","The singer’s aria on the TV show made the whole audience cry.","From Italian aria, “air.”","Aria sounds like “air-ia”: one voice singing a beautiful air, or tune."],["obsequious","uhb-SEE-kwee-uhs","adjective","Too eager to please or obey.","The obsequious waiter refilled our water every single minute.","From Latin obsequi, “to comply.”","Obsequious sounds like “oh, sequel, yes”: always says yes."],["brindle","BRIN-dl","adjective","Brownish or gray with darker streaks, used for animals.","Our brindle dog has brown fur with dark stripes, like a tiger.","","Brindle sounds like “brown kindle”: brown with darker streaks."],["meander","mee-AN-der","verb","To wander slowly with no set path.","We meandered through the mall with no plan, just looking around.","From Greek Maiandros, a winding river.","Meander sounds like “me wander”: I wander slowly."],["delegate","DEL-i-gayt","verb","To give part of your work to someone else.","Mom delegated the dishes to us.","","Delegate sounds like “dele-gate”: you hand work through the gate to someone else."],["duplicity","doo-PLIS-i-tee","noun","Deceiving people by saying one thing and doing another.","The friend’s duplicity came out when we saw what he said behind our backs.","From Latin duplex, “double.”","Duplicity has duplicate in it: two faces."],["flux","FLUKS","noun","Constant change.","With the new baby, our sleep schedule is in constant flux.","From Latin fluxus, “flowing.”","Flux sounds like “flows”: always flowing and changing."],["capstone","KAP-stohn","noun","The final, crowning achievement.","Graduating from college was the capstone of her years of hard work.","","A capstone is the top stone that caps off a wall."],["verdant","VUR-dnt","adjective","Green with lots of growing plants.","The park was verdant after a week of spring rain.","","Verdant sounds like “very green”; it comes from the French word for green."],["askance","uh-SKANS","adverb","With doubt or distrust, often looking sideways.","The cat looked askance at the new robot vacuum.","","Askance sounds like “a side glance”: a doubtful look sideways."],["nudge","NUJ","verb","To push gently, or to remind softly.","She nudged me to call my grandmother on her birthday.","","A nudge is a gentle elbow poke."],["bohemian","boh-HEE-mee-uhn","adjective","Living in a free, artistic and unusual way.","The painter’s bohemian apartment was full of plants and colorful rugs.","","Bohemian: think of a free spirit who lives by her own rules."],["gaffe","GAF","noun","A small, awkward social mistake.","I made a gaffe when I called my boss “Mom.”","From French gaffe, “blunder.”","Gaffe sounds like “laugh”: an awkward slip that makes people laugh."],["compendium","kuhm-PEN-dee-uhm","noun","A collection of facts or works in one place.","Her recipe binder is a compendium of family favorites.","","Compendium sounds like “compiled”: lots of things gathered in one book."],["celestial","suh-LES-chuhl","adjective","Having to do with the sky or the stars.","On a clear night, the moon and stars put on a celestial show.","From Latin caelum, “sky.”","Celestial sounds like “ceiling”: things up on the sky’s ceiling."],["lament","luh-MENT","verb","To show sadness or regret.","Dad lamented that his favorite diner had closed.","From Latin lamentum, “a wailing.”","Lament sounds like “lame-ent”: sad and sorry it happened."],["burnish","BUR-nish","verb","To polish until shiny.","He burnished the old brass doorknob until it gleamed.","","Burnish sounds like “burn shine”: rub until it shines."],["zilch","ZILCH","noun","Nothing at all.","I looked for my keys and found zilch.","","Zilch sounds like “zero”: nothing at all."],["desolate","DES-uh-lit","adjective","Empty, lonely and bare.","The mall parking lot felt desolate at midnight.","","Desolate has sole in it: all alone."],["anachronism","uh-NAK-ruh-niz-uhm","noun","Something that seems to belong to a different time.","A rotary phone in a modern home feels like an anachronism.","From Greek ana, “back,” and chronos, “time.”","Chronos means time: something stuck in the wrong time."],["chastise","chas-TYZ","verb","To scold someone sharply.","The coach chastised the team for showing up late.","","Chastise sounds like “chase-ties”: a parent chasing you to scold."],["obscure","uhb-SKYOOR","adjective","Not well known, or hard to understand.","He loves obscure facts about bugs that most people never hear.","From Latin obscurus, “dark.”","Obscure sounds like “ob-secure”: hidden and locked away."],["pulchritude","PUL-krih-tood","noun","Physical beauty.","The bride was a picture of pulchritude.","From Latin pulcher, “beautiful.”","Funny twist: this lumpy-sounding word means beauty."],["breadth","BREDTH","noun","Width; also wide range or scope.","The breadth of the menu was amazing; they had over a hundred dishes.","","Breadth is like “broad”: how wide something is."],["peccadillo","pek-uh-DIL-oh","noun","A small, harmless fault.","Eating dessert first is my favorite peccadillo.","From Spanish pecadillo, “small fault.”","Peccadillo sounds like armadillo: a small, harmless critter of a fault."],["recalcitrant","ri-KAL-si-truhnt","adjective","Stubborn and hard to control.","The recalcitrant toddler would not put on his shoes.","From Latin recalcitrare, “to kick back.”","Recalcitrant sounds like “recall, can’t”: won’t listen no matter how many times."],["carapace","KAR-uh-pays","noun","The hard shell on the back of a turtle or crab.","The turtle pulled its head inside its carapace.","","Carapace sounds like “car-a-place”: a turtle’s car-shaped shell to hide in."],["deign","DAYN","verb","To agree to do something you think is beneath you.","The cat finally deigned to sit on my lap.","From Latin dignari, “to think worthy.”","Deign sounds like “dane”: a royal who barely bends down to you."],["frivolous","FRIV-uh-luhs","adjective","Silly and not serious or important.","Buying a fourth phone case felt frivolous.","From Latin frivolus, “silly.”","Frivolous sounds like “frilly”: fussy, silly and not important."],["wrangle","RANG-guhl","verb","To argue, or to round up and control something.","It took ten minutes to wrangle the kids into the car.","","Wrangle sounds like “wrangler,” a cowboy who rounds up horses."],["gravitas","GRAV-i-tahs","noun","A serious, dignified manner.","The judge spoke with gravitas, and the room went quiet.","From Latin gravitas, “weight.”","Gravitas sounds like gravity: a heavy, serious presence."],["kibitz","KIB-its","verb","To give advice nobody asked for, especially while watching a game.","Grandpa likes to kibitz while we play cards.","From Yiddish kibitsen.","Kibitz sounds like “kid bits”: little bits of advice no one asked for."],["polymath","POL-ee-math","noun","A person who knows a lot about many subjects.","The polymath next door can fix cars and play the cello.","From Greek polymathes, “having learned much.”","Poly means many, and math means learning: many skills."],["virtuoso","vur-choo-OH-soh","noun","A person with amazing skill, especially in music.","The young piano virtuoso played without any sheet music.","From Italian virtuoso, “skilled.”","Virtuoso sounds like “virtue-oh-so-good”: oh so good at it."],["paltry","PAWL-tree","adjective","Very small and not worth much.","He won a paltry prize of one sticker, but he was proud.","","Paltry sounds like poultry: worth about as much as a chicken feather."],["impasse","IM-pas","noun","A point where no progress can be made.","We reached an impasse about where to go for dinner.","From French impasse, “dead end.”","Impasse: “im-pass,” you can’t pass. A dead end."],["perpetual","per-PECH-oo-uhl","adjective","Never ending or changing.","Our little brother is in perpetual motion, never sitting still.","From Latin perpetuus, “continuing.”","Perpetual sounds like “purr forever”: it never stops."],["commiserate","kuh-MIZ-uh-rayt","verb","To share in someone’s sadness or trouble.","We commiserated over how hard it is to get toddlers to sleep.","From Latin miser, “wretched.”","Com means with. Miser is like misery. Share the misery with someone."],["coerce","koh-URS","verb","To force someone to do something.","You can’t coerce a cat into a bath.","","Coerce sounds like “co-force”: forcing someone to go along."],["adjunct","AJ-uhngkt","noun","Something added to a main thing but not essential to it.","The after-school club is an adjunct to the regular school day.","","Adjunct sounds like “add junk”: an extra piece attached on."],["dapper","DAP-er","adjective","Neat and stylish in dress.","Grandpa looked dapper in his bow tie at the wedding.","","Dapper sounds like “dad’s dapper”: Dad all dressed up sharp."],["respite","RES-pit","noun","A short rest from something tiring.","A cup of tea gave me a short respite from cleaning the house.","","Respite has “rest” at the start: a short rest."],["brittle","BRIT-l","adjective","Hard but easily broken.","Old phone chargers get brittle and crack.","","Brittle sounds like “brick-little”: hard but snaps easily."],["perceptive","per-SEP-tiv","adjective","Quick to notice and understand things.","The perceptive teacher saw I was nervous before the test.","","Perceptive sounds like perceive: you notice what others miss."],["defiant","dih-FY-uhnt","adjective","Boldly refusing to obey.","The defiant toddler would not put on his shoes.","","Defiant: you defy the rules."],["fracas","FRAY-kuhs","noun","A noisy quarrel or fuss.","A fracas broke out at the store over the last box of diapers.","","Fracas sounds like “fracture”: a noisy fight that breaks things up."],["reciprocate","ri-SIP-ruh-kayt","verb","To give back the same kind of thing you got.","When she helped me move, I reciprocated by cooking her dinner.","From Latin reciprocus, “moving back and forth.”","Reciprocate starts with “re,” meaning back: you give back."],["precarious","prih-KAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Not safe or steady; likely to fall.","The stack of dishes in the sink was precarious.","From Latin precarius, “obtained by asking.”","Precarious sounds like “pre-scary-ous”: scary, about to fall."],["emissary","EM-uh-ser-ee","noun","A person sent with a message or a job to do.","The kids sent their little sister as an emissary to ask Dad for ice cream.","From Latin emissarius, “one sent out.”","An emissary is emitted, sent out with a message."],["bough","BOW","noun","A large branch of a tree.","A tire swing hung from the lowest bough of the backyard tree.","","Bough rhymes with “cow”: a cow could sit on a big branch."],["kernel","KUR-nuhl","noun","A seed, or the most important part of something.","There’s a kernel of truth in my son’s wild excuse.","","Kernel: like a popcorn kernel, the small center."],["motif","moh-TEEF","noun","A pattern or idea that keeps showing up.","Stars are a motif in all of her drawings.","From French motif.","A motif is a pattern that pops up again and again."],["scallywag","SKAL-ee-wag","noun","A playful rascal.","That little scallywag ate all the cherries.","","Scallywag sounds like “silly wag”: a silly, tail-wagging rascal."],["panacea","pan-uh-SEE-uh","noun","A cure for every problem.","A nap is not a panacea, but it helps a lot.","From Greek pan, “all,” and akos, “cure.”","Pan means all: a cure for all."],["mundane","mun-DAYN","adjective","Plain and ordinary.","Even mundane chores like folding laundry feel fun with good music.","From Latin mundus, “world.”","Mundane sounds like Monday: plain and ordinary."],["oomph","OOMF","noun","Energy and excitement.","A little hot sauce gives the soup some oomph.","","Oomph is the sound of a big burst of energy."],["elucidate","ih-LOO-si-dayt","verb","To make something clear by explaining it.","The teacher drew a picture to elucidate the math problem.","From Latin lucidus, “bright, clear.”","Lucid means clear, so elucidate means make it clear."],["piecemeal","PEES-meel","adverb","Bit by bit, a little at a time.","We fixed up the old house piecemeal, one room each year.","","Piece by piece, a meal at a time."],["docile","DOS-uhl","adjective","Calm and easy to handle.","The docile dog let the toddler hug it.","From Latin docilis, “easily taught.”","Docile sounds like “dozy”: calm and easy."],["amass","uh-MAS","verb","To gather a large amount of something over time.","Over the years he amassed a huge pile of old phones in a drawer.","","Amass has “mass”: making a big mass of stuff."],["cairn","KAIRN","noun","A pile of stones used to mark a place or a trail.","On our hike, a cairn of stacked rocks showed us which way to go.","From Scottish Gaelic carn, “heap of stones.”","Cairn sounds like “care-in”: stones stacked with care to point the way."],["luscious","LUSH-uhs","adjective","Rich and delicious.","The luscious chocolate cake disappeared in minutes.","","Luscious sounds like “lush and delicious”: rich and yummy."],["grimace","GRIM-uhs","noun","A twisted face showing dislike or pain.","My son made a grimace when he tasted the broccoli.","","Grimace sounds like “grim face”: a twisted, yucky look."],["miscellany","MIS-uh-lay-nee","noun","A mix of many different things.","The kitchen drawer held a miscellany of keys, string and stamps.","From Latin miscellanea, “mixed things.”","Miscellany sounds like “mixed sell-any”: a mixed bag."],["torrent","TOR-uhnt","noun","A fast, strong rush of water or other things.","A torrent of rain poured off the roof.","From Latin torrens, “rushing, boiling.”","Torrent sounds like “pouring”: a pouring rush."],["desiccate","DES-i-kayt","verb","To dry out completely.","The summer heat desiccated the lawn until it was brown and crunchy.","From Latin siccus, “dry.”","Desiccate sounds like “desert-cate”: dry as a desert."],["avid","AV-id","adjective","Very eager and keen.","My dad is an avid reader of mystery novels and finishes one a week.","From Latin avidus, “eager.”","Avid sounds like “a vid fan”: someone who watches every video."],["wry","RY","adjective","Dryly funny in a quiet, clever way.","She gave a wry smile when the cake fell.","","Wry sounds like “rye bread”: dry and a little sharp."],["sheepish","SHEE-pish","adjective","A little embarrassed about something you did.","He gave a sheepish smile after tripping over the rug.","","Sheepish has “sheep”: shy and timid, like a little sheep."],["impeccable","im-PEK-uh-buhl","adjective","Without any flaws.","The waiter had impeccable manners.","","Impeccable: im means not, peccare means to sin: no faults at all."],["quirky","KWUR-kee","adjective","Odd or unusual in a charming way.","My sister has a quirky habit of eating pizza crust first.","","A quirk is a small odd habit; quirky means full of them."],["goad","GOHD","verb","To push someone to do something.","My friends goaded me into singing at karaoke.","","Goad sounds like “go, go!”: poking someone to act."],["morale","muh-RAL","noun","How cheerful and hopeful a group feels.","Pizza Friday is great for team morale at work.","","Morale sounds like “more ale”: more cheer for the group."],["palpable","PAL-puh-buhl","adjective","So strong you can almost touch it.","The excitement before the show was palpable.","From Latin palpare, “to touch gently.”","Palp sounds like palm: so real you can touch it with your palm."],["rarefied","RAIR-uh-fyd","adjective","Thin, high or special, as if far above everyday life.","The fancy dinner felt too rarefied for us; we wanted burgers.","From Latin rarus, “thin, rare.”","Rarefied starts with “rare”: so special it feels far from normal life."],["intrepid","in-TREP-id","adjective","Brave and fearless.","The intrepid kid climbed to the top of the jungle gym.","From Latin intrepidus, “not alarmed.”","Intrepid: in means not, trepid like trepidation, fear: not scared."],["dazzle","DAZ-uhl","verb","To amaze with brightness or skill.","The bright holiday lights dazzled the kids.","","Dazzle sounds like daze: so bright you feel dazed."],["murmuration","mur-myuh-RAY-shuhn","noun","A huge flock of birds swirling together in the sky.","At sunset we watched a murmuration, a huge flock of birds swooping like one cloud.","","The birds murmur and swirl together in the sky."],["artisan","AR-tuh-zuhn","noun","A skilled worker who makes things by hand.","An artisan baker made our bread by hand this morning.","","Artisan has “art” inside: a person who makes things by hand."],["maelstrom","MAYL-struhm","noun","A wild, swirling mess of action or feeling.","The kitchen was a maelstrom of pots, pans and spilled flour before the big dinner.","From early Dutch maelstroom, “grinding stream.”","Picture a mail storm: letters swirling wildly all over the room."],["covet","KUHV-it","verb","To want something very much, often something that belongs to someone else.","He coveted his friend’s new phone.","","Covet sounds like “cover it”: you want to cover it and keep it."],["frisson","free-SOHN","noun","A sudden thrill or shiver of excitement.","I felt a frisson of excitement when I opened the birthday gift.","From French frisson, “a shiver.”","Frisson sounds like “freeze-on”: a quick shiver of thrill."],["putter","PUT-er","verb","To do small, easy tasks in a relaxed way.","Grandpa loves to putter around in his garage on Saturdays.","","Putter sounds like putt-putt, a slow little engine."],["harrowing","HAR-oh-ing","adjective","Very upsetting or frightening.","Driving home in the ice storm was a harrowing trip.","","Harrowing sounds like “horror-ing”: scary and upsetting."],["kaput","kuh-POOT","adjective","Broken and no longer working.","Our old dryer finally went kaput.","From German kaputt.","Kaput sounds like “ka-poot”: broken, done, finished."],["parsimonious","par-suh-MOH-nee-uhs","adjective","Very unwilling to spend money.","The parsimonious man reused tea bags three times to save money.","From Latin parcere, “to spare.”","Parsimonious sounds like “parsley money”: won’t spend even on parsley."],["myriad","MEER-ee-uhd","adjective","Too many to count.","There are myriad ways to cook an egg.","From Greek myrias, “ten thousand.”","Myriad sounds like “my, so many,” said with wide eyes."],["ruse","ROOZ","noun","A clever trick meant to fool someone.","Asking Dad to “help in the garage” was a ruse to get him to his surprise party.","","Ruse sounds like “ruse, I fooled you!”: a sneaky trick."],["keepsake","KEEP-sayk","noun","A small thing kept to remember someone or something.","I kept my baby’s first shoe as a keepsake.","","Keepsake: you keep it for the sake of memory."],["buoyant","BOY-uhnt","adjective","Able to float; also cheerful and hopeful.","She was in a buoyant mood on the first warm day of spring.","","Buoyant has “buoy”: floating up, light and happy."],["decoy","DEE-koy","noun","Something used to lure or distract.","The empty box by the door was a decoy to fool package thieves.","","Decoy sounds like “the coy”: a fake that lures you in."],["profuse","pruh-FYOOS","adjective","Given in large amounts.","He gave profuse thanks for the help with the move.","From Latin profundere, “to pour forth.”","Profuse sounds like “pour fuse”: it pours out."],["farrago","fuh-RAH-goh","noun","A confused mix of things.","His excuse was a farrago of half-truths and silly stories.","From Latin farrago, “mixed feed for cattle.”","Farrago sounds like “far-a-go”: everything thrown in a pile."],["lagoon","luh-GOON","noun","A shallow body of water cut off from the sea.","We swam in a calm lagoon on vacation.","From Italian laguna.","Lagoon sounds like “lake-goon”: a calm little lake by the sea."],["oddball","OD-bawl","noun","A person who is fun and a little strange.","Every family has an oddball, and ours wears bright, silly socks.","","An odd ball that bounces in its own way."],["bountiful","BOUN-tuh-fuhl","adjective","Plentiful, or giving freely.","Thanksgiving dinner was bountiful, with food covering the whole table.","","Bountiful has “bounty”: full of good things."],["concoct","kuhn-KOKT","verb","To make something by mixing things together.","The kids concocted a wild smoothie with bananas and peanut butter.","From Latin concoquere, “to cook together.”","Concoct has “coct,” Latin for cook: cook things up together."],["dynamo","DY-nuh-moh","noun","A very energetic person.","Our school principal is a dynamo who never stops moving.","From Greek dynamis, “power.”","Dynamo sounds like dynamite: full of power."],["behold","bih-HOHLD","verb","To see or look at something special.","The birthday cake was a sight to behold.","","Behold: be and hold your eyes on it."],["oblivion","uh-BLIV-ee-uhn","noun","The state of being forgotten or unaware.","The old song faded into oblivion until a band brought it back.","From Latin oblivisci, “to forget.”","Oblivion sounds like “oh, blivvy-gone”: totally forgotten."],["minstrel","MIN-struhl","noun","A traveling singer or musician of long ago.","At the fair, a minstrel walked around singing old songs.","","Minstrel sounds like “mini-stroll”: a singer who strolls around."],["discombobulate","dis-kuhm-BOB-yuh-layt","verb","To confuse or fluster someone.","The sudden change of plans discombobulated us.","","Discombobulate sounds as jumbled as it means: all mixed up."],["derelict","DER-uh-likt","adjective","Abandoned and run-down.","Neighbors turned the derelict lot into a playground.","From Latin derelinquere, “to abandon.”","Derelict sounds like “deserted”: left behind and run-down."],["renounce","ri-NOWNS","verb","To give something up formally or on purpose.","She renounced sweets for a month to see how she felt.","","Renounce has “announce”: you announce you are giving it up."],["auspicious","aw-SPISH-uhs","adjective","Showing signs of future success; a good start.","Sunshine on our wedding day felt like an auspicious start.","From Latin auspicium, “a reading of birds.”","Auspicious sounds like “awesome-spicious”: a sign of good things."],["furtive","FUR-tiv","adjective","Sneaky, trying not to be seen.","The dog gave a furtive look before stealing the sandwich.","From Latin furtivus, “stolen.”","Furtive sounds like “fur thief”: a sneaky dog stealing food."],["festoon","fe-STOON","verb","To decorate with chains of flowers, ribbons or lights.","We festooned the porch with lights for the holidays.","From Italian festone, “festive garland.”","Festoon sounds like festival: hang things up for a party."],["twilight","TWY-lyt","noun","The soft light just after sunset.","We sat on the porch and talked in the twilight.","","Twilight: “twi,” like two, the light between day and night."],["deft","DEFT","adjective","Quick and skillful.","With a deft flick, she flipped the pancake.","","Deft sounds like left: even with your left hand, you’re skillful."],["usher","UHSH-er","verb","To guide someone to a place.","The waiter ushered us to our table by the window.","","Usher sounds like “rush her”: you lead someone to their spot."],["improvise","IM-pruh-vyz","verb","To make or do something without a plan, using what you have.","With no eggs, Mom had to improvise and use a banana in the cake.","From Latin improvisus, “unforeseen.”","Improvise: im means not, provisus means foreseen: nothing planned ahead."],["fjord","fee-ORD","noun","A long, narrow arm of the sea between tall cliffs.","On our cruise, the ship slid through a fjord with cliffs on both sides.","From Norwegian fjord.","Fjord sounds like “fee-ord”: a long sea road between cliffs."],["boffin","BOF-in","noun","A playful word for a scientist or expert.","The computer boffin at the store fixed my laptop in a minute.","","Boffin sounds like “boffo brain”: a fun word for a smart expert."],["gawk","GAWK","verb","To stare in a silly or rude way.","People gawked at the fancy car parked outside the grocery store.","","Gawk sounds like “gock!”: a silly, open-mouthed stare."],["tawdry","TAW-dree","adjective","Showy and cheap looking.","The gas station sold tawdry plastic toys.","","Tawdry sounds like “tacky”: cheap and showy."],["meteoric","mee-tee-OR-ik","adjective","Very fast and bright, like a shooting star.","The little bakery had a meteoric rise after one great online review.","","A meteor zooms across the sky, fast and bright."],["poignant","POYN-yuhnt","adjective","Touching the heart in a sad or tender way.","The old family photo was a poignant reminder of Grandpa.","From French poindre, “to prick.”","Poignant (POY-nyunt) means pointed: it pokes your heart."],["diurnal","dy-UR-nl","adjective","Active in the daytime.","Dogs are diurnal, but our cat roams the house at night.","From Latin diurnus, “of the day.”","Diurnal comes from Latin dies, day, like a daily journal."],["cerebral","suh-REE-bruhl","adjective","Thoughtful and focused on ideas more than feelings.","My sister likes cerebral movies that make you think for days.","From Latin cerebrum, “brain.”","Cerebral comes from the Latin for brain: all in the head."],["pungent","PUN-juhnt","adjective","Having a strong, sharp smell or taste.","The pungent garlic made our eyes water.","From Latin pungere, “to prick.”","Pungent sounds like “punch-ent”: a smell that punches."],["dearth","DURTH","noun","A lack or shortage.","There is no dearth of love in this house.","","Dearth sounds like “dirt”: dirt poor, not enough."],["oaf","OHF","noun","A clumsy, awkward person.","I felt like an oaf when I tripped over the rug.","","Oaf rhymes with loaf: a big, clumsy loaf of bread."],["opine","oh-PYN","verb","To give your opinion.","Uncle Joe likes to opine on the best way to grill corn.","From Latin opinari, “to think.”","Opine sounds like opinion."],["ennui","on-WEE","noun","A tired, bored feeling of having nothing fun to do.","After a week stuck inside with the flu, I was filled with ennui.","From French ennui, “boredom.”","Ennui sounds like “on we” go, yawning, with nothing to do."],["tenacious","tuh-NAY-shuhs","adjective","Not giving up; holding on firmly.","The tenacious puppy would not let go of the rope toy.","From Latin tenax, “holding fast.”","Tenacious sounds like “ten nails”: holding on with all ten fingers."],["guffaw","guh-FAW","noun","A loud, hearty laugh.","Dad let out a guffaw at the silly cat video.","","Guffaw sounds like a big “GUH-FAW!” laugh out loud."],["disabuse","dis-uh-BYOOZ","verb","To free someone from a wrong belief.","Let me disabuse you of the idea that cats like baths.","","Disabuse: take away a wrong idea, not abuse anyone."],["whittle","WIT-l","verb","To carve wood by cutting off small bits.","Grandpa whittled a little bird from a stick.","","Whittle sounds like “little”: cutting a stick into little bits."],["nomad","NOH-mad","noun","A person who moves from place to place.","After college he lived like a nomad, working in a new town every few months.","From Greek nomas, “roaming.”","Nomad sounds like “no mad”: no home address."],["apothecary","uh-POTH-uh-ker-ee","noun","An old word for a shop or person that made and sold medicines.","The old apothecary in town had glass jars of medicine on the shelves.","","Apothecary sounds like “a pot to carry”: pots of medicine."],["coax","KOHKS","verb","To gently persuade.","She coaxed the kitten out from under the bed with a treat.","","Coax rhymes with jokes: use kind jokes to win someone over."],["virga","VUR-guh","noun","Rain that falls from a cloud but dries up before reaching the ground.","We saw gray streaks of virga hanging under the clouds, but not a drop hit our car.","From Latin virga, “rod, streak.”","Virga sounds like “vanishing rain”: it vanishes before it lands."],["devour","dih-VOUR","verb","To eat or read hungrily.","The hungry teenagers devoured the whole pizza in five minutes.","From Latin devorare, “to swallow down.”","Devour sounds like “the hour”: gone in no time."],["legible","LEJ-uh-buhl","adjective","Clear enough to read.","Please write your name so it is legible.","From Latin legere, “to read.”","Legible comes from Latin “legere,” to read: easy to read."],["germane","jer-MAYN","adjective","Related to the topic being discussed.","The teacher said my story about my dog was not germane to the lesson.","","Germane sounds like Jermaine, the friend who always stays on topic."],["vacillate","VAS-uh-layt","verb","To keep changing your mind.","I vacillated between the blue shirt and the green one for ten minutes.","From Latin vacillare, “to sway.”","Vacillate sounds like “vacuum back and forth”: going back and forth."],["awry","uh-RY","adverb","Away from the planned or right course.","Our picnic plans went awry when the rain started.","","Awry sounds like “a wry” face: things went crooked."],["outspoken","out-SPOH-kuhn","adjective","Saying what you think openly and boldly.","My outspoken aunt always tells us exactly what she thinks.","","Out-spoken: she speaks it right out loud."],["salient","SAY-lee-uhnt","adjective","Most important or easy to notice.","The salient point of the meeting was that we need more help.","From Latin salire, “to leap.”","Salient sounds like “sail”: it sticks out like a sail."],["brazen","BRAY-zuhn","adjective","Bold and without shame.","The brazen seagull stole a french fry right from my hand.","","Brazen sounds like “brass”: bold as brass, no shame."],["proliferate","pruh-LIF-uh-rayt","verb","To grow or spread quickly.","Dandelions proliferated across the lawn.","","Proliferate sounds like “pro-life-rate”: life spreading at a fast rate."],["noodle","NOO-dl","verb","To play around with an idea or tune in a relaxed way.","He noodled on the guitar while dinner cooked.","","Like playing with your noodles, just messing around."],["echelon","ESH-uh-lon","noun","A level or rank in a group.","Our neighbor rose to the top echelon of her company.","From French échelon, “rung of a ladder.”","Echelon comes from French for a ladder rung: one level on the ladder."],["overcast","OH-ver-kast","adjective","Covered with gray clouds.","It was overcast all day, so we packed umbrellas.","","Over-cast: clouds cast over the whole sky."],["pertinent","PUR-tn-uhnt","adjective","Related to the subject at hand.","Please ask questions that are pertinent to today’s lesson.","","Pertinent sounds like “pertaining”: it pertains to the topic."],["serene","suh-REEN","adjective","Calm and peaceful.","The house felt serene once the kids were asleep.","From Latin serenus, “clear, calm.”","Serene sounds like “siren-free”: calm with no noise."],["agog","uh-GOG","adjective","Very eager and excited to hear or see something.","The kids were agog at the news of a snow day.","","Agog sounds like “a go-go”: eager, eyes wide, ready to go."],["transfix","trans-FIKS","verb","To hold someone still with wonder or surprise.","The kids were transfixed by the fireworks.","From Latin transfigere, “to pierce through.”","Transfix has “fix”: you are fixed in place with wonder."],["astound","uh-STOUND","verb","To surprise someone very much.","The tiny dog’s high jump astounded everyone at the park.","","Astound sounds like “astonish” plus “sound”: so surprised you make a sound."],["delectable","dih-LEK-tuh-buhl","adjective","Delicious and delightful.","The delectable smell of fresh bread filled the house.","From Latin delectare, “to delight.”","Delectable sounds like delight: a delight to eat."],["moxie","MOK-see","noun","Courage and spunk.","It took moxie to sing in front of the whole school.","","Moxie rhymes with foxy: bold and full of spunk."],["limpid","LIM-pid","adjective","Clear and easy to see through.","The pool water was limpid; we could see the bottom.","From Latin limpidus, “clear.”","Limpid sounds like “limp-clear”: clear as glass."],["brood","BROOD","verb","To think long and deeply about something that worries you.","Instead of brooding over the lost game, the kids went for ice cream.","","A hen broods, sitting still on her eggs for a long time."],["disinclined","dis-in-KLYND","adjective","Not wanting to do something.","On rainy days, I’m disinclined to leave my bed.","","Disinclined: you are not inclined, or leaning, toward it."],["haphazard","hap-HAZ-erd","adjective","Done without any plan or order.","The kids threw their shoes in a haphazard pile by the door.","","Haphazard has “hap,” chance: things happen any which way."],["banish","BAN-ish","verb","To send away or get rid of.","A warm bath banished my worries after a long day.","","Banish sounds like “vanish”: send it away so it is gone."],["malapropism","MAL-uh-prop-iz-uhm","noun","Using a wrong word that sounds like the right one, often by mistake.","Saying “for all intensive purposes” instead of “intents and” is a malapropism.","","Mal means bad: a badly picked word that sounds right."],["quandary","KWON-dree","noun","A tricky spot where you are not sure what to do.","I was in a quandary about which birthday cake to pick for my son.","","Quandary starts like “question”: you have a big question with no answer."],["doodle","DOO-dl","verb","To draw little shapes without much thought.","He doodles stars in the corners of his notebook.","","Doodle sounds like noodle: loose, wiggly drawings."],["jettison","JET-i-suhn","verb","To throw away something you no longer need.","We jettisoned old toys before moving to the new house.","","Jettison sounds like “jet it out”: throw it away fast."],["contagious","kuhn-TAY-juhs","adjective","Easily spread from one person to another.","Her laugh was so contagious that the whole room joined in.","","Contagious: like a cold, it spreads by contact."],["finesse","fi-NES","noun","Skill and grace in handling something.","She folded the fitted sheet with real finesse.","From French fin, “fine.”","Finesse has “fine” in it: doing something finely, with grace."],["kismet","KIZ-met","noun","Fate or destiny.","Meeting my husband at the grocery store was kismet.","From Turkish kismet, “fate.”","Kismet sounds like “kiss met”: fate brought you together."],["fledgling","FLEJ-ling","noun","A young bird just learning to fly, or a beginner.","My fledgling business sold its first ten cupcakes today.","","A fledgling is fresh and fragile, just learning to fly."],["milieu","mil-YOO","noun","The setting or world around a person.","The cozy milieu of the coffee shop made us stay for hours.","From French milieu, “middle.”","Milieu means middle: the world you are in the middle of."],["insatiable","in-SAY-shuh-buhl","adjective","Never satisfied; always wanting more.","My teenage son has an insatiable appetite.","","Insatiable: in means not, so you can never be satisfied."],["loquacious","loh-KWAY-shuhs","adjective","Very talkative.","My loquacious neighbor chats for an hour at the mailbox.","From Latin loqui, “to speak.”","Loquacious has “loqu,” to speak, like eloquent: speaks a lot."],["trifle","TRY-fuhl","noun","Something small and unimportant, or a layered dessert.","Don’t worry about the spilled milk; it’s a trifle.","","Trifle sounds like “trivial”: just a small thing."],["bard","BARD","noun","A poet or storyteller, especially one who sings.","At camp, our counselor was the bard, telling stories by the fire.","","Bard sounds like “bird”: a storyteller who sings."],["jeopardy","JEP-er-dee","noun","Danger of harm or loss.","Texting while driving puts everyone in jeopardy.","","Jeopardy sounds like “leopard-y”: a leopard nearby means danger."],["demur","dih-MUR","verb","To politely object or hesitate.","She demurred when asked to sing, then sang anyway.","","Demur sounds like “de-murmur”: a quiet murmur of “maybe not.”"],["dilatory","DIL-uh-tor-ee","adjective","Slow and likely to delay.","His dilatory packing made us late for the flight.","","Dilatory sounds like delay: slow and putting things off."],["muster","MUS-ter","verb","To gather up, like courage or people.","She mustered the nerve to ask a question in class.","From Latin monstrare, “to show.”","Muster sounds like must: you must find the courage."],["eclectic","ih-KLEK-tik","adjective","Made up of many different kinds of things.","Grandpa’s music mix is eclectic: rock, jazz, country and cartoon songs.","From Greek eklektikos, “picking out.”","Eclectic sounds like electric: lots of different sparks mixed together."],["outstrip","out-STRIP","verb","To grow bigger or faster than something else.","Orders for her cookies outstripped what her oven could bake.","","Out-strip: racing out ahead of the rest."],["dutiful","DOO-ti-fuhl","adjective","Doing what is expected, with care.","The dutiful son called his mom every Sunday.","","Dutiful has duty in it: doing your duty."],["confection","kuhn-FEK-shuhn","noun","A sweet treat, like candy or fancy cake.","The bakery window was full of pink and white confections.","","Confection sounds like confetti: little sweet treats for a party."],["allure","uh-LOOR","noun","A strong, attractive charm.","The allure of fresh donuts pulled us into the shop.","","Allure has “lure”: like a fishing lure, it pulls you in."],["inkling","INK-ling","noun","A slight idea or hint.","I had an inkling the kids were planning a surprise.","","Inkling: a tiny drop of ink, just a hint of an idea."],["delineate","dih-LIN-ee-ayt","verb","To describe or mark out clearly.","The new job description delineates exactly what she must do each day.","From Latin linea, “line.”","Delineate has line in it: you draw clear lines."],["larder","LAR-der","noun","A cool room or cupboard for storing food.","Grandma keeps jars of jam in her larder.","","Larder comes from lard: a cool cupboard where food was stored."],["jovial","JOH-vee-uhl","adjective","Cheerful and friendly.","Our jovial neighbor greets us with a big laugh every morning.","","Jovial sounds like “joy-vial”: a bottle full of cheer."],["redress","ri-DRES","verb","To set right something that was wrong or unfair.","The store redressed the mistake by sending a new lamp.","","Redress sounds like “re-dress”: you dress it right again."],["lassitude","LAS-i-tood","noun","A tired, lazy feeling.","The summer heat filled us with lassitude.","From Latin lassus, “weary.”","Lassitude sounds like “lazy-tude”: tired, sleepy and slow."],["rubicund","ROO-bi-kuhnd","adjective","Having a healthy red color in the face.","After shoveling snow, Dad came in with rubicund cheeks.","From Latin rubicundus, “reddish.”","Rubicund starts like “ruby”: cheeks as red as a ruby."],["touchstone","TUCH-stohn","noun","Something used to judge the quality of other things.","Her mother’s kindness was the touchstone for how she treated others.","","Touchstone: the stone you touch to test if something is good."],["alpenglow","AL-puhn-gloh","noun","The pink glow on mountain tops near sunrise or sunset.","We watched the alpenglow turn the snowy mountain tops pink at sunset.","From German Alpenglühen, “Alps glow.”","Alpen means the Alps, mountains: the glow on mountain tops."],["vitality","vy-TAL-i-tee","noun","Lively energy and strength.","After a good night’s sleep, she was full of vitality.","From Latin vita, “life.”","Vita means life, like vitamins: full of life."],["brink","BRINGK","noun","The edge of a steep place, or the point just before something happens.","The team was on the brink of winning the championship.","","Brink sounds like “brim”: right at the edge."],["vertiginous","ver-TIJ-uh-nuhs","adjective","So high it makes you dizzy.","Looking down from the vertiginous glass elevator made me dizzy.","From Latin vertigo, “a whirling.”","Vertiginous sounds like “vertigo”: the dizzy feeling."],["redoubtable","ri-DOW-tuh-buhl","adjective","Strong and worthy of respect, even a little scary.","My grandmother is a redoubtable card player; nobody beats her.","From French redouter, “to fear.”","Redoubtable has “doubt”: you would never doubt her strength."],["rapture","RAP-cher","noun","A feeling of great joy and delight.","The kids watched the birthday cake come out in pure rapture.","From Latin raptus, “carried off.”","Rapture sounds like “wrapped”: you are wrapped up in joy."],["parley","PAR-lee","noun","A talk between two sides to settle something.","The kids held a parley to decide who gets the swing first.","From French parler, “to speak.”","Parley comes from French parler, to speak: a sit-down talk."],["spurious","SPYOOR-ee-uhs","adjective","False, even though it looks real.","The ad made spurious claims about shoes that make you run faster.","From Latin spurius, “false.”","Spurious sounds like “spoof-ious”: a spoof, not the real thing."],["flamboyant","flam-BOY-uhnt","adjective","Bold, bright and showy.","Our flamboyant neighbor wears bright pink suits to the store.","From French flamboyer, “to flame.”","Flamboyant has “flam,” flame: bold and bright as fire."],["unruly","uhn-ROO-lee","adjective","Hard to control or keep in order.","I tried to tame my unruly curls with a clip.","","Unruly: “un” and “rule,” so it follows no rules."],["gregarious","gri-GAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Loving to be with other people.","Our gregarious son makes a new friend at every playground.","From Latin grex, “flock.”","Gregarious: from Latin “flock”: always with the group."],["blatant","BLAYT-nt","adjective","Very obvious and done openly.","The crumbs on his shirt were a blatant clue that he ate the cookies.","","Blatant sounds like “blaring”: loud and plain to see."],["categorical","kat-i-GOR-i-kuhl","adjective","Clear and without any doubt.","Her answer was a categorical no to staying out past midnight.","","Categorical: it goes in one category only, no maybes."],["surmise","ser-MYZ","verb","To guess something based on clues.","From the muddy paw prints, I surmised the dog had been outside.","","Surmise sounds like “sure, my eyes”: guessing from what your eyes see."],["wanderlust","WON-der-luhst","noun","A strong wish to travel and explore.","Her wanderlust took her to a new country every summer.","From German Wanderlust, “desire to wander.”","Wanderlust is German: wander plus lust, which means desire."],["negate","nih-GAYT","verb","To cancel out or make useless.","Eating a whole cake can negate the good of a long walk.","From Latin negare, “to deny.”","Negate is like negative: it cancels things out."],["disheveled","dih-SHEV-uhld","adjective","Messy and untidy, especially hair or clothes.","He came home disheveled after the windy walk.","","Disheveled: your hair is all a mess, like a shovel went through it."],["cosset","KOS-it","verb","To pamper and care for someone.","We cosseted Mom on Mother’s Day with breakfast in bed.","","Cosset sounds like “cozy set”: setting someone up all cozy."],["clemency","KLEM-uhn-see","noun","Mercy shown to someone who did wrong.","The teacher showed clemency and let me turn in my homework late.","From Latin clemens, “mild.”","Clemency sounds like “Clem’s mercy”: kind Clem lets you off."],["disposition","dis-puh-ZISH-uhn","noun","A person’s usual mood or nature.","Our dog has a sunny disposition.","","Disposition: your usual position, or mood."],["acclaim","uh-KLAYM","noun","Loud and public praise.","Her apple pie won wide acclaim at the school bake sale.","From Latin acclamare, “to shout at.”","Acclaim has “claim” inside: everyone claims it is great, out loud."],["tandem","TAN-duhm","noun","Two things working together, one behind the other.","My partner and I work in tandem: one washes, one dries.","From Latin tandem, “at length.”","Tandem: think of a tandem bike, two riders working together."],["jocular","JOK-yuh-ler","adjective","Playful and joking.","The jocular bus driver told jokes to every passenger.","From Latin jocus, “joke.”","Jocular comes from Latin “jocus,” joke: a jokey person."],["alcove","AL-kohv","noun","A small space set back into a wall of a room.","She made a cozy reading nook in the alcove by the stairs.","From Arabic al-qubba, “the vault.”","Alcove sounds like “a cove”: a small tucked-in space, like a tiny bay."],["widdershins","WID-er-shinz","adverb","In the opposite direction to the sun’s path; counterclockwise.","The kids skipped widdershins around the kitchen table.","","Widdershins sounds like “wrong way shins”: walking the wrong way around."],["kaleidoscope","kuh-LY-duh-skohp","noun","A tube of mirrors and colored bits that make changing patterns, or any bright, changing mix.","The sidewalk chalk drawings were a kaleidoscope of colors.","From Greek kalos, “beautiful,” and eidos, “form.”","Kaleidoscope: Greek for beautiful form, seen through a scope."],["monotony","muh-NOT-n-ee","noun","Boring sameness with no change.","Singing in the car broke the monotony of the long drive.","From Greek monos, “one,” and tonos, “tone.”","Mono means one, so monotony is one tone, over and over."],["gallant","GAL-uhnt","adjective","Brave and polite, especially in a kind way.","The gallant teen held the door and carried my groceries.","From French galant, “courtly.”","Gallant sounds like “gal-ant”: a kind helper who treats everyone well."],["demure","dih-MYOOR","adjective","Quiet, modest and shy.","The demure little girl hid behind her mom’s legs.","","Demure sounds like “the mure”: quiet as a mouse."],["clairvoyant","klair-VOY-uhnt","adjective","Seeming to know things before they happen.","Mom seems clairvoyant about when we skip brushing our teeth.","From French clair, “clear,” and voyant, “seeing.”","Clair means clear. Voyant means seeing, in French. Seeing clearly."],["misanthrope","MIS-uhn-throhp","noun","A person who does not like other people.","The grumpy neighbor seemed like a misanthrope until he brought us soup.","From Greek misein, “to hate,” and anthropos, “human.”","Mis means hate, and anthro means people."],["choreograph","KOR-ee-uh-graf","verb","To plan the steps of a dance or event.","The kids choreographed a dance for the school talent show.","From Greek choreia, “dance.”","Choreo means dance. Graph means write. Writing a dance."],["apprise","uh-PRYZ","verb","To tell someone about something; to inform.","Please apprise me if the flight time changes so I can pick you up.","From French appris, “taught.”","Apprise sounds like “a prize”: someone hands you the news."],["proclivity","proh-KLIV-ih-tee","noun","A natural leaning toward something.","My dog has a proclivity for napping in sunny spots.","From Latin proclivis, “sloping forward.”","Pro means forward: leaning forward toward a habit."],["judicious","joo-DISH-uhs","adjective","Wise and careful in choosing.","She made a judicious choice and saved money for a rainy day.","From Latin judicium, “judgment.”","Judicious sounds like judge: wise and careful."],["cupidity","kyoo-PID-i-tee","noun","Strong greed, especially for money.","The man’s cupidity made him charge his own family for rides.","","Cupid wants love. Cupidity wants money."],["conceit","kuhn-SEET","noun","Too much pride in yourself.","His conceit faded after he lost the office cooking contest.","","Conceit sounds like “con-seat”: he thinks he has the best seat."],["outpace","out-PAYS","verb","To go faster or do better than.","The little dog outpaced the big one to the ball.","","Out-pace: going at a faster pace."],["patina","puh-TEE-nuh","noun","A soft shine or color that comes with age.","The old copper pot had a lovely green patina.","","Patina sounds like “pat it, Tina”: worn soft by years of touch."],["enigma","ih-NIG-muh","noun","A puzzle or mystery that is hard to figure out.","How the cat got onto the roof was a total enigma.","From Greek ainigma, “a riddle.”","Enigma sounds like “a riddle, ma!”: a mystery no one solves."],["bereft","bih-REFT","adjective","Sad and lonely because something is missing.","The kids felt bereft when their best friend moved away.","","Bereft sounds like “bereaved and left”: something missing."],["tumbleweed","TUM-buhl-weed","noun","A dry plant that breaks off and rolls in the wind.","A tumbleweed rolled across the empty road in the old cowboy movie.","","Tumbleweed: a weed that tumbles in the wind."],["antecedent","an-tuh-SEE-duhnt","noun","Something that came before and led to something else.","The flip phone was an antecedent of today’s smartphone.","From Latin antecedere, “to go before.”","Ante means before: something that came before."],["glacial","GLAY-shuhl","adjective","Very slow or very cold, like a glacier.","The line at the bank moved at a glacial pace.","From Latin glacies, “ice.”","Glacial comes from glacier: very slow, very cold."],["exuberant","ig-ZOO-ber-uhnt","adjective","Full of joy, energy and excitement.","The exuberant puppy ran circles around the yard.","From Latin exuberare, “to be fruitful.”","Exuberant sounds like “exciting, abundant”: full of bouncy joy."],["abate","uh-BAYT","verb","To become less strong or less intense.","We waited inside until the rain began to abate and the sun came out.","From Old French abatre, “to beat down.”","Abate sounds like “a bat” flying off: the storm flies away and calms."],["maven","MAY-vuhn","noun","A person who knows a lot about something.","My neighbor is a coffee maven who can tell you about every bean.","From Yiddish meyvn, “one who understands.”","A maven knows everything, like a walking map of the subject."],["humility","hyoo-MIL-i-tee","noun","Not thinking you are better than others.","Despite winning, she accepted the trophy with humility.","From Latin humilis, “low.”","Humility sounds like humble: staying low-key and kind."],["disingenuous","dis-in-JEN-yoo-uhs","adjective","Not fully honest or sincere.","It was disingenuous of him to say he didn’t want dessert.","","Disingenuous: not genuine."],["opportune","op-er-TOON","adjective","Happening at just the right time.","The bus arrived at an opportune moment, right as it started raining.","From Latin opportunus, “toward the harbor.”","Opportune sounds like opportunity: just the right time."],["hijinks","HY-jinks","noun","Playful, silly fun.","The cousins’ hijinks at the sleepover kept everyone up late.","","Hijinks sounds like “high jinks”: high-flying, silly fun."],["voluminous","vuh-LOO-muh-nuhs","adjective","Very large or full, holding a lot.","She wore a voluminous coat with deep pockets.","","Voluminous has “volume”: lots of volume, lots of room."],["drivel","DRIV-uhl","noun","Silly talk or nonsense.","The reality show was fun drivel for a rainy day.","","Drivel sounds like drool: silly stuff that just dribbles out."],["ornate","or-NAYT","adjective","Covered in fancy decoration.","The old theater had an ornate ceiling covered in gold swirls.","From Latin ornare, “to adorn.”","Ornate sounds like ornament: covered in decorations."],["ambivalent","am-BIV-uh-luhnt","adjective","Having mixed feelings about something.","I feel ambivalent about moving, excited but also a little sad.","","Ambi means both: feeling both ways at once."],["unscathed","uhn-SKAYTHD","adjective","Not hurt or harmed at all.","The phone fell off the table but landed unscathed.","","Unscathed sounds like “un-scratched”: not a scratch on it."],["menagerie","muh-NAJ-uh-ree","noun","A mixed group of animals, or of odd things.","Their house is a menagerie of cats, fish and one loud parrot.","From French ménagerie.","Picture a manager trying to run a house full of animals."],["filigree","FIL-i-gree","noun","Delicate, lacy designs made of fine wire.","Grandma’s silver necklace has delicate filigree, like tiny lace.","","Filigree: fine wire like thin filaments, twisted into lace."],["tumult","TOO-muhlt","noun","A loud, confused noise or uproar.","The tumult in the kitchen grew as everyone tried to cook at once.","From Latin tumultus, “commotion.”","Tumult sounds like “tumble”: loud, tumbling chaos."],["caveat","KAV-ee-aht","noun","A warning or condition to keep in mind.","You can borrow the car, with one caveat: fill up the gas tank.","From Latin caveat, “let him beware.”","Caveat is Latin for “let him beware,” like a warning sign."],["modicum","MOD-ih-kuhm","noun","A small amount.","With a modicum of practice, you can learn to juggle.","From Latin modicus, “moderate.”","Modicum sounds like modest: a modest, small amount."],["unison","YOO-nuh-suhn","noun","Doing something together at the same time.","The class said “good morning” in unison.","From Latin unisonus, “having one sound.”","Uni means one: everyone as one voice."],["salubrious","suh-LOO-bree-uhs","adjective","Good for your health.","A daily walk is a salubrious habit for anyone.","From Latin salus, “health.”","Salubrious sounds like “salute”: salute to good health."],["outmoded","out-MOH-did","adjective","Out of date; no longer in style or use.","We traded the outmoded flip phone for a newer one.","","Out of mode: out of style."],["downcast","DOUN-kast","adjective","Sad and low in spirit.","He was downcast until his friend called.","","Downcast: your eyes are cast down."],["adamant","AD-uh-muhnt","adjective","Firm and refusing to change your mind.","Grandpa was adamant that we wear our coats in the snow.","","Adamant sounds like “a diamond”: hard and will not bend."],["concede","kuhn-SEED","verb","To admit something is true, often unwillingly.","He conceded that her lasagna was better than his.","From Latin concedere, “to yield.”","Concede sounds like “con seed”: you give up the seed and let them win."],["narrative","NAR-uh-tiv","noun","A story, or the way a story is told.","Her story about the trip had a funny narrative from start to finish.","From Latin narrare, “to tell.”","A narrator tells the narrative."],["disembark","dis-em-BARK","verb","To get off a ship, plane or train.","We disembarked from the plane and headed to baggage claim.","","Disembark: get off the bark, an old word for boat."],["ravenous","RAV-uh-nuhs","adjective","Very, very hungry.","After a long day at work, I was ravenous and ate two plates.","","Ravenous sounds like “raven”: a hungry bird eating everything in sight."],["propitious","pruh-PISH-uhs","adjective","Giving a good chance of success.","Sunny skies made it a propitious day for a picnic.","From Latin propitius, “favorable.”","Propitious sounds like “pro-precious”: a precious good chance."],["begrudge","bih-GRUHJ","verb","To feel unhappy that someone has something, or to give unwillingly.","I don’t begrudge my brother his new car; he saved for years.","","Begrudge has “grudge”: holding a grudge over what others have."],["lush","LUSH","adjective","Full of thick, green growth.","Our backyard was lush and green after all the rain.","","Lush sounds like plush: thick and soft and full."],["obdurate","OB-duh-rit","adjective","Very stubborn and hard to move.","The obdurate toddler would not put on his coat, no matter what.","From Latin obdurare, “to harden.”","Obdurate sounds like “ob-durable”: hard like a rock."],["bonhomie","bon-uh-MEE","noun","Cheerful, friendly good nature.","The block party was full of bonhomie and laughter.","From French bonhomie, “good nature.”","Bon means good, homme means man in French: good fellow feeling."],["potpourri","poh-puh-REE","noun","A bowl of dried petals and spices, or a mix of things.","The talent show was a potpourri of songs, jokes and dances.","From French pot pourri, “rotten pot.”","Potpourri (poh-puh-REE) is a pot of mixed things."],["tirade","TY-rayd","noun","A long, angry speech.","The cook went on a funny tirade about burnt toast.","","Tirade sounds like “tired-raid”: a long rant that tires you out."],["periphery","puh-RIF-uh-ree","noun","The outer edge of something.","I saw something move at the periphery of my eye.","From Greek periphereia, “circumference.”","Peri means around: the edge, like your peripheral vision."],["contrite","kuhn-TRYT","adjective","Feeling sorry for doing wrong.","The contrite boy said sorry for breaking the window with his ball.","","Contrite: you are trite, worn down, by being sorry."],["quidnunc","KWID-nungk","noun","A person who always wants to know the latest news.","Our neighbor is a quidnunc who knew about the new bakery first.","From Latin quid nunc, “what now?”","Quidnunc is Latin for “what now?”: always asking what’s new."],["sartorial","sar-TOR-ee-uhl","adjective","Having to do with clothes and how you dress.","His bright bow tie was a sartorial treat at the wedding.","From Latin sartor, “tailor.”","Sartorial sounds like “sorted, tailor”: your outfit sorted by a tailor."],["sanguine","SANG-gwin","adjective","Cheerful and hopeful, even when things are hard.","Mom stayed sanguine even when the car broke down.","From Latin sanguis, “blood.”","Sanguine sounds like “sunny-guine”: sunny and hopeful."],["avail","uh-VAYL","noun","Use or help, as in the phrase “to no avail” meaning without success.","We searched the whole house for the lost key, but to no avail.","","Avail sounds like “a veil”: no use, like looking through a veil."],["sibilant","SIB-uh-luhnt","adjective","Making a hissing “s” sound.","The sibilant hiss of the tea kettle filled the kitchen.","From Latin sibilare, “to hiss.”","Sibilant sounds like “sssibilant”: full of hissing s sounds."],["élan","ay-LAHN","noun","Style, energy and confidence.","She walked into the party with élan, smiling and full of style.","From French élan, “dash, rush.”","Élan comes from French for a dash: dashing style and energy."],["alchemy","AL-kuh-mee","noun","A kind of magic that seems to turn ordinary things into something special.","By some kitchen alchemy, leftovers became a delicious soup.","","Alchemy sounds like “all chemistry”: magic that turns plain into gold."],["suffuse","suh-FYOOZ","verb","To spread slowly over or through something.","The smell of fresh coffee suffused the whole house.","From Latin suffundere, “to pour beneath.”","Suffuse sounds like “soft fuse”: it softly spreads through everything."],["knotty","NOT-ee","adjective","Full of knots, or hard to solve.","Sharing one bathroom with five kids is a knotty problem.","","Knotty has “knot”: twisted up and hard to fix."],["savor","SAY-ver","verb","To enjoy something slowly so it lasts.","She savored every bite of the warm peach pie.","From Latin sapor, “taste.”","Savor sounds like “flavor”: enjoy every bit of flavor slowly."],["aerie","AIR-ee","noun","A high nest of an eagle or other large bird; also any high, safe spot.","From her attic bedroom, a high aerie, she could see the whole town.","","Aerie sounds like “airy”: a nest high up in the air."],["cynosure","SY-nuh-shoor","noun","Someone or something everyone looks at.","The new baby was the cynosure of the family dinner.","","Cynosure sounds like “sign of sure”: the sure sign everyone looks at."],["duress","doo-RES","noun","Pressure or threats used to make someone act.","He ate his broccoli only under duress.","","Duress sounds like “dur-stress”: done because of stress and pressure."],["illustrious","ih-LUS-tree-uhs","adjective","Famous and admired for great deeds.","The illustrious chef opened a new restaurant in town.","From Latin illustris, “bright.”","Illustrious sounds like “lustrous”: shining with fame."],["fathom","FATH-uhm","verb","To understand something after thinking hard.","I can’t fathom how my phone bill got so high.","From Old English fæthm, “outstretched arms.”","Fathom sounds like “find the bottom”: getting to the deep meaning."],["mystique","mih-STEEK","noun","A special air of mystery that draws you in.","The old house on the hill has a certain mystique.","","Mystique sounds like mystery: something special and secret."],["badinage","bad-n-AHZH","noun","Light, playful teasing talk.","The friends traded playful badinage over breakfast.","From French badiner, “to joke.”","Badinage sounds like “bad-in-age”: silly teasing, bad jokes on purpose."],["eminent","EM-uh-nuhnt","adjective","Famous and respected.","An eminent doctor came to speak at our school.","From Latin eminere, “to stand out.”","Eminent sounds like “a mountain”: someone who stands out high above."],["mirage","mih-RAHZH","noun","Something that seems real but is not there.","The puddle on the hot road turned out to be a mirage.","From French mirer, “to look at.”","Mirage sounds like mirror: it only reflects what is not there."],["foible","FOY-buhl","noun","A small, harmless weakness or odd habit.","Dad’s little foible is humming loudly while he cooks.","From old French foible, “weak.”","Foible sounds like “feeble”: a small weak spot in a person."],["incognito","in-kog-NEE-toh","adverb","In disguise, so no one knows who you are.","She wore sunglasses and a hat to shop incognito.","From Italian incognito, “unknown.”","Incognito sounds like “in-cog-neat-o”: unknown, in disguise."],["vigilant","VIJ-uh-luhnt","adjective","Watchful and alert.","The vigilant lifeguard kept an eye on every swimmer.","From Latin vigilare, “to keep watch.”","Vigilant has “vigil”: staying up to keep watch."],["topsy-turvy","top-see-TUR-vee","adjective","Upside down or in a happy mess.","The house was topsy-turvy after the birthday party.","","Topsy-turvy: the top is turned to the bottom."],["cogent","KOH-juhnt","adjective","Clear and convincing.","She made a cogent case for getting a dishwasher.","","Cogent: an argument where every cog fits and it works."],["allay","uh-LAY","verb","To calm or ease a fear or worry.","The nurse’s kind smile allayed my little brother’s fears about the shot.","","Allay sounds like “a lay down”: calm worries and lay them to rest."],["altruism","AL-troo-iz-uhm","noun","Caring about others and helping them without wanting a reward.","Her altruism showed when she gave her lunch to a hungry classmate.","From French altruisme, from Latin alter, “other.”","Altruism comes from “alter,” other: thinking of others first."],["captivate","KAP-tuh-vayt","verb","To hold someone’s attention, as if they cannot look away.","The new video game captivated my son for the whole afternoon.","From Latin captivus, “taken captive.”","Captivate has captive in it: your eyes are held captive."],["beacon","BEE-kuhn","noun","A light or signal that guides or warns; also a source of hope.","The porch light was a beacon guiding us home in the dark.","","Beacon sounds like “beckon”: a light that calls you home."],["panoply","PAN-uh-plee","noun","A big, impressive collection.","The grocery store had a panoply of cereals, filling a whole aisle.","","Pan means all: all of it, on full display."],["atrium","AY-tree-uhm","noun","A large open space inside a building, often with a glass roof.","The mall has a sunny atrium with a glass roof and big plants.","From Latin atrium, “an entrance hall.”","Atrium sounds like “a tree room”: a bright open room with plants."],["jest","JEST","noun","A joke.","Grandpa said in jest that he was turning twenty-one again.","","Jest sounds like “just” kidding."],["omnibus","OM-nih-buhs","noun","A book or collection with many works in one.","I borrowed an omnibus with ten mystery stories in one book.","From Latin omnibus, “for all.”","Omni means all: all the stories on one bus."],["amplify","AM-pluh-fy","verb","To make louder or stronger.","The microphone amplified her small voice so everyone could hear.","From Latin amplus, “large.”","Amplify sounds like “amp”: a speaker that makes things louder."],["maudlin","MAWD-lin","adjective","Too sad or teary in a silly way.","The old movie got maudlin at the end, with everyone crying over nothing.","","Maudlin sounds like “mud-lin”: crying until it’s muddy."],["accolade","AK-uh-layd","noun","An award or words of high praise.","The nurse received many accolades for her years of caring work.","","Accolade sounds like “a cool aid”: a cool award for good work."],["autodidact","aw-toh-DY-dakt","noun","A person who has taught themselves.","An autodidact, she learned to fix cars from online videos.","From Greek autodidaktos, “self-taught.”","Auto means self, didact means teach: self-taught."],["illuminate","ih-LOO-muh-nayt","verb","To light up, or to make clear.","The porch light illuminated the path to our door.","From Latin lumen, “light.”","Illuminate has “lumen,” Latin for light: it lights things up."],["sundry","SUN-dree","adjective","Of many different kinds.","The junk drawer held sundry buttons, pens and keys.","","Sundry sounds like “some dry, some wet”: all sorts of things."],["asunder","uh-SUHN-der","adverb","Into pieces or apart.","The storm tore our old fence asunder, and boards flew everywhere.","","Asunder sounds like “a thunder”: torn apart with a crash."],["uproarious","uhp-ROR-ee-uhs","adjective","Very funny, or full of loud laughter.","The school play was uproarious from start to finish.","","Uproarious has “roar”: roaring with laughter."],["calibrate","KAL-uh-brayt","verb","To adjust something so it works or measures just right.","I had to calibrate the bathroom scale because it said I weighed nothing.","","Think “caliber”: setting a tool to the exact right level."],["affinity","uh-FIN-i-tee","noun","A natural liking for something or someone.","My son has an affinity for dogs; every dog runs right to him.","","Affinity sounds like “a finity of friends”: a pull toward something."],["biennial","by-EN-ee-uhl","adjective","Happening every two years.","Our family has a biennial reunion, every two years in July.","From Latin bi, “two,” and annus, “year.”","Bi means two, like a bicycle’s two wheels: every two years."],["astray","uh-STRAY","adverb","Off the right path or away from the right way.","My package went astray and ended up at the house next door.","","Astray has “stray,” like a stray dog: lost off the path."],["hanker","HANG-ker","verb","To have a strong wish for something.","After a long day, I hanker for a bowl of ice cream.","","Hanker sounds like “hunger”: a strong wish for something."],["mettle","MET-l","noun","Courage and strength to keep going.","Running a marathon really tested his mettle.","","Mettle sounds like metal: strong as steel inside."],["dander","DAN-der","noun","Temper, as in getting angry.","Don’t get your dander up over a little spilled milk.","","Dander sounds like anger: when your dander is up, you’re mad."],["circadian","ser-KAY-dee-uhn","adjective","Following a daily 24-hour cycle.","Working night shifts threw off his circadian rhythm, and he could not sleep.","From Latin circa, “about,” and dies, “day.”","Circa means about. Dian means day. About a day."],["belfry","BEL-free","noun","A tower or room where bells hang.","Pigeons nested high in the old church belfry.","","Belfry starts with “bel,” like bell: where the bells hang."],["benchmark","BENCH-mark","noun","A standard used to measure or compare things.","Grandma’s apple pie is the benchmark for every pie in our family.","","Benchmark: a mark on the bench you measure everything against."],["ambrosia","am-BROH-zhuh","noun","Something that tastes or smells wonderful.","Fresh, warm cookies from the oven taste like pure ambrosia.","From Greek ambrosia, “food of the gods.”","Ambrosia was the food of the Greek gods: heavenly taste."],["restive","RES-tiv","adjective","Restless and hard to keep still.","The kids grew restive as the long church service went on.","","Restive has “rest” but means no rest: wiggly and restless."],["impromptu","im-PROMP-too","adjective","Done without planning.","We had an impromptu picnic when the sun came out.","From Latin in promptu, “in readiness.”","Impromptu: not prompted, not planned, just happened."],["felicity","fi-LIS-i-tee","noun","Great happiness, or a well-chosen way of saying something.","The new parents were full of felicity holding their baby.","From Latin felix, “happy.”","Felicity has “feli,” like Felix the happy cat: great happiness."],["witticism","WIT-uh-siz-uhm","noun","A clever, funny remark.","His witticisms made the long car ride fly by.","","Witticism has “wit”: a witty line."],["oasis","oh-AY-sis","noun","A green spot with water in a desert, or any peaceful place.","The little park is an oasis in the busy city.","From Greek oasis.","Oasis sounds like “oh, ease”: a place to rest."],["beeline","BEE-lyn","noun","A straight, quick path to somewhere.","The kids made a beeline for the snack table.","","A bee flies straight home: a beeline."],["brisk","BRISK","adjective","Quick and lively; also cool and fresh.","We took a brisk walk around the block in the cool air.","","Brisk sounds like “quick”: fast and fresh."],["attire","uh-TYR","noun","Clothes, especially for a special event.","The wedding invitation asked for formal attire, so Dad wore a suit.","","Attire sounds like “a tie”: the clothes you dress up in."],["palatable","PAL-uh-tuh-buhl","adjective","Pleasant enough to taste or to accept.","Adding honey made the cough medicine palatable.","","Your palate is the roof of your mouth, where you taste."],["zephyr","ZEF-er","noun","A soft, gentle breeze.","A cool zephyr drifted through the open window.","From Greek zephyros, “the west wind.”","Zephyr sounds like “zip-fur”: a breeze that softly ruffles fur."],["travesty","TRAV-uh-stee","noun","A silly or shocking copy that misses the point.","The burnt, store-bought cake at the fancy party was a travesty.","","Travesty sounds like “tragedy”: a sad, silly mess."],["deprecate","DEP-ri-kayt","verb","To express disapproval of something.","He deprecated his own cooking, but the meal was great.","","Deprecate sounds like “depress the rate”: you rate it low."],["almanac","AWL-muh-nak","noun","A yearly book of facts, weather and calendars.","Grandpa checks the almanac to see when the first frost might come.","","Almanac sounds like “all-man-act”: all the facts for the year."],["commodious","kuh-MOH-dee-uhs","adjective","Roomy and comfortable.","The new minivan is commodious enough for all five kids and the dog.","From Latin commodus, “convenient.”","Commodious is related to accommodate: room to fit everyone."],["neologism","nee-OL-uh-jiz-uhm","noun","A brand new word or phrase.","“Selfie” was once a neologism, and now everyone uses it.","From Greek neos, “new,” and logos, “word.”","Neo means new, and log means word."],["curtail","ker-TAYL","verb","To cut short or reduce.","The snowstorm curtailed our shopping trip.","","Curtail: you cut the tail off something to make it shorter."],["acoustic","uh-KOO-stik","adjective","Having to do with sound, or played without electric power.","He played a soft song on his acoustic guitar with no plug or speaker.","From Greek akouein, “to hear.”","Acoustic sounds like “a cue to listen”: it is all about sound."],["backwater","BAK-waw-ter","noun","A quiet place where little changes or happens.","The town was a sleepy backwater until the new highway came.","","Backwater: water left behind, still and quiet."],["ultimatum","uhl-tuh-MAY-tuhm","noun","A final demand, with a result if it is not met.","Mom gave an ultimatum: clean your room or no phone tonight.","","Ultimatum has “ultimate”: the final, last-chance demand."],["dynamic","dy-NAM-ik","adjective","Full of energy and change.","The dynamic teacher kept every class exciting.","From Greek dynamis, “power.”","Dynamic sounds like dynamite: bursting with energy."],["onerous","ON-er-uhs","adjective","Heavy, hard and tiring to do.","Moving the piano up the stairs was an onerous task.","From Latin onus, “burden.”","Onerous sounds like “oh, nervous”: heavy and hard work."],["aplomb","uh-PLOM","noun","Calm confidence, especially in a hard moment.","She handled the spilled coffee at her job interview with aplomb.","From French à plomb, “straight down, like a plumb line.”","Aplomb comes from “plumb,” standing straight: calm and steady."],["decipher","dih-SY-fer","verb","To figure out the meaning of something hard to read.","Can you decipher the doctor’s messy handwriting?","","Decipher: undo the cipher, a secret code."],["ovation","oh-VAY-shuhn","noun","Loud, long clapping to show praise.","The school choir got a standing ovation.","From Latin ovare, “to rejoice.”","Ovation sounds like “oh, nation!”: the whole crowd cheers."],["culpable","KUHL-puh-buhl","adjective","Deserving blame.","The dog was clearly culpable for the chewed-up shoe.","From Latin culpa, “fault.”","Culpable comes from culpa, Latin for fault: the culprit."],["allegory","AL-uh-gor-ee","noun","A story with a hidden meaning or lesson.","The tortoise and the hare is an allegory: slow and steady wins.","From Greek allegoria, “speaking otherwise.”","Allegory sounds like “a lesson story”: a tale with a hidden lesson."],["pensive","PEN-siv","adjective","Quietly deep in thought.","He sat pensive by the window, watching the snow fall.","From French penser, “to think.”","Pensive comes from French penser, to think."],["flâneur","flah-NUR","noun","Someone who strolls around a city just to enjoy it.","On Sundays, Dad plays the flâneur, strolling downtown with his coffee.","From French flâner, “to stroll.”","Flâneur sounds like “flan-er”: strolling slowly, eating flan, no rush."],["grotto","GROT-oh","noun","A small cave, often a pretty one.","The kids built a little grotto under the dining table with blankets.","From Italian grotta, “cave.”","Grotto sounds like “got a cave”: a cozy little cave."],["brash","BRASH","adjective","Bold and loud in a way that can seem rude.","The brash new salesman interrupted everyone in the meeting.","","Brash sounds like “rash” and “brass”: loud and bold."],["truism","TROO-iz-uhm","noun","A statement so obviously true it hardly needs saying.","It’s a truism that kids grow up fast.","","Truism starts with “true”: so true it’s obvious."],["liminal","LIM-uh-nuhl","adjective","In between two stages or places.","The airport waiting area feels liminal, not here and not there.","From Latin limen, “threshold.”","Liminal comes from Latin “threshold,” the doorway between rooms."],["nondescript","non-dih-SKRIPT","adjective","Plain and hard to describe.","The best tacos came from a nondescript little shop by the gas station.","","Non means not: not easy to describe."],["lilt","LILT","noun","A light, happy rise and fall in a voice or song.","Grandma’s voice has a happy lilt when she sings in the kitchen.","","Lilt sounds like “lift”: a voice that lifts up and down."],["vindicate","VIN-di-kayt","verb","To show that someone was right after all.","The sunny weather vindicated our choice to picnic.","From Latin vindicare, “to claim, set free.”","Vindicate sounds like “victory-cate”: proven right, a win for you."],["precocious","prih-KOH-shuhs","adjective","Showing skills much earlier than usual.","The precocious girl was reading at age three.","From Latin praecox, “ripening early.”","Pre means before: talents that show up before you expect."],["indefatigable","in-di-FAT-i-guh-buhl","adjective","Never seeming to get tired.","The indefatigable nurse worked a double shift without a break.","From Latin indefatigabilis, “untiring.”","Indefatigable: in means not, fatigue means tired: never tired."],["jaded","JAY-did","adjective","Tired and bored from having too much of something.","After years of fast food, he was jaded about burgers.","","Jaded sounds like “faded”: the fun has worn off."],["hone","HOHN","verb","To sharpen or improve a skill.","She practiced every night to hone her piano skills.","","Hone sounds like “honed knife”: sharpening a skill."],["dappled","DAP-uhld","adjective","Marked with spots of light and shade or color.","Sunlight came through the leaves and made dappled spots on the sidewalk.","","Dappled: like a dapple-gray horse, covered in spots."],["unabashed","uhn-uh-BASHT","adjective","Not embarrassed or shy at all.","She sang along in the car with unabashed joy.","","Unabashed: “un” means not, and abashed means embarrassed."],["alabaster","AL-uh-bas-ter","noun","A smooth white stone; also used to mean very pale and smooth.","Her skin was alabaster pale after a long winter indoors.","","Alabaster sounds like “a lab plaster”: smooth and white."],["append","uh-PEND","verb","To add something to the end.","She appended a smiley face to the end of her text.","From Latin appendere, “to hang on.”","Append sounds like “a pendant”: something hung on at the end."],["cardinal","KAR-dn-l","adjective","Most important; main.","The cardinal rule at the pool is no running.","From Latin cardo, “hinge.”","Cardinal comes from Latin cardo, hinge: the rule everything hangs on."],["afoot","uh-FOOT","adjective","Happening or being planned, often secretly.","The giggling in the next room told me a surprise was afoot.","","Afoot: something is on foot, walking around, about to happen."],["ingrained","in-GRAYND","adjective","Deeply fixed and hard to change.","Saying please is ingrained in our kids.","","Ingrained: deep in the grain, like lines in wood."],["diorama","dy-uh-RAM-uh","noun","A small 3D model of a scene.","He built a diorama of a farm in a shoebox for school.","","Diorama sounds like drama: a little scene on a tiny stage."],["bandy","BAN-dee","verb","To toss words or ideas back and forth.","We bandied about names for the new puppy at dinner.","","Bandy sounds like “bounce”: bouncing ideas back and forth."],["aesthetic","es-THET-ik","adjective","Having to do with beauty and what looks pleasing.","She chose the blue curtains for aesthetic reasons; they just looked nice.","From Greek aisthetikos, “of the senses.”","Aesthetic: think “ah, so pretty” when something looks good."],["kiln","KILN","noun","A very hot oven for baking clay or pottery.","At pottery class, we put our mugs in the kiln to bake.","From Latin culina, “kitchen.”","Kiln comes from Latin for kitchen: a kitchen oven’s much hotter cousin."],["manifold","MAN-uh-fohld","adjective","Many and of different kinds.","The good things about having a dog are manifold: walks, cuddles and a happy welcome home.","","Many folds, like a map with many parts."],["snazzy","SNAZ-ee","adjective","Stylish and eye-catching.","He wore snazzy red sneakers to the dance.","","Snazzy sounds like “jazzy”: flashy and fun."],["charlatan","SHAR-luh-tuhn","noun","A person who falsely claims to have special skills.","The man selling “magic” weight loss pills was a charlatan.","","Charlatan sounds like “charlie-tan”: a fake with a fake tan."],["prosaic","proh-ZAY-ik","adjective","Plain and lacking excitement.","He gave the prosaic answer that he ate toast for breakfast.","From Latin prosa, “prose.”","Prosaic sounds like “prose”: plain words, not a poem."],["jaunt","JAWNT","noun","A short trip for fun.","We took a jaunt to the beach for the afternoon.","","Jaunt sounds like “jump out”: a quick fun trip."],["kindred","KIN-drid","adjective","Similar or closely related.","My best friend and I are kindred spirits; we laugh at the same things.","","Kindred has “kin,” like family: close and alike."],["blissful","BLIS-fuhl","adjective","Full of great joy and calm.","We spent a blissful Sunday in our pajamas with pancakes.","","Blissful: full of bliss, pure joy."],["smattering","SMAT-er-ing","noun","A small amount of something.","She speaks a smattering of Spanish from her summer trip.","","Smattering sounds like “scattering”: just a few bits scattered around."],["penultimate","pih-NUL-tuh-mit","adjective","Second to last.","The penultimate episode of the show is my favorite.","From Latin paene, “almost,” and ultimus, “last.”","Pen means almost, and ultimate means last: almost last."],["obsidian","uhb-SID-ee-uhn","noun","A shiny black glass made by volcanoes.","The museum had a smooth piece of obsidian, black glass from a volcano.","","Obsidian sounds like “ob-side-of-volcano”: shiny black glass."],["paragon","PAIR-uh-gon","noun","A perfect example of something.","Our coach is a paragon of patience; she never yells.","","Paragon sounds like “pair of gold”: the gold standard."],["vivacious","vi-VAY-shuhs","adjective","Lively and full of fun.","Our vivacious neighbor always has a funny story.","From Latin vivax, “lively.”","Viva means life: vivacious means lively."],["droll","DROHL","adjective","Funny in an odd or dry way.","Her droll remarks kept the dinner lively.","From French drôle, “funny.”","Droll sounds like “troll”: a funny, odd little joker."],["hibernate","HY-ber-nayt","verb","To spend the winter in a deep sleep.","Bears hibernate all winter, and so does my cat on the couch.","From Latin hibernare, “to spend the winter.”","Hibernate sounds like “hide-in-bed-ate”: sleeping all winter long."],["rebuke","ri-BYOOK","verb","To scold someone sharply.","Mom rebuked my brother for leaving his bike in the driveway.","","Rebuke sounds like “rebuff”: sharp words that push you back."],["partake","par-TAYK","verb","To join in or take a share.","Everyone partook of the birthday cake.","","Partake: take a part of it."],["obsolete","ob-suh-LEET","adjective","No longer used because something newer came along.","Our old DVD player is obsolete now that we stream everything.","From Latin obsolescere, “to grow old.”","Obsolete sounds like “absolutely old”: not used anymore."],["bumptious","BUHMP-shuhs","adjective","Too sure of yourself in an annoying way.","The bumptious new player told the coach how to run practice.","","Bumptious has “bump”: bumping in where you’re not asked."],["disconcert","dis-kuhn-SURT","verb","To unsettle or confuse someone.","The sudden silence on the phone call disconcerted her.","","Disconcert: your concert is thrown off, out of tune."],["incisive","in-SY-siv","adjective","Sharp and clear in thinking.","Her incisive question got right to the heart of the problem.","From Latin incidere, “to cut into.”","Incisive sounds like incisor teeth: sharp and cutting."],["knickknack","NIK-nak","noun","A small decorative object.","Grandma’s shelf is full of tiny knickknacks.","","Knickknack: a little knick and a little knack, small things."],["stoic","STOH-ik","adjective","Calm and patient, not showing upset feelings.","She stayed stoic while the doctor gave her a shot.","From Greek stoa, “porch,” where early thinkers taught.","Stoic sounds like “stone-ic”: calm and steady as a stone."],["incandescent","in-kuhn-DES-uhnt","adjective","Glowing with heat, or shining brightly.","The incandescent light bulb glowed warm and bright.","From Latin incandescere, “to glow.”","Incandescent has “candle” inside: glowing with heat."],["morsel","MOR-suhl","noun","A small bite of food.","The dog gobbled up every morsel I dropped from the table.","From Old French morsel, “little bite.”","A morsel is a small bite, like a little nibble."],["endeavor","en-DEV-er","noun","A serious try at doing something.","Painting the whole house was a family endeavor.","","Endeavor sounds like “end ever”: a big try you hope ends well."],["aloft","uh-LAWFT","adverb","Up in the air.","The kite stayed aloft all afternoon at the beach.","","Aloft sounds like “a loft”: up high, like a loft room."],["kelp","KELP","noun","A large brown seaweed.","At the beach, my toes got tangled in slimy kelp.","","Kelp sounds like “help!”: when the seaweed tangles your feet."],["quench","KWENCH","verb","To end your thirst by drinking.","A cold glass of lemonade quenched my thirst after mowing the lawn.","From Old English cwencan, “to put out (a fire).”","Quench sounds like “drench”: drench your dry mouth with water."],["vestige","VES-tij","noun","A small trace of something that is mostly gone.","A vestige of birthday cake was still on the kitchen counter.","From Latin vestigium, “footprint.”","Vestige sounds like “vest”: a small leftover piece."],["boorish","BOOR-ish","adjective","Rude and rough in manners.","His boorish habit of talking with his mouth full annoyed everyone.","","Boorish sounds like “boar-ish”: manners like a wild pig."],["fey","FAY","adjective","Strange and dreamy, with a touch of magic.","The quiet girl had a fey, dreamy look, like she knew a secret.","","Fey sounds like fairy: a little dreamy and magic."],["camaraderie","kah-muh-RAH-duh-ree","noun","A warm feeling of friendship among people in a group.","After a long shift, the camaraderie at work made everyone laugh together.","From French camarade, “comrade.”","Camaraderie starts like comrade: a friend on your team."],["dissipate","DIS-uh-payt","verb","To fade away or scatter.","The morning fog dissipated by noon.","","Dissipate: like steam from hot soup that drifts away."],["pundit","PUN-dit","noun","An expert who gives opinions to the public.","The sports pundit on TV guessed the winner correctly.","From Hindi pandit, “learned man.”","Pundit sounds like “pun-dit”: talking all day on TV."],["quixotic","kwik-SOT-ik","adjective","Full of big, noble dreams that are hard to make real.","His quixotic plan was to never let the kitchen get messy again.","From Don Quixote, the dreamy hero of a Spanish novel.","Think “Quick, fix it all!”: a dreamer who wants to fix everything."],["incorrigible","in-KOR-i-juh-buhl","adjective","Not able to be changed or corrected.","Our incorrigible cat keeps jumping on the counter no matter what.","","Incorrigible: in means not, so it can’t be corrected."],["akin","uh-KIN","adjective","Similar to or like something.","Learning to drive is akin to learning to ride a bike; it takes practice.","","Akin has “kin,” meaning family: alike, like family."],["platitude","PLAT-ih-tood","noun","A saying used so much it has lost its meaning.","“It is what it is” is a common platitude.","From French plat, “flat.”","Plat means flat: a saying that falls flat."],["oxymoron","ok-see-MOR-on","noun","Two words with opposite meanings used together, like jumbo shrimp.","“Pretty ugly” is a funny oxymoron.","From Greek oxys, “sharp,” and moros, “dull.”","Oxy means sharp, and moron means dull: sharp and dull together."],["aquamarine","ak-wuh-muh-REEN","noun","A light blue-green color, like clear sea water.","The water in the hotel pool was a bright aquamarine.","From Latin aqua marina, “sea water.”","Aqua means water, marine means sea: the color of sea water."],["variegated","VAIR-ee-uh-gay-tid","adjective","Having patches or streaks of different colors.","My houseplant has variegated leaves of green and cream.","From Latin variegare, “to make varied.”","Variegated has “vari,” like various: many colors."],["flair","FLAIR","noun","A natural talent or a stylish way of doing things.","She has a flair for making any room look cozy.","","Flair sounds like flare: your talent shines bright."],["rueful","ROO-fuhl","adjective","Showing a little regret, often with a gentle smile.","He gave a rueful grin after dropping his ice cream.","","Rueful has “rue,” which means regret: full of regret."],["insouciant","in-SOO-see-uhnt","adjective","Calm and carefree.","She gave an insouciant shrug when she missed the bus.","From French insouciant, “not caring.”","Insouciant: in means not, souci is French for worry: no worries."],["poise","POYZ","noun","Calm and graceful control.","She spoke with poise in front of the whole school.","","Poise sounds like “poised”: standing tall and steady."],["bucolic","byoo-KOL-ik","adjective","Having to do with peaceful country life.","We rented a cabin in a bucolic valley full of cows and farms.","From Greek boukolos, “a cowherd.”","Bucolic sounds like “cow-colic”: peaceful country life."],["symbiosis","sim-bee-OH-sis","noun","A partnership where both sides help each other.","My sister cooks and I clean, so we live in happy symbiosis.","From Greek symbiōsis, “living together.”","Sym means together, and bio means life: living together."],["dignity","DIG-ni-tee","noun","A calm sense of self-respect.","She handled losing her job with grace and dignity.","From Latin dignus, “worthy.”","Dignity sounds like dignified: standing tall."],["dilettante","DIL-i-tahnt","noun","Someone who dabbles in an interest without going deep.","He’s a dilettante who has tried ten hobbies this year.","From Italian dilettare, “to delight.”","Dilettante sounds like “delight”: just doing it for fun, not deep."],["benighted","bih-NY-tid","adjective","Lacking knowledge or understanding.","He joked that he was benighted about anything to do with phones.","","Benighted has “night”: stuck in the dark, not knowing."],["crux","KRUHKS","noun","The most important part of a problem.","The crux of the problem is who will drive the kids to school.","From Latin crux, “cross.”","Crux is Latin for cross: where the roads cross, the key spot."],["bellow","BEL-oh","verb","To shout in a deep, loud voice.","The coach bellowed instructions across the soccer field.","","Bellow sounds like “yellow” and “below”: a deep, loud yell."],["idyllic","eye-DIL-ik","adjective","Peaceful and perfect, like a pretty picture.","The cabin by the lake was idyllic, quiet and sunny.","From Greek eidyllion, “little picture.”","Idyllic sounds like “ideal”: a perfect little picture."],["cadence","KAY-duhns","noun","The rise and fall of a voice or the beat of a sound.","Grandpa reads bedtime stories in a slow, sleepy cadence.","From Latin cadere, “to fall.”","Cadence sounds like “cade-dance”: words that dance up and down."],["ersatz","ER-zahts","adjective","Fake, used in place of the real thing.","The ersatz leather bag peeled after one month.","From German Ersatz, “replacement.”","Ersatz sounds like “her sats” on a fake throne: not the real thing."],["ineffable","in-EF-uh-buhl","adjective","Too great or beautiful to put into words.","The joy of holding my newborn was ineffable.","From Latin ineffabilis, “unutterable.”","Ineffable: in means not, so you cannot say it."],["murky","MUR-kee","adjective","Dark, cloudy or unclear.","We could not see the bottom of the murky pool after the storm.","","Murky sounds like “mud-key”: dark and muddy."],["memoir","MEM-wahr","noun","A true story someone writes about their own life.","Grandma is writing a memoir about growing up on a farm.","From French mémoire, “memory.”","Memoir sounds like “memory”: a book of my memories."],["aptitude","AP-ti-tood","noun","A natural ability to do or learn something.","My daughter has an aptitude for math; numbers just make sense to her.","From Latin aptus, “fitted.”","Apt means fit or able: an aptitude is a natural ability."],["doggerel","DAW-ger-uhl","noun","Silly, clumsy verse.","He wrote some doggerel for Mom’s birthday card.","","Doggerel: poems so bad, only the dog would like them."],["quip","KWIP","noun","A quick, clever, funny remark.","Dad made a quip about the rain that made everyone laugh.","","Quip sounds like “quick”: a quick, funny line."],["brackish","BRAK-ish","adjective","Slightly salty, like where a river meets the sea.","The water where the river meets the ocean tastes brackish, a little salty.","","Brackish sounds like “brine-ish”: a bit salty."],["oodles","OO-dlz","noun","A lot of something.","We have oodles of zucchini from the garden.","","Oodles sounds like noodles: piles and piles of them."],["conscientious","kon-shee-EN-shuhs","adjective","Careful to do things well and right.","A conscientious worker, she checks every email twice before sending.","","Conscientious has conscience in it: you care about doing it right."],["chortle","CHOR-tl","verb","To laugh in a happy, breathy way.","Dad chortled at the funny video on his phone.","","Chortle mixes chuckle and snort: a happy, snorty laugh."],["nostalgia","no-STAL-juh","noun","A warm longing for the past.","Old songs from the radio fill my mom with nostalgia.","From Greek nostos, “return home,” and algos, “pain.”","Nostalgia sounds like “nose tells ya” the smells of home."],["leitmotif","LYT-moh-teef","noun","A theme that keeps coming back.","Being late is the leitmotif of my brother’s life.","From German Leitmotiv, “leading motive.”","Leitmotif: German for “leading motive,” a theme that keeps leading back."],["extol","ik-STOHL","verb","To praise something highly.","Mom loves to extol the joys of a good night’s sleep.","From Latin extollere, “to lift up.”","Extol sounds like “extra toll”: you ring the bell of praise."],["spindly","SPIND-lee","adjective","Long, thin and weak looking.","The new puppy stood on spindly legs.","","Spindly sounds like “spindle”: thin as a stick."],["chutzpah","HOOT-spuh","noun","Bold nerve or daring.","It took chutzpah to ask for a raise on her first week.","From Yiddish khutspe, “boldness.”","Chutzpah sounds like “cuts in”: bold enough to cut in line."],["thrifty","THRIF-tee","adjective","Careful with money and not wasteful.","My thrifty aunt reuses old jars to store leftovers.","","Thrifty sounds like “thrift store”: saves money, wastes nothing."],["presage","PRES-ij","verb","To be a sign of something to come.","Dark clouds presaged a stormy night.","","Pre means before: a sign that comes before."],["nourish","NUR-ish","verb","To feed and help grow.","A hot bowl of soup nourishes you on a cold day.","From Latin nutrire, “to feed.”","Nourish sounds like nurse: to feed and care for."],["contemplate","KON-tuhm-playt","verb","To think about something slowly and deeply.","She sat on the porch to contemplate whether to take the new job.","","Contemplate: you stare at your plate and think deeply."],["ameliorate","uh-MEEL-yuh-rayt","verb","To make something bad better.","A new fan helped ameliorate the heat in our tiny apartment.","From Latin melior, “better.”","Ameliorate has “melior,” Latin for better: make it better."],["undaunted","uhn-DAWN-tid","adjective","Not scared off by difficulty.","Undaunted by the rain, we finished our walk.","","Undaunted: “un” means not, and daunted means scared off."],["dissuade","dih-SWAYD","verb","To talk someone out of doing something.","We dissuaded him from swimming in the cold lake.","From Latin dissuadere, “to advise against.”","Dissuade: the opposite of persuade, you talk them out of it."],["sesquipedalian","ses-kwi-puh-DAY-lee-uhn","adjective","Using very long words.","His sesquipedalian email made us reach for a dictionary.","From Latin sesquipedalis, “a foot and a half long.”","Sesquipedalian means “a foot and a half long”: words that long."],["accord","uh-KORD","noun","Agreement, or a deal that settles a disagreement.","The neighbors reached an accord about where to put the new fence.","","Accord sounds like “a cord” tying two sides together in agreement."],["yammer","YAM-er","verb","To talk loudly and on and on.","The kids yammered all the way home from camp.","","Yammer sounds like “yak and hammer”: talking nonstop."],["breakneck","BRAYK-nek","adjective","Very fast, often too fast.","The kids sledded down the hill at breakneck speed.","","Breakneck: so fast you could break your neck."],["hyperbole","hy-PUR-buh-lee","noun","Big exaggeration, not meant to be taken literally.","Saying “I’m starving to death” before lunch is hyperbole.","From Greek hyperbole, “a throwing beyond.”","Hyper means over, as in hyperactive: an over-the-top claim."],["reconcile","REK-uhn-syl","verb","To become friends again after a fight.","The two sisters reconciled after their fight and shared a long hug.","From Latin reconciliare, “to bring together again.”","Reconcile sounds like “re-council”: sit down again and make peace."],["equanimity","ee-kwuh-NIM-i-tee","noun","Staying calm and steady, even when things go wrong.","Mom handled the flat tire with equanimity, calmly calling for help.","From Latin aequus, “even,” and animus, “mind.”","Equanimity: equal mind, staying even and calm."],["helix","HEE-liks","noun","A spiral shape, like a spring.","The kids’ slide at the water park twists down like a helix.","From Greek helix, “spiral.”","Picture a helix as a slinky toy, twisting round and round."],["tantalize","TAN-tl-yz","verb","To tease someone with something they want but cannot have yet.","The smell of pizza in the oven tantalized us all afternoon.","From Tantalus, a figure in Greek myth who could never reach his food.","Tantalize sounds like “tantrum-ize”: so tempting you could throw a tantrum."],["succor","SUK-er","noun","Help and comfort in a hard time.","Neighbors brought succor to the family after their house flooded.","From Latin succurrere, “to run to help.”","Succor sounds like “soccer”: teammates helping you up when you fall."],["dispense","dih-SPENS","verb","To hand out.","The machine at work dispenses free coffee.","","A soap dispenser hands out soap."],["denote","dih-NOHT","verb","To be a sign of something.","A star on the map denotes our hotel.","","Denote has note in it: a note that marks it."],["kerfuffle","ker-FUF-uhl","noun","A fuss or commotion.","There was a kerfuffle at the checkout over a coupon.","","Kerfuffle sounds like a ruffle: a little fuss."],["evocative","ih-VOK-uh-tiv","adjective","Bringing strong memories or feelings to mind.","The smell of pumpkin pie is evocative of holidays at Grandma’s house.","From Latin evocare, “to call out.”","Evocative has “voc,” voice: it calls up a memory."],["dovetail","DUHV-tayl","verb","To fit together neatly.","Her work schedule dovetailed with the kids’ school day.","","A dovetail joint locks two wood pieces like puzzle pieces."],["transcend","tran-SEND","verb","To go beyond the usual limits.","Music can transcend language and bring people together.","From Latin transcendere, “to climb over.”","Trans means across, and scend means climb: you climb beyond."],["vim","VIM","noun","Energy and enthusiasm.","She cleaned the whole house with vim and energy.","","Vim sounds like “vroom”: full of get-up-and-go."],["ornithology","or-nuh-THOL-uh-jee","noun","The study of birds.","Her love of ornithology began with a bird feeder in the yard.","From Greek ornis, “bird.”","Ornith means bird, and ology means study."],["baroque","buh-ROHK","adjective","Very fancy and full of decoration and detail.","The old theater had baroque gold carvings on every wall.","From French baroque, “irregular in shape.”","Baroque sounds like “bare rock? No!”: covered in fancy decoration."],["wallflower","WAWL-flow-er","noun","A shy person who stays on the side at parties.","The wallflower at the party turned out to be a wonderful dancer.","","Wallflower: a person stuck to the wall like a flower painted on it."],["cryptic","KRIP-tik","adjective","Mysterious and hard to understand.","My son’s cryptic text just said “we need to talk.”","From Greek kryptos, “hidden.”","Cryptic sounds like crypt: hidden and secret."],["dally","DAL-ee","verb","To move slowly or waste time.","Don’t dally at the store, or the ice cream will melt.","","Dally sounds like “dilly-dally”: slow and poking around."],["orchard","OR-cherd","noun","A field of fruit trees.","We picked apples at the orchard on Saturday.","","Orchard sounds like “orch-yard”: a yard full of fruit trees."],["axiom","AK-see-uhm","noun","A statement accepted as true without needing proof.","It is an axiom in our house that dessert comes after vegetables.","From Greek axioma, “something worthy.”","Axiom sounds like “accept-iom”: a rule everyone accepts."],["mesmerize","MEZ-muh-ryz","verb","To hold someone’s full attention like a spell.","The dancing campfire mesmerized the children.","","Mesmerize sounds like “mess my eyes”: you can’t look away."],["enamored","ih-NAM-erd","adjective","Filled with love or delight for something.","My daughter is enamored with her new puppy and won’t put him down.","From Old French enamourer, “to fill with love.”","Enamored has “amor,” love: you are in love with it."],["haughty","HAW-tee","adjective","Acting like you are better than others.","The haughty shopper wouldn’t speak to the cashier.","","Haughty sounds like “high-ty”: nose high up in the air."],["tendril","TEN-dril","noun","A thin, curling stem that a plant uses to climb.","The bean plant in our yard wrapped its tendrils around the fence.","","Tendril sounds like “tender drill”: a soft curl that twists and grabs."],["keel","KEEL","noun","The long beam along the bottom of a boat.","The boat’s keel scraped the bottom near the dock.","","Keel sounds like “keep”: it keeps the boat steady."],["perplex","per-PLEKS","verb","To confuse or puzzle.","The new TV remote perplexed Grandpa.","","Perplex sounds like “pretty complex”: it leaves you puzzled."],["lodestar","LOHD-star","noun","A star used to guide travelers, or a guiding idea.","Kindness is the lodestar that guides how we raise our kids.","","Lodestar: a load-star, the guiding star that leads the way."],["hoard","HORD","verb","To collect and hide away lots of something.","My dad hoards rubber bands in the kitchen drawer.","","Hoard sounds like horde: a whole big pile, hidden away."],["umami","oo-MAH-mee","noun","A rich, savory taste, like in broth or mushrooms.","The mushroom soup had a deep umami flavor.","From Japanese umami, “delicious taste.”","Umami is Japanese for “tasty”: that rich, savory taste."],["connoisseur","kon-uh-SUR","noun","An expert who knows and enjoys fine things.","Grandma is a connoisseur of good coffee.","From old French conoistre, “to know.”","Connoisseur sounds like “con-oh-sir”: a sir who really knows his stuff."],["intrinsic","in-TRIN-sik","adjective","Belonging to the basic nature of something.","Kindness is intrinsic to who she is.","","Intrinsic sounds like “in-trench”: built deep inside."],["churlish","CHUR-lish","adjective","Rude and bad-mannered.","It would be churlish not to thank your aunt for the sweater.","","Churlish sounds like “churl-ish”: acting like a grumpy grump."],["quiescent","kwee-ES-uhnt","adjective","Quiet and still, at least for now.","The baby was quiescent for an hour, so I finally drank my coffee.","From Latin quiescere, “to rest.”","Quiescent starts like “quiet”: still and quiet for now."],["crestfallen","KREST-faw-luhn","adjective","Sad and let down.","He was crestfallen when the rain canceled his soccer game.","","Crestfallen: your crest, or the top of your head, falls down in sadness."],["alight","uh-LYT","verb","To land gently, or to step down from a vehicle.","A fly alighted on the rim of my glass of juice.","","Alight sounds like “a light” landing: something gently coming down."],["dulcet","DUHL-sit","adjective","Sweet and soothing to hear.","We woke to the dulcet sound of Grandma singing in the kitchen.","From Latin dulcis, “sweet.”","Dulce means sweet in Spanish, like dulce de leche."],["serrated","SER-ay-tid","adjective","Having a jagged edge like a saw.","Use the serrated knife to slice the bread.","From Latin serra, “saw.”","Serrated sounds like “saw-rated”: rated as sharp as a saw."],["pinnacle","PIN-uh-kuhl","noun","The highest point.","Winning the spelling bee was the pinnacle of her school year.","","Pinnacle sounds like pin: the sharp tip at the top."],["timorous","TIM-er-uhs","adjective","Shy and easily frightened.","The timorous little boy hid behind his mom’s legs.","From Latin timor, “fear.”","Timorous sounds like “timid”: shy and scared."],["grueling","GROO-uh-ling","adjective","Very tiring and hard.","The grueling move took us all weekend.","","Grueling sounds like “gruel”: hard work that leaves you wiped out."],["exemplary","ig-ZEM-pluh-ree","adjective","So good it is worth copying.","His exemplary manners at dinner impressed Grandma.","From Latin exemplum, “example.”","Exemplary has “example”: so good everyone should follow it."],["blunder","BLUHN-der","noun","A careless, silly mistake.","Putting salt in the cake instead of sugar was quite a blunder.","","Blunder sounds like “blunt thunder”: a big, clumsy mistake."],["apocryphal","uh-POK-ruh-fuhl","adjective","Widely told but probably not true.","The story that Grandpa once met a famous movie star is probably apocryphal.","","Apocryphal sounds like “a lot of cry-foul”: a tale people doubt."],["marginalia","mar-juh-NAY-lee-uh","noun","Notes written in the edges of a book page.","I love reading the marginalia people scribble in old library books.","From Latin margo, “edge.”","Notes in the margin, the edge of the page."],["unequivocal","uhn-i-KWIV-uh-kuhl","adjective","Totally clear, with no doubt.","When I asked if he wanted pizza, his answer was an unequivocal yes.","","Unequivocal: “un” means not, so no wishy-washy “equal” maybe."],["raconteur","rak-on-TUR","noun","A person who tells stories in a lively, fun way.","Uncle Sam is a born raconteur at every family dinner.","From French raconter, “to tell.”","Raconteur sounds like “racket tour”: a fun, loud tour through his stories."],["terse","TURS","adjective","Using few words, sometimes in a short, sharp way.","Her terse reply to my long text was simply, “Got it.”","From Latin tersus, “wiped clean.”","Terse sounds like “curse”: short and sharp."],["crepuscular","kri-PUHS-kyuh-ler","adjective","Having to do with twilight; active at dusk or dawn.","Rabbits are crepuscular, so we see them in the yard at sunset and early morning.","From Latin crepusculum, “twilight.”","Crepuscular sounds like “creep”: creatures that creep out at dusk."],["staunch","STAWNCH","adjective","Loyal and firm in support.","She has always been my staunch friend.","","Staunch sounds like “stand”: she stands by you."],["peripatetic","pair-uh-puh-TET-ik","adjective","Traveling from place to place.","My peripatetic cousin works in a new city every few months.","From Greek peripatein, “to walk around.”","Peri means around, and patetic sounds like “paddle”: always walking around."],["equivocate","ih-KWIV-uh-kayt","verb","To answer in a vague way to avoid saying what you mean.","When asked who ate the last cookie, my brother began to equivocate.","From Latin aequus, “equal,” and vocare, “to call.”","Equivocate: equal voices, saying both yes and no at once."],["condone","kuhn-DOHN","verb","To accept or allow behavior that is wrong.","Our family does not condone cheating at board games.","","Condone sounds like “Don’s okay with it”: he lets wrong things slide."],["revere","ri-VEER","verb","To deeply respect and admire someone.","The whole town revered the kind old doctor.","From Latin revereri, “to stand in awe of.”","Revere sounds like “reverend”: someone you deeply respect."],["levity","LEV-i-tee","noun","Light humor at a serious time.","His joke added some levity to the long meeting.","From Latin levis, “light.”","Levity sounds like “levitate”: humor that lifts the mood."],["vouchsafe","vowch-SAYF","verb","To kindly give or grant something.","The shy cat finally vouchsafed a purr.","","Vouchsafe: picture someone vouching it is safe to give."],["magnanimous","mag-NAN-uh-muhs","adjective","Generous and kind, even to someone who has wronged you.","She was magnanimous and shared her prize with the friend who beat her last year.","From Latin magnus, “great,” and animus, “soul.”","Magna means great, like a magnet with a great big heart."],["doughty","DOU-tee","adjective","Brave and determined.","The doughty little tugboat pulled the big ship.","","Doughty sounds like doughnut: small and round, but tough to stop."],["wistful","WIST-fuhl","adjective","Gently sad and longing for something.","She looked at her old photos with a wistful smile.","","Wistful sounds like “wish-ful”: full of wishes for the past."],["yen","YEN","noun","A strong wish or craving.","I have a sudden yen for chocolate chip cookies.","","Yen sounds like “yearn”: a quick craving."],["squander","SKWON-der","verb","To waste something, like money or time.","Don’t squander your paycheck on snacks from the gas station.","","Squander sounds like “squash under”: money squashed and wasted."],["deplore","dih-PLOR","verb","To strongly dislike or disapprove of.","We deplore litter in the park.","","Deplore sounds like “the floor”: you hate trash on the floor."],["skittish","SKIT-ish","adjective","Easily scared or nervous.","The skittish kitten hid every time the doorbell rang.","","Skittish sounds like “skitter”: jumpy and quick to run."],["opus","OH-puhs","noun","A piece of work, especially music.","The singer called her new album her greatest opus.","From Latin opus, “work.”","Opus sounds like “oh, plus!”: a big work."],["linchpin","LINCH-pin","noun","The most important part that holds things together.","Our babysitter is the linchpin of our busy week.","","Linchpin: the pin that keeps the wheel on the wagon."],["picayune","pik-ee-YOON","adjective","Small and not important.","Let’s not argue about picayune details like napkin colors.","","Picayune sounds like “pick a tune”: tiny, picky stuff."],["jigsaw","JIG-saw","noun","A puzzle of pieces that fit together.","We worked on a jigsaw puzzle at the kitchen table all weekend.","","A jigsaw cuts wavy shapes that fit together like puzzle pieces."],["censure","SEN-sher","noun","Strong, formal disapproval.","The school’s censure made it clear that bullying would not be allowed.","","Censure sounds like “censor”: a strong, official “no.”"],["coherent","koh-HEER-uhnt","adjective","Clear and making sense as a whole.","After a cup of coffee, his morning texts were finally coherent.","From Latin cohaerere, “to stick together.”","Coherent: all the parts cohere, or stick together, and make sense."],["lyrical","LIR-i-kuhl","adjective","Expressing feeling in a beautiful, song-like way.","She gave a lyrical speech about her mom.","From Greek lyra, “lyre.”","Lyrical has “lyric,” like song words: flowing and pretty."],["masquerade","mas-kuh-RAYD","noun","A party where people wear masks, or a false show.","The school held a masquerade where everyone wore paper masks.","","It starts with “mask,” so think of a mask party."],["lackadaisical","lak-uh-DAY-zi-kuhl","adjective","Lazy and without much care.","His lackadaisical cleaning left crumbs all over the table.","","Lackadaisical: lacking care, lazy as a daisy in the sun."],["desultory","DES-uhl-tor-ee","adjective","Aimless and jumping from one thing to another.","We had a desultory chat while waiting for the bus.","From Latin desultor, “a leaper.”","Desultory sounds like “dust-story”: drifting like dust, no aim."],["perturb","per-TURB","verb","To worry or upset someone.","The loud thunder did not perturb the sleeping baby.","From Latin perturbare, “to throw into confusion.”","Perturb sounds like disturb."],["officious","uh-FISH-uhs","adjective","Too eager to give orders or help when not wanted.","The officious hall monitor told us where to stand three times.","","Officious sounds like “office-y”: bossy like an office boss."],["divvy","DIV-ee","verb","To share out among people.","Let’s divvy up the pizza so everyone gets two slices.","","Divvy is short for divide."],["bandwidth","BAND-width","noun","The time and energy someone has for a task.","I don’t have the bandwidth to cook tonight, so let’s order pizza.","","Bandwidth is like your phone data: you only have so much."],["gauntlet","GAWNT-lit","noun","A challenge, or a heavy glove.","My sister threw down the gauntlet: whoever loses cleans the kitchen.","","Throw down the gauntlet, your glove, to start a challenge."],["aplenty","uh-PLEN-tee","adjective","In large amounts; more than enough.","There were snacks aplenty at the family reunion.","","Aplenty has “plenty” inside: more than enough."],["rustic","RUS-tik","adjective","Simple and charming, like life in the country.","We ate at a rustic wooden table with a checkered cloth.","From Latin rusticus, “of the countryside.”","Rustic sounds like “rusty”: old, simple, country style."],["rambunctious","ram-BUNGK-shuhs","adjective","Wild, noisy and full of energy.","The rambunctious kids jumped on every couch in the living room.","","Rambunctious sounds like “ram bouncing”: wild, loud and bouncy."],["garrulous","GAR-uh-luhs","adjective","Very talkative, often about small things.","Our garrulous neighbor can talk for an hour about her tomatoes.","From Latin garrulus, “chattering.”","Garrulous sounds like “gargle-us”: words gurgling out nonstop."],["lambent","LAM-buhnt","adjective","Glowing softly or flickering.","The lambent candlelight flickered on the dinner table.","From Latin lambere, “to lick.”","Lambent comes from Latin “to lick”: soft flames licking the air."],["chagrin","shuh-GRIN","noun","Embarrassment from failing or being let down.","To my chagrin, I sent the text to the wrong person.","From French chagrin, “sorrow.”","Chagrin: your grin turns into a “sha!” of embarrassment."],["surreptitious","sur-uhp-TISH-uhs","adjective","Done secretly so no one notices.","He took a surreptitious bite of the birthday cake frosting.","","Surreptitious sounds like “sneaky-titious”: done when no one is looking."],["ulterior","uhl-TEER-ee-er","adjective","Hidden on purpose, beyond what is shown.","His ulterior motive for helping was a slice of cake.","From Latin ulterior, “farther.”","Ulterior sounds like “ultra-interior”: hidden deep inside."],["vogue","VOHG","noun","What is popular or in fashion right now.","Big puffy jackets are in vogue again.","From French vogue, “fashion.”","Vogue sounds like “vote”: what everyone votes is cool now."],["gumption","GUMP-shuhn","noun","Courage, common sense and get-up-and-go.","It took gumption to ask for a raise.","","Gumption sounds like “gum-shun”: stick to it, like gum, and go."],["yearn","YURN","verb","To want something very much, often with longing.","On cold days, I yearn for summer at the lake.","From Old English giernan, “to desire.”","Yearn sounds like “year”: waiting all year for it."],["debacle","day-BAH-kuhl","noun","A sudden, total failure.","The first family camping trip was a debacle: the tent leaked and the car got stuck.","From French débâcler, “to unbar.”","Debacle sounds like “de-buckle”: everything comes unbuckled at once."],["blather","BLATH-er","verb","To talk on and on about silly things.","He blathered about his fantasy football team all through dinner.","","Blather sounds like “blah-blah-ther”: talking on and on."],["moot","MOOT","adjective","No longer worth talking about, or open to debate.","Whether to have a picnic was moot once it started to rain.","","Moot sounds like mute: no need to talk about it anymore."],["octogenarian","ok-tuh-juh-NAIR-ee-uhn","noun","A person in their eighties.","The octogenarian runner finished the race with a big grin.","From Latin octogenarius, “of eighty.”","Octo means eight, like an octopus: someone in their eighties."],["panache","puh-NASH","noun","Bold, stylish flair.","She served the pancakes with real panache, flipping them high in the air.","","Panache sounds like “pan-ash”: flipping a pan with flair."],["ancillary","AN-suh-ler-ee","adjective","Helping or supporting something more important.","The hospital has ancillary staff like cooks and cleaners who help the doctors.","From Latin ancilla, “a maidservant.”","Ancillary sounds like “an extra helper”: support, not the main job."],["palatial","puh-LAY-shuhl","adjective","Grand and large, like a palace.","The library has a palatial reading room with tall windows.","","Palatial sounds like palace."],["aphorism","AF-uh-riz-uhm","noun","A short, wise saying.","Grandma’s favorite aphorism is “Early to bed, early to rise.”","From Greek aphorismos, “a definition.”","Aphorism sounds like “a four-word-ism”: a short, wise saying."],["brogue","BROHG","noun","A sturdy leather shoe with small holes in a pattern.","Dad polished his brown brogues for the wedding.","","Brogue sounds like “brogue-shoe”: a sturdy shoe with tiny holes."],["gingerly","JIN-jer-lee","adverb","Very carefully and gently.","She gingerly carried the full cup of coffee up the stairs.","","Gingerly: like stepping softly on a sore toe."],["novel","NOV-uhl","adjective","New and different in an interesting way.","Using a sock as a puppet was a novel idea.","From Latin novus, “new.”","Nov means new, like a brand-new novel."],["bedazzle","bih-DAZ-uhl","verb","To amaze someone with brightness or cleverness.","The fireworks bedazzled the crowd on the Fourth of July.","","Bedazzle has “dazzle” inside: amaze with shine."],["credence","KREED-ns","noun","Belief that something is true.","Nobody gave credence to his story that the dog ate his homework.","From Latin credere, “to believe.”","Cred means believe, like credit and credible."],["allude","uh-LOOD","verb","To mention something in an indirect way.","Dad alluded to a surprise but would not say more.","From Latin alludere, “to play with.”","Allude sounds like “a lure”: a hint that pulls you toward the idea."],["oeuvre","OO-vruh","noun","All the works made by an artist or writer.","The art show had her whole oeuvre, from early sketches to big murals.","From French oeuvre, “work.”","Oeuvre (UHV-ruh) means work: all of your work together."],["rancor","RANG-ker","noun","Bitter, lasting anger or ill will.","The two neighbors settled their fight over the fence without any rancor.","From Latin rancor, “rankness, a grudge.”","Rancor sounds like “rank”: anger that goes rank and sour."],["bumbling","BUHM-bling","adjective","Clumsy and confused in a likable way.","The bumbling dad in the movie still saved the day.","","Bumbling sounds like a bumblebee bumping into things."],["glimmer","GLIM-er","noun","A faint, wavering light.","A glimmer of light came from under the bedroom door.","","Glimmer sounds like glitter: a faint, twinkly light."],["clandestine","klan-DES-tin","adjective","Done in secret.","The kids held a clandestine meeting to plan Mom’s birthday party.","From Latin clandestinus, “secret.”","Clandestine sounds like “clan’s destiny”: a secret family plan."],["wunderkind","VOON-der-kint","noun","A young person who is amazingly talented.","The chess wunderkind beat players three times her age.","From German Wunderkind, “wonder child.”","Wunderkind is German for “wonder child,” a kid with amazing talent."],["comeuppance","kuhm-UHP-uhns","noun","A fair result someone gets for bad behavior.","The driver who cut everyone off got his comeuppance when the police pulled him over.","","Comeuppance: what you had coming up for you."],["comport","kuhm-PORT","verb","To behave in a certain way.","The kids comported themselves well at the fancy restaurant.","From Latin comportare, “to carry together.”","Comport: how you carry yourself. Port means carry, like a porter."],["knoll","NOHL","noun","A small, round hill.","We had a picnic on a grassy knoll in the park.","","Knoll sounds like “knoll-ball”: a round little hill."],["craven","KRAY-vuhn","adjective","Lacking courage.","The craven bully ran away when the teacher showed up.","","Craven sounds like “crave-in”: caving in from fear."],["contemporary","kuhn-TEM-puh-rer-ee","adjective","Of the present time; modern.","Our new kitchen has a clean, contemporary look.","From Latin tempus, “time.”","Con means with. Tempor means time. With our time, modern."],["esoteric","es-uh-TER-ik","adjective","Understood by only a small group of people.","My uncle loves esoteric rules for old card games no one else knows.","From Greek esoterikos, “inner.”","Esoteric sounds like “so-secret-ic”: known by only a few."],["junket","JUNG-kit","noun","A pleasure trip, often paid for by someone else.","The boss went on a junket to a beach resort, paid for by the company.","","Junket sounds like “junk-it” work: a free fun trip."],["albeit","awl-BEE-it","adverb","Although; even though.","It was a good vacation, albeit a short one.","","Albeit is “all be it”: even though it is so."],["fortitude","FOR-ti-tood","noun","Courage and strength when things are hard.","It took fortitude to keep going after she lost her job.","From Latin fortis, “strong.”","Fortitude: fort, a strong fort inside you."],["jocund","JOK-uhnd","adjective","Cheerful and merry.","The jocund crowd sang along at the holiday party.","From Latin jucundus, “pleasant.”","Jocund sounds like jolly and jokey: merry and cheerful."],["clarion","KLAIR-ee-uhn","adjective","Loud and clear, like a call to act.","The empty fridge was a clarion call to go grocery shopping.","","Clarion sounds like clear: a loud, clear call."],["hubbub","HUB-ub","noun","A loud, busy noise of many people.","There was a hubbub at the store when the sale began.","","Hubbub sounds like noisy babble: hub-bub-bub-bub."],["disdain","dis-DAYN","noun","A feeling that something is not worth respect.","The cat looked at its new food with disdain.","","Disdain: you think it’s “dis” and not worth your time."],["gloaming","GLOH-ming","noun","The soft light of dusk.","We sat on the porch in the gloaming, watching the street lights come on.","From Old English glomung, “twilight.”","Gloaming sounds like “glow-ming”: the last glow before dark."],["antithesis","an-TITH-uh-sis","noun","The exact opposite of something.","Her calm voice was the antithesis of his loud shouting.","From Greek antithesis, “a setting against.”","Anti means against: the exact opposite."],["tenterhooks","TEN-ter-hooks","noun","A state of nervous waiting.","We were on tenterhooks waiting for the test results.","","Tenterhooks sound like hooks that keep you stretched tight with worry."],["juncture","JUNGK-cher","noun","A key point in time.","At this juncture, we have to decide which house to buy.","From Latin junctura, “a joining.”","Juncture sounds like junction: the point where roads meet."],["aviary","AY-vee-er-ee","noun","A large cage or building for keeping birds.","The zoo’s aviary was a huge cage full of bright parrots.","From Latin avis, “bird.”","Avis is Latin for bird: a house for birds."],["brawn","BRAWN","noun","Physical strength and muscle.","Moving the couch took brains and brawn.","","Brawn sounds like “brown muscle”: strength."],["muddle","MUD-l","noun","A state of confusion or mess.","My closet was in such a muddle that I couldn’t find my shoes.","","Muddle sounds like puddle: a muddy, mixed-up mess."],["proverbial","pruh-VUR-bee-uhl","adjective","Well known, as in a common saying.","My phone is the proverbial needle in a haystack in my purse.","","Proverbial has proverb in it: like a well-known saying."],["cyclical","SIK-li-kuhl","adjective","Happening in a repeating cycle.","Our grocery bill is cyclical, going up every holiday season.","From Greek kyklos, “circle.”","Cyclical has cycle in it: it goes round and round, like a bicycle wheel."],["outwit","out-WIT","verb","To beat someone by being clever.","The kids outwitted their parents and found the hidden candy.","","Out-wit: you have more wits than them."],["transient","TRAN-shuhnt","adjective","Lasting only a short time.","My bad mood was transient and gone after lunch.","From Latin transire, “to go across.”","Transient starts with “trans,” meaning across: passing through."],["curate","KYOOR-ayt","verb","To carefully pick and arrange things to share.","She curated a playlist for the road trip.","","Curate sounds like “cure it”: you care for and pick the best."],["concur","kuhn-KUR","verb","To agree.","I concur that pancakes are the best breakfast.","From Latin concurrere, “to run together.”","Concur sounds like “can occur”: yes, I agree it can be so."],["staccato","stuh-KAH-toh","adjective","Made of short, sharp, separate sounds.","The rain made staccato taps on the car roof.","From Italian staccato, “detached.”","Staccato sounds like “stop, stop, stop”: short, choppy sounds."],["abridge","uh-BRIJ","verb","To make a book or speech shorter while keeping its main ideas.","The kids read an abridged version of the long book, only fifty pages.","","Abridge sounds like “a bridge”: a shortcut over a long road."],["hoopla","HOOP-lah","noun","Excited fuss or noise.","There was a lot of hoopla around the new phone’s launch.","","Hoopla sounds like “whoop!”: a whole lot of excited fuss."],["nimble","NIM-buhl","adjective","Quick and light in moving or thinking.","The nimble waiter carried four plates without spilling a drop.","","Nimble rhymes with thimble, worn by quick, light sewing fingers."],["timbre","TAM-ber","noun","The special quality of a sound or voice.","I love the warm timbre of my grandmother’s voice.","From French timbre, “bell, tone.”","Timbre sounds like “tamber”: the special color of a sound."],["caricature","KAR-i-kuh-cher","noun","A funny drawing that stretches someone’s features.","At the fair, an artist drew a caricature of Dad with a giant nose.","From Italian caricare, “to load, exaggerate.”","Caricature sounds like “carry-a-feature”: it carries one feature too far."],["array","uh-RAY","noun","A large, orderly group or display of things.","The bakery had a huge array of cakes and cookies in the window.","","Array sounds like “a ray”: things lined up in neat rows."],["propensity","pruh-PEN-sih-tee","noun","A natural habit or tendency.","My brother has a propensity for losing his keys.","","Propensity sounds like “pro-pens”: you always lose pens."],["swashbuckling","SWOSH-buk-ling","adjective","Bold and adventurous, like a movie hero.","We watched a swashbuckling pirate movie on family night.","","Swashbuckling: picture a hero swashing a sword and buckling on a cape."],["ebullient","ih-BUL-yuhnt","adjective","Full of cheer and bubbly energy.","My ebullient aunt hugs everyone and laughs at every joke.","From Latin ebullire, “to boil over.”","Ebullient sounds like a bubbling pot, boiling over with joy."],["niche","NICH","noun","A spot or role that suits you perfectly.","She found her niche teaching art to kids.","From French niche.","A niche is a little nook where you fit just right."],["criterion","kry-TEER-ee-uhn","noun","A standard used to judge something.","Price was her main criterion for picking a new phone.","From Greek kriterion, “means of judging.”","Criterion sounds like critic: the test a critic judges by."],["capitulate","kuh-PICH-uh-layt","verb","To give in after holding out.","After my kids begged all week, I capitulated and bought the puppy.","","Capitulate: you cap the fight and let the other side win."],["muffle","MUF-uhl","verb","To make a sound softer.","The thick walls muffled the noise from the street.","","A muffler on a car makes it quieter."],["deflect","dih-FLEKT","verb","To turn something aside.","She deflected the compliment with a shy laugh.","From Latin flectere, “to bend.”","Deflect: like a shield that bounces a ball away."],["natty","NAT-ee","adjective","Neat and stylish in dress.","Grandpa looked natty in his bow tie at the wedding.","","Natty sounds like neat-y: neat and sharp."],["visceral","VIS-er-uhl","adjective","Felt deep in your gut, not just thought.","I had a visceral thrill on the roller coaster.","From Latin viscera, “inner organs.”","Visceral comes from “viscera,” your insides: a gut feeling."],["sprightly","SPRYT-lee","adjective","Lively and full of energy.","The sprightly old man walked five miles every day.","","Sprightly sounds like “sprite”: lively as a little sprite."],["auburn","AW-bern","adjective","Reddish-brown in color, usually of hair.","Her auburn hair looked like a shiny reddish-brown penny.","","Auburn sounds like “autumn burn”: the color of fall leaves."],["avow","uh-VOW","verb","To say something openly and boldly.","He avowed his love of pineapple on pizza in front of the whole family.","","Avow has “vow” inside: saying it openly like a promise."],["voracious","vaw-RAY-shuhs","adjective","Wanting a great amount of something.","She is a voracious reader who finishes a book a day.","From Latin vorare, “to swallow up.”","Voracious sounds like “ravenous”: hungry for more and more."],["askew","uh-SKYOO","adjective","Not straight; tilted to one side.","After the door slammed, the picture on the wall hung askew.","","Askew sounds like “a screw loose”: tilted and crooked."],["dwindle","DWIN-dl","verb","To slowly get smaller.","Our money dwindled as the end of the month came near.","","Dwindle sounds like “wind down”: getting smaller and smaller."],["exonerate","ig-ZON-uh-rayt","verb","To show that someone did nothing wrong.","The muddy paw prints exonerated my son, since the dog made the mess.","From Latin exonerare, “to unburden.”","Exonerate: take the onus, the blame, off someone."],["provenance","PROV-uh-nuhns","noun","Where something came from.","The store owner told us the provenance of the old clock.","From French provenir, “to come from.”","Provenance sounds like “proven from”: where it came from."],["jamboree","jam-buh-REE","noun","A big, lively party or gathering.","The neighborhood had a summer jamboree with games and food.","","Jamboree sounds like “jam and jelly party”: big and fun."],["anew","uh-NOO","adverb","Again, in a new or fresh way.","After the long weekend, I started the work week anew.","","Anew has “new” inside: again, fresh and new."],["dissemble","dih-SEM-buhl","verb","To hide your true feelings or reasons.","He dissembled, pretending he didn’t want the last slice.","","Dissemble sounds like “resemble”: you make yourself look like something else."],["bewitch","bih-WICH","verb","To charm or delight someone very much.","The baby’s giggle bewitched everyone in the room.","","Bewitch has “witch”: charmed as if by a spell."],["destiny","DES-tuh-nee","noun","What is meant to happen in the future.","She felt it was her destiny to become a nurse.","","Destiny has destination in it: where your life is headed."],["butte","BYOOT","noun","A steep, flat-topped hill standing alone.","On our road trip out West, the sun set behind a tall, flat-topped butte.","From French butte, “a mound.”","Butte sounds like “beaut”: a beautiful lone hill with a flat top."],["luster","LUS-ter","noun","A soft shine.","Grandma polished the silver until it had a soft luster.","","Luster sounds like “lustrous” and “shine”: a gentle glow."],["itinerant","eye-TIN-er-uhnt","adjective","Traveling from place to place.","The itinerant food truck parks in a new spot every day.","From Latin iter, “journey.”","Itinerant sounds like itinerary: always on the trip."],["kumquat","KUM-kwot","noun","A tiny orange fruit you eat with the skin on.","My kids tried a kumquat and were surprised you eat the skin.","From Cantonese kam kwat, “golden orange.”","Kumquat sounds like “come quick”: pop the tiny fruit in whole."],["nebula","NEB-yuh-luh","noun","A huge cloud of gas and dust in space.","The space picture showed a glowing nebula, a cloud of gas far away.","From Latin nebula, “mist.”","Nebula sounds like “nebby blur”: a blurry cloud in space."],["agile","AJ-uhl","adjective","Able to move quickly and easily.","The agile little boy ran around the chairs without bumping one.","From Latin agilis, “nimble.”","Agile sounds like “a gel”: bendy and quick."],["reclusive","ri-KLOO-siv","adjective","Liking to live alone, away from other people.","Our reclusive neighbor rarely opens his door or waves.","","Reclusive sounds like “recluse” or “closed”: someone who keeps the door closed."],["diminutive","dih-MIN-yuh-tiv","adjective","Very small.","A diminutive puppy sat in the big dog bed.","","Diminutive sounds like diminish: made small."],["oblong","OB-long","adjective","Longer than it is wide.","We bought an oblong table so the whole family fits at dinner.","","Oblong: it is long, longer than it is wide."],["granular","GRAN-yuh-ler","adjective","Made of small grains, or very detailed.","My boss wants a granular budget that lists every cup of coffee.","From Latin granum, “grain.”","Granular sounds like grain: made of tiny bits."],["opalescent","oh-puh-LES-uhnt","adjective","Shining with soft, shifting colors like an opal.","The inside of the shell was opalescent pink and blue.","","Opalescent has opal in it, a gem that shines many colors."],["disgruntled","dis-GRUHN-tld","adjective","Annoyed and unhappy.","The disgruntled customer grumbled about the cold fries.","","Disgruntled sounds like grunt: grunting with annoyance."],["splendor","SPLEN-der","noun","Great and shining beauty.","We gazed at the splendor of the city lights from the plane.","From Latin splendere, “to shine.”","Splendor sounds like “splendid”: big, shining beauty."],["obelisk","OB-uh-lisk","noun","A tall, thin stone pillar with a pointed top.","A tall obelisk stands in the middle of the town square.","From Greek obeliskos, “small spit.”","Obelisk sounds like “oh, bell stick”: a tall, pointy stick."],["omnivore","OM-nuh-vor","noun","An animal that eats both plants and meat.","Bears are omnivores that eat berries and fish.","From Latin omnis, “all,” and vorare, “to eat.”","Omni means all, and vore means eat: eats it all."],["pastoral","PAS-ter-uhl","adjective","Having to do with peaceful country life.","The calendar photo showed a pastoral scene with cows and green hills.","From Latin pastor, “shepherd.”","Pastoral sounds like pasture, where cows graze."],["limerick","LIM-er-ik","noun","A funny five-line poem.","My son wrote a funny limerick about our lazy cat.","","Limerick sounds like “lime rick”: a silly five-line poem."],["vaunted","VAWN-tid","adjective","Praised or boasted about a lot.","We finally tried the vaunted lemon pie, and it was great.","","Vaunted sounds like “flaunted”: bragged about a lot."],["erstwhile","ERST-wyl","adjective","Former; from an earlier time.","My erstwhile roommate is now my next-door neighbor.","","Erstwhile: “first while,” meaning from an earlier time."],["debonair","deb-uh-NAIR","adjective","Charming, confident and well-dressed.","The debonair waiter greeted every table with a smile.","From French de bon air, “of good manner.”","Debonair comes from French “de bon air,” of good manner."],["mull","MUHL","verb","To think about something slowly and carefully.","She mulled over the job offer for a whole week.","","Like warm cider you heat slowly, you think slowly."],["idiom","ID-ee-uhm","noun","A phrase whose meaning is not clear from its words alone.","“It’s raining cats and dogs” is a common idiom.","From Greek idioma, “a special phrasing.”","Idiom shares idio, one’s own, with idiosyncrasy: a phrase with its own meaning."],["rapport","ra-POR","noun","A warm, easy connection between people.","The new teacher quickly built a rapport with her students.","From French rapporter, “to bring back.”","Rapport sounds like “rap for”: you click and talk easily."],["hullabaloo","hul-uh-buh-LOO","noun","A noisy fuss.","There was a hullabaloo when the dog ran through the wedding.","","Hullabaloo sounds just like a loud, silly fuss: hulla-ba-LOO."],["contingency","kuhn-TIN-juhn-see","noun","Something that might happen and needs a backup plan.","We keep a spare tire in the trunk as a contingency for a flat.","","Contingency: a backup plan for things that are “contingent,” or might happen."],["shimmer","SHIM-er","verb","To shine with a soft, wavering light.","The swimming pool shimmered in the evening sun.","","Shimmer sounds like “glimmer”: a soft, wavy shine."],["arbitrary","AR-bi-trer-ee","adjective","Chosen by chance or whim, not for a good reason.","The bedtime rule felt arbitrary to the kids; why 8:07 and not 8:00?","","Arbitrary sounds like “a bit random”: picked for no good reason."],["celerity","suh-LER-i-tee","noun","Swiftness; speed.","The waiter cleared our table with great celerity.","From Latin celer, “swift.”","Celerity is related to accelerate: speeding up."],["ostensible","o-STEN-suh-buhl","adjective","Seeming true on the surface, but maybe not really.","The ostensible reason for the visit was to borrow sugar.","From Latin ostendere, “to show.”","Ostensible sounds like “it’s ’tensibly”: it seems that way."],["juxtapose","JUK-stuh-pohz","verb","To place things side by side to compare them.","The store juxtaposes healthy snacks with candy at the register.","From Latin juxta, “next to.”","Juxtapose: “just a pose” side by side to compare."],["brainchild","BRAYN-chyld","noun","An idea or plan that someone thought up.","The neighborhood book swap was my mom’s brainchild.","","Brainchild: a child born in your brain, an idea."],["genteel","jen-TEEL","adjective","Polite and refined, sometimes a bit fussy.","Grandma’s genteel tea party had lace napkins and tiny sandwiches.","","Genteel sounds like “gentle”: polite, proper and a bit fussy."],["cerulean","suh-ROO-lee-uhn","adjective","Deep sky blue.","She painted the baby’s room a soft cerulean, like a clear summer sky.","From Latin caeruleus, “sky blue.”","Cerulean comes from Latin caelum, sky: the color of the sky."],["bewilder","bih-WIL-der","verb","To confuse someone completely.","The giant parking garage bewildered us; we forgot where we parked.","","Bewilder has “wild”: lost like in the wild woods."],["byword","BY-wurd","noun","A word or name that stands for a certain quality.","In our town, that bakery is a byword for good bread.","","Byword: the word everyone says by habit for something."],["conciliatory","kuhn-SIL-ee-uh-tor-ee","adjective","Meant to make peace or calm someone.","She made a conciliatory offer to do the dishes after the argument.","","Conciliatory sounds like “council”: calm talk to make peace."],["evanescent","ev-uh-NES-uhnt","adjective","Fading away quickly, like mist.","The smell of fresh bread was evanescent; it faded in a minute.","From Latin evanescere, “to vanish.”","Evanescent has “vanish” hiding inside it: here, then gone."],["benevolent","buh-NEV-uh-luhnt","adjective","Kind and wanting to do good.","The benevolent baker gave leftover bread to the shelter every night.","From Latin bene, “well,” and volens, “wishing.”","Bene means good, like a benefit: wishing others well."],["smorgasbord","SMOR-guhs-bord","noun","A big spread of many foods, or a wide mix of things.","The hotel breakfast was a smorgasbord of eggs, waffles and fruit.","From Swedish smörgåsbord, “sandwich table.”","Smorgasbord sounds like “more-gas-board”: a table with way more food."],["zeitgeist","TSYT-gyst","noun","The general mood and ideas of a time.","The song captured the hopeful zeitgeist of that summer.","From German Zeitgeist, “spirit of the time.”","Zeitgeist is German for “time spirit”: the mood of the times."],["ornery","OR-nuh-ree","adjective","Grumpy and hard to deal with.","My grandpa gets ornery when he’s hungry.","","Ornery started as a country way of saying ordinary."],["iconoclast","eye-KON-uh-klast","noun","A person who challenges popular ideas.","My aunt is an iconoclast who serves tacos for Thanksgiving.","From Greek eikon, “image,” and klastes, “breaker.”","Iconoclast: Greek for image-breaker, someone who smashes old ideas."],["barnstorm","BARN-storm","verb","To travel from town to town giving shows or speeches.","The candidate barnstormed across the state, giving speeches in every town.","","Barnstorm: storming from barn to barn, town to town."],["gambit","GAM-bit","noun","A clever move made to gain an advantage.","Offering to wash the car was my son’s gambit to borrow it.","From Italian gambetto, “a tripping up.”","Gambit sounds like gamble: a clever move to win something."],["circumvent","sur-kuhm-VENT","verb","To find a way around a problem.","We circumvented the long line by ordering the food online.","From Latin circumvenire, “to come around.”","Circum means around, like circle. Vent means come. Come around it."],["mottled","MOT-ld","adjective","Marked with spots or patches of color.","The old banana was mottled with brown spots.","","Mottled sounds like “mottle-dotted”: covered in spots."],["brunt","BRUHNT","noun","The main force or worst part of something.","Our front porch took the brunt of the storm.","","Brunt sounds like “blunt”: the hardest hit."],["verisimilitude","ver-uh-si-MIL-i-tood","noun","The look or feel of being real or true.","The toy kitchen had such verisimilitude that my son tried to eat the plastic bread.","From Latin verisimilis, “like the truth.”","Veri means true, and similis means like: looking true."],["nifty","NIF-tee","adjective","Clever and handy.","He showed me a nifty way to fold a fitted sheet.","","Nifty rhymes with thrifty: clever and handy."],["revel","REV-uhl","verb","To enjoy something with great delight.","The children reveled in the first snow day of winter.","","Revel sounds like “level up”: fun at the highest level."],["magpie","MAG-py","noun","A person who collects all sorts of small things.","My aunt is a magpie who saves every pretty button and ribbon she finds.","","A magpie bird grabs shiny things, just like a collector does."],["minutiae","mih-NOO-shee-ee","noun","Tiny, small details.","Dad loves the minutiae of planning a road trip, down to every snack stop.","From Latin minutia, “smallness.”","Minute (my-NOOT) means tiny, so minutiae are tiny details."],["welkin","WEL-kin","noun","The sky, or the heavens above.","The kids’ happy shouts made the welkin ring.","From Old English wolcen, “cloud.”","Welkin sounds like “well, look up”: the sky above."],["doleful","DOHL-fuhl","adjective","Sad and gloomy.","The puppy gave us a doleful look when we left for work.","","Doleful sounds like “dull-full”: full of sad feelings."],["mitigate","MIT-uh-gayt","verb","To make something less bad.","Using sunscreen can mitigate the harm from a long day at the beach.","From Latin mitigare, “to soften.”","Mitigate sounds like “mitten gate”: a mitten softens the cold."],["discourse","DIS-kors","noun","Serious talk or discussion.","The family dinner turned into lively discourse about the news.","","Discourse sounds like discuss: serious talk."],["ambience","AM-bee-uhns","noun","The feeling or mood of a place.","The soft music and candles gave the restaurant a cozy ambience.","","Ambience sounds like “ambient”: the feeling all around you."],["bravado","bruh-VAH-doh","noun","A show of boldness to hide fear.","With a little bravado, he jumped into the cold pool first.","From Spanish bravada, “a boast.”","Bravado has “brave”: acting brave on the outside."],["shenanigans","shuh-NAN-i-guhnz","noun","Silly or mischievous tricks.","The cousins got up to shenanigans at the sleepover.","","Shenanigans sounds like “she-and-again”: silly trick after silly trick."],["devoid","dih-VOID","adjective","Completely without something.","The soup was devoid of salt.","","Devoid has void in it: totally empty."],["enthrall","en-THRAWL","verb","To hold someone's full attention with delight.","The magician enthralled the kids at the birthday party.","","Enthrall: the show holds you in its thrall, you can’t look away."],["dicey","DY-see","adjective","Risky and uncertain.","Driving home in the snowstorm looked dicey.","","Dicey has dice in it: a roll of the dice, risky."],["scuttlebutt","SKUT-l-but","noun","Rumor or gossip.","The scuttlebutt at work was that we’d get a long weekend.","From a sailors’ word for a water cask where crews swapped gossip.","Scuttlebutt was a sailors’ water barrel, where they gossiped like at a water cooler."],["twaddle","TWOD-l","noun","Silly or foolish talk.","Grandpa said the rumor was pure twaddle.","","Twaddle sounds like “twitter-babble”: silly, empty chatter."],["glower","GLOU-er","verb","To stare angrily.","Dad glowered at the dog that chewed his new shoes.","","Glower sounds like “glare-lower”: a low, angry stare."],["forage","FOR-ij","verb","To search for food or supplies.","The kids foraged in the pantry for something to snack on.","","Forage sounds like “for a gem”: searching for food you can find."],["hinterland","HIN-ter-land","noun","The quiet land far from towns or coasts.","We drove far into the hinterland, where there were more cows than cars.","From German Hinterland, “land behind.”","Hinterland: German for “land behind,” far from the city."],["congenial","kuhn-JEEN-yuhl","adjective","Friendly and pleasant to be around.","Our congenial neighbors always wave and offer us lemonade.","","Congenial sounds like “genial”: warm and friendly."],["threadbare","THRED-bair","adjective","Worn so thin you can see the threads.","He still loves his threadbare old teddy bear.","","Threadbare: worn down until the threads are bare."],["outskirts","OUT-skurts","noun","The outer edges of a town or city.","They live on the outskirts of town, near the farms.","","Out-skirts: like the hem at the outside edge of a skirt."],["spate","SPAYT","noun","A sudden rush of many things at once.","We got a spate of birthday cards this week.","","Spate sounds like “spray”: a sudden spray of things."],["cusp","KUHSP","noun","A point where one thing is about to change into another.","My daughter is on the cusp of learning to ride her bike without help.","From Latin cuspis, “point.”","Cusp: the tip of the edge, just before the change."],["bricolage","bree-koh-LAHZH","noun","Something made from whatever odds and ends are at hand.","The kids’ fort was a bricolage of boxes, blankets and chairs.","From French bricoler, “to tinker.”","Bricolage sounds like “bric-a-brac collage”: made from odds and ends."],["coruscate","KOR-uh-skayt","verb","To sparkle and flash.","Sunlight coruscated on the pool water.","From Latin coruscare, “to glitter.”","Coruscate sounds like “chorus-skate”: lights skating and sparkling."],["simpatico","sim-PAH-ti-koh","adjective","Easy to get along with; on the same wavelength.","The two new neighbors were instantly simpatico.","","Simpatico sounds like “sympathy”: you feel each other’s vibe right away."],["numismatist","noo-MIZ-muh-tist","noun","A person who studies or collects coins.","My uncle is a numismatist with coins from fifty countries.","From Greek nomisma, “coin.”","Numis sounds like “new miss”: a new coin to collect."],["bailiwick","BAY-luh-wik","noun","A person's area of skill or interest.","Fixing computers is my brother’s bailiwick.","","Bailiwick has “wick”: his corner, where his candle burns."],["opaque","oh-PAYK","adjective","Not see-through, or hard to understand.","We hung opaque curtains so the baby could nap in the dark.","From Latin opacus, “shaded.”","Opaque sounds like “oh, pay-k”: no peeking through."],["dispassionate","dis-PASH-uh-nit","adjective","Calm and fair, not swayed by feelings.","A dispassionate referee made fair calls for both teams.","","Dispassionate: without passion, calm and fair."],["cobble","KOB-uhl","verb","To put something together quickly from what you have.","We cobbled together dinner from leftovers in the fridge.","","A cobbler fixes shoes with whatever is on hand."],["decorum","dih-KOR-uhm","noun","Proper and polite behavior.","The kids showed good decorum at the fancy wedding dinner.","From Latin decorus, “fitting.”","Decorum sounds like decor: everything neat and proper."],["debut","day-BYOO","noun","A first public appearance.","Her piano debut was at the school concert.","From French début, “a beginning.”","Debut sounds like “day-boo”: the day you first show yourself."],["obfuscate","OB-fuh-skayt","verb","To make something confusing on purpose.","Long words can obfuscate a simple idea.","From Latin obfuscare, “to darken.”","Obfuscate sounds like “fog-a-skate”: you fog up the message."],["mellifluous","muh-LIF-loo-uhs","adjective","Sweet and smooth to hear.","The nurse had a mellifluous voice that calmed the crying baby.","From Latin mel, “honey,” and fluere, “to flow.”","Mel means honey: a voice that flows like honey."],["zeal","ZEEL","noun","Great energy and eagerness for something.","She cleaned the garage with surprising zeal.","From Greek zēlos, “eagerness.”","Zeal sounds like “zip”: full of zip and eagerness."],["dither","DITH-er","verb","To be unable to make up your mind.","She dithered over which puppy to adopt.","","Dither sounds like “this or that?”: back and forth."],["imbue","im-BYOO","verb","To fill with a feeling or quality.","Grandma imbued her home with warmth and love.","","Imbue sounds like “in-dew”: soaking in, like dew on grass."],["kudos","KOO-dohs","noun","Praise for something done well.","Kudos to Mom for cooking dinner for twelve people.","From Greek kydos, “glory.”","Kudos sounds like “cool dudes”: praise for a job well done."],["posterity","po-STAIR-ih-tee","noun","All the people who will live in the future.","We saved Grandma’s recipes for posterity.","From Latin posterus, “coming after.”","Post means after: for those who come after us."],["nefarious","nih-FAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Wicked or up to no good.","The cat had a nefarious plan to steal the fish off the counter.","From Latin nefas, “wrong.”","Nefarious sounds like “nasty and furious”: up to no good."],["dexterity","dek-STER-i-tee","noun","Skill with the hands or the mind.","Typing fast on a phone takes real dexterity.","From Latin dexter, “right-handed, skillful.”","Dexter means right hand in Latin: handy and skilled."],["betwixt","bih-TWIKST","adverb","An old word for between.","The cat squeezed betwixt the two sleeping dogs on the couch.","","Betwixt sounds like “between” with a twist."],["quorum","KWOR-uhm","noun","The smallest number of members needed for a meeting to make decisions.","The parent group had a quorum, so we voted on the bake sale date.","From Latin quorum, “of whom.”","Quorum sounds like “four ’em”: you need enough of ’em to vote."],["monochrome","MON-uh-krohm","adjective","Using just one color or shades of one color.","She wore a monochrome outfit: blue shirt, blue pants, blue shoes.","From Greek monos, “one,” and khroma, “color.”","Mono means one, and chrome means color."],["trite","TRYT","adjective","Used so often that it is dull.","The card had a trite message, but it was sweet.","From Latin tritus, “worn down.”","Trite sounds like “tired”: a tired, worn-out phrase."],["pare","PAIR","verb","To trim or cut away the outer part.","She pared the apple in one long curl of peel.","From Latin parare, “to prepare.”","Pare sounds like pear: you pare the peel off a pear."],["avant-garde","ah-vahnt-GARD","adjective","New, bold and ahead of its time, especially in art.","The restaurant served avant-garde food, like ice cream that tasted like bacon.","From French avant-garde, “front guard.”","Avant means before in French: ahead of everyone else."],["dusky","DUHS-kee","adjective","Dim, like the light at dusk.","We walked home under a dusky pink sky.","","Dusky has dusk in it: the dim light after sunset."],["bombast","BOM-bast","noun","Fancy, puffed-up talk with little meaning.","The speech was full of bombast but short on real facts.","","Bombast sounds like “bomb blast”: big noise, little meaning."],["plaudit","PLAW-dit","noun","Praise or applause.","The young cook earned plaudits for her soup.","From Latin plaudite, “applaud!”","Plaudit sounds like applaud."],["offing","OF-ing","noun","The near future; if something is in the offing, it is coming soon.","With clouds rolling in, rain is in the offing.","","In the offing: just off in the distance, coming soon."],["composure","kuhm-POH-zher","noun","A calm and steady state of mind.","She kept her composure when the power went out during dinner.","","Composure: you stay composed, calm and put together."],["amity","AM-i-tee","noun","Friendship and good will between people or groups.","The two families on our street have lived in amity for years.","","Amity, like “ami,” French for friend: friendship."],["empathy","EM-puh-thee","noun","The ability to feel and understand what someone else feels.","She showed empathy by sitting with her friend who lost his dog.","From Greek em-, “in,” and pathos, “feeling.”","Empathy: you step into someone’s shoes and feel their path."],["bashful","BASH-fuhl","adjective","Shy and easily embarrassed.","The bashful toddler hid behind his mom’s legs.","","Bashful: picture a shy kid blushing, full of shyness."],["hearken","HAR-kuhn","verb","To listen, or to call back to an earlier time.","That old song hearkens back to my high school days.","","Hearken has “hear” in it: listen up."],["amalgam","uh-MAL-guhm","noun","A mix of different things blended together.","Our family dinner was an amalgam of Mom’s and Dad’s favorite dishes.","","Amalgam sounds like “a mall gum”: everything mixed up together."],["satiate","SAY-shee-ayt","verb","To fill up all the way, so you want no more.","The big holiday meal satiated everyone at the table.","From Latin satis, “enough.”","Satiate sounds like “sated” or “sat”: you ate so much you just sat."],["quintessential","kwin-tuh-SEN-shuhl","adjective","Being the perfect example of something.","Hot cocoa on a snowy day is the quintessential winter treat.","From Latin quinta essentia, “fifth essence.”","Quintessential has “essential” inside: the most essential example of a thing."],["kitsch","KICH","noun","Showy, silly things that are fun because they are tacky.","Grandma’s flamingo lamp is pure kitsch, and we love it.","From German Kitsch.","Kitsch sounds like kitchen: a silly kitchen gadget that is tacky but fun."],["arbor","AR-ber","noun","A shady garden shelter with plants growing over it.","Grapevines grew over the arbor in Grandpa’s backyard, making shade.","","Arbor is Latin for tree: a leafy shelter."],["belated","bih-LAY-tid","adjective","Coming late.","I sent my friend a belated birthday card a week late.","","Belated has “late” inside."],["anthology","an-THOL-uh-jee","noun","A collection of poems, stories or songs in one book.","We bought an anthology of bedtime stories, fifty tales in one book.","From Greek anthologia, “a gathering of flowers.”","Anthology sounds like “ant colony”: many pieces gathered in one place."],["maxim","MAK-sim","noun","A short saying that gives a rule for living.","Dad’s favorite maxim is “measure twice, cut once.”","From Latin maxima, “greatest.”","A maxim is a saying of the maximum, greatest kind."],["permeate","PUR-mee-ayt","verb","To spread through every part.","The smell of fresh bread permeated the whole house.","From Latin permeare, “to pass through.”","Permeate sounds like “perm-meat”: the smell gets into everything."],["sublime","suh-BLYM","adjective","So beautiful or great it fills you with wonder.","The sunset over the ocean was sublime.","From Latin sublimis, “lofty, raised up.”","Sublime sounds like “sub-line”: so great it’s above the line."],["abstruse","ab-STROOS","adjective","Hard to understand.","The instructions for the new printer were so abstruse that we gave up.","From Latin abstrusus, “hidden.”","Abstruse sounds like “obtuse and confused”: too hard to follow."],["attest","uh-TEST","verb","To show or prove that something is true.","Her full trophy shelf attests to years of hard practice.","From Latin testis, “a witness.”","Attest has “test” inside: proof that passed the test."],["sylvan","SIL-vuhn","adjective","Having to do with woods and forests.","We picnicked in a quiet, sylvan corner of the park under tall trees.","From Latin silva, “forest.”","Sylvan comes from Latin silva, which means woods."],["dire","DYR","adjective","Very serious or urgent.","We were in dire need of coffee after the baby kept us up.","From Latin dirus, “fearful.”","Dire sounds like dire straits: real trouble."],["aver","uh-VUR","verb","To say firmly that something is true.","She avers that her chili is the best in town.","","Aver sounds like “a very” sure: saying it very firmly."],["nettle","NET-l","verb","To annoy or bother someone.","Loud chewing at the dinner table nettles my brother.","","A nettle is a plant that stings and bugs you."],["foliage","FOH-lee-ij","noun","The leaves of plants or trees.","In fall, we drive out to see the colorful foliage on the trees.","From Latin folium, “leaf.”","Foliage is like a folio: a big book made of leaves."],["apportion","uh-POR-shuhn","verb","To divide and share out.","Dad apportioned the chores fairly among the three kids.","","Apportion has “portion” inside: give out portions."],["lark","LARK","noun","Something done just for fun.","We drove to the next town for ice cream, just for a lark.","","Lark sounds like “a park day”: something done just for fun."],["broach","BROHCH","verb","To bring up a subject for talking about.","She waited until dessert to broach the idea of getting a puppy.","","Broach sounds like “approach”: approaching a topic."],["pastiche","pa-STEESH","noun","A work that copies or mixes styles from others.","The play was a pastiche of old fairy tales mixed together.","From Italian pasticcio, “a pie, a mix.”","Pastiche sounds like pasta dish: a mix of many things in one bowl."],["jetsam","JET-suhm","noun","Things thrown off a ship that wash ashore.","Jetsam from the cruise ship washed up on the beach.","","Jetsam was jettisoned, thrown off the ship on purpose."],["enclave","EN-klayv","noun","A small area that is different from the area around it.","The little bakery is a quiet enclave on a busy street.","From French enclaver, “to enclose.”","Enclave has “enclose”: a small closed-in space unlike its neighbors."],["divulge","dih-VUHLJ","verb","To reveal a secret.","She refused to divulge the secret recipe.","","Divulge sounds like “bulge”: the secret bulges out."],["klutz","KLUTS","noun","A clumsy person.","I’m such a klutz; I tripped over my own shoelace.","From Yiddish klots, “block of wood.”","Klutz sounds like “clunks”: someone who clunks into things."],["euphony","YOO-fuh-nee","noun","A pleasing, sweet sound, especially of words.","I love the euphony of rain tapping on the roof at night.","From Greek euphonia, “good sound.”","Eu means good, phone means sound: euphony is a good sound."],["contiguous","kuhn-TIG-yoo-uhs","adjective","Touching or sharing a border.","Our two backyards are contiguous, so the kids play together.","","Contiguous sounds like “contact”: touching side by side."],["denounce","dih-NOUNS","verb","To publicly say something is wrong.","The parents denounced the plan to close the playground.","","Denounce sounds like announce: you announce it’s wrong."],["colloquial","kuh-LOH-kwee-uhl","adjective","Used in everyday talk, not formal writing.","“Gonna” is a colloquial way to say “going to.”","From Latin colloquium, “conversation.”","Colloquial sounds like “call a pal”: how you talk to a friend."],["obtuse","uhb-TOOS","adjective","Slow to understand, or an angle wider than a right angle.","I was a bit obtuse and missed the joke at first.","From Latin obtusus, “blunt.”","Obtuse means blunt: like a dull pencil, slow to get the point."],["dismay","dis-MAY","noun","Sudden worry or disappointment.","To our dismay, the store was closed.","","Dismay: the sinking feeling when the store sign says “Closed.”"],["reverie","REV-uh-ree","noun","A pleasant daydream.","The ringing phone broke her happy reverie.","From French rêverie, “daydreaming.”","Reverie sounds like “reverie… zzz”: drifting off into a daydream."],["svelte","SVELT","adjective","Slim and graceful.","The svelte cat slipped easily through the gap in the fence.","From Italian svelto, “slender, quick.”","Svelte sounds like “slim belt”: slim and sleek."],["quibble","KWIB-uhl","verb","To argue about a tiny, unimportant detail.","Let’s not quibble over who gets the bigger slice of pizza.","","Quibble sounds like “nibble”: picking at tiny bits of an argument."],["beset","bih-SET","verb","To trouble from all sides.","Our road trip was beset by flat tires and rain.","","Beset has “set”: troubles set on you from every side."],["dumbfound","duhm-FOUND","verb","To shock and amaze someone so they can’t speak.","The magic trick dumbfounded the whole class.","","Dumbfound: so amazed you’re found dumb, with no words."],["dystopia","dis-TOH-pee-uh","noun","An imagined world where life is very bad.","The movie is set in a dystopia where nobody is allowed to laugh.","","Dys means bad. Utopia is the perfect place. Dystopia is the bad one."],["nemesis","NEM-uh-sis","noun","A rival or enemy you cannot seem to beat.","The Sunday crossword puzzle is my nemesis.","","Nemesis sounds like “enemy-sis”: your enemy."],["meager","MEE-ger","adjective","Small and not enough.","Our garden gave a meager crop of three tomatoes.","","Meager sounds like “meek-er”: smaller and weaker than you hoped."],["compunction","kuhm-PUNGK-shuhn","noun","A small feeling of guilt about doing something.","He had no compunction about eating the last slice of cake.","From Latin compungere, “to prick sharply.”","Compunction: a little puncture, or prick, of guilt."],["prattle","PRAT-l","verb","To talk on and on about silly things.","The toddler prattled happily in the back seat.","","Prattle sounds like rattle: a baby’s noisy chatter."],["galvanize","GAL-vuh-nyz","verb","To shock or excite someone into action.","The flooded basement galvanized us to finally buy a pump.","","Galvanize: like a jolt of electricity that gets you moving."],["dowdy","DOU-dee","adjective","Plain and out of style.","She turned a dowdy old coat into something stylish.","","Dowdy sounds like “dusty”: old-fashioned and plain."],["jabber","JAB-er","verb","To talk fast in a way that is hard to follow.","The toddlers jabbered away in the back seat.","","Jabber sounds like jibber-jabber: fast, silly talk."],["insipid","in-SIP-id","adjective","Dull and without flavor.","The hospital soup was insipid, with no salt at all.","From Latin insipidus, “tasteless.”","Insipid: in means not, plus Latin for tasty: no taste at all."],["sophistry","SOF-uh-stree","noun","Clever-sounding arguments that are really false.","Don’t be fooled by his sophistry; ask for the facts.","","Sophistry sounds like “so-fishy”: clever talk that smells fishy."],["hiatus","hy-AY-tuhs","noun","A break or pause.","The TV show took a hiatus for the summer.","From Latin hiatus, “an opening.”","Hiatus sounds like “hi, I’m out”: a break or pause."],["bamboozle","bam-BOO-zuhl","verb","To trick or fool someone.","My little sister bamboozled me into doing her chores.","","Bamboozle sounds like “bam, fooled you”: a sneaky trick."],["nonplussed","non-PLUST","adjective","So surprised you don’t know what to say.","He was nonplussed when the parrot said his name.","From Latin non plus, “no more.”","Non plus means “no more”: you have no more words."],["spry","SPRY","adjective","Lively and active, especially for an older person.","At ninety, Grandma is still spry enough to dance.","","Spry sounds like “spring”: springy and full of pep."],["zigzag","ZIG-zag","verb","To move in a line with sharp turns from side to side.","The rabbit zigzagged across the backyard.","","Zigzag: draw a Z, then another, back and forth."],["augur","AW-ger","verb","To be a sign of what will happen.","A strong first week of sales augurs well for the new shop.","From Latin augur, a Roman reader of signs.","Augur sounds like “a guru”: someone who sees the future."],["sedulous","SEJ-uh-luhs","adjective","Hard-working and careful over a long time.","The sedulous student studied a little every single night.","From Latin sedulus, “busy, careful.”","Sedulous sounds like “seed-ulous”: planting seeds day after day."],["dispel","dih-SPEL","verb","To make a worry or idea go away.","Her smile dispelled all my nerves.","From Latin dispellere, “to drive apart.”","Dispel: a spell that makes worries disappear."],["purloin","per-LOYN","verb","To take something that isn’t yours, often sneakily.","The dog purloined a sock from the laundry basket.","","Purloin sounds like “purr, loin”: a cat that sneaks your steak."],["mosaic","moh-ZAY-ik","noun","A picture made of many small pieces.","The children made a mosaic from colored bottle caps.","","Mosaic sounds like “most say it’s”: lots of tiny pieces."],["alfresco","al-FRES-koh","adverb","Outdoors, especially when eating.","On warm nights we eat alfresco on the back porch.","From Italian al fresco, “in the fresh air.”","Alfresco has “fresco” like fresh: eating in the fresh air."],["ubiquitous","yoo-BIK-wi-tuhs","adjective","Found everywhere.","Phones are ubiquitous; everyone at the bus stop had one.","From Latin ubique, “everywhere.”","Ubiquitous sounds like “you-big-with-us”: big and with us everywhere."],["whippersnapper","WIP-er-snap-er","noun","A young person who acts too bold or sure of themselves.","“Listen here, you little whippersnapper,” Grandpa teased.","","Whippersnapper: a young kid who snaps back like a whip."],["zany","ZAY-nee","adjective","Wildly silly and funny.","Our zany uncle wore a chicken hat to the picnic.","From Italian zanni, a clown in old comic plays.","Zany sounds like “crazy”: silly and wild."],["outlandish","out-LAN-dish","adjective","Very strange or unusual.","He wore an outlandish hat with feathers and bells.","","Out-land-ish: from way out in some strange land."],["succinct","suhk-SINGKT","adjective","Short and clear, with no extra words.","Her succinct note said just, “Back at five. Love you.”","From Latin succinctus, “tucked up.”","Succinct sounds like “sucked in”: words sucked in to be short."],["lollygag","LOL-ee-gag","verb","To waste time doing nothing much.","Stop lollygagging and put your shoes on, we’re late!","","Lollygag: slowly licking a lollipop while the day slips by."],["resilient","ri-ZIL-yuhnt","adjective","Able to bounce back after something hard.","Kids are resilient and often get used to a new school fast.","From Latin resilire, “to leap back.”","Resilient sounds like “re-silly-ent”: a rubber ball that bounces back."],["bask","BASK","verb","To lie in and enjoy warmth or attention.","Our cat loves to bask in the sunny spot by the window.","","Bask sounds like “basking in a bath” of sunlight."],["gibberish","JIB-er-ish","noun","Speech or writing that makes no sense.","My baby speaks gibberish but seems to know what she means.","","Gibberish sounds like jibber-jabber: words that make no sense."],["plummet","PLUM-it","verb","To fall fast and far.","The temperature plummeted after sunset.","","Plummet sounds like plum: a plum drops fast from a tree."],["badger","BAJ-er","verb","To keep asking someone again and again.","The kids badgered Dad to take them to the pool all day.","","A badger is a stubborn animal that digs and digs at you."],["gist","JIST","noun","The main point of something.","I didn’t read the whole email, but I got the gist.","","Gist sounds like “just”: just the main point."],["cranny","KRAN-ee","noun","A small, narrow opening or corner.","We searched every nook and cranny of the car for the lost earring.","","Cranny sounds like “cramped”: a tiny tight spot."],["vex","VEKS","verb","To annoy or puzzle someone.","The missing sock vexed me all morning.","From Latin vexare, “to shake, disturb.”","Vex sounds like “vexed, perplexed”: annoyed and puzzled."],["rapacious","ruh-PAY-shuhs","adjective","Greedy and grabbing for more and more.","The rapacious seagulls snatched every last fry from our plates.","From Latin rapax, “grasping.”","Rapacious sounds like “wrap it all”: grabbing and wrapping up everything."],["ember","EM-ber","noun","A small glowing piece of wood or coal in a dying fire.","We roasted marshmallows over the last glowing embers of the campfire.","","Ember sounds like “remember”: the fire’s last glowing memory."],["yeoman","YOH-muhn","adjective","Steady, hard-working and useful.","She did yeoman work organizing the school fair.","","Yeoman sounds like “yes-man”: always saying yes to hard work."],["liaison","lee-AY-zon","noun","A person who links two groups.","The school counselor acts as a liaison between parents and teachers.","From French liaison, “a binding.”","Liaison sounds like “link-zone”: the go-between who links groups."],["nuance","NOO-ahns","noun","A small, fine difference in meaning or feeling.","A good actor can show every nuance of a feeling with just her face.","From French nuance, “shade of color.”","Nuance sounds like “new shade”: a tiny change."],["tapestry","TAP-uh-stree","noun","A heavy cloth with pictures woven in, or a rich mix of things.","Our street is a tapestry of families from all over the world.","","Tapestry sounds like “tape-stitch”: many threads stitched into one picture."],["beckon","BEK-uhn","verb","To signal someone to come closer.","Grandma beckoned us into the warm kitchen with a wave.","","Beckon: a hand saying “come here.”"],["thrum","THRUM","verb","To make a steady humming or strumming sound.","Rain thrummed on the car roof all night.","","Thrum sounds like “hum” and “strum”: a steady humming sound."],["veracity","vuh-RAS-i-tee","noun","Being true and honest.","No one doubted the veracity of Grandma’s story.","From Latin verax, “truthful.”","Veracity sounds like “very-true-city”: truthfulness."],["lore","LOR","noun","Stories and knowledge passed down over time.","Our family lore says Grandpa once caught a fish as big as a dog.","","Lore sounds like “lore of yore”: old stories passed down."],["ken","KEN","noun","The range of what someone knows.","How the TV remote works is beyond my grandpa’s ken.","","Ken sounds like “can”: what you can know."],["coddle","KOD-l","verb","To treat someone too gently or protect them too much.","Grandma coddles the dog with treats all day.","","Coddle sounds like cuddle: too many cuddles, too much fuss."],["migratory","MY-gruh-tor-ee","adjective","Moving from place to place with the seasons.","Migratory geese fly over our house every fall.","From Latin migrare, “to move.”","Migrate means move, so migratory things move with the seasons."],["prescient","PRESH-uhnt","adjective","Seeming to know what will happen before it does.","Packing umbrellas turned out to be prescient when it rained.","From Latin praescire, “to know beforehand.”","Pre means before, and sci means know: knowing before."],["moonlight","MOON-lyt","verb","To work a second job, often at night.","Mom works at the bank by day and moonlights as a piano teacher.","","Working by the light of the moon, after your day job."],["temerity","tuh-MER-i-tee","noun","Bold daring that might be a bit too much.","The squirrel had the temerity to steal food from our picnic.","From Latin temere, “rashly.”","Temerity sounds like “dare-ity”: daring way too much."],["abyss","uh-BIS","noun","A very deep hole or gap that seems to have no bottom.","I dropped my keys into the abyss between the car seat and the door.","From Greek abyssos, “bottomless.”","Picture an abyss as a hole so deep you can’t see the bottom."],["vapid","VAP-id","adjective","Dull and lacking in interest or ideas.","The TV show was pretty but vapid.","From Latin vapidus, “flat, gone stale.”","Vapid sounds like “vapor”: thin air, nothing inside."],["parlance","PAR-luhns","noun","A way of speaking used by a certain group.","In restaurant parlance, “86” means the kitchen has run out of something.","From French parler, “to speak.”","Parl means speak, like parlor, a room for talking."],["sagacious","suh-GAY-shuhs","adjective","Wise and good at judging things.","My sagacious grandma always knows which melon is ripe.","From Latin sagax, “of keen sense.”","Sagacious sounds like “sage”: a sage is a wise person."],["malaise","ma-LAYZ","noun","A vague feeling that something is not quite right.","After a gray, rainy week, the whole office felt a mild malaise.","From French malaise, “ill ease.”","Mal means bad, and “aise” sounds like ease: bad ease."],["obstreperous","uhb-STREP-er-uhs","adjective","Noisy and hard to control.","The obstreperous puppies tumbled and barked across the yard.","From Latin obstrepere, “to make noise against.”","Obstreperous sounds like “obstacle trumpet”: loud and wild."],["anomaly","uh-NOM-uh-lee","noun","Something that is different from what is normal or expected.","A warm, sunny day in January is an anomaly here.","From Greek anomalia, “unevenness.”","A-nomaly: “not normal,” something that breaks the pattern."],["relinquish","ri-LING-kwish","verb","To let go of something or give it up.","The cat finally relinquished the sunny spot on the couch.","From Latin relinquere, “to leave behind.”","Relinquish sounds like “release it”: you let it go."],["expedite","EK-spi-dyt","verb","To make something happen faster.","We paid extra to expedite the package so it came the next day.","From Latin expedire, “to free the feet.”","Expedite sounds like “speed it”: make it go faster."],["trinket","TRING-kit","noun","A small, cheap ornament or toy.","Her shelf was full of trinkets from family trips.","","Trinket sounds like “tiny trinket-tinkle”: small, cheap and cute."],["vortex","VOR-teks","noun","A whirling mass of water or air.","The draining bathwater made a little vortex.","From Latin vortex, “whirlpool.”","Vortex sounds like “whirl-tex”: a swirling spin."],["legerdemain","lej-er-duh-MAYN","noun","Clever tricks with the hands, like magic.","Grandpa used legerdemain to pull a coin from my ear.","From French léger de main, “light of hand.”","Legerdemain is French for “light of hand”: sleight of hand."],["cultivate","KUHL-tuh-vayt","verb","To grow or develop something with care.","She cultivates good habits, like going to bed early.","","Cultivate is related to culture: you grow it with care."],["credulous","KREJ-uh-luhs","adjective","Too quick to believe things.","The credulous shopper believed the ad that said the pills would melt fat.","From Latin credere, “to believe.”","Credulous: you give credit to every story you hear."],["bistro","BEE-stroh","noun","A small, cozy restaurant.","We shared soup and bread at a little bistro on the corner.","From French bistro.","Bistro sounds like “quick-o”: a small, cozy place to eat."],["diverge","dih-VURJ","verb","To split off in different directions.","The paths diverged at the end of the parking lot.","","Diverge: like a fork in the road, splitting two ways."],["beget","bih-GET","verb","To cause or bring about.","Kindness tends to beget more kindness.","","Beget has “get”: one thing gets you another."],["nascent","NAY-suhnt","adjective","Just starting to grow or form.","Their nascent garden has tiny green sprouts.","From Latin nasci, “to be born.”","Nascent sounds like “nest-sent”: just hatched from the nest."],["arbiter","AR-bi-ter","noun","A person who settles arguments or decides what is right.","Grandma is the family arbiter when we can’t agree on a game.","From Latin arbiter, “a judge.”","Arbiter sounds like “referee-ter”: the one who decides."],["callow","KAL-oh","adjective","Young and not yet experienced.","The callow new driver forgot to check his mirrors.","From Old English calu, “bald.”","Callow sounds like “shallow”: not much experience yet."],["encomium","en-KOH-mee-uhm","noun","A speech or piece of writing full of high praise.","At her retirement party, her boss gave an encomium about all her hard work.","From Greek enkomion, “praise.”","Encomium sounds like “uncommon yum”: words of praise that taste sweet."],["nonpareil","non-puh-REL","adjective","Having no equal; the very best.","Her apple pie is nonpareil; nobody else’s even comes close.","From French non, “not,” and pareil, “equal.”","Non pareil means no equal, nobody on par."],["nonchalant","non-shuh-LAHNT","adjective","Calm and relaxed, not worried.","She was nonchalant about the spider on the wall and kept eating lunch.","From French nonchalant, “not caring.”","Non means not: not worried at all."],["cadenza","kuh-DEN-zuh","noun","A showy solo part near the end of a piece of music.","At the school concert, the violin player’s cadenza made the parents cheer.","From Italian cadenza, “cadence.”","Cadenza sounds like cadence: a fancy solo moment near the end."],["orchestrate","OR-kuh-strayt","verb","To plan and arrange the parts of something.","Mom orchestrated a surprise party without anyone guessing.","","Like a conductor running an orchestra."],["volatile","VOL-uh-tl","adjective","Likely to change suddenly.","Gas prices are volatile, so they change every week.","From Latin volare, “to fly.”","Volatile sounds like “volcano”: it can blow up any time."],["besmirch","bih-SMURCH","verb","To harm someone's good name.","He would never besmirch his friend’s good name with lies.","","Besmirch sounds like “smear”: smearing dirt on someone’s name."],["deadpan","DED-pan","adjective","Showing no feeling while being funny.","His deadpan jokes make everyone giggle.","","Deadpan: a pan, or face, that shows nothing."],["aspire","uh-SPYR","verb","To hope and work hard to reach a goal.","She aspires to be a nurse like her mom.","From Latin aspirare, “to breathe upon.”","Aspire sounds like “a spire”: reaching high like a church tower."],["overt","oh-VURT","adjective","Done openly, not hidden.","His overt joy at the news made us all smile.","From Old French overt, “open.”","Overt sounds like “out there”: open for all to see."],["contentment","kuhn-TENT-muhnt","noun","A calm feeling of being happy with what you have.","A warm blanket, hot cocoa and a good movie bring pure contentment.","","Contentment: you are content, happy with what you have."],["perspicacious","pur-spih-KAY-shuhs","adjective","Able to see and understand things clearly.","The perspicacious mom knew from the muddy boots that the kids had been in the creek.","From Latin perspicax, “sharp-sighted.”","Spic sounds like spy: you can spy what others miss."],["lissome","LIS-uhm","adjective","Slim and graceful.","The lissome dancer moved across the stage like water.","","Lissome sounds like “lithe-some”: slim and graceful."],["slapdash","SLAP-dash","adjective","Done quickly and carelessly.","His slapdash paint job left drips all over the floor.","","Slapdash: slap it on, dash away, done in a careless rush."],["elusive","ih-LOO-siv","adjective","Hard to find, catch or remember.","The TV remote is always elusive when I want to watch something.","From Latin eludere, “to escape by tricks.”","Elusive sounds like “he’s lose-ive,” always slipping away from you."],["adversity","ad-VUR-suh-tee","noun","Hard times or bad luck.","The family stayed strong through adversity after the house fire.","","Adversity sounds like “adverse city”: a town where things go wrong."],["conflagration","kon-fluh-GRAY-shuhn","noun","A very large fire.","The fire trucks raced to the conflagration that had spread across three houses.","From Latin conflagrare, “to burn up.”","Conflagration comes from Latin for “burn up”: a fire that eats everything."],["sashay","sa-SHAY","verb","To walk in a showy, swinging way.","She sashayed across the room in her new dress.","","Sashay sounds like “sassy”: a sassy, swinging walk."],["beseech","bih-SEECH","verb","To beg someone urgently.","“I beseech you, just one more cookie,” my son begged.","","Beseech sounds like “beg and seek”: begging hard."],["testy","TES-tee","adjective","Easily annoyed.","He gets a bit testy before his morning coffee.","","Testy sounds like “test”: it tests your patience."],["reverberate","ri-VUR-buh-rayt","verb","To echo again and again.","Laughter reverberated through the big empty hallway.","From Latin reverberare, “to beat back.”","Reverberate sounds like “rev it back”: sound bouncing back and forth."],["stratagem","STRAT-uh-juhm","noun","A clever plan to get what you want.","Her stratagem to get the kids to bed was a bedtime story.","","Stratagem sounds like “strategy gem”: a clever little plan."],["lugubrious","luh-GOO-bree-uhs","adjective","Sad and gloomy, often too much so.","The dog gave a lugubrious howl when we left.","From Latin lugere, “to mourn.”","Lugubrious sounds like “lug-glubby-us”: so gloomy and sad."],["serendipity","ser-uhn-DIP-i-tee","noun","Finding something good by happy accident.","By pure serendipity, we met our new best friends at the bus stop.","Coined in 1754 by the writer Horace Walpole, from a fairy tale.","Serendipity sounds like “seren-dippity”: a happy lucky dip."],["solace","SOL-is","noun","Comfort in a sad or hard time.","She found solace in long walks with her dog.","From Latin solacium, “comfort.”","Solace sounds like “sole lace,” tied tight: something that holds you up."],["blandishment","BLAN-dish-muhnt","noun","Sweet talk or flattery used to persuade.","No blandishments could get the cat off my warm laptop.","","Blandishment sounds like “bland, sweet talk”: flattery to win you over."],["predilection","pred-l-EK-shuhn","noun","A special liking for something.","He has a predilection for old jazz records.","","Dilection sounds like delight: something you delight in."],["arduous","AR-joo-uhs","adjective","Very hard and tiring.","Moving all our furniture up four flights of stairs was arduous.","From Latin arduus, “steep.”","Arduous sounds like “hard-uous”: very hard work."],["fiasco","fee-AS-koh","noun","A total and often funny failure.","The birthday cake fell on the floor; the party was a fiasco.","From Italian fiasco, “flask.”","Fiasco sounds like “fire-ask-o”: everything goes wrong at once."],["oxbow","OKS-boh","noun","A U-shaped bend in a river.","We paddled our canoe around the oxbow in the river.","","Shaped like the U-shaped collar on an ox."],["grandeur","GRAN-jer","noun","Great beauty and size that fills you with awe.","The grandeur of the big hotel lobby made us stop and stare.","From French grand, “great.”","Grandeur has “grand”: big, beautiful and impressive."],["nugatory","NOO-guh-tor-ee","adjective","Of no value or importance.","The tiny scratch on the table is nugatory; nobody will notice it.","From Latin nugae, “trifles.”","Nugatory sounds like “nothing-atory”: of no value."],["rejuvenate","ri-JOO-vuh-nayt","verb","To make someone feel young and fresh again.","A long nap and a hot shower rejuvenated me after work.","From Latin re-, “again,” and juvenis, “young.”","Rejuvenate has “juven,” like juvenile: it makes you feel young."],["misnomer","mis-NOH-mer","noun","A name that does not fit the thing.","Calling it a “quick lunch” was a misnomer, since it took two hours.","","Mis means wrong, and nomer is like name: a wrong name."],["ethereal","ih-THEER-ee-uhl","adjective","Light, delicate and almost too lovely to be real.","The bride looked ethereal in her soft, white dress.","From Greek aither, “upper air.”","Ethereal sounds like “ether,” light as air in the sky."],["artifice","AR-tuh-fis","noun","Clever tricks or skill used to fool or impress.","The salesman’s smile was pure artifice to get us to buy.","","Artifice has “art” and looks like “artificial”: a clever trick."],["blueprint","BLOO-print","noun","A detailed plan for building or doing something.","Mom’s weekly meal chart is the blueprint for our dinners.","","Building plans used to be printed in blue: a blueprint."],["petrichor","PET-rih-kor","noun","The lovely earthy smell after rain falls on dry ground.","After the summer storm, we stepped outside to smell the petrichor.","From Greek petra, “stone.”","Petra means stone: the smell of rain on stones."],["cartography","kar-TOG-ruh-fee","noun","The art and science of making maps.","His love of cartography began when he drew maps of his neighborhood as a kid.","From French carte, “map,” and Greek graphein, “to write.”","Carto means map, like chart. Graph means draw. Drawing maps."],["nectar","NEK-ter","noun","The sweet liquid in flowers, or any delicious drink.","The ice-cold lemonade tasted like nectar on that hot day.","","Nectar is the sweet drink that bees love."],["iota","eye-OH-tuh","noun","A very small amount.","There is not one iota of cake left after the party.","From Greek iota, the smallest Greek letter.","Iota is the tiniest Greek letter: a very small bit."],["fecund","FEE-kuhnd","adjective","Able to produce a lot; very fruitful.","Our fecund garden gave us more tomatoes than we could eat.","From Latin fecundus, “fruitful.”","Fecund sounds like “feed a crowd”: it grows so much."],["bivouac","BIV-oo-ak","noun","A simple camp with little or no shelter.","When the car broke down, we made a bivouac by the road with blankets.","","Bivouac sounds like “bivy whack”: a quick, simple camp."],["boon","BOON","noun","A helpful gift or blessing.","The new grocery store nearby has been a boon for our family.","","Boon sounds like “bonus”: a lucky gift."],["hardy","HAR-dee","adjective","Strong and able to survive hard conditions.","These hardy tomato plants survived the cold snap.","","Hardy has “hard” in it: tough enough to last."],["osmosis","oz-MOH-sis","noun","Learning something slowly just by being around it.","He learned to cook by osmosis, just by watching his dad.","","Osmosis is how a sponge soaks up water, little by little."],["incessant","in-SES-uhnt","adjective","Never stopping.","The incessant barking next door kept me up all night.","","Incessant: in means not, cease means stop: never stops."],["cache","KASH","noun","A hidden store of things.","My son keeps a cache of candy hidden under his bed.","From French cacher, “to hide.”","Cache sounds like cash: a hidden stash, like cash under a mattress."],["monsoon","mon-SOON","noun","A season of strong winds and heavy rain.","During the monsoon, it rained hard every afternoon for weeks.","","Monsoon sounds like “mon-soon”: the rain is coming soon."],["cherish","CHER-ish","verb","To love and care for something deeply.","She cherishes the blanket her mother knitted for her.","From French cher, “dear.”","Cherish sounds like cherry: something sweet you hold dear."],["wonderment","WUHN-der-muhnt","noun","A feeling of happy amazement.","The toddler stared at the snow in wonderment.","","Wonderment has “wonder”: full of wonder."],["incipient","in-SIP-ee-uhnt","adjective","Just beginning.","I felt an incipient cold, so I went to bed early.","From Latin incipere, “to begin.”","Incipient sounds like “in-beginning”: just starting."],["copious","KOH-pee-uhs","adjective","In large amounts.","She took copious notes at her first day of work.","From Latin copia, “plenty.”","Copious sounds like “copy-ous”: so many copies, there’s plenty."],["whet","WET","verb","To sharpen, or to make someone more eager.","The smell of popcorn whetted our appetite for the movie.","From Old English hwettan, “to sharpen.”","Whet sounds like “wet”: it makes your mouth water."],["betoken","bih-TOH-kuhn","verb","To be a sign of something.","Dark clouds betokened a storm, so we brought the laundry in.","","Betoken has “token”: a token, or sign, of what is coming."],["memento","muh-MEN-toh","noun","A small thing kept to help you remember.","She kept a ticket stub as a memento of her first concert.","From Latin memento, “remember!”","Memento starts with “memo,” a note to remember."],["ascetic","uh-SET-ik","adjective","Living very simply and giving up comforts.","He lives an ascetic life with no TV, no car and very few clothes.","From Greek asketes, “one who trains.”","Ascetic sounds like “a skeptic of stuff”: living with very little."],["overture","OH-ver-cher","noun","The music at the start of a show, or a first friendly move.","The new neighbors made an overture with a plate of cookies.","From Old French overture, “opening.”","An overture opens a show, or opens a friendship."],["confidant","KON-fi-dant","noun","A trusted friend you share secrets with.","Her best friend is her closest confidant.","","Confidant has confide in it: the one you confide in."],["concise","kuhn-SYS","adjective","Saying a lot in few words.","Her concise text said just what we needed: “Running late. Start without me.”","From Latin concidere, “to cut up.”","Concise has cis, meaning cut: cut down to the few words you need."],["cosmopolitan","koz-muh-POL-i-tn","adjective","Familiar with many countries and cultures.","Our city has a cosmopolitan mix of food from all over the world.","From Greek kosmopolites, “citizen of the world.”","Cosmo means world. Polis means city. A city of the world."],["boreal","BOR-ee-uhl","adjective","Having to do with the cold north, or its forests.","The boreal wind from the north made us zip up our winter coats.","From Greek Boreas, the north wind.","Boreas was the Greek god of the north wind."],["superfluous","soo-PUR-floo-uhs","adjective","More than is needed; extra.","Ten reminder texts for one dentist visit felt superfluous.","From Latin superfluere, “to overflow.”","Superfluous starts with “super,” meaning over: overflowing, more than needed."],["cavalier","kav-uh-LEER","adjective","Careless about things that matter.","He was cavalier about leaving his phone on the café table.","From Italian cavaliere, “horseman.”","A cavalier rode a horse and acted like nothing could go wrong."],["promenade","prom-uh-NAYD","noun","A slow walk for pleasure, or a path for one.","We took an evening promenade along the beach.","From French promener, “to take for a walk.”","Promenade sounds like “prom lemonade”: a slow, fancy stroll."],["concord","KON-kord","noun","Peace and agreement.","The two families lived in happy concord on the same street.","From Latin concors, “of one heart.”","Con means with. Cord comes from heart. Hearts together."],["bonanza","buh-NAN-zuh","noun","A sudden source of good luck or plenty.","The yard sale was a bonanza of cheap toys for the kids.","From Spanish bonanza, “fair weather.”","Bonanza sounds like “bonus”: a big lucky haul."],["bespectacled","bih-SPEK-tuh-kuhld","adjective","Wearing glasses.","The bespectacled teacher pushed her glasses up her nose.","","Bespectacled has “spectacles,” another word for glasses."],["ephemeral","ih-FEM-er-uhl","adjective","Lasting only a very short time.","Our snowman was ephemeral; it melted by lunch.","From Greek ephemeros, “lasting a day.”","Ephemeral comes from Greek for “lasting a day,” like a snowman."],["yore","YOR","noun","Long ago; the old days.","Grandma told stories about the days of yore.","From Old English geāra, “long ago.”","Yore sounds like “your old days”: long, long ago."],["mellow","MEL-oh","adjective","Soft, calm and relaxed.","The evening was mellow, with tea, a blanket and soft music.","","Mellow rhymes with yellow, like a soft, warm light."],["bluster","BLUHS-ter","noun","Loud, boastful talk that means little; also strong gusty wind.","Behind all his bluster, Grandpa is a gentle soul.","","Bluster sounds like “blow and fluster”: loud wind with no harm."],["abiding","uh-BY-ding","adjective","Lasting a long time and not changing.","She has an abiding love for her hometown, even after forty years away.","","Abide means to stay; abiding love stays and stays."],["ascend","uh-SEND","verb","To go up or climb.","We took the elevator to ascend to the tenth floor.","From Latin ascendere, “to climb up.”","Ascend sounds like “a send up”: going up."],["crucible","KROO-suh-buhl","noun","A hard test that changes someone.","Her first year as a nurse was a crucible that made her strong.","","Crucible sounds like crucial: a hard test at a crucial time."],["calamity","kuh-LAM-i-tee","noun","A great disaster or trouble.","Dropping the wedding cake was a calamity.","From Latin calamitas, “disaster.”","Calamity rhymes with “a lot of pity”: a disaster everyone feels sorry about."],["onus","OH-nuhs","noun","A duty or responsibility.","The onus is on me to bring snacks this week.","From Latin onus, “burden.”","Onus sounds like “on us”: it’s our job."],["lurk","LURK","verb","To wait quietly, out of sight.","The cat lurked under the bed, waiting to pounce on my feet.","","Lurk sounds like “lurking shark”: hiding and waiting."],["nuzzle","NUZ-uhl","verb","To rub gently with the nose.","The puppy nuzzled my hand looking for treats.","","Nuzzle sounds like nose and snuggle together."],["perfunctory","per-FUNK-tuh-ree","adjective","Done quickly, just to get it over with.","He gave the dishes a perfunctory rinse and left them in the sink.","","Per-FUNK-tory: done in a funk, just to get it over with."],["handiwork","HAN-dee-wurk","noun","Something made by a person's own hands.","The kids’ messy kitchen was their handiwork from baking cookies.","","Handiwork: the work of your own hands."],["pliable","PLY-uh-buhl","adjective","Easy to bend, or easy to influence.","Soak the clay to make it pliable.","","Pliable sounds like pliers: easy to bend."],["mollify","MOL-uh-fy","verb","To calm someone who is upset.","A warm cookie mollified the tired toddler.","From Latin mollis, “soft.”","Mollify sounds like “mollie-fy”: calm someone with a treat."],["decant","dih-KANT","verb","To pour liquid gently from one container into another.","She decanted the lemonade into a glass pitcher.","","Decant: you pour it out of the can into something nicer."],["isthmus","IS-muhs","noun","A narrow strip of land joining two larger lands.","We drove across a narrow isthmus with water on both sides.","From Greek isthmos, “neck.”","Isthmus sounds like “it’s a must” bridge of land between two lands."],["addendum","uh-DEN-duhm","noun","Something added to the end of a book or document.","The lease came with an addendum about the rules for pets.","From Latin addendum, “a thing to be added.”","Addendum starts with “add”: something added at the end."],["solstice","SOL-stis","noun","The longest or shortest day of the year.","We stayed up late to enjoy the summer solstice.","From Latin sol, “sun,” and sistere, “to stand still.”","Sol means sun: the solstice is the sun’s longest or shortest day."],["vignette","vin-YET","noun","A short, vivid scene or story.","Her book is full of sweet vignettes about family life.","From French vignette, “little vine.”","Vignette sounds like “vine yet”: a tiny snapshot story."],["quaff","KWAF","verb","To drink something in big, happy gulps.","After the soccer game, the kids quaffed big cups of cold water.","","Quaff sounds like a loud, happy gulp of a big drink."],["versatile","VUR-suh-tl","adjective","Able to do many different things well.","A versatile cook, he can make soup, bread and pie.","From Latin versatilis, “turning easily.”","Versatile sounds like “very skillful”: good at many things."],["subterfuge","SUB-ter-fyooj","noun","A sneaky trick used to hide something.","We used a little subterfuge to plan Dad’s surprise party.","","Subterfuge sounds like “sub-tear-refuge”: hiding under cover."],["haven","HAY-vuhn","noun","A safe, peaceful place.","Our quiet kitchen is my haven after a long workday.","","Haven sounds like heaven: a safe, peaceful place."],["avarice","AV-er-is","noun","Too much desire for money or things.","His avarice made him keep every penny and never share.","From Latin avarus, “greedy.”","Avarice sounds like “have a rice”: wanting all the rice, never sharing."],["naive","nah-EEV","adjective","Trusting too easily because you lack experience.","I was naive to think the puppy would not chew my shoes.","From French naïf, “natural.”","Naive sounds like “knife-free”: never expecting anything sharp."],["civility","si-VIL-i-tee","noun","Polite and respectful behavior.","Even when they disagree, the neighbors speak with civility.","","Civility sounds like civil: polite, like good citizens."],["bicker","BIK-er","verb","To argue about small things.","The twins bickered over who got the front seat.","","Bicker sounds like “bicker-bicker,” the sound of small squabbles."],["yawp","YAWP","noun","A loud, wild cry.","He let out a happy yawp when he jumped into the pool.","","Yawp sounds like “yelp”: a loud, wild shout."],["pantomime","PAN-tuh-mym","noun","Acting out a story with only movements, no words.","At the party, the kids used pantomime to act out animals without talking.","","Pantomime has mime in it: acting with no words."],["obstinate","OB-stuh-nit","adjective","Stubborn and not willing to change your mind.","The obstinate toddler wore rain boots on a sunny day.","From Latin obstinatus, “determined.”","Obstinate sounds like “obstacle”: it won’t move."],["dote","DOHT","verb","To show great love, often a lot.","Grandma dotes on her new grandson.","","Dote sounds like “dot”: she puts kisses all over like dots."],["upshot","UHP-shot","noun","The final result of something.","The upshot of our talk was a family game night every Friday.","","Upshot: what shoots up at the very end."],["confluence","KON-floo-uhns","noun","A place where things flow or come together.","The busy corner is a confluence of three main roads.","From Latin confluere, “to flow together.”","Con means together. Flu means flow, like fluid. Flowing together."],["talisman","TAL-is-muhn","noun","An object believed to bring good luck.","Her grandfather’s old coin was her talisman on test days.","","Talisman sounds like “tally man”: a lucky charm that keeps your luck tally up."],["absurd","ab-SURD","adjective","Silly in a way that makes no sense.","The idea of a cat driving a school bus is absurd, but it made us laugh.","From Latin absurdus, “out of tune.”","Absurd sounds like “a bird” wearing a hat: silly and makes no sense."],["mawkish","MAW-kish","adjective","Sweet and sad in a way that feels fake.","The birthday card was a bit mawkish, with too many teary poems.","","Mawkish sounds like “mock-ish”: so sappy it feels fake."],["olfactory","ol-FAK-tuh-ree","adjective","Having to do with the sense of smell.","The bakery was an olfactory delight, with the smell of fresh bread everywhere.","From Latin olfacere, “to smell.”","Olfactory sounds like “old factory” full of smells."],["facile","FAS-uhl","adjective","Too simple; done easily but without much thought.","“Just be happy” is a facile answer to a hard problem.","From Latin facilis, “easy.”","Facile is like facility, ease: too easy and too quick."],["redolent","RED-uh-luhnt","adjective","Full of a strong, pleasant smell, or bringing back memories.","The kitchen was redolent of cinnamon and fresh bread.","From Latin redolere, “to give off a smell.”","Redolent sounds like “red-oh-scent”: a room full of scent."],["petulant","PECH-uh-luhnt","adjective","Cranky and sulky like a spoiled child.","The petulant toddler pouted until she got her juice.","","Petulant sounds like “pet-you-lant”: a spoiled pet who sulks."],["effulgent","ih-FUL-juhnt","adjective","Shining brightly.","The effulgent sunset turned the whole sky orange.","From Latin effulgere, “to shine out.”","Effulgent sounds like “full gent”: full of shining light."],["lexicon","LEK-si-kon","noun","All the words a person or group uses.","“Bae” is now part of my teen’s lexicon.","From Greek lexikon, “of words.”","Lexicon: lex means word, like a dictionary of your words."],["momentous","moh-MEN-tuhs","adjective","Very important.","Moving to a new country was a momentous choice for our family.","","A moment so big you remember it forever."],["vitriol","VIT-ree-uhl","noun","Bitter, harsh words or feelings.","Let’s keep our family talk kind and free of vitriol.","","Vitriol is an old name for a harsh acid: words that burn."],["portmanteau","port-MAN-toh","noun","A word made by blending two words, like brunch from breakfast and lunch.","“Smog” is a portmanteau of smoke and fog.","From French porter, “to carry,” and manteau, “cloak.”","A portmanteau is a suitcase that holds two things in one."],["afterglow","AF-ter-gloh","noun","The light in the sky after sunset, or a warm feeling after a happy event.","We sat in the afterglow of the birthday party, full and happy.","","Afterglow: the glow that stays after the fun is over."],["corporeal","kor-POR-ee-uhl","adjective","Having a body you can touch; physical.","The ghost in the movie had no corporeal body, so you could see right through it.","From Latin corpus, “body.”","Corp means body, like a corpse or a corporation."],["ballad","BAL-uhd","noun","A song or poem that tells a story.","Grandpa sang an old ballad about a sailor lost at sea.","","Ballad sounds like “ball add”: a song that tells a whole story."],["ensconce","en-SKONS","verb","To settle in a safe, cozy spot.","The kids were ensconced under blankets on the couch, watching a movie.","","Ensconce sounds like “in sconce”: tucked in snug like a candle in its holder."],["minimalism","MIN-uh-muh-liz-uhm","noun","A style that uses very few, simple things.","Her minimalism means one couch, one plant and lots of empty space.","","Minimal means the minimum: as little stuff as possible."],["detritus","dih-TRY-tuhs","noun","Bits of waste or debris left behind.","We swept up the detritus after the birthday party.","","Detritus sounds like “debris”: the bits left behind."],["anecdote","AN-ik-doht","noun","A short, interesting story about a real event.","Grandpa told a funny anecdote about his first car breaking down.","From Greek anekdota, “things unpublished.”","Anecdote sounds like “an extra dot”: a little story to add."],["gargantuan","gar-GAN-choo-uhn","adjective","Huge.","The kids built a gargantuan snowman taller than Dad.","","Gargantuan sounds like “gigantic giant”: super huge."],["bespoke","bih-SPOHK","adjective","Made to order for one person.","He wore a bespoke suit, made just for him, to his daughter’s wedding.","","Bespoke: it was spoken for, ordered just for you."],["otherworldly","uh-ther-WURLD-lee","adjective","Strange and dreamy, as if from another world.","The foggy beach at dawn looked otherworldly.","","Other world: as if from another planet."],["corroborate","kuh-ROB-uh-rayt","verb","To back up a story or idea with proof.","The doorbell camera corroborated her story about the stolen package.","From Latin robur, “strength.”","Corroborate has robust in its roots: it makes your story stronger."],["decibel","DES-uh-bel","noun","A unit for measuring how loud a sound is.","The concert was so loud my phone app said it hit 100 decibels.","","Decibel has bel in it, like bell: a unit for how loud."],["repartee","rep-er-TEE","noun","Quick, witty back-and-forth talk.","Their friendly repartee kept the whole dinner table laughing.","From French repartie, “a quick reply.”","Repartee sounds like “repeat party”: a party of quick replies."],["austere","aw-STEER","adjective","Very plain and simple, without comfort or decoration.","The hotel room was austere, with just a bed, a lamp and bare walls.","From Greek austeros, “harsh.”","Austere sounds like “a stern”: plain and strict, no extras."],["balmy","BAH-mee","adjective","Pleasantly warm and gentle, used for weather.","We sat outside on a balmy summer evening with ice cream.","","Balmy sounds like “calm-y”: gentle, warm weather."],["alleviate","uh-LEE-vee-ayt","verb","To make something hard or painful easier to bear.","A cool, wet cloth helped alleviate my headache.","From Latin levis, “light.”","Alleviate sounds like “a lever”: it lifts the weight off you."],["deleterious","del-i-TEER-ee-uhs","adjective","Harmful.","Too much sugar can be deleterious to your teeth.","From Greek deleterios, “harmful.”","Deleterious sounds like delete: it deletes your health."],["impish","IM-pish","adjective","Playfully naughty.","My son gave an impish grin after hiding my keys.","","Impish has “imp,” a playful little troublemaker."],["dwell","DWEL","verb","To live somewhere, or to keep thinking about something.","Try not to dwell on small mistakes.","","Dwell sounds like well: you stay put, like water in a well."],["tortuous","TOR-choo-uhs","adjective","Full of twists and turns.","We drove up a tortuous road with hairpin turns.","From Latin tortus, “twisted.”","Tortuous sounds like “tort-twist”: twisty like a pretzel."],["berth","BURTH","noun","A place to sleep on a ship or train; also a safe distance.","We gave the barking dog a wide berth on our walk.","","Berth sounds like “birth”: give it room, lots of space."],["quagmire","KWAG-myr","noun","A soggy bog, or a messy problem that is hard to get out of.","The backyard turned into a quagmire after three days of rain.","","Quagmire has “mire,” which means deep mud you get stuck in."],["sardonic","sar-DON-ik","adjective","Dryly mocking, with a crooked kind of humor.","He gave a sardonic smile when the bus was late again.","","Sardonic sounds like “sardine-ic”: a salty, fishy kind of humor."],["beleaguered","bih-LEE-gerd","adjective","Having lots of troubles or pressure.","The beleaguered waiter smiled through the busy lunch rush.","","Beleaguered sounds like “big league worried”: under heavy pressure."],["glib","GLIB","adjective","Smooth and quick in speech, but not sincere.","His glib apology sounded too smooth to be real.","","Glib sounds like “glide”: words glide out but don’t mean much."],["egress","EE-gres","noun","A way out, or the act of going out.","The back door is our egress if there is ever a fire.","From Latin egredi, “to go out.”","Egress: e means out, so egress is the way out."],["hoary","HOR-ee","adjective","Gray or white with age; very old.","My grandpa’s hoary beard is white as snow.","","Hoary sounds like frosty “hoar” frost: white and old."],["acrid","AK-rid","adjective","Sharp and bitter to smell or taste.","The acrid smell of burnt toast filled the whole kitchen.","From Latin acer, “sharp.”","Acrid sounds like “a crud”: a sharp, nasty smell."],["mainstay","MAYN-stay","noun","The main thing or person that holds something up.","Soup is the mainstay of our winter dinners; we have it almost every night.","","The main thing that stays and holds everything up."],["origami","or-ih-GAH-mee","noun","The art of folding paper into shapes.","She made an origami crane for each guest at the party.","From Japanese ori, “folding,” and kami, “paper.”","Ori means fold, and gami means paper, in Japanese."],["tarry","TAIR-ee","verb","To stay longer than planned, or to be slow to leave.","We tarried at the park until the sun went down.","","Tarry sounds like “tardy”: you stay so long you’re late."],["barren","BAIR-uhn","adjective","Bare and empty, unable to grow plants.","The barren backyard had no grass, just dry dirt.","","Barren sounds like “bare-in”: bare, nothing grows."],["dollop","DOL-uhp","noun","A soft lump of something, like whipped cream.","Add a dollop of whipped cream to your pie.","","Dollop sounds like “plop”: a soft blob plopped on top."],["dauntless","DAWNT-lis","adjective","Brave and not easily scared.","The dauntless firefighter ran back in to save the cat.","","Daunt means scare. Dauntless: not scared at all."],["epiphany","ih-PIF-uh-nee","noun","A sudden moment when you understand something.","While washing dishes, I had an epiphany about where I left my keys.","From Greek epiphaneia, “appearance.”","Epiphany: a light bulb pops on over your head."],["teem","TEEM","verb","To be full of moving things.","The mall teemed with shoppers the day before the holiday.","","Teem sounds like “team”: a whole team crowding in."],["balk","BAWK","verb","To stop and refuse to go on.","The puppy balked at going outside in the rain.","","Balk sounds like “block”: stopping dead in your tracks."],["brouhaha","BROO-hah-hah","noun","A noisy fuss or stir over something.","There was a big brouhaha over who got the last cupcake.","From French brouhaha.","Brouhaha sounds like “brew-ha-ha”: a noisy stir."],["circuitous","ser-KYOO-i-tuhs","adjective","Taking a long, winding way instead of a direct one.","The taxi took a circuitous route, and the ride cost twice as much.","","Circuitous has circuit in it: going around and around."],["implore","im-PLOR","verb","To beg someone eagerly.","The kids implored me to let them stay up late.","From Latin implorare, “to beg with tears.”","Implore sounds like “I’m pleading for more,” begging with big eyes."],["boutique","boo-TEEK","noun","A small shop that sells special or stylish items.","We found the scarf at a little boutique downtown.","From French boutique, “a shop.”","Boutique sounds like “boot chic”: a small, stylish shop."],["potable","POH-tuh-buhl","adjective","Safe to drink.","The water from the kitchen tap is potable.","From Latin potare, “to drink.”","Potable sounds like “pot-able”: fine to put in your pot."],["braggadocio","brag-uh-DOH-shee-oh","noun","Empty, boastful talk.","His braggadocio about his fishing skills made us laugh.","","Braggadocio has “brag” inside: big boasting."],["mulish","MYOO-lish","adjective","Stubborn, like a mule.","He was mulish about wearing a coat until he felt the cold.","","Mulish has mule in it, and mules are stubborn."],["penchant","PEN-chuhnt","noun","A strong liking for something.","She has a penchant for spicy food and adds hot sauce to everything.","From French pencher, “to lean.”","Penchant sounds like “lean-chant”: what you lean toward."],["luxuriate","lug-ZHOOR-ee-ayt","verb","To relax and enjoy something fully.","I luxuriated in a hot bath after a long day.","From Latin luxuria, “excess.”","Luxuriate sounds like “luxury”: soak it all in."],["bedeck","bih-DEK","verb","To decorate in a showy way.","The house was bedecked with lights and wreaths for the holidays.","","Bedeck has “deck”: deck the halls with decorations."],["odyssey","OD-uh-see","noun","A long, adventurous journey.","Our drive to the beach became an odyssey of wrong turns and traffic.","","Odyssey sounds like “odd sea”: a long, strange trip."],["rendezvous","RAHN-day-voo","noun","A planned meeting at a set time and place.","Our rendezvous point was the front door of the grocery store.","From French rendez-vous, “present yourselves.”","Rendezvous is French for “present yourselves”: a planned meeting spot."],["consummate","KON-suh-mit","adjective","Showing great skill; complete in every way.","She is a consummate host who remembers every guest’s favorite drink.","","Consummate has sum in it: the total package, complete."],["mentor","MEN-tor","noun","A wise person who guides someone with less experience.","My first boss became my mentor and helped me grow at every job since.","","A mentor is the one who helps your mind grow."],["ilk","ILK","noun","A type or kind.","My aunt hates reality TV and shows of that ilk.","","Ilk sounds like milk: same kind, same type."],["slake","SLAYK","verb","To satisfy a thirst or a strong wish.","A cold glass of water slaked my thirst after the long drive.","","Slake sounds like “lake”: a lake of water for your thirst."],["plethora","PLETH-er-uh","noun","Much more than you need.","The buffet had a plethora of desserts.","From Greek plethore, “fullness.”","Plethora sounds like “plenty-ora”: way more than plenty."],["steadfast","STED-fast","adjective","Firm, loyal and not changing.","The old dog was steadfast by the boy’s side.","From Old English stedefæst, “firm in place.”","Steadfast has “steady”: steady and fast in place."],["dossier","DOS-ee-ay","noun","A file of papers about a person or topic.","She kept a dossier of ideas for her kitchen remodel.","From French dossier, “bundle of papers.”","Dossier sounds like “dozen sheets”: a file full of papers."],["dint","DINT","noun","Means or effort, used in the phrase “by dint of” something.","By dint of hard work, she paid off her car early.","","Dint sounds like dent: you made a dent by pushing hard."],["brevity","BREV-i-tee","noun","Being short or brief.","The speaker’s brevity was welcome on such a hot day.","From Latin brevis, “short.”","Brevity sounds like “brief”: short and sweet."],["atoll","AT-awl","noun","A ring-shaped island with a pool of sea water in the middle.","From the plane, the atoll looked like a green ring around a blue pool.","From Maldivian atolu.","Atoll sounds like “a doll’s” ring: a tiny ring of land in the sea."],["nub","NUHB","noun","The main point, or a small lump.","The nub of the problem is that we need more chairs.","","A nub is a little bump, the key point that sticks out."],["inundate","IN-uhn-dayt","verb","To flood or overwhelm.","After the sale, the store was inundated with shoppers.","From Latin unda, “wave.”","Inundate has “unda,” Latin for wave: a wave floods in."],["docent","DOH-suhnt","noun","A guide who teaches visitors at a museum.","At the museum, a docent told us the story behind each painting.","From Latin docere, “to teach.”","Docent comes from Latin for teach, like doctor and document."],["byway","BY-way","noun","A small side road; also a less-known area of a subject.","We took a quiet byway to avoid the highway traffic.","","Byway: a road by the side of the main way."],["aegis","EE-jis","noun","The protection or support of someone.","The school fair was held under the aegis of the parents’ group.","From Greek aigis, the shield of Zeus.","Aegis sounds like “a cage, is safe”: a shield that protects."],["amble","AM-buhl","verb","To walk in a slow, relaxed way.","We ambled around the mall with no rush and no list.","From Latin ambulare, “to walk.”","Amble sounds like “ramble”: a slow, easy walk."],["traipse","TRAYPS","verb","To walk around in a tired or aimless way.","We traipsed through three stores looking for the right shoes.","","Traipse sounds like “trip-trap”: tired feet tramping around."],["curmudgeon","ker-MUHJ-uhn","noun","A grumpy, stubborn person, often older.","The office curmudgeon complains, but he always brings donuts.","","Curmudgeon sounds like “cur” plus “mud”: a grumpy old dog in the mud."],["multifarious","mul-tuh-FAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Of many different kinds.","Her multifarious hobbies include knitting, chess and kayaking.","","Multi means many, so it means many different kinds."],["convalesce","kon-vuh-LES","verb","To rest and recover after being sick.","He convalesced on the couch with soup and movies after his flu.","From Latin convalescere, “to grow strong.”","Convalesce has “vale,” Latin for be well, like valid. Getting well."],["parody","PAIR-uh-dee","noun","A funny copy of something that pokes fun at it.","They wrote a parody of a famous song about their lazy cat.","","Parody sounds like parrot: it copies, but in a silly way."],["appraise","uh-PRAYZ","verb","To judge the value or quality of something.","The jeweler appraised Grandma’s old ring and said it was worth a lot.","","Appraise sounds like “a price”: figure out the price."],["pedantic","puh-DAN-tik","adjective","Too fussy about small rules and details.","He was pedantic about the right way to load the dishwasher.","","Pedantic sounds like “pedal-antic”: pedaling every tiny rule."],["jubilant","JOO-buh-luhnt","adjective","Very happy, especially after a win.","The kids were jubilant when they won the spelling bee.","From Latin jubilare, “to shout with joy.”","Jubilant sounds like jubilee: a big happy celebration."],["avert","uh-VURT","verb","To turn away, or to stop something bad from happening.","Quick thinking averted a disaster when the pot boiled over.","From Latin avertere, “to turn away.”","Avert sounds like “a vert,” turn away: steer away from trouble."],["flummox","FLUM-uhks","verb","To confuse someone completely.","The new TV remote completely flummoxed Grandpa.","","Flummox sounds like “flummoxed fox”: a fox spinning, totally confused."],["opprobrium","uh-PROH-bree-uhm","noun","Strong public blame or shame.","The team faced opprobrium for leaving trash all over the park.","From Latin opprobrium, “reproach.”","Opprobrium sounds like “oh, probe ’em”: everyone blames them."],["congregate","KONG-gri-gayt","verb","To gather together in a group.","Teens congregate at the mall on Saturday afternoons.","From Latin grex, “flock.”","Greg means flock, like a gregarious person who loves groups."],["unflappable","uhn-FLAP-uh-buhl","adjective","Calm, even when things go wrong.","The unflappable bus driver handled the snow with ease.","","Unflappable: no flapping around in a panic."],["contour","KON-toor","noun","The outline or shape of something.","She ran her finger along the contour of the baby’s cheek.","","Contour sounds like “con-tour”: a tour around the outline."],["overweening","oh-ver-WEE-ning","adjective","Too proud or too sure of yourself.","His overweening pride made him skip practice, and he lost.","","Over-weening: thinking way too much of yourself."],["mountebank","MOUN-tuh-bank","noun","A person who tricks people to get their money.","The mountebank sold “magic” water that was really just tap water.","From Italian montambanco, “one who climbs on a bench.”","A mountebank climbs up on a bench to trick a crowd."],["somnolent","SOM-nuh-luhnt","adjective","Sleepy or drowsy.","The warm classroom after lunch made everyone somnolent.","From Latin somnus, “sleep.”","Somnolent comes from Latin somnus, sleep, like in insomnia."],["nosegay","NOHZ-gay","noun","A small bunch of flowers.","The child gave her teacher a nosegay of daisies from the yard.","","A small bunch of flowers held up to make your nose happy."],["unfettered","uhn-FET-erd","adjective","Free from limits or restraints.","The kids had unfettered fun on the last day of school.","","Unfettered: fetters are chains, so no chains holding you back."],["anagram","AN-uh-gram","noun","A word made by mixing up the letters of another word.","“Listen” is an anagram of “silent”; they use the same letters.","","Ana means again, gram means letters: the same letters again."],["glut","GLUT","noun","Too much of something.","After the holidays, we had a glut of leftover cookies.","","Glut sounds like glutton: way too much."],["unbridled","uhn-BRY-dld","adjective","Free and not held back.","The kids ran to the pool with unbridled excitement.","","Unbridled: a horse with no bridle runs totally free."],["maroon","muh-ROON","verb","To leave someone stuck in a lonely place.","The snowstorm marooned us at the cabin for two cozy days.","","Picture someone stuck on an island with only a maroon shirt."],["folly","FOL-ee","noun","A foolish act or idea.","Driving in the snowstorm without gas was pure folly.","From French folie, “madness.”","Folly sounds like “foolie”: a foolish move."],["munificent","myoo-NIF-uh-suhnt","adjective","Very generous with gifts.","A munificent neighbor paid for new benches at the park.","From Latin munus, “gift.”","Munificent sounds like magnificent: a magnificent giver."],["kith","KITH","noun","Your friends and neighbors.","All our kith and kin came to the wedding.","","Kith and kin: friends and family, side by side."],["labyrinth","LAB-uh-rinth","noun","A maze of winding paths.","The hospital hallways were a labyrinth, and we got lost.","From Greek labyrinthos.","Labyrinth sounds like “lab-maze”: a maze you get lost in."],["coalesce","koh-uh-LES","verb","To come together into one whole.","Our ideas for the party coalesced into one great plan.","From Latin coalescere, “to grow together.”","Coalesce: like drops of water that join into one puddle."],["protean","PROH-tee-uhn","adjective","Able to change shape or do many different things.","The protean actor played a king, a dog and a tree in one show.","","Protean comes from Proteus, a sea god who changed shapes."],["kooky","KOO-kee","adjective","Odd in a fun way.","My kooky aunt wears a different colored sock on each foot.","","Kooky sounds like cuckoo: odd in a fun way."],["antiquarian","an-ti-KWAIR-ee-uhn","adjective","Having to do with old and rare objects or books.","The antiquarian bookshop sells old, rare books.","","Antiquarian has “antique” inside: all about old things."],["discrepancy","dih-SKREP-uhn-see","noun","A difference between things that should match.","There was a discrepancy between the price on the shelf and the receipt.","","Discrepancy: two numbers that disagree."],["artless","ART-lis","adjective","Simple, honest and natural, without trying to impress.","The child’s artless question, “Why is your hair gray?” made Grandma laugh.","","Artless means without art or tricks: simple and honest."],["effervescent","ef-er-VES-uhnt","adjective","Bubbly, like a fizzy drink, or lively and cheerful.","My effervescent friend makes even a trip to the grocery store fun.","From Latin effervescere, “to boil up.”","Effervescent is fizzy, like soda bubbles that won’t sit still."],["devious","DEE-vee-uhs","adjective","Sneaky and not straightforward.","The cat had a devious plan to steal the ham off the counter.","","Devious: you deviate, or go off, the straight path."],["catalyst","KAT-uh-list","noun","Something that makes a change happen faster.","A new baby was the catalyst for us to buy a bigger car.","From Greek katalysis, “a dissolving.”","Catalyst: the spark that makes things happen faster."],["hodgepodge","HOJ-poj","noun","A messy mix of things.","The junk drawer is a hodgepodge of keys, batteries and tape.","","Hodgepodge: a hodge and a podge, all mixed up together."],["exult","ig-ZULT","verb","To feel or show great joy.","The kids exulted when school was canceled for snow.","From Latin exsultare, “to leap up.”","Exult sounds like “exalt”: jumping up and down with joy."],["wisp","WISP","noun","A thin, small piece of something, like hair or smoke.","A wisp of steam rose from my tea.","","Wisp sounds like “whisper”: a tiny, thin bit."],["boulevard","BUL-uh-vard","noun","A wide city street, often lined with trees.","We drove down the wide, tree-lined boulevard to the mall.","From French boulevard.","Boulevard sounds like “bowl of yard”: a big, wide street."],["obliging","uh-BLY-jing","adjective","Happy and willing to help.","The obliging clerk carried our bags to the car.","","Obliging sounds like “oh, I’ll bring”: happy to help."],["flotsam","FLOT-suhm","noun","Bits of wreckage or junk floating on the water.","After the storm, flotsam like bottles and boards washed onto the beach.","","Flotsam floats: junk floating on the water."],["zugzwang","TSOOK-tsvahng","noun","A spot where any move you make will hurt you, as in chess.","I was in zugzwang: either answer would spoil the surprise party.","From German Zugzwang, “compulsion to move.”","Zugzwang is German for “forced to move”: every move hurts."],["hygge","HOO-guh","noun","A cozy feeling of comfort and togetherness.","Hot soup, candles and a family movie night: that’s hygge.","From Danish hygge.","Hygge sounds like “hoo-ga,” like a warm hug: pure coziness."],["harbinger","HAR-bin-jer","noun","A sign that something is coming.","The first pumpkin coffee is a harbinger of fall.","","Harbinger sounds like “harbor-bringer”: it brings news of what’s coming."],["compatriot","kuhm-PAY-tree-uht","noun","A person from your own country.","Far from home, she met a compatriot at the airport and they chatted for hours.","","Compatriot has patriot in it: someone from your own country."],["tantamount","TAN-tuh-mownt","adjective","Equal to, or as good as.","Skipping practice was tantamount to giving up his spot on the team.","","Tantamount sounds like “same amount”: equal to."],["bootstrap","BOOT-strap","verb","To start something using only what you already have.","She bootstrapped her bakery from her home kitchen with no loans.","","Pull yourself up by your own bootstraps: start with what you have."],["buttress","BUH-tris","verb","To support or strengthen.","She buttressed her request for a raise with numbers about her work.","","A buttress is a support that holds up a wall."],["abstemious","ab-STEE-mee-uhs","adjective","Careful not to eat or drink too much.","He is abstemious at parties and takes just one small slice of cake.","","Abstemious starts like “abstain”: you hold back from too much."],["diffuse","dih-FYOOZ","verb","To spread out over a wide area.","The lamp diffused a soft glow across the room.","From Latin diffundere, “to pour out.”","Diffuse: like a smell from the kitchen that spreads through the house."],["wane","WAYN","verb","To slowly get smaller or weaker.","My interest in the gym waned after a few weeks.","From Old English wanian, “to lessen.”","Wane sounds like “wane-ing away”: slowly fading."],["accrue","uh-KROO","verb","To build up slowly over time.","The coins in the jar accrued until we had enough for a pizza night.","","Accrue sounds like “a crew”: coins join the crew one by one."],["jargon","JAR-guhn","noun","Special words used by one group that others may not understand.","The doctor’s jargon confused us, so we asked her to explain.","","Jargon sounds like “jar-gone”: words locked in a jar for insiders."],["gesticulate","je-STIK-yuh-layt","verb","To wave your hands while you talk.","Grandpa gesticulates wildly when he tells a story.","","Gesticulate has “gesture” inside: waving hands as you talk."],["flabbergast","FLAB-er-gast","verb","To amaze someone completely.","We were flabbergasted when the kids cleaned their rooms without being asked.","","Flabbergasted: so shocked your flabby jaw drops with a gasp."],["novice","NOV-is","noun","A person who is new at something.","The novice skater held on to the wall the whole time.","From Latin novus, “new.”","Nov means new: a newbie."],["oblique","oh-BLEEK","adjective","Slanting, or not said in a direct way.","She made an oblique hint about her birthday by leaving a cake catalog out.","From Latin obliquus, “slanting.”","Oblique sounds like “oh, sleek”: it slides in at a slant."],["balderdash","BAWL-der-dash","noun","Silly talk or nonsense.","He said the dog did his homework, which was pure balderdash.","","Balderdash sounds like “bald and dash”: silly nonsense."],["renaissance","REN-uh-sahns","noun","A rebirth or fresh burst of interest in something.","Board games are having a renaissance in our house.","From French renaissance, “rebirth.”","Renaissance means “rebirth” in French: something comes back to life."],["bauble","BAW-buhl","noun","A small, cheap, shiny decoration or trinket.","The baby grabbed a shiny bauble off the Christmas tree.","","Bauble sounds like “bubble”: shiny, small and not worth much."],["disarray","dis-uh-RAY","noun","A messy, untidy state.","After the sleepover, the living room was in total disarray.","","Disarray is the opposite of array, a neat, orderly row."],["untoward","uhn-TORD","adjective","Unexpected and not wanted or proper.","The move to the new house went smoothly with nothing untoward.","","Untoward: not going toward where you wanted."],["nimbus","NIM-buhs","noun","A rain cloud, or a glow around something.","On the foggy night, a soft nimbus of light glowed around each street lamp.","From Latin nimbus, “cloud.”","Nimbus sounds like “nimble bus” floating up into a cloud."],["eddy","ED-ee","noun","A small swirl of water or air.","Leaves spun in a little eddy in the gutter after the rain.","","Eddy, like a little friend named Eddie, spinning in circles."],["beguile","bih-GYL","verb","To charm or delight, sometimes in a tricky way.","The puppy beguiled us with his big brown eyes.","","Beguile has “guile,” sneaky charm."],["catharsis","kuh-THAR-sis","noun","A feeling of relief after letting out strong feelings.","After a hard day, a good cry in the car gave her a sense of catharsis.","From Greek katharsis, “cleansing.”","Catharsis sounds like “cart-harsh-is”: you cart the harsh feelings away."],["conduit","KON-doo-it","noun","A channel or path that something flows through.","The family group chat is a conduit for news and baby photos.","","Conduit sounds like “conduct it”: it carries things along."],["boondoggle","BOON-dog-uhl","noun","A project that wastes time and money.","The fancy fountain that never worked was a real boondoggle.","","Boondoggle sounds like “boondoggy”: a silly, costly waste."],["jinx","JINGKS","noun","Something thought to bring bad luck.","Don’t say we’ll win; you’ll jinx it!","","Jinx: say it out loud, and the good luck slips away."],["adroit","uh-DROYT","adjective","Clever and skillful, especially with hands or words.","The adroit waiter carried six plates without spilling a thing.","From French à droit, “rightly.”","Adroit has “droit,” French for right: right-handed and skillful."],["breezy","BREE-zee","adjective","Windy and fresh; also light and relaxed in manner.","She waved with a breezy smile and said, “See you later!”","","Breezy has “breeze”: light and easy like a soft wind."],["fresco","FRES-koh","noun","A painting made on wet plaster on a wall.","The restaurant has a fresco of a sunny beach painted on its wall.","From Italian fresco, “fresh.”","Fresco means fresh: painted on fresh, wet plaster."],["brandish","BRAN-dish","verb","To wave something about in a showy way.","Grandma brandished her wooden spoon and called us to dinner.","","Brandish sounds like “brand new dish”: waving it to show off."],["vociferous","voh-SIF-er-uhs","adjective","Loud and forceful in speaking out.","The fans were vociferous in their cheering.","From Latin vox, “voice,” and ferre, “to carry.”","Voc means voice, like vocal: loud with your voice."],["trove","TROHV","noun","A collection of valuable or delightful things.","The attic was a trove of old photos and letters.","","Trove: think of “treasure trove,” a stash of good stuff."],["obviate","OB-vee-ayt","verb","To remove the need for something.","A good map app obviates the need to ask for directions.","","Obviate sounds like obvious: it obviously isn’t needed anymore."],["lithe","LYTH","adjective","Bending easily and gracefully.","The lithe cat jumped onto the top of the fridge.","","Lithe sounds like “light”: light, bendy and graceful."],["ratify","RAT-uh-fy","verb","To give official approval to something.","The parents voted to ratify the new school dress code.","From Latin ratus, “fixed, settled.”","Ratify sounds like “rate it fine”: you give it an official okay."],["convivial","kuhn-VIV-ee-uhl","adjective","Friendly, lively and fun to be with.","The convivial dinner with friends went late into the night.","From Latin convivium, “a feast.”","Viv means life, like vivid. A party full of life."],["tranquil","TRANG-kwil","adjective","Calm, quiet and peaceful.","We spent a tranquil afternoon reading on the porch.","From Latin tranquillus, “calm.”","Tranquil sounds like “tranquilizer”: calm and quiet."],["deluge","DEL-yooj","noun","A great flood, or a huge amount of something.","After his birthday post, he got a deluge of kind messages.","","Deluge sounds like “del-huge”: a huge flood of water or anything."],["distraught","dih-STRAWT","adjective","Very upset and worried.","The distraught mom found her son at the lost and found.","","Distraught sounds like “distressed, fraught”: very upset."],["dishearten","dis-HAR-tn","verb","To make someone lose hope or courage.","Don’t let one bad grade dishearten you.","","Dishearten: it takes the heart out of you."],["dawdle","DAW-dl","verb","To be slow and take your time.","The kids dawdled on the way home from school.","","Dawdle sounds like doodle: slow and drifting along."],["raucous","RAW-kuhs","adjective","Loud and rough in a noisy way.","Raucous cheers filled the gym when the team won.","From Latin raucus, “hoarse.”","Raucous sounds like “ruckus”: loud and rowdy."],["conjecture","kuhn-JEK-cher","noun","A guess made without full proof.","Why the dog barks at nothing is pure conjecture.","","Conjecture: your guess is “jected,” or thrown, out there."],["tenet","TEN-it","noun","A main belief or rule that a group holds.","A key tenet of our family is to always say thank you.","From Latin tenet, “he holds.”","Tenet sounds like “ten,” like the Ten Commandments: a key rule."],["medley","MED-lee","noun","A mix of different things, like songs.","At the wedding, the band played a medley of old favorite songs.","","A medley is a mix, like a meddle of many songs."],["amiss","uh-MIS","adjective","Wrong or not as it should be.","She knew something was amiss when the dog did not run to the door.","","Amiss has “miss”: something is missing or wrong."],["largesse","lar-ZHES","noun","Generosity in giving.","Thanks to Grandma’s largesse, every grandchild got a new bike.","From French largesse, “generosity.”","Largesse has “large” inside: big, generous giving."],["harangue","huh-RANG","noun","A long, loud speech scolding someone.","The coach gave the team a long harangue after they lost.","","Harangue sounds like “hair-hang”: a scolding so long your hair hangs down."],["dialect","DY-uh-lekt","noun","A way of speaking a language in one area.","Grandma’s dialect has words we’ve never heard.","From Greek dialektos, “way of speaking.”","Dialect sounds like dialogue: how people talk in one area."],["gloat","GLOHT","verb","To show off about your own success.","My brother gloated for days after he beat me at checkers.","","Gloat sounds like “goat”: a goat bragging at the top of the hill."],["glisten","GLIS-uhn","verb","To shine softly, like something wet.","The wet road glistened under the street lights.","","Glisten sounds like “glitter and listen”: shining softly."],["decrepit","dih-KREP-it","adjective","Old and worn out.","The decrepit old car barely made it up the hill.","From Latin decrepitus, “very old.”","Decrepit sounds like “decayed”: old and falling apart."],["bequeath","bih-KWEETH","verb","To leave something to someone, often in a will; to pass on.","Grandpa bequeathed his watch to me in his will.","","Bequeath sounds like “be-keep”: things you leave for others to keep."],["forlorn","for-LORN","adjective","Sad and lonely.","The forlorn puppy sat by the door, waiting for its family.","","Forlorn sounds like “for-lone”: sad and all alone."],["gambol","GAM-buhl","verb","To run and jump playfully.","The puppies gamboled around the backyard.","","Gambol sounds like “gamble” around: hop and leap with no plan."],["monolith","MON-uh-lith","noun","A single huge block of stone.","The new office tower stood like a giant glass monolith downtown.","From Greek monos, “one,” and lithos, “stone.”","Mono means one, and lith means stone: one big rock."],["ominous","OM-uh-nuhs","adjective","Seeming to warn that something bad is coming.","Ominous clouds gathered before the storm.","From Latin omen, “sign.”","Ominous comes from omen: a sign that trouble is coming."],["obtrusive","uhb-TROO-siv","adjective","Too noticeable in an unwelcome way.","The bright flashing sign was obtrusive on the quiet street.","","Obtrusive sounds like intrusive: pushing in where it isn’t wanted."],["regale","ri-GAYL","verb","To entertain someone with stories or good food.","Grandpa regaled us with stories about his first car.","From French régaler, “to treat to a feast.”","Regale sounds like “royal”: you treat guests like kings."],["knack","NAK","noun","A natural skill.","Dad has a knack for fixing things around the house.","","Knack sounds like knock: you knock it out every time."],["devise","dih-VYZ","verb","To come up with a plan or idea.","The kids devised a game with only a ball and a box.","","Devise sounds like device: you invent a clever plan."],["disparage","dih-SPAR-ij","verb","To speak badly of someone or something.","Never disparage someone for trying their best.","","Disparage: you pull someone down, below par."],["usurp","yoo-SURP","verb","To take someone’s place or power without the right to.","The new kitten usurped the old cat’s favorite chair.","From Latin usurpare, “to seize for use.”","Usurp sounds like “you slurp”: you slurp up what is not yours."],["ostracize","OS-truh-syz","verb","To leave someone out of a group on purpose.","Our class agreed never to ostracize the new kid.","From Greek ostrakon, “piece of pottery.”","Ostracize sounds like ostrich: hiding someone away in the sand."],["mimic","MIM-ik","verb","To copy how someone acts or sounds.","My little brother likes to mimic everything I say.","From Greek mimos, “actor.”","Mimic sounds like mime, someone who copies actions."],["prevaricate","prih-VAIR-ih-kayt","verb","To avoid telling the whole truth.","Don’t prevaricate; just tell me who ate the cookies.","From Latin praevaricari, “to walk crookedly.”","Prevaricate sounds like “pre-vary”: varying the story."],["cavalcade","KAV-uhl-kayd","noun","A long parade or line of riders or cars.","A cavalcade of cars with balloons honked down the street after the wedding.","","Cavalcade sounds like cavalry: a long line of riders."],["limber","LIM-ber","adjective","Able to bend and move easily.","Stretching every morning keeps Grandma limber.","","Limber sounds like “limbs”: loose, bendy arms and legs."],["loam","LOHM","noun","Rich, crumbly soil.","We bought rich loam for the backyard vegetable garden.","","Loam sounds like “home” for plants: rich, crumbly dirt."],["plausible","PLAW-zuh-buhl","adjective","Seeming likely to be true.","“The wind blew my homework away” was a plausible excuse.","","Plausible sounds like applause-ible: believable enough to clap for."],["nonagenarian","non-uh-juh-NAIR-ee-uhn","noun","A person in their nineties.","My great-grandma is a nonagenarian who still bakes bread every week.","From Latin nonageni, “ninety each.”","Nona sounds like nine: someone in their nineties."],["detour","DEE-toor","noun","A longer way taken to avoid something.","The road work forced a detour, and we found a great bakery.","From French détour, “a turning aside.”","Detour has tour in it: a side trip around the block."],["ossify","OS-uh-fy","verb","To become hard and unable to change.","Without new ideas, old habits can ossify.","From Latin os, “bone.”","Os means bone: to harden like bone."],["frolic","FROL-ik","verb","To play and run around happily.","The kids frolicked in the sprinkler all afternoon.","From Dutch vrolijk, “cheerful.”","Frolic sounds like “frog-lick”: hopping around just for fun."],["utilitarian","yoo-til-i-TAIR-ee-uhn","adjective","Made to be useful rather than pretty.","Her kitchen is utilitarian, with plain pots and no fancy gadgets.","From Latin utilis, “useful.”","Utilitarian has “utility,” like a utility knife: useful, not pretty."],["cynical","SIN-i-kuhl","adjective","Believing people act only for selfish reasons.","After being lied to by a car salesman, he became cynical about all of them.","","Cynical sounds like “sin-ical”: you think everyone is up to something."],["translucent","trans-LOO-suhnt","adjective","Letting some light through, but not fully clear.","The translucent shower curtain let in soft light.","From Latin trans, “through,” and lucere, “to shine.”","Trans means through, and lucent means light: some light comes through."],["assiduous","uh-SIJ-oo-uhs","adjective","Working with steady care and effort.","His assiduous studying every night helped him pass his driving test.","From Latin assiduus, “sitting down to.”","Assiduous sounds like “a sitting-down-us”: sitting down to work hard."],["deem","DEEM","verb","To judge or consider something in a certain way.","The cook deemed the soup ready.","","Deem rhymes with team: the team judges and decides."],["plucky","PLUK-ee","adjective","Brave and full of spirit.","The plucky little team never gave up, even when losing.","","Plucky: you have the pluck, the nerve, to keep going."],["immaculate","ih-MAK-yuh-lit","adjective","Perfectly clean and neat.","After the party, Mom left the kitchen immaculate.","From Latin immaculatus, “unstained.”","Immaculate: im means not, macula means stain: not one spot."],["portent","POR-tent","noun","A sign of something about to happen.","The long line at the store was a portent of a busy holiday.","","Portent sounds like “point at it”: a sign of what’s ahead."],["potluck","POT-luk","noun","A meal where each guest brings a dish to share.","The neighborhood potluck had twelve kinds of salad.","","Pot luck: whatever luck brings to your pot."],["nexus","NEK-suhs","noun","A central link or meeting point.","The library is the nexus of our small town, where everyone meets.","From Latin nexus, “a binding.”","Nexus sounds like “next to us”: where things connect."],["hindsight","HYND-syt","noun","Understanding something only after it happens.","In hindsight, I should have brought an umbrella.","","Hindsight: sight from behind, seeing clearly after the fact."],["trellis","TREL-is","noun","A frame of crossed strips for plants to climb on.","Our neighbor grows tomato vines on a wooden trellis.","","Trellis sounds like “trestle”: a frame that holds things up."],["feckless","FEK-lis","adjective","Lacking skill or energy; not able to get things done.","The feckless babysitter let the kids eat cookies for dinner.","","Feckless: has less sense and less effort than needed."],["brimming","BRIM-ing","adjective","Full to the top.","Her eyes were brimming with happy tears at the wedding.","","Brimming: full to the brim, like a cup."],["behemoth","bih-HEE-muhth","noun","Something huge and powerful.","The new fridge was a behemoth that barely fit through the door.","","Behemoth sounds like “be huge moth”: a giant thing."],["acme","AK-mee","noun","The highest point of something.","Winning the spelling bee was the acme of her school year.","From Greek akme, “the highest point.”","Acme sounds like “ack, me!”: shouted from the very top of the hill."],["exquisite","ek-SKWIZ-it","adjective","Very beautiful and delicate.","The wedding cake was exquisite, with tiny sugar roses on top.","From Latin exquisitus, “sought out.”","Exquisite comes from Latin for “sought out”: so lovely everyone wants it."],["undulate","UHN-juh-layt","verb","To move in smooth waves.","The tall grass by the road undulated in the breeze.","From Latin unda, “wave.”","Undulate sounds like “under-wave”: rolling up and down like waves."],["burgeon","BUR-juhn","verb","To grow or spread quickly.","Her small online shop has burgeoned into a real business.","","Burgeon sounds like “burger-grow-on”: growing fast."],["gauche","GOHSH","adjective","Awkward and clumsy in social settings.","It felt gauche to check my phone during the wedding toast.","From French gauche, “left.”","Gauche sounds like “gosh!”: an awkward moment that makes you blush."],["cornucopia","kor-nuh-KOH-pee-uh","noun","A large, rich supply of good things.","The grocery store was a cornucopia of fruit and vegetables.","From Latin cornu copiae, “horn of plenty.”","Cornucopia means horn of plenty: a horn spilling with food."],["denouement","day-noo-MAHN","noun","The final part of a story where things are wrapped up.","In the denouement of the movie, everyone finally makes up and goes home.","From French dénouer, “to untie.”","Denouement is French for “untying”: the knots of the story come undone."],["demeanor","dih-MEE-ner","noun","The way a person looks and acts.","Her calm demeanor made the nervous kids feel safe.","","Demeanor sounds like “the manner”: how you carry yourself."],["hurtle","HUR-tl","verb","To move very fast, often out of control.","The shopping cart hurtled down the parking lot hill.","","Hurtle sounds like “hurl”: flying fast like something thrown."],["holistic","hoh-LIS-tik","adjective","Looking at the whole thing, not just the parts.","The doctor took a holistic look at my sleep, food and stress.","From Greek holos, “whole.”","Holistic sounds like “whole-istic”: looking at the whole thing."],["pervasive","per-VAY-siv","adjective","Spread everywhere.","The pervasive smell of popcorn filled the whole movie theater.","From Latin pervadere, “to go through.”","Pervasive sounds like “pervade”: it spreads everywhere."],["burble","BUR-buhl","verb","To make a soft bubbling sound, or to talk on happily.","The coffee pot burbled on the counter every morning.","","Burble sounds like “bubble”: soft bubbling sounds."],["mystify","MIS-tuh-fy","verb","To puzzle someone completely.","The magic trick mystified the whole family.","","Mystify has “mystery” in it: it leaves you puzzled."],["bedrock","BED-rok","noun","The solid rock under soil; also the strong base of something.","Trust is the bedrock of any good friendship.","","Bedrock: the rock bed that everything sits on."],["mercurial","mer-KYOOR-ee-uhl","adjective","Changing mood quickly and often.","My toddler is mercurial: happy one minute, crying the next.","","Mercury in a thermometer jumps up and down quickly."],["charisma","kuh-RIZ-muh","noun","A special charm that draws people to someone.","Our new manager has such charisma that everyone wants to work with her.","From Greek charisma, “gift of grace.”","Charisma sounds like “charm-isma”: a charm that pulls people in."],["camouflage","KAM-uh-flahzh","noun","Colors or covers that help something blend in.","The soldier’s green and brown clothes worked as camouflage in the woods.","From French camoufler, “to disguise.”","Camouflage sounds like “came-a-flash”: you came, but nobody saw you."],["coy","KOY","adjective","Shy in a playful way.","She gave a coy smile when asked who sent her the flowers.","","Coy sounds like “koi”: a shy fish that hides, then peeks out."],["autumnal","aw-TUHM-nuhl","adjective","Having to do with autumn, or like autumn.","The kitchen smelled autumnal, full of cinnamon and baked apples.","","Autumnal has “autumn” inside: feeling like fall."],["imminent","IM-uh-nuhnt","adjective","About to happen.","Dark clouds told us rain was imminent.","From Latin imminere, “to hang over.”","Imminent sounds like “in a minute”: about to happen."],["deter","dih-TUR","verb","To discourage someone from doing something.","The barking dog deters people from walking into our yard.","From Latin deterrere, “to frighten off.”","Deter: you put up a “de-terror” to scare them off."],["aura","AWR-uh","noun","A special feeling or quality that seems to surround a person or place.","The old library has an aura of calm the moment you walk in.","From Greek aura, “a breeze.”","Aura sounds like “aurora”: a soft glow around someone."],["birdsong","BURD-sawng","noun","The musical sounds birds make.","We woke up to birdsong outside the bedroom window.","","Birdsong: a bird’s song, just as it sounds."],["tutelage","TOOT-l-ij","noun","Teaching and guidance from someone.","Under her aunt’s tutelage, she learned to bake bread.","From Latin tutela, “protection.”","Tutelage sounds like “tutor”: being taught by a tutor."],["doyen","DOY-uhn","noun","The most respected or senior person in a group.","She is the doyen of the hospital nurses, there for forty years.","From French doyen, “dean.”","Doyen is related to dean: the head of the group."],["facsimile","fak-SIM-uh-lee","noun","An exact copy.","The museum shop sells a facsimile of the famous old map.","From Latin fac simile, “make similar.”","Facsimile: fac means make, simile means similar: a copy."],["bravura","bruh-VYOOR-uh","noun","Great skill and flair, especially in a performance.","The pianist at the school concert played with dazzling bravura.","From Italian bravura, “bravery.”","Bravura sounds like “bravo”: so skilled you shout bravo."],["quizzical","KWIZ-i-kuhl","adjective","Showing that you are puzzled or curious.","The puppy gave me a quizzical look when I sneezed.","","Quizzical starts like “quiz”: a look that asks a question."],["bide","BYD","verb","To wait patiently, as in “bide your time” before acting.","She bided her time until the cookies cooled, then grabbed two.","","Bide sounds like “abide”: stay and wait."],["canopy","KAN-uh-pee","noun","A cover overhead, like treetops forming a roof.","We parked under a canopy of trees to keep the car cool.","","A canopy bed has a cover on top: a roof above you."],["notoriety","noh-tuh-RY-ih-tee","noun","Being well known, often for something bad.","The goat gained notoriety for eating the neighbor’s laundry.","","Notoriety sounds like noted: noted by all, often for trouble."],["palaver","puh-LAV-er","noun","A lot of fuss and talk over nothing much.","There was a big palaver about who sits where at dinner.","From Portuguese palavra, “word.”","Palaver sounds like “blabber”: lots of fussy talk."],["bloviate","BLOH-vee-ayt","verb","To talk a lot in a puffed-up, empty way.","Uncle Joe tends to bloviate about his golf game at every dinner.","","Bloviate sounds like “blow”: blowing a lot of hot air."],["purview","PUR-vyoo","noun","The area that someone is in charge of.","Weekend pancakes are in Dad’s purview.","","Purview has view: everything in your view to care for."],["wherewithal","WAIR-with-awl","noun","The money, means or skill needed to do something.","We finally had the wherewithal to fix the leaky roof.","","Wherewithal: where with all you need to do it."],["communal","kuh-MYOON-l","adjective","Shared by a whole group.","Our office has a communal fridge that everyone shares.","","Communal sounds like community: shared by all."],["doppelganger","DOP-uhl-gang-er","noun","Someone who looks exactly like another person.","I met my doppelganger at the grocery store, and we both laughed.","From German Doppelgänger, “double-goer.”","Doppelganger is German for “double-goer”: your double."],["consternation","kon-ster-NAY-shuhn","noun","Worry and alarm, often sudden.","To our consternation, the dog ate the birthday cake.","","Consternation sounds like “constant nation”: a whole nation in alarm."],["omen","OH-muhn","noun","A sign that something good or bad will happen.","A rainbow on moving day felt like a good omen.","From Latin omen.","Omen sounds like “Oh, men, look!”: a sign of what’s coming."],["swelter","SWEL-ter","verb","To feel uncomfortably hot and sweaty.","We sweltered in the car until the air conditioning kicked in.","","Swelter sounds like “sweat-er”: so hot you sweat."],["stupendous","stoo-PEN-duhs","adjective","Amazingly great or large.","The view from the top of the tall building was stupendous.","From Latin stupere, “to be amazed.”","Stupendous sounds like “super-tremendous”: amazingly great."],["joust","JOWST","verb","To fight on horseback with long poles, or to compete playfully.","My kids jousted with foam pool noodles in the backyard.","","Joust sounds like “just us”: two knights facing off."],["chivalry","SHIV-uhl-ree","noun","Being polite, kind and brave, especially toward others who need help.","He showed chivalry by giving his seat on the bus to an older woman.","From French chevalier, “knight.”","Chivalry comes from old French for knight: act like a kind knight."],["surfeit","SUR-fit","noun","Too much of something.","After the party, we had a surfeit of cupcakes.","","Surfeit sounds like “surf it”: a whole wave of too much."],["interlude","IN-ter-lood","noun","A short break between parts of something.","There was a short musical interlude between the speeches.","From Latin inter, “between,” and ludus, “play.”","Interlude: inter means between, like intermission."],["pristine","pris-TEEN","adjective","Clean, fresh and like new.","The new white sneakers were pristine for exactly one day.","From Latin pristinus, “former.”","Pristine sounds like “pressed and clean”: like new."],["recompense","REK-uhm-pens","noun","Payment or reward for work or for a loss.","As recompense for raking the leaves, we got hot apple pie.","","Recompense has “compensate” in it: being paid back."],["pernicious","per-NISH-uhs","adjective","Harmful in a slow, quiet way.","Too much screen time can have a pernicious effect on sleep.","","Pernicious sounds like “pretty vicious”: quiet harm."],["qualm","KWAHM","noun","A small, uneasy feeling of doubt.","I had no qualms about lending my neighbor my ladder.","","Qualm sounds like “calm”: a qualm is when you are not calm inside."],["nebulous","NEB-yuh-luhs","adjective","Vague and not clear.","Our weekend plans are still nebulous, so I can’t say yes yet.","From Latin nebula, “mist.”","Nebulous comes from nebula, a cloud: cloudy and unclear."],["resplendent","ri-SPLEN-duhnt","adjective","Shining and splendid to look at.","The bride looked resplendent in her sparkly dress.","From Latin resplendere, “to shine brightly.”","Resplendent has “splendid” inside: shining and splendid."],["zenith","ZEE-nith","noun","The highest point of something.","At its zenith, the sun was right above our heads.","","Zenith: picture the sun at noon, right on top of your head."],["gusto","GUS-toh","noun","Great enjoyment and energy.","The kids ate the pizza with gusto.","From Italian gusto, “taste.”","Gusto sounds like “gust”: a big blast of energy and joy."],["nitpick","NIT-pik","verb","To fuss about tiny, unimportant faults.","Please don’t nitpick my drawing; I’m still learning.","","Picking out tiny nits, like checking every hair for bugs."],["amenity","uh-MEN-i-tee","noun","Something that makes a place comfortable or pleasant.","The hotel’s best amenity was a free breakfast every morning.","From Latin amoenus, “pleasant.”","Amenity sounds like “a many treaty”: extras that make life nice."],["ingenious","in-JEEN-yuhs","adjective","Very clever and inventive.","My son came up with an ingenious way to keep the cat off the couch.","From Latin ingenium, “talent.”","Ingenious sounds like “genius”: very clever."],["segue","SEG-way","noun","A smooth move from one topic or part to the next.","Talking about pie made a nice segue into dessert.","From Italian segue, “it follows.”","Segue sounds like “Segway”: a smooth glide to the next topic."],["rhapsody","RAP-suh-dee","noun","A burst of joyful feeling, or a free and emotional piece of music.","Her text was a rhapsody about how good the new pizza place was.","From Greek rhapsōidia, from rhaptein, “to stitch,” and ōidē, “song.”","Rhapsody sounds like “rap song”: words that gush with feeling."],["inimitable","ih-NIM-i-tuh-buhl","adjective","So special it cannot be copied.","Grandma’s inimitable apple pie cannot be copied.","","Inimitable: in means not, imitate means copy: no one can copy it."],["aficionado","uh-fish-ee-uh-NAH-doh","noun","A person who knows a lot about and loves a hobby or subject.","My aunt is a coffee aficionado and grinds fresh beans every morning.","From Spanish aficionado, “fond of.”","Aficionado sounds like “a fish in a donut”: a big fan of something."],["dabble","DAB-uhl","verb","To try something a little, without going deep.","She dabbles in baking on lazy Sundays.","","Dabble: like a duck that dips just its beak in the water."],["rollicking","ROL-i-king","adjective","Full of lively, noisy fun.","We had a rollicking time at the family picnic.","","Rollicking sounds like “rolling”: rolling around laughing."],["feral","FEER-uhl","adjective","Wild, especially an animal that was once tame.","A feral cat lives under our porch and runs if you get close.","From Latin fera, “wild animal.”","Feral sounds like “fear all”: a wild animal scared of people."],["palindrome","PAL-in-drohm","noun","A word or phrase that reads the same backward and forward.","“Racecar” is a palindrome; read it backward and it’s the same.","From Greek palin, “again,” and dromos, “running.”","A palindrome runs back and forth, like a racecar on a track."],["dismantle","dis-MAN-tl","verb","To take something apart piece by piece.","We dismantled the crib when the baby got a big-kid bed.","","Dismantle: like taking down a tent, pole by pole."],["wayfarer","WAY-fair-er","noun","A person who travels on foot.","The roadside diner welcomed tired wayfarers with hot coffee.","","Wayfarer: someone who fares, or travels, along the way."],["wont","WAWNT","noun","A habit or usual way of doing things.","As is his wont, Dad hummed while cooking.","","Wont sounds like “want”: what you always want to do, your habit."],["carousel","kar-uh-SEL","noun","A merry-go-round, or a belt that goes around in a circle.","We waited at the airport carousel for our bags to come around.","","Carousel sounds like “carry-sell”: horses carried round and round."],["bristle","BRIS-uhl","verb","To show sudden anger or offense; also to stand up stiffly, like hair.","The cat’s fur bristled when it saw the dog.","","A bristle brush has stiff hairs standing up."],["unwieldy","uhn-WEEL-dee","adjective","Too big or awkward to carry or handle easily.","The unwieldy couch would not fit through the door.","","Unwieldy: hard to wield, or hold and handle."],["chimera","ky-MEER-uh","noun","A wild dream or idea that can never come true.","A clean house with three kids is a lovely chimera.","From Greek chimaira, a mythical monster.","Chimera sounds like “Kim-era”: an era that only lives in dreams."],["arcane","ar-KAYN","adjective","Known or understood by only a few people.","He loves arcane trivia, like what year the first toaster was sold.","From Latin arcanus, “secret.”","Arcane sounds like “arcade secret”: hidden facts few people know."],["unfathomable","uhn-FATH-uh-muh-buhl","adjective","Too deep or strange to understand.","The cost of a new house today feels unfathomable.","","Unfathomable: a fathom measures deep water, so this is too deep to measure."],["ululate","UHL-yuh-layt","verb","To make a long, high, wavering cry.","At the wedding, the aunties ululated with joy as the couple walked in.","From Latin ululare, “to howl.”","Ululate sounds like what it is: “lu-lu-lu-lu,” a long, high cry."],["castigate","KAS-ti-gayt","verb","To criticize someone harshly.","The boss castigated him in front of everyone for missing the deadline.","From Latin castigare, “to correct, punish.”","Castigate sounds like “cast at the gate”: thrown out with harsh words."],["profound","pruh-FOUND","adjective","Very deep in meaning or feeling.","Her kind words had a profound effect on me.","From Latin profundus, “deep.”","Profound sounds like “pro-found”: found very deep down."],["venerable","VEN-er-uh-buhl","adjective","Respected because of age and wisdom.","The venerable family doctor had cared for three generations.","From Latin venerari, “to honor.”","Venerable sounds like “veteran”: old, wise and honored."],["retrospect","RET-ruh-spekt","noun","A look back at what has already happened.","In retrospect, leaving early to beat traffic was a wise choice.","From Latin retro, “back,” and specere, “to look.”","Retro means back, and spect means look: looking back."],["mores","MOR-ayz","noun","The customs and manners of a group.","In our house, the mores at dinner are no phones and always say thank you.","From Latin mores, “customs.”","Mores (MOR-ayz) are the manners a group lives by."],["squelch","SKWELCH","verb","To make a wet, sucking sound, or to stop something.","Our boots squelched in the muddy yard.","","Squelch sounds just like wet boots stepping in deep mud."],["ocher","OH-ker","noun","A warm earthy yellow or orange-brown color.","We painted the living room a warm ocher, like mustard.","From Greek okhros, “pale yellow.”","Ocher sounds like “oak-er”: a warm, earthy brown-yellow."],["curio","KYOOR-ee-oh","noun","A small, unusual object people collect.","Her shelf is full of curios she picked up on trips.","","Curio is short for curiosity: an odd little thing you can’t stop looking at."],["ungainly","uhn-GAYN-lee","adjective","Awkward and clumsy in how you move.","The baby took a few ungainly steps across the kitchen.","","Ungainly sounds like “un-graceful”: clumsy and awkward."],["hue","HYOO","noun","A color or shade.","She painted the bedroom a soft blue hue.","","Hue sounds like “who”: who picked this paint color."],["maladroit","mal-uh-DROYT","adjective","Clumsy or awkward in how you act.","His maladroit joke made the room go quiet, but he laughed it off.","From French mal, “badly,” and adroit, “skillful.”","Mal means bad; maladroit means bad at moving smoothly."],["archetype","AR-ki-typ","noun","A perfect or typical example of something.","Our school nurse is the archetype of a caring helper.","From Greek archetypon, “a pattern.”","Arch means first, type means model: the first, perfect model."],["apiary","AY-pee-er-ee","noun","A place where bees are kept.","Our neighbor’s apiary gives us jars of honey every fall.","From Latin apis, “bee.”","Apis is Latin for bee: a place for bees."],["bygone","BY-gawn","adjective","Belonging to the past.","The old diner reminds Grandpa of a bygone time.","","Bygone: gone by, in the past."],["dilapidated","dih-LAP-i-day-tid","adjective","Old and falling apart.","They fixed up the dilapidated house together.","","Dilapidated has lapis, Latin for stone: the stones fall apart."],["ostentatious","os-ten-TAY-shuhs","adjective","Showy in a way meant to impress.","The ostentatious car had gold wheels and a loud horn.","From Latin ostentare, “to display.”","Ostentatious sounds like “show-ten-tatious”: showing off."],["deference","DEF-er-uhns","noun","Polite respect for someone’s wishes.","In deference to the sleeping baby, we spoke softly.","","Deference sounds like defer: you let someone else go first."],["amulet","AM-yuh-lit","noun","A small charm worn for good luck.","She wears a small amulet on a chain that her grandma gave her for luck.","","Picture an amulet as a little lucky lock hanging from your neck."],["waggish","WAG-ish","adjective","Playful and joking.","Our waggish mail carrier always has a joke ready.","","Waggish sounds like “wag”: a joker, like a happy wagging tail."],["patter","PAT-er","noun","Quick, light sounds, or fast smooth talk.","We fell asleep to the patter of rain on the roof.","","Pitter-patter, like little feet or raindrops."],["paramount","PAIR-uh-mount","adjective","More important than anything else.","Safety is paramount when kids ride bikes in the street.","","Paramount sounds like “para-mountain”: on top of the mountain."],["domicile","DOM-uh-syl","noun","A home.","Their cozy domicile sat at the end of the street.","From Latin domus, “house.”","Domicile is related to domestic: your home."],["canny","KAN-ee","adjective","Smart and careful, especially with money or plans.","The canny shopper waited for the big sale to buy a new TV.","","Canny: someone who “can” figure out the smart move."],["eloquent","EL-uh-kwuhnt","adjective","Able to speak or write in a clear, moving way.","The student gave an eloquent speech that made the parents cry.","From Latin eloqui, “to speak out.”","Eloquent has “loqu,” Latin for speak: someone who speaks well."],["impetus","IM-pi-tuhs","noun","A push or reason that makes something happen.","A doctor’s warning was the impetus for Dad to start walking daily.","From Latin impetus, “attack, force.”","Impetus sounds like “impulse”: the push that gets things going."],["orotund","OR-uh-tund","adjective","Full and rich in sound, sometimes too grand.","The radio host had an orotund voice that filled the room.","From Latin ore rotundo, “with round mouth.”","Oro means mouth, and rotund means round: a big round voice."],["bittersweet","BIT-er-sweet","adjective","Happy and sad at the same time.","Dropping my son off at college was bittersweet for me.","","Bitter plus sweet: sad and happy mixed."],["rigmarole","RIG-muh-rohl","noun","A long, fussy, confusing process.","Getting a new driver’s license was quite a rigmarole.","","Rigmarole sounds like “rig, more, roll”: step after step after step."],["operetta","op-uh-RET-uh","noun","A short, light, funny opera.","The school put on a cheerful operetta in the spring.","From Italian operetta, “little work.”","An operetta is a little opera, short and funny."],["cogitate","KOJ-i-tayt","verb","To think deeply about something.","He sat at the kitchen table to cogitate over which job to take.","From Latin cogitare, “to think.”","Cogitate: the cogs in your head turn as you think."],["peruse","puh-ROOZ","verb","To read or look over, often slowly.","I like to peruse the cookbook on lazy Sundays.","","Peruse sounds like “browse”: look it over slowly."],["blarney","BLAR-nee","noun","Charming talk that flatters or stretches the truth.","Don’t believe his blarney about catching a fish this big.","","Blarney is named for an Irish stone said to give the gift of gab."],["prodigy","PROD-ih-jee","noun","A young person with amazing talent.","The piano prodigy played for the whole town at age seven.","","Prodigy sounds like “prod-a-genius”: a young genius."],["vulpine","VUL-pyn","adjective","Like a fox, sly and clever.","The cat had a vulpine grin after stealing the ham.","From Latin vulpes, “fox.”","Vulpes is Latin for fox: sly as a fox."],["riposte","ri-POHST","noun","A quick, clever reply.","When he teased her cooking, her riposte made everyone laugh.","From Italian risposta, “an answer.”","Riposte sounds like “rip post”: a quick reply you rip right back."],["keystone","KEE-stohn","noun","The top stone of an arch that holds it together, or the key part of something.","Grandma is the keystone that holds our family together.","","Keystone: the key stone that holds up the arch."],["reticent","RET-uh-suhnt","adjective","Quiet and slow to share your thoughts.","He was reticent at first but opened up over lunch.","From Latin reticere, “to keep silent.”","Reticent sounds like “resisting”: resisting the urge to talk."],["pittance","PIT-ns","noun","A very small amount of money.","He worked for a pittance but loved the job.","","Pittance sounds like “pity-ance”: a pitiful amount of money."],["dregs","DREGZ","noun","The last bits left at the bottom of a drink.","He drank the coffee down to the dregs.","","Dregs sounds like drags: the bits that drag at the bottom of the cup."],["consensus","kuhn-SEN-suhs","noun","An agreement shared by most of a group.","The family reached a consensus: pizza on Friday night.","From Latin consentire, “to agree.”","Consensus: everyone has the same sense."],["bower","BOW-er","noun","A shady, leafy spot in a garden.","We ate lunch in the shady bower behind the restaurant, under the vines.","","Bower sounds like “flower bower”: a leafy, shady nook."],["gamut","GAM-uht","noun","The whole range of something.","Her feelings ran the gamut from happy to sad during the movie.","","Gamut sounds like “game, A to Z”: the whole range."],["gestalt","guh-SHTAHLT","noun","A whole that is more than the sum of its parts.","The meal’s gestalt was perfect, even if each dish was simple.","From German Gestalt, “shape, form.”","Gestalt sounds like “guest salt”: the whole meal tastes better than each bite."],["tousled","TOW-zuhld","adjective","Messy and rumpled, like hair after sleep.","He came to breakfast with tousled hair and a big yawn.","","Tousled sounds like “tussle”: hair that had a tussle with the pillow."],["tenuous","TEN-yoo-uhs","adjective","Very thin or weak.","We had only a tenuous phone signal on the country road.","From Latin tenuis, “thin.”","Tenuous sounds like “thin-uous”: thin and weak."],["boisterous","BOY-ster-uhs","adjective","Loud, lively and full of energy.","The boisterous kids raced around the backyard, yelling and laughing.","","Boisterous sounds like “boy roosters”: loud and full of energy."],["constellation","kon-stuh-LAY-shuhn","noun","A group of stars that forms a shape.","From the backyard, Dad showed us a constellation, a group of stars shaped like a man with a belt.","From Latin stella, “star.”","Stella means star, like a stellar job. A group of stars."],["clarity","KLAIR-i-tee","noun","The quality of being clear and easy to understand.","A good night’s sleep gave her clarity about the decision.","From Latin clarus, “clear.”","Clarity sounds like clear: everything is clear."],["bode","BOHD","verb","To be a sign of what will happen.","Clear skies bode well for our picnic on Saturday.","","Bode sounds like “road”: shows where the road is going."],["procrastinate","proh-KRAS-tuh-nayt","verb","To put off doing something until later.","I procrastinated on my taxes, then did them in one night.","From Latin crastinus, “of tomorrow.”","Cras means tomorrow: always putting it off till tomorrow."],["erudite","ER-yoo-dyt","adjective","Having a lot of knowledge from reading and study.","Our erudite neighbor has read every book in her big bookshelf.","From Latin erudire, “to teach.”","Erudite sounds like “very read-ite”: someone who has read a lot."],["sinuous","SIN-yoo-uhs","adjective","Curving and twisting gracefully.","The sinuous road wound up the hill in big curves.","From Latin sinus, “a curve.”","Sinuous sounds like “snake-uous”: curving like a snake."],["countenance","KOUN-tn-uhns","noun","A person’s face or the look on it.","Her countenance brightened when she saw the puppy.","","Countenance: the face you can count on to show how you feel."],["intuition","in-too-ISH-uhn","noun","Knowing something without reasoning it out.","My intuition told me the dog had eaten my sandwich.","From Latin intueri, “to look at.”","Intuition: an inner tutor that whispers the answer."],["gossamer","GOS-uh-mer","adjective","Very light, thin and delicate.","The baby’s blanket was soft and gossamer.","From Middle English gossomer, “goose summer.”","Gossamer sounds like “gauzy summer”: thin and light as a breeze."],["arable","AIR-uh-buhl","adjective","Good for growing crops.","The farmer bought arable land where corn and beans could grow.","From Latin arare, “to plow.”","Arable sounds like “a-ROW-able”: land you can plant in rows."],["hapless","HAP-lis","adjective","Unlucky.","The hapless waiter dropped the tray twice in one night.","From Old Norse happ, “luck.”","Hapless: less hap, less luck."],["acquiesce","ak-wee-ES","verb","To accept or agree quietly without arguing.","I wanted pizza, but I acquiesced when everyone else chose tacos.","From Latin acquiescere, “to rest.”","Acquiesce has “quiet” in it: you quietly go along."],["balm","BAHM","noun","Something that soothes, heals or comforts.","Her kind words were a balm after my long, hard day.","","Balm sounds like “calm”: it soothes and heals."],["drowsy","DROU-zee","adjective","Sleepy.","The warm car ride made everyone drowsy.","","Drowsy sounds like “drowse-y”: your head droops."],["amber","AM-ber","noun","A warm golden-orange color; also a fossil tree resin.","The traffic light turned amber, so I slowed down.","From Arabic anbar.","Amber sounds like “ember”: the golden-orange of a glowing fire."],["whirligig","WUR-li-gig","noun","A toy or object that spins around.","A wooden whirligig spun in the wind on the porch.","","Whirligig has “whirl”: something that whirls around."],["laudable","LAW-duh-buhl","adjective","Worthy of praise.","Raising money for the food bank was a laudable effort.","From Latin laudare, “to praise.”","Laud means praise, like applaud: worth praising."],["approbation","ap-ruh-BAY-shuhn","noun","Approval or praise.","The chef nodded in approbation after tasting my soup.","From Latin approbare, “to approve.”","Approbation starts like “approve”: a nod of approval."],["despondent","dih-SPON-duhnt","adjective","Very sad and without hope.","He was despondent until his lost dog came home.","","Despondent sounds like “desperate pond”: sinking low."],["ramble","RAM-buhl","verb","To walk with no set path, or to talk on and on.","Grandpa rambled on about his old job until the soup got cold.","","Ramble sounds like “ram,” wandering around the field with no plan."],["unwitting","uhn-WIT-ing","adjective","Not knowing or aware.","The dog was an unwitting star in our home video.","","Unwitting: “un” means not, and wit means knowing."],["nocturne","NOK-turn","noun","A gentle, dreamy piece of music suited to night.","Before bed, Mom played a soft nocturne on the piano.","From French nocturne, “of the night.”","Noct means night: a nocturne is a night song."],["draconian","dray-KOH-nee-uhn","adjective","Very harsh or strict.","No phone for a month seemed a bit draconian.","","Draconian sounds like dragon: harsh and fierce."],["diatribe","DY-uh-tryb","noun","A long, angry speech against something.","Uncle went on a diatribe about cold coffee at the diner.","","Diatribe sounds like “dire tribe”: an angry speech that goes on and on."],["locale","loh-KAL","noun","A place where something happens.","The coffee shop is a popular locale for studying.","","Locale sounds like local: the place where it happens."],["bemused","bih-MYOOZD","adjective","Puzzled and a little confused.","She gave a bemused smile when her son asked if dogs have birthdays.","","Bemused is not amused: it means puzzled, scratching your head."],["dogged","DAW-gid","adjective","Stubbornly determined.","Her dogged practice paid off at the piano recital.","","Dogged: like a dog with a bone, it won’t let go."],["nook","NOOK","noun","A small, cozy corner.","I read in a sunny nook by the window.","","Nook rhymes with book: a cozy corner to read."],["conviction","kuhn-VIK-shuhn","noun","A strong belief.","She spoke with conviction about keeping the park open.","","Conviction: you are convinced, sure deep down."],["lagniappe","LAN-yap","noun","A small extra gift, like a bonus.","The baker gave us one extra cookie as a lagniappe.","From Louisiana French, from Spanish la ñapa, “the gift.”","Lagniappe sounds like “lan-yap”: a little bonus gift."],["replete","ri-PLEET","adjective","Full, or well supplied with something.","The fridge was replete with food after our big shopping trip.","From Latin repletus, “filled up.”","Replete sounds like “complete”: filled up all the way."],["cumbersome","KUHM-ber-suhm","adjective","Large, heavy and hard to carry or use.","The cumbersome couch would not fit through the front door.","","Cumbersome sounds like “cucumber-some”: like carrying a giant cucumber."],["xenophile","ZEN-uh-fyl","noun","A person who loves foreign people, cultures and food.","As a true xenophile, she tries a new world recipe every week.","From Greek xenos, “stranger,” and philos, “loving.”","Xeno means foreign, and phile means lover: loves the foreign."],["abreast","uh-BREST","adverb","Side by side; also, up to date with news.","We walked three abreast down the sidewalk, holding hands.","","Abreast: chest to chest, side by side, “breast” to breast in a line."],["canter","KAN-ter","verb","To move at a smooth, easy speed, like a horse.","The horse at the fair cantered around the ring as the kids watched.","","Canter sounds like “can’t stir”: a smooth ride that doesn’t shake you."],["ballyhoo","BAL-ee-hoo","noun","Noisy fuss and excitement to get attention.","After all the ballyhoo on TV, the new store was rather small.","","Ballyhoo sounds like a “hullabaloo”: big noisy fuss."],["mien","MEEN","noun","The way a person looks or carries themselves.","The new teacher had a calm and friendly mien.","","Mien sounds like mean, but it means how you look."],["philately","fih-LAT-l-ee","noun","The hobby of collecting stamps.","Grandpa’s philately began with one stamp from his pen pal.","","Picture Phil, who collects stamps on his letters."],["cavernous","KAV-er-nuhs","adjective","Huge and hollow, like a cave.","Our voices echoed in the cavernous, empty new house.","","Cavernous has cavern in it: as big as a cave."],["adjourn","uh-JURN","verb","To stop a meeting and plan to meet again later.","The meeting adjourned at noon, and we all went to lunch.","From Old French ajorner, “to set a day.”","Adjourn has “jour,” French for day: stop for the day."],["bioluminescence","by-oh-loo-muh-NES-uhns","noun","Light made by living things, like fireflies.","On our night beach walk, the waves glowed blue with bioluminescence.","From Greek bios, “life,” and Latin lumen, “light.”","Bio means life, lumin means light: light from living things."],["reminisce","rem-uh-NIS","verb","To talk or think about happy past times.","The old friends reminisced about their school days over coffee.","From Latin reminisci, “to remember.”","Reminisce sounds like “remember this?”: talking about good old days."],["causeway","KAWZ-way","noun","A raised road across water or wet ground.","We drove across the causeway to get to the island.","","Causeway: a way, or road, built across the water."],["disquiet","dis-KWY-it","noun","A feeling of worry or unease.","A sense of disquiet grew when our son didn’t answer his phone.","","Disquiet: the opposite of quiet, a worried, noisy mind."],["innocuous","ih-NOK-yoo-uhs","adjective","Harmless.","The question seemed innocuous, but it started a big argument.","From Latin innocuus, “harmless.”","Innocuous sounds like innocent: harmless."],["chicanery","shi-KAY-nuh-ree","noun","Sneaky tricks used to fool people.","The repair shop used chicanery to charge us for work they never did.","","Chicanery sounds like “chick and hen”: sneaky tricks in the hen house."],["oscillate","OS-uh-layt","verb","To swing back and forth.","The fan oscillates to cool the whole room.","From Latin oscillare, “to swing.”","Oscillate sounds like “oh, swing it”: back and forth."],["synchronicity","sing-kruh-NIS-i-tee","noun","Two things happening together by chance in a meaningful way.","By synchronicity, my friend called just as I picked up the phone to call her.","","Synchronicity has “sync,” like phones that sync at the same time."],["sumptuous","SUMP-choo-uhs","adjective","Rich, grand and costly looking.","The hotel breakfast was sumptuous, with pancakes, eggs and fruit.","","Sumptuous sounds like “sum-tous”: a big, costly sum of good things."],["ruminate","ROO-muh-nayt","verb","To think about something deeply for a long time.","She ruminated on the job offer during her evening walk.","From Latin ruminari, “to chew the cud.”","Ruminate sounds like “roomy mind”: turning a thought over and over."],["callous","KAL-uhs","adjective","Not caring about others’ feelings.","It was callous to laugh when he tripped and fell.","","A callus is hard skin. Callous means a hard heart."],["ambiguous","am-BIG-yoo-uhs","adjective","Having more than one possible meaning; unclear.","Her text just said “maybe,” which was so ambiguous I had to call her.","From Latin ambiguus, “doubtful.”","Ambi means both: it could mean both things."],["emulate","EM-yuh-layt","verb","To copy someone you admire.","My son tries to emulate his big cousin’s soccer moves.","From Latin aemulari, “to rival.”","Emulate sounds like “I’m you, mate”: copying someone you admire."],["sporadic","spuh-RAD-ik","adjective","Happening now and then, not on a regular plan.","The Wi-Fi was sporadic all day, so my videos kept freezing.","From Greek sporadikos, “scattered.”","Sporadic sounds like “spotty”: here, gone, here again."],["motley","MOT-lee","adjective","Made of many different, mismatched kinds.","A motley crew of neighbors, kids and dogs helped paint the fence.","","Motley sounds like “mottled”: all different spots and colors."],["behoove","bih-HOOV","verb","To be right or wise for someone to do.","It behooves us to leave early to beat the traffic.","","Behoove sounds like “be-good-to-move”: the smart thing to do."],["elixir","ih-LIK-ser","noun","A magic drink thought to cure anything, or a perfect fix.","A cup of hot tea is my elixir on a cold, rainy morning.","From Arabic al-iksir.","Elixir sounds like “Ella fixer,” a drink that fixes everything."],["diligent","DIL-i-juhnt","adjective","Careful and hardworking.","The diligent student did her homework every night.","From Latin diligere, “to value, love.”","Diligent sounds like “dig in”: hard workers dig in."],["dissonance","DIS-uh-nuhns","noun","A clash of sounds or ideas.","The kids banging on the piano made a terrible dissonance.","From Latin dissonare, “to sound apart.”","Dis means not. Son means sound, like sonic. Sounds that don’t fit."],["ascertain","as-er-TAYN","verb","To find out for sure.","I called the store to ascertain that it was open before driving there.","","Ascertain has “certain” inside: make certain."],["sonorous","SON-er-uhs","adjective","Deep, full and rich in sound.","Grandpa’s sonorous voice filled the whole room when he read aloud.","From Latin sonor, “sound.”","Sonorous has “sonor,” like sound: rich, full sound."],["penumbra","pih-NUM-bruh","noun","The soft, partly shaded edge around a shadow.","We sat in the penumbra of the porch light, half in the dark.","From Latin paene, “almost,” and umbra, “shadow.”","Umbra means shadow, like an umbrella’s shade."],["heirloom","AIR-loom","noun","A treasured object passed down in a family.","Grandma’s quilt is a family heirloom passed down to me.","","Heirloom: the heir, the next in line, gets it."],["diadem","DY-uh-dem","noun","A jeweled crown or headband.","The bride wore a sparkling diadem in her hair.","From Greek diadema, “band.”","Diadem sounds like “diamond gem”: a crown full of jewels."],["vagary","VAY-guh-ree","noun","An odd, unexpected change.","We packed coats and shorts for the vagaries of spring weather.","From Latin vagari, “to wander.”","Vagary sounds like “vague”: hard to predict."],["cantankerous","kan-TANG-ker-uhs","adjective","Grumpy and quick to argue.","Our cantankerous neighbor yells whenever a ball lands in his yard.","","Can tank: a grumpy old tank that rumbles at everyone."],["rapscallion","rap-SKAL-yuhn","noun","A playful little rascal.","That little rapscallion hid my slippers again.","","Rapscallion sounds like “rascal”: a cheeky little rascal."],["blight","BLYT","noun","Something that spoils or damages; also a plant disease.","Graffiti is a blight on our lovely park.","","Blight sounds like “bite”: something that eats away at beauty."],["galore","guh-LOR","adjective","In large amounts.","The yard sale had toys galore.","From Irish go leor, “to sufficiency.”","Galore sounds like “a lot more”: tons and tons."],["amenable","uh-MEE-nuh-buhl","adjective","Willing to agree or go along with something.","The kids were amenable to a long drive as long as snacks came too.","","Amenable sounds like “amen-able”: able to say amen, yes."],["ballast","BAL-uhst","noun","Heavy material that keeps a ship steady; also anything that gives balance.","Her calm voice was the ballast that kept our busy family steady.","","Ballast sounds like “balanced”: weight that keeps things steady."],["tableau","ta-BLOH","noun","A striking scene, like a picture.","The family laughing on the porch made a sweet tableau.","From French tableau, “picture.”","Tableau sounds like “table”: a scene set like a picture on a table."],["gaggle","GAG-uhl","noun","A group of geese, or a noisy group of people.","A gaggle of teens giggled outside the movie theater.","","Gaggle sounds like giggle: a noisy group of geese or people."],["augment","awg-MENT","verb","To make bigger or add to.","He augments his paycheck by delivering food on weekends.","From Latin augere, “to increase.”","Augment sounds like “add more”: to make bigger."],["prolix","proh-LIKS","adjective","Using too many words.","The prolix speech made everyone yawn.","From Latin prolixus, “extended.”","Prolix sounds like “pro-licks”: it just goes on and on."],["soporific","sop-uh-RIF-ik","adjective","Making you feel sleepy.","The soft hum of the fan was soporific.","From Latin sopor, “deep sleep.”","Soporific sounds like “soap-or-sleepy”: it makes you snooze."],["lumbering","LUM-ber-ing","adjective","Moving in a slow, heavy, clumsy way.","The big truck came lumbering up the narrow street.","","Lumbering: like a bear lugging heavy lumber."],["arpeggio","ar-PEJ-ee-oh","noun","The notes of a chord played one after another.","She warmed up on the piano by playing a few arpeggios, one note at a time.","From Italian arpeggiare, “to play on a harp.”","Arpeggio comes from “arpa,” Italian for harp: notes rolled like a harp."],["agrarian","uh-GRAIR-ee-uhn","adjective","Having to do with farms and farming land.","Our town started out as a small agrarian village full of farms.","From Latin ager, “a field.”","Agrarian sounds like “a grain”: all about farms and crops."],["tactile","TAK-tl","adjective","Having to do with touch.","Babies love tactile toys that are soft or bumpy.","From Latin tangere, “to touch.”","Tactile sounds like “tack”: you feel a tack with your finger."],["guile","GYL","noun","Clever, sly tricks.","The toddler used guile to get an extra cookie, flashing his sweetest smile.","","Guile sounds like “sly”: tricky and clever."],["lucrative","LOO-kruh-tiv","adjective","Making a lot of money.","Her lemonade stand turned out to be very lucrative.","From Latin lucrum, “profit.”","Lucrative sounds like “lucre,” money: it brings in lots of money."],["admonish","ad-MON-ish","verb","To warn or scold someone gently but firmly.","The teacher admonished the boys for talking during the test.","","Admonish sounds like “add a mom wish”: a mom’s firm warning."],["bathos","BAY-thos","noun","A sudden drop from serious to silly, often by accident.","The serious speech ended in bathos when the speaker’s pants fell down.","From Greek bathos, “depth.”","Bathos sounds like “bath,” think of a sudden slip in the tub."],["sycophant","SIK-uh-fuhnt","noun","Someone who flatters others too much to get ahead.","The boss was surrounded by sycophants who agreed with every word.","","Sycophant sounds like “sick of fans”: fake fans who flatter too much."],["windfall","WIND-fawl","noun","A sudden bit of good luck, often money.","Finding twenty dollars in my coat was a nice windfall.","","Windfall: fruit the wind knocks down, free for the taking."],["absolve","ab-ZOLV","verb","To free someone from blame or a duty.","The coach absolved him of blame for the lost game.","From Latin absolvere, “to set free.”","Absolve looks like “solve”: the problem is solved, the blame is gone."],["tremulous","TREM-yuh-luhs","adjective","Shaking a little, often from nerves.","She gave her first speech at work in a tremulous but brave voice.","From Latin tremulus, “trembling.”","Tremulous sounds like “tremble”: shaking a little."],["debunk","dee-BUHNGK","verb","To show that a claim is false.","My daughter debunked the story that swallowed gum stays in your belly for seven years.","","Debunk: take out the bunk, which means nonsense."],["aurora","uh-ROR-uh","noun","Bright bands of colored light in the night sky near the poles.","On our trip up north, we stayed up late to watch the green aurora light up the sky.","From Latin aurora, “dawn.”","Aurora was the Roman goddess of dawn: lights glowing in the sky."],["brusque","BRUHSK","adjective","Short and blunt in speech, in a way that can seem rude.","The busy clerk gave a brusque reply, then smiled an apology.","From French brusque, “sharp.”","Brusque sounds like “brisk”: quick and short with words."],["precipice","PRES-uh-pis","noun","A very steep cliff, or the edge of something big.","We stood well back from the precipice to take photos.","From Latin praeceps, “headlong.”","Precipice sounds like “press it, piece”: right at the edge."],["sidle","SY-dl","verb","To move sideways or slyly, as if shy.","The puppy sidled up to me for a treat.","","Sidle starts with “side”: you slide up sideways."],["clamber","KLAM-ber","verb","To climb awkwardly using hands and feet.","The kids clambered up the playground climbing wall.","","Clamber sounds like climb plus scramble."],["complacent","kuhm-PLAY-suhnt","adjective","Too pleased with yourself to keep trying.","After passing his driving test, he grew complacent and stopped using his blinker.","From Latin complacere, “to please.”","Complacent: too comfy in your place to keep trying."],["discreet","dih-SKREET","adjective","Careful not to draw attention or share secrets.","She was discreet about the surprise party, telling no one.","","Discreet sounds like “this secret”: you keep it quiet."],["wizened","WIZ-uhnd","adjective","Dried up and wrinkled with age.","The wizened apple in the back of the fridge still made a fine pie.","From Old English wisnian, “to wither.”","Wizened sounds like “wizard”: wrinkled like an old wizard."],["peckish","PEK-ish","adjective","A little bit hungry.","I’m feeling peckish, so I’ll grab an apple.","","Like a bird that wants to peck at a snack."],["bellwether","BEL-weth-er","noun","Something that shows where a trend is going.","The busy toy aisle in October is a bellwether for big holiday sales.","From the bell worn by the lead sheep, or wether, of a flock.","A bellwether was a sheep wearing a bell that the flock followed."],["reprieve","ri-PREEV","noun","A break or short relief from something hard.","The snow day gave us a reprieve from the school morning rush.","","Reprieve sounds like “relief”: a break from something hard."],["aubade","oh-BAHD","noun","A song or poem about the dawn.","The alarm clock played a gentle aubade, a soft wake-up song.","From French aube, “dawn.”","Aube is French for dawn: a song for the dawn."],["antidote","AN-ti-doht","noun","Something that cures or fixes a bad situation.","A good laugh is the best antidote to a bad day.","From Greek antidoton, “given against.”","Antidote: anti means against, it fights the poison."],["unrequited","uhn-ri-KWY-tid","adjective","Not returned or given back, especially love.","His love of broccoli was sadly unrequited by his kids.","","Unrequited: love that gets no answer back."],["disrepute","dis-ri-PYOOT","noun","A state of having a bad name.","The old diner fell into disrepute, then made a comeback.","","Disrepute: you lose your good reputation."],["bestow","bih-STOH","verb","To give something as an honor or gift.","The school bestowed an award on the kind lunch lady.","","Bestow sounds like “best owe”: giving your best as a gift."],["depict","dih-PIKT","verb","To show in a picture or in words.","The family photo on the wall depicts us at the beach.","From Latin depingere, “to paint.”","Depict sounds like picture: you show it."],["archaic","ar-KAY-ik","adjective","Very old and no longer in common use.","“Thou” is an archaic word for “you” that people no longer say.","From Greek archaios, “ancient.”","Archaic sounds like “ark-age”: as old as Noah’s ark."],["nurture","NUR-cher","verb","To care for and help grow.","She nurtures her tomato plants every morning with water and sun.","From Latin nutrire, “to feed.”","Nurture sounds like nurse: to care for and help grow."],["longevity","lon-JEV-i-tee","noun","Long life.","Walking every day may help your longevity.","From Latin longus, “long,” and aevum, “age.”","Longevity has “long” in it: a long life."],["multitude","MUL-tih-tood","noun","A very large number of people or things.","A multitude of shoppers lined up for the big sale.","From Latin multus, “many.”","Multi means many, so a multitude is a big crowd."],["tintinnabulation","tin-ti-nab-yuh-LAY-shuhn","noun","The ringing and jingling of bells.","The tintinnabulation of the ice cream truck made the kids run outside.","From Latin tintinnabulum, “a little bell.”","Tintinnabulation sounds like bells going “ting-ting-ting” all at once."],["dispatch","dih-SPACH","verb","To send off quickly.","She dispatched thank-you notes the day after the party.","","A dispatcher sends the police car out fast."],["aqueduct","AK-wi-duhkt","noun","A long channel or bridge that carries water.","The old city built an aqueduct to carry water from the hills to the town.","From Latin aqua, “water,” and ducere, “to lead.”","Aqua means water, duct means pipe: a water pipe."],["jolt","JOHLT","noun","A sudden jerk or shock.","The bus stopped with a jolt, and my coffee spilled.","","Jolt sounds like volt: a sudden shock."],["scrumptious","SKRUMP-shuhs","adjective","Extremely tasty.","Grandma’s scrumptious cookies vanished in minutes.","","Scrumptious sounds like “yum-tious”: so yummy."],["astute","uh-STOOT","adjective","Clever and quick to notice what matters.","An astute shopper, she always finds the best deals.","From Latin astutus, “crafty.”","Astute sounds like “a student”: a sharp student who notices things."],["doff","DOF","verb","To take off a hat or piece of clothing.","He doffed his cap to the ladies at the gate.","From Middle English do off, “to take off.”","Doff is short for “do off”: take it off. Don is “do on.”"],["tempestuous","tem-PES-choo-uhs","adjective","Stormy, or full of strong feelings.","The two-year-old had a tempestuous morning full of tears and giggles.","From Latin tempestas, “storm.”","Tempestuous has “tempest,” which means storm: stormy."],["accede","ak-SEED","verb","To agree to a request, often after thinking it over.","Mom finally acceded to our request for a later bedtime on Friday.","","Accede sounds like “I see it”: “Okay, I see it your way.”"],["appease","uh-PEEZ","verb","To calm someone by giving them what they want.","We appeased the crying toddler with a cracker.","","Appease has “peace” inside: give them what they want for peace."],["indomitable","in-DOM-i-tuh-buhl","adjective","Impossible to defeat or discourage.","The indomitable grandma ran a race at age eighty.","From Latin indomitus, “untamed.”","Indomitable: not able to be tamed, like an unbeatable spirit."],["inexorable","in-EK-ser-uh-buhl","adjective","Impossible to stop.","The inexorable ticking of the clock reminded me I was late.","From Latin inexorabilis, “not moved by pleading.”","Inexorable: in means not, exorare means to beg: begging can’t stop it."],["jasmine","JAZ-min","noun","A climbing plant with sweet-smelling white flowers.","The jasmine soap made the whole bathroom smell sweet.","From Persian yasaman.","Jasmine sounds like “jazz mine”: a sweet, lovely scent."],["frond","FROND","noun","A large leaf of a fern or palm.","A big palm frond blew into our yard during the storm.","From Latin frons, “leaf.”","Frond sounds like “fan”: a big leaf shaped like a fan."],["paraphernalia","pair-uh-fer-NAYL-yuh","noun","All the gear and stuff for an activity.","Her fishing paraphernalia filled the whole trunk of the car.","","A word as long as the pile of gear it names."],["dilemma","dih-LEM-uh","noun","A hard choice between two options.","Pay the rent early or fix the car first was a real dilemma.","From Greek di-, “two,” and lemma, “premise.”","Di means two: stuck between two choices."],["volition","voh-LISH-uhn","noun","The power to choose for yourself.","He cleaned his room of his own volition.","From Latin volo, “I wish.”","Volition sounds like “volunteer”: you choose it yourself."],["tyro","TY-roh","noun","A beginner at something.","As a tyro at knitting, she made a very lumpy scarf.","From Latin tiro, “a new recruit.”","Tyro sounds like “tie-row”: still learning to tie your first row."],["meritorious","mair-ih-TOR-ee-uhs","adjective","Deserving praise.","He got an award for meritorious work at the food bank.","From Latin meritum, “earned.”","It holds the word merit, which means earning praise."],["babble","BAB-uhl","verb","To talk fast in a way that is hard to follow, or to make a soft bubbling sound.","The baby babbled happily in her car seat all the way home.","","Babble sounds like “baby talk”: words tumbling out."],["circumspect","SUR-kuhm-spekt","adjective","Careful to think about risks before acting.","She was circumspect about giving her phone number to a stranger.","From Latin circumspicere, “to look around.”","Circum means around, like circle. Spect means look. Look all around."],["apposite","AP-uh-zit","adjective","Very fitting and right for the moment.","At the wedding, Grandpa gave an apposite toast about love and patience.","","Apposite sounds like “appropriate”: just right for the moment."],["hospitable","HOS-pi-tuh-buhl","adjective","Friendly and welcoming to guests.","Our hospitable neighbors brought us dinner on moving day.","From Latin hospes, “guest, host.”","Hospitable sounds like hospital: a place that takes good care of you."],["diaphanous","dy-AF-uh-nuhs","adjective","Light, delicate and see-through.","Thin, diaphanous curtains let the morning light into the bedroom.","From Greek diaphanes, “see-through.”","Diaphanous: so light and thin you can see through it."],["curiosity","kyoor-ee-OS-i-tee","noun","A strong wish to know or learn something.","Curiosity made the kids open every drawer in the new house.","From Latin curiosus, “careful, inquisitive.”","Curiosity sounds like curious: always asking why."],["chiaroscuro","kee-ar-uh-SKYOOR-oh","noun","The use of strong light and dark in a picture.","The photo used chiaroscuro, with one bright lamp and deep shadows all around.","From Italian chiaro, “clear,” and scuro, “dark.”","Chiaro means clear or light, oscuro means dark, in Italian. Light and dark."],["endemic","en-DEM-ik","adjective","Found naturally in only one place.","Bad parking is endemic to our busy downtown; it’s everywhere you look.","From Greek endemos, “native.”","Endemic: it lives IN this one place, and only here."],["fanfare","FAN-fair","noun","A short blast of trumpets, or a big showy fuss.","The new store opened with lots of fanfare, balloons and music.","","Fanfare: fans cheering and a big flare of trumpets."],["ajar","uh-JAR","adjective","Slightly open, used for a door or lid.","The cat slipped out through the door we had left ajar.","","Ajar sounds like “a jar”: a jar lid left a little open."],["insular","IN-suh-ler","adjective","Cut off from other people or ideas.","Our small town can feel insular, like everyone knows only each other.","From Latin insula, “island.”","Insular comes from Latin “island”: cut off, like an island."],["requite","ri-KWYT","verb","To pay back or return a kindness or feeling.","She requited his kindness with a handmade card.","","Requite sounds like “re-quit”: you square up and make it even."],["momentum","moh-MEN-tuhm","noun","The force that keeps something moving forward.","After the first chapter, my reading gained momentum.","From Latin momentum, “movement.”","Momentum is what keeps a bike rolling once you start."],["benefactor","BEN-uh-fak-ter","noun","A person who gives money or help to others.","A kind benefactor paid for the new playground at our school.","From Latin bene, “well,” and facere, “to do.”","Bene means good, like a benefit: someone who does good."],["gallivant","GAL-uh-vant","verb","To wander from place to place for fun.","My teenagers gallivant around the mall all Saturday.","","Gallivant sounds like “gallop away”: roaming around for fun."],["crescendo","kruh-SHEN-doh","noun","A slow build to a loud or strong peak.","The kids’ shouting rose to a crescendo when the ice cream truck came.","From Italian crescendo, “growing.”","Crescendo sounds like crescent: like the moon, it grows and grows."],["pugnacious","pug-NAY-shuhs","adjective","Quick to argue or fight.","The pugnacious little dog barked at every leaf.","From Latin pugnax, “fond of fighting.”","Pug means fight, like a pug dog ready to box."],["persnickety","per-SNIK-ih-tee","adjective","Very fussy about small things.","My cat is persnickety about her food bowl being perfectly clean.","","Persnickety sounds like “picky-ety”: very picky."],["skulduggery","skul-DUG-uh-ree","noun","Sneaky, dishonest tricks.","There was some skulduggery when someone hid all the game pieces.","","Skulduggery sounds like “skull digging”: sneaky, shady work."],["dainty","DAYN-tee","adjective","Small, delicate and pretty.","She served tea in dainty cups.","","Dainty sounds like “tiny”: small and pretty."],["cordial","KOR-juhl","adjective","Warm and polite.","The new neighbors gave us a cordial welcome with cookies.","From Latin cor, “heart.”","Cord comes from heart, like in cardiac. From the heart, warm."],["querulous","KWER-uh-luhs","adjective","Whiny and full of small complaints.","The tired kids grew querulous in the back seat after three hours of driving.","From Latin queri, “to complain.”","Querulous sounds like “quarrel-us”: always quarreling and whining."],["autonomy","aw-TON-uh-mee","noun","The freedom to make your own choices.","Teens love the autonomy of having their own car.","From Greek autonomia, “self-rule.”","Auto means self: making your own rules."],["deplete","dih-PLEET","verb","To use up a supply.","The long road trip depleted our snacks.","","Deplete is the opposite of complete: the supply runs out."],["botch","BOCH","verb","To do something badly; to mess up.","I botched the pancakes, but they still tasted good.","","Botch sounds like “blotch”: a messy smudge of a job."],["calligraphy","kuh-LIG-ruh-fee","noun","The art of beautiful handwriting.","She wrote the wedding invitations in fancy calligraphy.","From Greek kallos, “beauty,” and graphein, “to write.”","Calli means beautiful. Graph means writing. Beautiful writing."],["histrionic","his-tree-ON-ik","adjective","Overly dramatic.","My son gave a histrionic wail when I took away his phone.","From Latin histrio, “actor.”","Histrionic sounds like “his-drama-nic”: over the top, like a stage actor."],["amorphous","uh-MOR-fuhs","adjective","Having no clear shape or form.","The blob of slime on the table was amorphous and kept changing shape.","From Greek amorphos, “without shape.”","A means without, morph means shape: no shape."],["discern","dih-SURN","verb","To notice or understand something not obvious.","Can you discern the hint of lemon in this cake?","From Latin discernere, “to separate.”","Discern sounds like “this I learn”: you notice what others miss."]],"kids":[["fable","FAY-buhl","noun","A short story that teaches a lesson, often with talking animals.","In the fable, the slow turtle beats the bragging rabbit, and the lesson is “don’t show off.”","From Latin fabula, “story.”","Fable sounds like table: animals at a table telling a story with a lesson."],["prance","PRANS","verb","To move in a proud, bouncy way.","The pony pranced around the field, lifting its legs up high.","","Prance rhymes with dance: a proud, bouncy little dance."],["smudge","SMUJ","noun","A dirty or blurry mark.","There was a smudge of chocolate on my homework.","","Smudge sounds like fudge: a fudgy finger leaves a smudge."],["keen","KEEN","adjective","Very sharp, or very eager.","Our dog has a keen nose; she sniffs out every hidden treat.","From Old English cēne, “brave, bold.”","Keen rhymes with “mean machine”: sharp and ready to go."],["aloft","uh-LOFT","adverb","Up high in the air.","The wind kept my kite aloft, high in the sky, all afternoon.","","Aloft has “loft” in it: up high, like a loft bed."],["pamper","PAM-per","verb","To treat someone with lots of care and comfort.","On Mom’s birthday, we pampered her with breakfast in bed and a foot rub.","","Pamper rhymes with hamper: a basket of fluffy warm towels just for you."],["mutter","MUT-er","verb","To speak quietly, often when grumpy.","My sister muttered “not fair” when she had to do the dishes.","","Mutter rhymes with butter: grumpy words that slide out soft and low."],["droop","DROOP","verb","To hang down because it is weak or tired.","The flowers drooped until we gave them water.","","Droop sounds like drop: hanging down low and sad."],["swirl","SWURL","verb","To spin around in circles.","Watch the chocolate syrup swirl into the milk when you stir it.","","Swirl sounds like twirl: spinning round and round."],["amuse","uh-MYOOZ","verb","To make someone smile or laugh.","My little brother made goofy faces to amuse us, and we laughed so hard.","","A muse? No, a moose! Picture a moose dancing to make you laugh."],["assist","uh-SIST","verb","To help someone do something.","My friend made a great pass to assist me, and I scored the goal!","From Latin assistere, “to stand by.”","An assist in sports is the pass that helps your teammate score."],["babble","BAB-uhl","verb","To talk fast in a way that is hard to understand.","My baby cousin likes to babble, “ba ba goo goo,” at the dog.","","Babble sounds like “baby babble”: baba gaga goo."],["squint","SKWINT","verb","To look with your eyes partly closed.","I had to squint to see the ball in the bright sun.","","Squint has “squ” like squeeze: you squeeze your eyes almost shut."],["balmy","BAH-mee","adjective","Warm and pleasant, like nice weather.","It was so balmy outside that we ate dinner on the porch in T-shirts.","","Balmy sounds like “palm-y”: warm like a beach with palm trees."],["soggy","SOG-ee","adjective","Very wet and soft.","My cereal got soggy because I left it in the milk too long.","","Soggy sounds like foggy and boggy: wet, wet, wet."],["tremble","TREM-buhl","verb","To shake because you are cold, scared or excited.","My knees began to tremble before I sang in front of the whole school.","From Latin tremere, “to shake.”","Tremble sounds like a wobbly jelly going “tremble, tremble” on a plate."],["quench","KWENCH","verb","To stop your thirst by drinking.","After the soccer game, a cold glass of water will quench my thirst.","From Old English cwencan, “to put out.”","Quench sounds like a big gulp: “quench!” and your thirst is gone."],["blunt","BLUHNT","adjective","Not sharp, or saying something in a very plain way.","Our safety scissors have blunt tips, so they can’t poke anyone.","","Blunt sounds like “bump”: a blunt tip just bumps, it doesn’t poke."],["jumble","JUM-buhl","noun","A messy mix of things.","My toy box is a jumble of blocks, cars and crayons all mixed up.","","Jumble sounds like jungle: a big jungle of mixed-up stuff."],["outwit","owt-WIT","verb","To win by being more clever than someone.","My little sister outwitted me by hiding the last cookie in her pocket.","","Wit means cleverness, so outwit means out-clever someone."],["echo","EK-oh","noun","A sound that bounces back so you hear it again.","I yelled “Hello!” in the empty gym, and the echo yelled it right back.","From Greek ēchō, “sound.”","Echo, echo, echo: the word repeats itself, just like the sound."],["waddle","WOD-uhl","verb","To walk with short steps, rocking side to side.","The ducks waddle across the path, rocking side to side.","","Waddle sounds like a duck’s walk: waddle, waddle, wobble."],["oasis","oh-AY-sis","noun","A green spot with water in a dry desert.","The shady tree was an oasis on the hot, sunny playground.","From Greek oasis, a fertile spot in the desert.","Oasis sounds like “Oh, a sip!”: finding water in a dry desert."],["ladle","LAY-duhl","noun","A big, deep spoon for scooping soup.","Dad used a ladle to scoop hot soup into our bowls.","","Ladle sounds like “lay-dull”: lay down a big spoonful of soup."],["clutter","KLUHT-er","noun","A messy pile of things.","I couldn’t find my pencil in all the clutter on my desk.","","Clutter sounds like clatter: piles of stuff that crash when you touch them."],["stumble","STUM-buhl","verb","To trip or almost fall.","I tried not to stumble over the toys on my bedroom floor.","","Stumble sounds like tumble: you trip and almost tumble down."],["dart","DART","verb","To move suddenly and fast.","A tiny fish darted under a rock when I splashed.","","A dart flies fast and straight, just like you darting off."],["urge","URJ","verb","To push someone hard to do something.","Coach will urge us to drink water on hot days.","From Latin urgere, “to press.”","Urge sounds like nudge: pushing and nudging you to do it."],["ponder","PON-der","verb","To think about something carefully.","I pondered which ice cream flavor to pick for a whole minute.","From Latin ponderare, “to weigh.”","Ponder sounds like pond: sitting by a pond, thinking deep thoughts."],["dash","DASH","verb","To run very fast for a short way.","We dashed to the bus stop before it pulled away.","","Dash sounds like splash: zoom through the puddle, fast."],["leap","LEEP","verb","To jump high or far.","The frog can leap from one rock to the next in one big jump.","From Old English hlēapan, “to jump.”","Leap sounds like leapfrog: jump high and far like a frog."],["alcove","AL-kohv","noun","A small space set back into a wall.","I read my comics in a cozy alcove by the window.","From Arabic al-qubba, “the vault.”","An alcove is like a little cave in the wall: al-COVE, a cozy cove."],["rowdy","ROW-dee","adjective","Loud and wild.","The bus got rowdy after we won the big game.","","Rowdy sounds like loud-y: loud and wild."],["savor","SAY-ver","verb","To enjoy something slowly.","I like to savor every bite of my birthday cake so it lasts longer.","From Latin sapor, “taste.”","Savor sounds like “save it”: you save each bite to enjoy it slowly."],["colossal","kuh-LOS-uhl","adjective","Very, very big.","We built a colossal snowman taller than Dad.","From Greek kolossos, “giant statue.”","Colossal starts like “colossus,” a giant statue: really giant."],["delicate","DEL-i-kuht","adjective","Easy to break or hurt, so you must be gentle.","Be gentle with the soap bubble; it is very delicate.","From Latin delicatus, “dainty.”","Delicate things are like eggshells: gentle hands only."],["clever","KLEV-er","adjective","Quick to learn and good at solving problems.","My clever sister fixed the broken kite with tape and a straw.","","Clever has “lever” inside: a clever kid finds the trick that lifts the problem."],["overflow","oh-ver-FLOH","verb","To spill over the top because it is too full.","I left the bath running, and it overflowed onto the floor.","","Over plus flow: the water flows right over the top."],["hatch","HACH","verb","To break out of an egg.","We watched the egg crack open and the baby chick hatch out.","","Hatch sounds like latch: the chick opens the egg’s little door."],["huddle","HUD-uhl","verb","To crowd close together.","Our team will huddle in a tight circle to plan the next play.","","Huddle sounds like cuddle: everyone squeezes close together."],["nudge","NUJ","verb","To push someone gently, often with your elbow.","I nudged my friend when the teacher called her name.","","Nudge rhymes with budge: a little push to make someone budge."],["ramble","RAM-buhl","verb","To talk a long time without a clear point.","Grandpa likes to ramble on about his old dog until we all forget the question.","","Ramble sounds like a rambling road: twisting on and on, going nowhere."],["brisk","BRISK","adjective","Quick and full of energy, or cool and fresh.","We took a brisk walk to school because it was cold and we were late.","","Brisk sounds like “quick-brisk”: fast steps on a chilly day."],["current","KUR-uhnt","noun","Water or air moving in one direction.","The river current carried our stick fast downstream.","From Latin currere, “to run.”","Current comes from Latin currere, to run: water that runs."],["merry","MAIR-ee","adjective","Happy and full of fun.","We had a merry time playing in the snow until dinner.","From Old English myrige, “pleasant.”","Merry rhymes with cherry: as happy as the cherry on top."],["nourish","NUR-ish","verb","To give food and what is needed to grow.","A good breakfast will nourish you so you can play all morning.","From Latin nutrire, “to feed.”","Nourish and nurse come from the same old word: to feed and care for."],["trickle","TRIK-uhl","verb","To flow slowly in a thin stream.","Rain began to trickle down the bus window.","","Trickle sounds like tickle: a thin little drip tickling down."],["nibble","NIB-uhl","verb","To eat with small, quick bites.","My bunny likes to nibble on lettuce, one tiny bite at a time.","","Picture a mouse going “nib, nib, nib” with tiny bites."],["rustle","RUS-uhl","verb","To make a soft, crackling sound.","The bag of chips made a rustle when I tried to sneak a snack.","","Rustle sounds like the soft “rss rss” of a crinkly chip bag."],["pounce","POWNS","verb","To jump on something suddenly.","My cat crouched low and pounced on the toy mouse.","","Pounce rhymes with bounce: a cat bounces onto its toy all at once."],["jolt","JOHLT","noun","A sudden bump or shake.","The bus hit a bump and we all bounced up with a jolt.","","Jolt sounds like bolt: a lightning bolt shakes you awake."],["yelp","YELP","verb","To give a short, sharp cry.","The puppy let out a yelp when the cat swatted its nose.","","Yelp sounds like “help!” in a short, sharp puppy cry."],["aroma","uh-ROH-muh","noun","A nice smell.","The aroma of fresh popcorn made everyone run to the kitchen.","From Greek arōma, “spice.”","Aroma sounds like “a roamer”: a smell that roams through the house."],["lantern","LAN-tern","noun","A light inside a case that you can carry.","We carried a lantern to light the way to our tent at night.","From Latin lanterna, “lamp.”","Lantern sounds like “lamp turn”: a lamp you carry as you turn and walk."],["pliable","PLY-uh-buhl","adjective","Easy to bend without breaking.","Play dough is pliable, so you can bend it into any shape.","From French plier, “to bend.”","Pliable and pliers come from an old word that means to bend."],["creature","KREE-cher","noun","Any living animal.","A tiny creature with lots of legs crawled across the sidewalk.","From Latin creatura, “a created thing.”","Creature sounds like “critter”: any living animal."],["bicker","BIK-er","verb","To argue about small, silly things.","My brother and I bicker about who gets the front seat in the car.","","Bicker sounds like “bick-bick-bick”: little pecky arguing, like birds."],["thud","THUD","noun","A dull, heavy sound.","My backpack hit the floor with a loud thud.","","Thud sounds just like a heavy book landing: THUD."],["gallop","GAL-uhp","verb","To run fast, like a horse.","The horse began to gallop, running so fast its mane flew back.","","Gallop sounds like “giddy-up”: the horse runs fast, clip-clop, gallop."],["mope","MOHP","verb","To act sad and slow because you feel down.","My brother moped around all day after his team lost.","","Mope sounds like “no hope”: dragging your feet and sighing."],["yearn","YURN","verb","To want something very much.","During the school week, I yearn for Saturday morning cartoons.","From Old English giernan, “to desire.”","Yearn sounds like “yawn” with longing: wanting it so much."],["plume","PLOOM","noun","A big, fluffy feather, or something shaped like one.","A plume of smoke rose up from the campfire.","From Latin pluma, “feather.”","Plume rhymes with broom: a big fluffy feather sweeping the air."],["hover","HUV-er","verb","To stay in one spot in the air.","The drone can hover in one spot above our yard without moving.","","Hover sounds like “hold over”: it holds still over one spot."],["beverage","BEV-er-ij","noun","Something to drink.","Lemonade is my favorite beverage on a hot summer day.","From Old French bevrage, from beivre, “to drink.”","Beverage sounds like “bev-ridge”: a drink you sip at the top of a ridge."],["bog","BOG","noun","Soft, wet, muddy ground.","My boot got stuck in the muddy bog and came off my foot!","From Irish bogach, from bog, “soft.”","Bog sounds like “bog down”: mud that sucks your boot in."],["journey","JUR-nee","noun","A trip from one place to another.","Our long journey to Grandpa’s house took all day in the car.","From French journée, “a day’s travel.”","Journey has “journ” like the French word jour, meaning day: a day of travel."],["rugged","RUG-id","adjective","Rough, strong and bumpy.","Our hiking boots are rugged, so they don’t rip on the rocky path.","","Rugged has “rug” in it: a tough, bumpy rug that never wears out."],["brook","BROOK","noun","A small stream of water.","We hopped from rock to rock across the little brook.","From Old English brōc, “stream.”","Brook sounds like “brooks babble”: a small, chatty stream."],["ooze","OOZ","verb","To flow out slowly.","Ketchup oozed out of my burger and onto my shirt.","","Ooze sounds like “oozy goo”: slime sliding out nice and slow."],["doze","DOHZ","verb","To take a short, light sleep.","Grandpa dozed off in his chair during the movie.","","Doze sounds like “Z’s”: a quick little snooze."],["mound","MOWND","noun","A small hill or pile.","The dog dug a hole and left a big mound of dirt by the fence.","","Mound sounds like a mini mountain: a little hill or pile."],["nectar","NEK-ter","noun","The sweet juice inside flowers.","Bees drink the sweet nectar inside flowers to make honey.","From Greek nektar, the drink of the gods in old myths.","A nectarine is named after nectar because it is so sweet."],["rummage","RUM-ij","verb","To dig through things to find something.","I had to rummage through my messy backpack to find my lost pencil.","","Rummage sounds like rummaging rabbits digging through a toy box."],["fetch","FECH","verb","To go and get something and bring it back.","I threw the ball and our puppy ran to fetch it back to me.","From Old English feccan, “to bring.”","Fetch rhymes with stretch: stretch, run, grab, bring it back."],["astray","uh-STRAY","adverb","Off the right path.","Our dog went astray and wandered into the neighbor’s yard.","","Astray sounds like “a stray”: a stray dog that wandered off the path."],["zigzag","ZIG-zag","noun","A line with sharp turns back and forth.","The puppy ran in a zigzag across the yard, chasing a butterfly.","","Zigzag looks like it sounds: zig one way, zag the other."],["radiant","RAY-dee-uhnt","adjective","Shining bright, or glowing with joy.","My sister looked radiant when she won the spelling bee.","From Latin radius, “ray.”","Radiant starts like radio: beaming out bright, happy waves."],["antler","ANT-ler","noun","A branching horn on the head of a deer or moose.","The deer had big antlers that branched out like tree branches.","","Antler has “ant” in it: picture an ant climbing a deer’s antler."],["gorgeous","GOR-juhs","adjective","Very beautiful.","The sunset turned the sky pink and gold; it was gorgeous.","From Old French gorgias, “fine, elegant.”","Gorgeous sounds like “gorge-us”: so pretty you want to gaze and gaze."],["dizzy","DIZ-ee","adjective","Feeling like everything is spinning.","Spinning on the tire swing made me dizzy.","","Dizzy sounds like “busy, spinny”: the room whirls round and round."],["agile","AJ-uhl","adjective","Able to move quickly and easily.","The agile cat jumped from the couch to the shelf without knocking anything over.","From Latin agilis, “nimble,” from agere, “to do or drive.”","Agile sounds like “a jiggle”: a quick wiggle and jump, then you’re gone."],["flicker","FLIK-er","verb","To shine on and off in an unsteady way.","The flashlight began to flicker on and off, so we knew the battery was low.","From Old English flicorian, “to flutter.”","Flicker sounds like flick: flick a light switch on, off, on, off."],["orchard","OR-cherd","noun","A field where fruit trees grow.","We picked apples at the orchard and ate them right off the trees.","","Picture an orchestra of apple trees, all lined up in neat rows."],["puzzled","PUZ-uhld","adjective","Confused and not sure what is going on.","I was puzzled when my socks went missing again.","","Puzzled comes from puzzle: it feels like you’re missing a piece."],["sly","SLY","adjective","Sneaky and clever.","The sly cat waited until we left the room, then stole a bite of my sandwich.","","Sly rhymes with spy: sneaky and clever like a spy."],["dense","DENS","adjective","Packed very closely together.","The fog was so dense I couldn’t see the swings.","From Latin densus, “thick.”","Dense sounds like fence: packed so tight nothing gets through."],["anxious","ANGK-shuhs","adjective","Worried or nervous about what might happen.","I felt anxious and my tummy did flips before the big spelling test.","From Latin anxius, “troubled.”","Anxious has “anx” like “ants”: ants in your pants when you’re worried."],["nook","NUUK","noun","A small, cozy corner.","I love reading in the cozy nook under the stairs.","","Nook rhymes with book: a cozy corner to curl up with a book."],["hearty","HAR-tee","adjective","Warm, big and filling.","After sledding, a hearty bowl of hot soup filled us right up.","","Hearty has “heart” in it: a warm, big meal for your heart and belly."],["gaze","GAYZ","verb","To look at something for a long time.","We lay on the grass to gaze at the stars for a whole hour.","","Gaze rhymes with daze: you stare so long you go into a daze."],["capture","KAP-cher","verb","To catch and hold.","I captured a firefly in a jar, then let it go.","From Latin capere, “to take.”","Capture sounds like “catch you”: gotcha."],["damp","DAMP","adjective","A little bit wet.","My socks were still a little damp after jumping in the puddle.","","Damp is a little wet, like a damp towel after your bath."],["perch","PURCH","verb","To sit on something high or narrow.","My parrot likes to perch on top of the bookshelf.","From Latin pertica, “pole.”","Perch rhymes with church: a bird sitting way up on the church roof."],["glance","GLANS","verb","To take a quick look.","I took a quick glance at the clock, then kept writing my test.","","Glance sounds like glass: a quick peek through the window, then away."],["assemble","uh-SEM-buhl","verb","To put the parts of something together.","We had to assemble the new bookshelf by putting all the pieces together.","","Assemble sounds like “a symbol”: put the pieces together to make a shape."],["bleak","BLEEK","adjective","Cold, empty, and gloomy.","The playground looked bleak on the cold, gray, rainy day.","","Bleak sounds like “bleh”: gray, empty, and gloomy."],["dribble","DRIB-uhl","verb","To bounce a ball over and over while you move.","She dribbled the basketball all the way down the court.","","Dribble: bounce, bounce, bounce while you run."],["drizzle","DRIZ-uhl","noun","Light rain with very small drops.","It was just a light drizzle, so we kept playing soccer.","","Drizzle is like drizzling syrup: tiny drops sprinkling down."],["noble","NOH-buhl","adjective","Good, brave and kind in a way others admire.","It was noble of Maya to give her last cookie to the new kid.","From Latin nobilis, “well known.”","Think of a noble knight: brave, kind and always helping others."],["burrow","BUR-oh","noun","A hole in the ground that an animal digs to live in.","The bunny dug a burrow under the bush and hid inside.","","Burrow sounds like “borrow”: a bunny borrows a cozy hole in the ground."],["clasp","KLASP","verb","To hold something tightly.","She clasped her lunch box tight with both hands.","","Clasp sounds like grasp: hold on tight."],["enormous","ih-NOR-muhs","adjective","Very, very big.","The enormous pizza was so big it hung off both sides of the table.","From Latin enormis, “out of the usual shape.”","Enormous has “more” hiding inside it: more, more, MORE big."],["nifty","NIF-tee","adjective","Clever, handy or neat.","My friend has a nifty pencil case that turns into a ruler.","","Nifty rhymes with fifty: a gadget with fifty clever tricks."],["timid","TIM-id","adjective","Shy and easily scared.","The timid kitten hid under the bed when my friends came over.","From Latin timidus, “fearful.”","Timid sounds like “tiny me”: feeling small and shy."],["appetite","AP-uh-tite","noun","The feeling of wanting to eat.","Playing soccer all day gave me a huge appetite. I ate three tacos!","From Latin appetitus, “desire.”","Appetite sounds like “a pet bite”: so hungry you could bite like a pet."],["trudge","TRUJ","verb","To walk slowly with heavy steps.","We had to trudge through deep snow to get to the bus stop.","","Trudge sounds like sludge: walking slow through thick, heavy mud."],["weary","WEER-ee","adjective","Very tired.","After the long field trip, we were weary and fell asleep on the bus.","","Weary sounds like “wear-y”: all worn out and tired."],["collapse","kuh-LAPS","verb","To fall down or cave in suddenly.","Our sand castle collapsed when the big wave hit it.","From Latin collapsus, “fallen together.”","Collapse sounds like “all flops”: everything falls down at once."],["mellow","MEL-oh","adjective","Calm, soft and relaxed.","After a long swim, my sister was mellow and just wanted to lie on the couch.","","Mellow rhymes with yellow: a sleepy yellow cat lying in the warm sun."],["absorb","uhb-ZORB","verb","To soak up or take in something.","I spilled my milk, but the paper towel soaked it all up. Paper towels absorb!","From Latin absorbere, “to swallow up.”","A sponge says “ab-SORB!” as it slurps up every drop."],["gulp","GULP","verb","To swallow quickly in a big mouthful.","After soccer, I gulp down a whole bottle of water in one go.","","Say “gulp” and you even sound like a big swallow."],["boulder","BOHL-der","noun","A very large rock.","The boulder was so big that five of us sat on top of it.","","Boulder sounds like “bolder”: a rock even bigger and bolder than the rest."],["orbit","OR-bit","verb","To travel in a circle around something in space.","My puppy orbits around me in circles whenever I hold a treat.","From Latin orbita, “wheel track.”","The letter O is round, just like the path of an orbit."],["roam","ROHM","verb","To walk around with no set plan.","On Saturdays, our dog likes to roam around the backyard sniffing everything.","","Roam rhymes with home: you roam around before you go home."],["cackle","KAK-uhl","verb","To laugh in a loud, sharp way.","My aunt cackled so loud at the joke that we all jumped.","","Cackle sounds like a witch’s laugh: heh-heh-heh."],["brink","BRINGK","noun","The very edge of something.","The ball rolled to the brink of the pool and stopped right at the edge.","","Brink rhymes with “rink”: standing at the edge of the ice rink."],["moat","MOHT","noun","A deep ditch filled with water around a castle.","We dug a moat around our sandcastle and filled it with water.","From Old French mote, “mound.”","Moat rhymes with boat: you need a boat to cross a castle’s moat."],["brim","BRIM","noun","The top edge of a cup or bowl.","I filled my cup with cocoa right to the brim, so I had to sip carefully.","","Brim rhymes with “rim”: the top edge of the cup."],["bristle","BRIS-uhl","noun","A short, stiff hair, like on a brush.","One bristle fell out of my toothbrush.","","Bristle sounds like “bristly”: the stiff hairs on a brush."],["crater","KRAY-ter","noun","A large, bowl-shaped hole in the ground.","The meteor in the cartoon left a giant crater in the ground.","From Greek krater, “mixing bowl.”","Crater sounds like crate: a big hole you could drop a crate into."],["linger","LING-ger","verb","To stay longer than you need to.","We like to linger at the park, staying until the very last minute.","","Linger sounds like “long-er”: you stay a little longer."],["vanish","VAN-ish","verb","To disappear all at once.","My cookies seem to vanish whenever my brother is in the kitchen.","","Vanish sounds like “van-ish”: a van zooms off and is gone."],["crafty","KRAF-tee","adjective","Clever at tricking others.","The crafty raccoon figured out how to open the trash can.","","A crafty fox is full of sneaky tricks up its sleeve."],["vivid","VIV-id","adjective","Bright, strong and clear.","I had such a vivid dream that I thought it was real.","From Latin vividus, “full of life.”","Vivid sounds like “vivid video”: super bright and clear colors."],["crevice","KREV-is","noun","A narrow crack or opening.","A lizard hid in a crevice between two rocks.","","Crevice sounds like crack: a skinny crack to hide in."],["chuckle","CHUHK-uhl","verb","To laugh quietly.","Dad chuckled quietly at the silly joke on the cereal box.","","Chuckle sounds like a little “chuh-chuh” laugh hiding in your throat."],["doodle","DOO-duhl","verb","To draw little pictures without thinking much.","I doodled stars all over my notebook.","","Doodle sounds silly, just like the little drawings."],["canopy","KAN-uh-pee","noun","A roof of leaves and branches high above the ground.","Monkeys swing through the leafy canopy high above the jungle floor.","From Greek konopeion, “a net against mosquitoes.”","A canopy is like a tent roof made of leaves."],["blend","BLEND","verb","To mix things together.","We blend bananas and strawberries to make a smoothie.","","A blender blends: whirrr, everything mixes together."],["dazzle","DAZ-uhl","verb","To amaze someone with brightness or skill.","The fireworks dazzled everyone, and we all said “Wow!”","From the old word “daze.”","Dazzle sounds like razzle-dazzle: sparkly, flashy, wow."],["vibrant","VY-bruhnt","adjective","Bright and full of life.","My new sneakers come in vibrant colors like neon green and bright pink.","","Vibrant sounds like vibrating: colors so bright they seem to buzz."],["grumpy","GRUM-pee","adjective","In a bad, cranky mood.","My brother is grumpy until he eats breakfast, then he smiles again.","","Grumpy sounds like grump and lumpy: a lumpy, frowning face."],["boast","BOHST","verb","To talk proudly about yourself.","He liked to boast, “I’m the fastest kid in the whole school!”","","Boast sounds like “boat”: a boaster floats around on a big boat of brags."],["pantry","PAN-tree","noun","A small room or closet where food is kept.","We hid the birthday cake in the pantry behind the cereal boxes.","From Old French paneterie, “bread room.”","Pantry comes from an old word for bread: the room where food is kept."],["loyal","LOY-uhl","adjective","Always standing by a friend.","My dog is loyal; she waits by the door for me every single day.","From Latin legalis, “of the law.”","Loyal sounds like royal: a royal guard always stands by the king."],["rascal","RAS-kuhl","noun","A playful kid or pet who causes small trouble.","That little rascal of a kitten hid my sock under the couch again.","","Picture a raccoon rascal sneaking snacks and grinning with a mask on."],["glossy","GLOS-ee","adjective","Smooth and shiny.","My new sticker book has glossy pages that shine when you tilt them.","","Glossy sounds like lip gloss: smooth and shiny."],["bulge","BUHLJ","verb","To stick out in a round lump.","My pockets bulge with all the shells I found at the beach.","","Bulge sounds like “bulgy”: a lump that sticks out."],["gust","GUST","noun","A sudden, strong rush of wind.","A sudden gust of wind blew my hat right off my head.","From Old Norse gustr, “a blast of wind.”","Gust sounds like “gush”: a gush of wind that whooshes past."],["detect","dih-TEKT","verb","To notice or find something.","My dog can detect a treat hidden in my pocket.","From Latin detegere, “to uncover.”","A detective detects clues, just like a sniffing dog."],["harvest","HAR-vist","noun","The time when crops like fruits and vegetables are picked.","At harvest time, we picked so many apples our baskets overflowed.","From Old English hærfest, “autumn.”","Harvest sounds like “hard vest”: a vest with big pockets for picked apples."],["bouquet","boh-KAY","noun","A bunch of flowers held together.","I picked a bouquet of flowers from the garden for my mom.","From French bouquet, “a little wood or bunch.”","Bouquet sounds like “boo-KAY”: say “okay” with a big bunch of flowers."],["sputter","SPUT-er","verb","To make popping or spitting sounds.","The old lawn mower began to sputter and then it stopped.","","Sputter sounds like spit and splutter: pop, pop, cough."],["rumble","RUM-buhl","verb","To make a deep, rolling sound.","My tummy started to rumble so loud the whole class heard it before lunch.","","Rumble sounds like a tummy and thunder at the same time: rrrumble."],["admire","ad-MYRE","verb","To like and look up to someone or something.","I admire my big sister because she never gives up on hard things.","From Latin admirari, “to wonder at.”","Admire sounds like “add my cheer”: you cheer for the people you admire."],["bramble","BRAM-buhl","noun","A prickly bush, like a blackberry bush.","My sock got stuck on a prickly bramble on the trail.","","Bramble sounds like “scramble”: you scramble away from the prickly bush."],["glide","GLYDE","verb","To move smoothly and quietly.","The ice skater seemed to glide across the rink without a sound.","From Old English glīdan, “to slide.”","Glide sounds like slide: you slide along smoothly and quietly."],["journal","JUR-nuhl","noun","A book where you write about your days.","Every night I write about my day in my journal.","From French journal, “daily.”","Journal comes from jour, French for day: a book for your days."],["idle","EYE-duhl","adjective","Not busy or not working.","The video game sat idle because everyone was outside playing.","From Old English īdel, “empty, useless.”","Idle sounds like “I doll”: just sitting still like a doll."],["thrifty","THRIF-tee","adjective","Careful not to waste money or things.","My thrifty brother saves half his allowance every week.","","Thrifty sounds like “thrift” plus “fifty”: saving fifty cents, not spending it."],["ledge","LEJ","noun","A narrow shelf sticking out from a wall or rock.","A pigeon sat on the narrow window ledge outside my classroom.","","Ledge sounds like edge: a little shelf on the edge of a wall."],["scold","SKOHLD","verb","To speak to someone in an angry way about what they did.","Dad had to scold the puppy for chewing his slipper.","","Scold has “cold” in it: words said in a cold, cross voice."],["hush","HUSH","verb","To make quiet.","Mom whispered “hush” to quiet the baby so it could sleep.","","Say “hushhh” with a finger on your lips. It even sounds quiet."],["bough","BOW","noun","A big branch of a tree.","A bird built its nest on a low bough of the tree.","From Old English bōg, “shoulder, branch.”","Bough sounds like “bow”: a branch that bows down low."],["rickety","RIK-uh-tee","adjective","Weak and shaky, like it might fall apart.","The rickety old treehouse creaked and wobbled when we climbed in.","","Rickety sounds like creaky-rickety: a chair that goes “rick, rick” and wobbles."],["coil","KOYL","verb","To wind into rings or loops.","The garden hose coiled up in loops by the door.","","Coil like a spring: round and round and round."],["courage","KUR-ij","noun","Being brave when something is hard or scary.","It took courage to sing alone in front of the class.","From Latin cor, “heart.”","Courage comes from cor, Latin for heart: a lion heart."],["gloomy","GLOO-mee","adjective","Dark, or feeling sad.","It was a gloomy, gray, rainy day, and nobody wanted to go outside.","","Gloomy has “gloom” and “room”: a dark room makes you feel gloomy."],["cozy","KOH-zee","adjective","Warm, snug and comfy.","We built a cozy blanket fort and snuggled in with cocoa.","","Cozy sounds like dozy: so snug and warm you want to nap."],["blotch","BLOCH","noun","A large, messy spot or mark.","I spilled paint and got a big blue blotch on my shirt.","","Blotch sounds like “blob” and “splotch”: a messy spot."],["grateful","GRAYT-fuhl","adjective","Thankful for something.","I was so grateful when my friend shared her umbrella in the rain.","From Latin gratus, “pleasing, thankful.”","Grateful sounds like “great-full”: full of great thanks."],["overjoyed","oh-ver-JOYD","adjective","Very, very happy.","I was overjoyed when we got a puppy for my birthday.","","Over plus joy: so much joy it spills over the top."],["coax","KOHKS","verb","To gently get someone to do something.","I coaxed the shy kitten out from under the bed with a treat.","","Coax sounds like “Come on, folks!”: gently asking with a treat."],["twinkle","TWINK-uhl","verb","To shine with little flashes of light.","The stars twinkle above our tent when we camp in the backyard.","","Think of the song: “Twinkle, twinkle, little star,” little flashes of light."],["anchor","ANG-ker","noun","A heavy metal hook that keeps a boat in place.","The sailors dropped the heavy anchor so the boat would stay still.","From Greek ankyra, “anchor.”","Anchor sounds like “anker”: it sinks and keeps the boat from drifting."],["prowl","PROWL","verb","To move around quietly, looking for something.","Our cat likes to prowl around the yard at night, looking for bugs.","","Prowl rhymes with owl: a quiet hunter sneaking around at night."],["gigantic","jy-GAN-tik","adjective","Huge, like a giant.","The gigantic dog was taller than me when it stood up.","From Greek gigas, “giant.”","Gigantic starts like giant, because that is where it comes from."],["arrange","uh-RAYNJ","verb","To put things in a neat or useful order.","I arrange my crayons in rainbow order, red to purple.","","Arrange has “range” in it: put things in a neat row, like a range."],["glimmer","GLIM-er","noun","A small, faint light.","We saw a tiny glimmer of light from our friend’s flashlight far away.","","Glimmer sounds like shimmer: a little shimmer of light in the dark."],["preen","PREEN","verb","To clean and smooth feathers with a beak.","The duck preened its feathers after its swim.","","Preen sounds like “pretty and clean”: a bird making its feathers neat."],["paddle","PAD-uhl","verb","To move through water using a flat oar or your hands.","We paddled the canoe across the lake to the snack shop.","","Paddle sounds like puddle: splash, splash through the water."],["oblong","OB-long","adjective","Longer than it is wide.","Our dining table is oblong, so lots of people can sit along the sides.","From Latin oblongus, “rather long.”","Oblong has “long” in it: a shape that is long one way."],["plunge","PLUNJ","verb","To jump or dive in quickly.","I took a deep breath and plunged into the cold pool.","","Plunge rhymes with sponge: dunk it all the way down with a splash."],["prickly","PRIK-lee","adjective","Covered with sharp little points.","Be careful, that cactus is prickly!","","Prickly has “prick” in it: tiny pokes that sting your fingers."],["quarrel","KWOR-uhl","noun","An angry argument.","The twins had a loud quarrel over who got the top bunk.","From Latin querela, “complaint.”","A quarrel is a squabble: two squirrels fighting over one nut."],["lagoon","luh-GOON","noun","A shallow pool of water near the sea.","We swam in the calm lagoon where the water was shallow and warm.","From Latin lacuna, “pool, pit.”","Lagoon sounds like “lake-oon”: a little lake next to the sea."],["cluster","KLUHS-ter","noun","A small group of things close together.","A cluster of grapes hung together on one stem.","From Old English clyster.","A cluster is a bunch all clustered close, like grapes on a stem."],["frantic","FRAN-tik","adjective","Wild and rushed because you are worried.","The bus was coming, and I ran around in a frantic rush looking for my other shoe.","","Frantic sounds like panic: frantic means panic in a big hurry."],["jagged","JAG-id","adjective","Having sharp, rough points.","The broken cracker had jagged edges, sharp and pointy.","","Jagged sounds like jaggy: zig-zag, zig-zag, like shark teeth."],["flutter","FLUT-er","verb","To flap quickly and lightly.","The butterfly’s wings flutter so fast they look like a blur.","From Old English floterian, “to float back and forth.”","Flutter sounds like butter-fly: a butterfly’s wings flutter, flutter, flutter."],["fond","FOND","adjective","Liking someone or something a lot.","I am so fond of my old teddy bear that I still sleep with it.","","Fond sounds like found: you’re fond of things you’re glad you found."],["lively","LYVE-lee","adjective","Full of life and energy.","The class was lively on the last day of school, full of laughs and noise.","","Lively has “live” in it: full of life and bounce."],["sprout","SPROWT","verb","To start to grow.","The bean seeds in our class cup began to sprout after three days.","","Sprout has “out” in it: a tiny green shoot popping out of the dirt."],["stroll","STROHL","verb","To walk in a slow, relaxed way.","We stroll around the park after Sunday lunch, licking ice cream.","","Stroll sounds like a stroller: slow and easy, like walking a baby."],["bulky","BUHL-kee","adjective","Big and hard to carry.","My big puffy winter coat was so bulky it barely fit in my cubby.","","Bulky has “bulk” in it: big, lumpy, and hard to carry."],["unique","yoo-NEEK","adjective","The only one of its kind.","Every kid in class drew a unique monster, and no two looked the same.","From Latin unicus, “only one.”","Uni means one, like a unicorn’s one horn: one of a kind."],["lofty","LOF-tee","adjective","Very high up.","The kite flew so high it looked lofty, way above the trees.","From Old Norse lopt, “air, sky.”","Lofty sounds like loft: the highest room up in the house."],["camouflage","KAM-uh-flahzh","noun","Colors or patterns that help something blend in.","The green frog’s camouflage hid it in the grass.","From French camoufler, “to disguise.”","Camouflage sounds like “came-a-flag”: blending in so no one sees you."],["fumble","FUM-buhl","verb","To handle something in a clumsy way.","My cold fingers made me fumble the keys, and they fell in the snow.","","Fumble sounds like tumble: things tumble out of your clumsy hands."],["brew","BROO","verb","To make a drink by soaking or boiling things in water.","Mom likes to brew mint tea in hot water after dinner.","","Brew rhymes with “stew”: stir and simmer to make a drink."],["nugget","NUG-it","noun","A small lump of something.","I found a shiny nugget of gold paint stuck to my art table.","","Think chicken nugget: a small lump of something tasty."],["alert","uh-LURT","adjective","Watching closely and ready to act.","The alert goalie saw the ball coming and blocked it fast.","From Italian all’erta, “on the watch.”","An alert says “Look out!” An alert kid is ready for anything."],["vow","VOW","verb","To make a serious promise.","I vow to feed my goldfish every single day.","","Vow sounds like “wow, I promise!” a big, serious promise."],["clatter","KLAT-er","noun","A loud rattling noise.","The pots fell off the shelf with a loud clatter.","","Clatter sounds like the noise: clang, clank, clatter."],["applaud","uh-PLAWD","verb","To clap your hands to show you liked something.","When the class play ended, everyone stood up to applaud and cheer.","From Latin applaudere, “to clap at.”","Applaud has “plaud” like “loud”: loud clapping for a great show."],["pebble","PEB-uhl","noun","A small, smooth stone.","I skipped a flat pebble across the pond, and it bounced three times.","","Pebble sounds like “pet bubble”: a smooth little stone you keep as a pet."],["predict","pri-DIKT","verb","To say what you think will happen next.","I predict it will rain, because the sky is dark and gray.","From Latin praedicere, “to say before.”","Pre means before, and dict means say: you say it before it happens."],["precious","PRESH-uhs","adjective","Very special and worth a lot to someone.","Grandma’s old ring is precious to her, so she keeps it in a special box.","From Latin pretiosus, “costly.”","Precious comes from an old word for price: worth a whole lot."],["crisp","KRISP","adjective","Firm, dry and crunchy.","I love the crisp crunch when I bite a fresh apple.","From Latin crispus, “curly.”","Crisp sounds like a crunchy chip: snap, crackle, crisp."],["plentiful","PLEN-tih-fuhl","adjective","More than enough.","Snacks were plentiful at the party, so everyone got seconds.","From Latin plenus, “full.”","Plentiful has “plenty” in it: plenty, and then some more."],["swift","SWIFT","adjective","Very fast.","The swift soccer player raced past everyone and scored.","From Old English swift, “moving quickly.”","Swift sounds like swish: so fast you only hear a swish."],["drift","DRIFT","verb","To float along slowly.","Our paper boat drifted slowly down the creek.","","Drift like a snowdrift slowly blown across the yard."],["cascade","kas-KAYD","noun","A small waterfall, or a lot of something falling at once.","A cascade of marbles rolled down the stairs.","From Italian cascare, “to fall.”","Cascade sounds like “case spilled”: everything tumbling down at once."],["gleam","GLEEM","verb","To shine with a soft, bright light.","After I washed my bike, it seemed to gleam in the sun.","From Old English glǣm, “bright light.”","Gleam rhymes with beam: a shiny beam of light bounces off it."],["croak","KROHK","verb","To make a deep, rough sound like a frog.","Frogs croak in the pond every summer night.","","Croak sounds like a frog: croak, croak."],["gadget","GAJ-it","noun","A small, handy tool or machine.","Dad’s new gadget can peel an apple in one long, curly strip.","","Gadget sounds like “got it”: a gadget that gets the job done."],["quiver","KWIV-er","verb","To shake with small, quick moves.","My little brother’s lip began to quiver when his ice cream fell on the ground.","","Quiver sounds like shiver: shaking with tiny, quick wiggles."],["fizz","FIZ","verb","To make lots of tiny bubbles with a hissing sound.","When I opened the shaken soda, it started to fizz all over my shoes.","","Say “fizz” slowly. It sounds just like soda bubbles: fzzzzz."],["beacon","BEE-kuhn","noun","A bright light that guides or warns people.","The lighthouse beacon flashed so the boats could find their way home.","From Old English bēacen, “sign.”","Beacon sounds like “beckon”: a bright light that calls you over."],["marsh","MARSH","noun","Soft, wet land with tall grasses.","Our shoes got soaked when we walked through the marsh by the lake.","","Marsh sounds like mush: wet, squishy ground under your boots."],["dune","DOON","noun","A hill of sand made by the wind.","We rolled down the giant sand dune at the beach.","From Dutch duin.","Dune rhymes with moon: a sand hill shaped like a half moon."],["dusk","DUHSK","noun","The time just after the sun sets, when it starts to get dark.","The streetlights came on at dusk, so we went home.","","Dusk and dark both start with D: dusk comes right before dark."],["crave","KRAYV","verb","To want something very much.","On hot days, I crave ice-cold lemonade.","","Crave sounds like cave: a hungry cave in your tummy wanting more."],["jolly","JOL-ee","adjective","Happy and cheerful.","The jolly lunch lady laughed and gave everyone an extra cookie.","From Old French jolif, “merry.”","Jolly sounds like lolly: happy as a kid with a lollipop."],["cove","KOHV","noun","A small, curved bay along a shore.","Our rowboat rested in a quiet little cove by the lake.","","A cove is like a cozy cave of water, tucked in from the waves."],["adore","uh-DOR","verb","To love something or someone very much.","I adore my puppy so much that I give him a hug every morning.","From Latin adorare, “to worship, to pray to.”","Adore sounds like “a door”: you run to the door to hug them."],["knack","NAK","noun","A natural skill for doing something.","Ava has a knack for puzzles; she finishes them twice as fast as us.","","Knack sounds like snack: doing it is as easy as eating a snack."],["hobby","HOB-ee","noun","Something you like to do for fun in your free time.","My hobby is drawing comics after school, just for fun.","","Hobby sounds like hop-be: hop over to the thing you love to do."],["daring","DAIR-ing","adjective","Brave and willing to take chances.","The daring squirrel leaped from one tree to the next.","","Daring has “dare” in it: brave enough to take the dare."],["wriggle","RIG-uhl","verb","To twist and turn your body.","The worm began to wriggle in my hand when I picked it up.","","Wriggle sounds like wiggle: twisting and squirming all over."],["whimper","WIM-per","verb","To make small, sad crying sounds.","The puppy will whimper at the door if we leave it alone too long.","","Whimper sounds like a tiny whine: “mmm, mmm,” soft and sad."],["delight","dih-LYTE","noun","Great joy.","The kids squealed with delight at the new puppy.","From Latin delectare, “to charm.”","Delight has “light” in it: joy that lights you up."],["ornament","OR-nuh-muhnt","noun","A pretty thing used for decoration.","My sister hung a shiny ornament on the tree.","From Latin ornare, “to decorate.”","Ornament starts like orange: a round, shiny ball hanging on a tree."],["astonish","uh-STON-ish","verb","To surprise someone a lot.","The magician pulled a bunny out of a hat to astonish the kids.","","Astonish sounds like “a stone-ish” face: so surprised you freeze like a stone."],["mumble","MUM-buhl","verb","To speak softly so the words are hard to hear.","When Mom asked who ate the cookies, my brother mumbled into his sleeve.","","Mumble rhymes with bumble: words that bumble out too soft to hear."],["mend","MEND","verb","To fix something that is broken or torn.","Grandma mended the hole in my favorite jeans.","","Mend rhymes with end: fix the rip and make the problem end."],["hazy","HAY-zee","adjective","Misty or not clear.","On the hazy morning, the hills looked blurry, like a foggy window.","","Hazy sounds like lazy: a lazy, fuzzy, sleepy-looking sky."],["ripple","RIP-uhl","noun","A tiny wave on the top of water.","When I dropped a pebble in the pool, a ripple spread out in a circle.","","Ripple sounds like little ripples saying “rip, rip, rip” across the water."],["bustle","BUHS-uhl","verb","To move around in a busy, hurried way.","Kids bustle down the hall when the lunch bell rings.","","Bustle sounds like “hustle”: everyone hurrying around, busy busy busy."],["nuzzle","NUZ-uhl","verb","To gently rub or push with the nose.","My dog nuzzled my hand with her cold nose until I petted her.","","Nuzzle sounds like “nose-le”: a puppy pressing its nose into you."],["pluck","PLUK","verb","To pull something off quickly.","I plucked a strawberry off the plant and ate it right away.","","Pluck sounds like “pull quick”: grab it and pull it off fast."],["bonfire","BON-fire","noun","A big outdoor fire.","We roasted marshmallows over the big bonfire at camp.","From Middle English bone fire, “bone fire.”","Bonfire: once it was a “bone fire.” Now it’s just a big fun fire."],["attic","AT-ik","noun","A room or space right under the roof of a house.","We climbed the ladder up to the attic and found old toys in a dusty box.","","Attic sounds like “at the tip”: the room at the tip-top."],["moist","MOYST","adjective","A little bit wet.","The cake was soft and moist, not dry at all.","","Moist sounds like mist: a little mist makes things a little wet."],["humble","HUM-buhl","adjective","Not bragging about yourself.","Even after she won, Mia stayed humble and said her team did the work.","From Latin humilis, “low, near the ground.”","Humble sounds like “hum-bull”: a quiet bull just humming, not bragging."],["burly","BUR-lee","adjective","Big, strong, and sturdy.","The burly man carried our heavy couch all by himself.","","Burly sounds like “bear-ly”: big and strong like a bear."],["magnify","MAG-nuh-fy","verb","To make something look bigger than it is.","Dad’s reading glass can magnify the tiny print on the cereal box.","From Latin magnificare, “to make great.”","Magni means big, like magnificent. Magnify makes things look big."],["blizzard","BLIZ-erd","noun","A big snowstorm with strong winds.","The blizzard was so snowy and windy that school was closed.","","Blizzard sounds like “blitz” and “lizard”: a lizard zooming through a wild snowstorm."],["icicle","EYE-sih-kuhl","noun","A hanging spike of ice.","A long, pointy icicle hung from the roof after the snow storm.","","Icicle starts with “ice”: an ice stick hanging down."],["muffle","MUF-uhl","verb","To make a sound quieter by covering it.","I wore earmuffs to muffle the noise of my brother’s drum.","","Muffle sounds like earmuffs: they cover your ears and make sounds quiet."],["zest","ZEST","noun","Lots of energy and joy.","My little sister eats pancakes with such zest that syrup gets everywhere.","","Zest sounds like “best”: doing things with your best energy."],["mischief","MIS-chif","noun","Playful behavior that causes small trouble.","The puppy got into mischief and hid all our socks under the couch.","From Old French meschief, “misfortune.”","Mischief sounds like “miss chief”: sneaky fun while the chief is away."],["curious","KYUR-ee-uhs","adjective","Eager to learn or know about something.","The curious puppy sniffed every box to see what was inside.","From Latin curiosus, “careful, eager to know.”","Curious kids ask “What’s this?” about everything, like a puppy sniffing around."],["bland","BLAND","adjective","Not having much taste or flavor.","The plain noodles were so bland that I added cheese to give them taste.","From Latin blandus, “soft, smooth.”","Bland sounds like “blah”: food that tastes like nothing."],["marvel","MAR-vuhl","verb","To feel amazed by something.","We marveled at how my little brother stacked twenty cups without one falling.","From Old French merveille, “a wonder.”","Marvel sounds like marble: you stare at a shiny marble, amazed."],["batch","BACH","noun","A group of things made at the same time.","Grandpa baked a batch of cookies, all on one tray at the same time.","","Batch rhymes with “patch”: a whole patch of cookies at once."],["ruckus","RUK-uhs","noun","A noisy fuss.","The chickens made a big ruckus when the dog ran into the yard.","","Ruckus sounds like a ruck-sack full of pots and pans crashing."],["discover","dis-KUHV-er","verb","To find something for the first time.","We discovered a bird’s nest hidden in the bush.","","Dis-cover: take the cover off and find what’s under it."],["avoid","uh-VOYD","verb","To stay away from something.","I hopped around the big puddle to avoid getting my shoes wet.","","Avoid has “void,” which means empty: keep far away from it."],["crumble","KRUHM-buhl","verb","To break into tiny pieces.","The dry cookie crumbled into tiny bits all over my shirt.","From Old English, related to “crumb.”","Crumble makes crumbs: a cookie falling apart in your hand."],["gobble","GOB-uhl","verb","To eat fast and greedily.","Our dog will gobble his dinner in ten seconds flat.","","A turkey says “gobble gobble” while it eats its food super fast."],["polish","POL-ish","verb","To rub something until it shines.","I polished my shoes until they shone like mirrors.","From Latin polire, “to make smooth.”","Polish your shoes until you can see your own face shine back."],["massive","MAS-iv","adjective","Very large and heavy.","A massive wave knocked over our sandcastle in one splash.","From Latin massa, “lump.”","Massive has “mass,” an old word for a lump: huge and heavy."],["drench","DRENCH","verb","To make something soaking wet.","The sprinkler drenched my little brother from head to toe.","From Old English drencan, “to give a drink.”","Drench sounds like drink: soaked like you drank a whole pool."],["beckon","BEK-uhn","verb","To wave or nod to call someone over.","Grandma will beckon us with her finger when the cookies are ready.","From Old English bīecnan, “to make a sign.”","Beckon sounds like “bacon”: someone waving you over for bacon."],["morsel","MOR-suhl","noun","A tiny piece of food.","My cat ate every last morsel of tuna in her bowl.","From Latin morsus, “a bite.”","Morsel starts with “more”: one tiny bite, and you want more."],["meek","MEEK","adjective","Quiet, gentle and shy.","The meek little kitten hid behind my legs when the doorbell rang.","","Meek sounds like a tiny mouse saying “eek” very, very quietly."],["wander","WON-der","verb","To walk around slowly with no set path.","Our puppy likes to wander around the yard sniffing every flower.","","Wander sounds like wonder: you wonder as you wander."],["aisle","ILE","noun","A path between rows of seats or shelves.","The cereal is in the third aisle of the grocery store.","","Aisle sounds like “I’ll”: I’ll walk down the aisle to the snacks."],["snug","SNUG","adjective","Warm, cozy and comfy.","The puppy curled up snug in my blanket on the couch.","","Snug rhymes with hug: warm and cozy like a big hug."],["heap","HEEP","noun","A big, messy pile.","I dumped all my clean clothes in a big heap on my bed.","From Old English hēap, “a pile.”","Heap sounds like “heap-o-stuff”: think of a heap of leaves you jump in."],["aware","uh-WAIR","adjective","Knowing about something.","I wasn’t aware my cat was in my backpack until it meowed!","From Old English gewær, “watchful.”","Aware is in “beware”: when you beware, you are aware of danger."],["crouch","KROWCH","verb","To bend your knees and get low to the ground.","The cat crouched low in the grass, ready to pounce.","","Crouch rhymes with ouch: bend low so you don’t bump your head."],["banquet","BANG-kwit","noun","A big, fancy meal for many people.","After the season, our team had a big banquet with tons of yummy food.","From French banquet, “a little bench.”","Banquet sounds like “bank it”: so much food you could bank the leftovers."],["plush","PLUSH","adjective","Soft, thick and fluffy.","My new plush bunny is so soft I sleep with it every night.","","A plush toy is a soft stuffed animal, so plush means super soft."],["dodge","DOJ","verb","To move quickly out of the way.","I dodged the ball and stayed in the game.","","Dodgeball: you dodge the ball by jumping out of the way."],["slither","SLITH-er","verb","To slide along the ground like a snake.","We watched the worm slither across the wet sidewalk after the rain.","","Slither has “sssss” like a snake: slide, slide, slither."],["cooperate","koh-OP-uh-rayt","verb","To work together to get something done.","We cooperated to carry the big box of books together.","From Latin co-, “together,” and operari, “to work.”","Co means together, like co-pilots flying one plane."],["nimble","NIM-buhl","adjective","Quick and light in the way you move.","The nimble goalie jumped and caught the ball before it hit the net.","","Nimble sounds like nibble: a quick little mouse nibbling and darting away."],["soar","SOR","verb","To fly high in the air.","My paper airplane will soar across the whole playground if I throw it right.","","Soar sounds like “roar” up high: a plane roaring up into the sky."],["pester","PES-ter","verb","To keep bothering someone.","My little brother pestered me all day to play his game.","","Pester sounds like pest: a buzzing fly that won’t leave you alone."],["buoy","BOO-ee","noun","A floating marker in the water.","We swam out to the floating red buoy and back.","","Buoy sounds like “boo-ee”: a floaty that bobs up and says boo."],["bundle","BUHN-duhl","noun","A group of things tied or wrapped together.","Dad carried a bundle of sticks tied together for the campfire.","","A bundle is a bunch all bundled up, like a baby in a blanket."],["bumble","BUHM-buhl","verb","To move or act in a clumsy way.","The puppy will bumble around the yard, tripping over its own paws.","","A bumblebee bumbles: it flies in a wobbly, clumsy way."],["shimmer","SHIM-er","verb","To shine with a soft, shaky light.","The swimming pool seemed to shimmer in the bright afternoon sun.","","Shimmer sounds like glimmer: soft, wiggly sparkles."],["mammoth","MAM-uhth","adjective","Very, very big.","My brother made a mammoth sandwich that was taller than his face.","From Russian mamont, the name of the giant ice age animal.","A mammoth was a giant hairy elephant, so mammoth means huge."],["drowsy","DROW-zee","adjective","Sleepy and tired.","The warm car ride home made me so drowsy my eyes kept closing.","","Drowsy sounds like drooping eyes and a big drowsy yawn."],["disguise","dis-GYZE","noun","Clothes or makeup that hide who you are.","I wore a fake mustache as a disguise so no one knew me.","","Disguise: “this guy’s” not who you think."],["feast","FEEST","noun","A big, special meal with lots of food.","Grandma cooked a feast with turkey, pasta, pies and ten other dishes.","From Latin festa, “holidays.”","Feast has “east” in it: food from east to west fills the table."],["cinder","SIN-der","noun","A small piece of burned wood or coal.","A tiny cinder floated up from the campfire, glowing orange.","From Old English sinder.","Cinder sounds like Cinderella, who sat by the fireplace ashes."],["mural","MYUR-uhl","noun","A big painting made right on a wall.","Our class painted a giant mural of the ocean on the gym wall.","From Latin murus, “wall.”","Mur means wall in French, and a mural lives on a wall."],["fragile","FRAJ-uhl","adjective","Easy to break.","The box said “Fragile” because Mom’s glass bowl was inside, and glass breaks easily.","From Latin fragilis, “easily broken.”","Fragile sounds like “fra-jail”: keep breakable things safe in a jail."],["zany","ZAY-nee","adjective","Silly and wild in a funny way.","Our class made up a zany dance with chicken moves for the talent show.","","Zany sounds like brainy gone silly: wild, goofy ideas."],["clamber","KLAM-ber","verb","To climb using your hands and feet.","We clambered up the big rocks using our hands and feet.","","Clamber sounds like climb and scramble mixed together."],["igloo","IG-loo","noun","A round house made of snow blocks.","We built a small igloo out of snow blocks and crawled inside.","From Inuktitut iglu, “house.”","Igloo sounds like “ice glue”: blocks of ice glued into a house."],["expert","EK-spurt","noun","A person who knows a lot about something.","My sister is an expert at paper planes; hers always fly the farthest.","From Latin expertus, “tried, experienced.”","An expert is an “ex-PERT”: pretty great at one thing."],["portion","POR-shuhn","noun","A part or share of something.","Mom cut the pizza so everyone got an equal portion.","From Latin portio, “share.”","Portion sounds like part: your part, or share, of the pie."],["blaze","BLAYZ","noun","A big, bright fire.","The campfire grew into a bright blaze that kept us warm.","","Blaze sounds like “ablaze”: a big fire, blazing and bright."],["vast","VAST","adjective","Very, very large.","From the top of the slide, the beach looked vast and went on forever.","From Latin vastus, “huge, empty.”","Vast sounds like “vast-t-t”: stretching super far, way past."],["peer","PEER","verb","To look closely, as if it is hard to see.","I peered through the fog, trying to find my friend’s house.","","Peer sounds like pear: squinting hard to spot a tiny pear in a tree."],["blossom","BLOS-uhm","noun","A flower on a tree or plant.","A pink blossom popped open on the tree in our yard.","From Old English blōstm, “flower.”","Blossom has “bloss” like “bloom”: a flower blooming open."],["frisky","FRIS-kee","adjective","Playful and full of energy.","The frisky puppy bounced, rolled and chased its tail all morning.","","Frisky sounds like Frisbee: a frisky dog leaps for every Frisbee."],["crinkle","KRING-kuhl","verb","To wrinkle and make a crackly sound.","The candy wrapper crinkled in my hand.","","Crinkle sounds just like a crinkly wrapper."],["fierce","FEERS","adjective","Very strong and wild.","The fierce wind bent the trees and ripped the umbrella out of my hand.","From Latin ferus, “wild.”","Fierce sounds like fear-ce: something so wild it makes you afraid."],["crest","KREST","noun","The top of a hill or wave.","We stopped at the crest of the hill to catch our breath.","From Latin crista, “tuft, plume.”","Crest sounds like crown: the very top."],["plod","PLOD","verb","To walk slowly with heavy steps.","We plodded home through the deep snow in our heavy boots.","","Plod sounds like “plop, plop”: slow, heavy boots stomping along."],["invent","in-VENT","verb","To make something new that no one made before.","Jay wants to invent a robot that cleans his room for him.","From Latin invenire, “to come upon, find.”","Invent sounds like “in-vent”: a brand new idea pops out of the air vent."],["scamper","SKAM-per","verb","To run quickly and lightly, like a small animal.","The kittens scamper around the living room chasing a ball of yarn.","","Scamper sounds like scurry plus hamper: tiny feet running from the laundry."],["latch","LACH","noun","A small bar that holds a door or gate shut.","Close the gate latch so the dog can’t push it open.","","Latch rhymes with catch: it catches the door and holds it shut."],["quirky","KWUR-kee","adjective","A little strange in a fun way.","My friend’s quirky habit is giving every pencil a name.","","Quirky has “quirk” inside: a funny little thing that makes you you."],["amble","AM-buhl","verb","To walk slowly and in a relaxed way.","We like to amble through the park, taking our time and looking at the ducks.","From Latin ambulare, “to walk.”","Amble sounds like “ramble”: a slow, easy walk with no hurry."],["tattered","TAT-erd","adjective","Old and torn into rags.","My favorite teddy bear has a tattered ear from years of hugs.","","Tattered sounds like shattered: torn into little rags."],["hollow","HOL-oh","adjective","Empty inside.","We knocked on the hollow log and it sounded empty inside.","From Old English holh, “a hole.”","Hollow has two O’s, like two empty holes."],["dappled","DAP-uhld","adjective","Covered with spots of color, or spots of light and shade.","The pony had a dappled gray coat with white spots.","","Dappled sounds like “dabbled”: spots dabbed on like paint."],["depart","dih-PART","verb","To leave.","The train will depart at noon, so don’t be late.","","Depart sounds like “de-part”: you part ways and leave."],["creak","KREEK","verb","To make a long, squeaky sound.","The old stairs creak when I sneak down for a snack.","","Creak sounds just like a squeaky door: creeeak."],["tangle","TANG-guhl","noun","A messy knot of things twisted together.","My headphone cord was one big tangle at the bottom of my bag.","","Tangle sounds like spaghetti all tangled up on your fork."],["bashful","BASH-fuhl","adjective","Shy and easily embarrassed.","The bashful kitten hid behind the couch when guests came over.","","Bashful: so shy you’d rather hide than go to a big bash."],["awning","AW-ning","noun","A cloth roof over a window or door to give shade.","We waited under the store’s awning until the rain stopped.","","Awning sounds like “yawning”: a big open shade, like a yawning mouth."],["ancient","AYN-shuhnt","adjective","Very, very old.","My grandpa’s old toy car is so old it feels ancient.","","Ancient sounds like “ant’s cent”: a coin so old even the ants forgot it."],["bellow","BEL-oh","verb","To shout in a loud, deep voice.","The coach had to bellow, “Time out!” so we could hear him across the field.","","Bellow sounds like “bell low”: a deep, loud BONG of a voice."],["pollen","POL-uhn","noun","Yellow powder made by flowers.","When I sniffed the flower, yellow pollen got on my nose.","From Latin pollen, “fine flour.”","Pollen sounds like “pile on”: yellow dust piles onto bees and noses."],["scurry","SKUR-ee","verb","To run with quick, short steps.","The mice scurry under the fridge when the cat walks into the kitchen.","","Scurry sounds like hurry: tiny feet hurrying fast."],["bold","BOHLD","adjective","Brave and not afraid to take chances.","The bold kid raised her hand first, even though the question was hard.","From Old English bald, “brave.”","Bold letters stand out, and so do bold kids who are brave."],["chirp","CHURP","verb","To make a short, high sound like a small bird.","The baby birds chirp loudly when Mom brings food.","","Chirp sounds like a bird: chirp, chirp."],["instant","IN-stuhnt","noun","A very short moment.","The soap bubble popped in an instant, quicker than a blink.","From Latin instans, “being near, pressing.”","Instant noodles are ready in a flash, just like an instant."],["tidbit","TID-bit","noun","A small, tasty bit of food or news.","I gave my dog a tidbit of cheese for sitting nicely.","","Tidbit has “bit” in it: just a little bit of something good."],["wilt","WILT","verb","To droop because of no water or too much heat.","The flowers on my windowsill wilt if I forget to water them.","","Wilt sounds like “wilt-ed lettuce”: floppy, droopy and sad."],["observe","uhb-ZURV","verb","To watch something carefully.","If you observe the cat closely, you’ll see her tail twitch before she jumps.","From Latin observare, “to watch over.”","Observe rhymes with swerve: watch closely, or you’ll miss the ball’s swerve."],["swoop","SWOOP","verb","To dive down fast through the air.","The seagull will swoop down and grab your fries if you’re not careful.","","Swoop sounds like a swing going “swooooop” down fast."],["speck","SPEK","noun","A very tiny spot or bit.","There is a tiny speck of ketchup on your shirt.","","Speck sounds like a pecking bird: one tiny peck-sized spot."],["awkward","AWK-werd","adjective","Clumsy or a little uncomfortable.","I waved back at someone, but they were waving at the kid behind me. So awkward!","","Awkward sounds like “awk!”: the noise a bird makes when it trips."],["splendid","SPLEN-did","adjective","Very good or very grand.","We had a splendid time at the class picnic with games and cake.","From Latin splendidus, “shining.”","Splendid sounds like “splendid splash”: a big, happy, sparkly day."],["cocoon","kuh-KOON","noun","A silky case a caterpillar wraps itself in before it changes.","I wrapped up in my blanket like a cocoon.","From French cocon.","Cocoon sounds like “cozy room”: a caterpillar’s snug sleeping bag."],["acrobat","AK-ruh-bat","noun","A person who does flips, jumps, and balancing tricks.","The acrobat did three flips in the air and landed on one foot.","From Greek akrobatēs, “one who walks on tiptoe.”","Acrobat sounds like “a crow bat”: flipping and flying in the air."],["scrawny","SKRAW-nee","adjective","Very thin and bony.","The scrawny stray cat got fat and happy after we fed it.","","Scrawny sounds like a skinny scarecrow: all bones and straw."],["kennel","KEN-uhl","noun","A small house or shelter for a dog.","Our dog naps in his kennel, his own little dog house.","From Latin canis, “dog.”","Kennel sounds like “canine-el”: a canine’s little home."],["snoop","SNOOP","verb","To look around secretly where you should not.","My little sister likes to snoop in my room when I’m at school.","From Dutch snoepen, “to eat sweets on the sly.”","Snoop sounds like snoot: poking your nose where it shouldn’t be."],["chomp","CHOMP","verb","To bite or chew noisily.","My dog chomped on his crunchy bone.","","Chomp sounds like the bite: chomp, chomp, chomp."],["sturdy","STUR-dee","adjective","Strong and hard to break.","We built a sturdy pillow fort that didn’t fall even when the dog jumped on it.","","Sturdy sounds like “stood-y”: it stood strong and never fell."],["wobble","WOB-uhl","verb","To move from side to side in a shaky way.","My bike would wobble every time I tried to ride with no hands.","","Wobble sounds like a jelly going wibble-wobble on a plate."],["brittle","BRIT-uhl","adjective","Hard but easy to break or snap.","The cookie was so brittle that it snapped into pieces when I picked it up.","","Brittle sounds like “break little”: it breaks into little bits."],["drab","DRAB","adjective","Dull and not colorful.","We painted the drab gray fence bright yellow.","","Drab rhymes with blah: dull, plain and blah."],["notch","NOCH","noun","A small cut shaped like a V.","Dad made a notch on the door to show how much I grew this year.","","Notch sounds like “not much”: just a tiny V cut, not much at all."],["clumsy","KLUHM-zee","adjective","Likely to trip, drop or bump into things.","The clumsy puppy tripped over its own big paws.","","Clumsy sounds like clunky: big clunky feet bumping into everything."],["grove","GROHV","noun","A small group of trees.","We played hide and seek in a small grove of trees by the park.","From Old English grāf, “small wood.”","Grove sounds like groove: a little group of trees dancing together."],["knapsack","NAP-sak","noun","A bag you carry on your back.","I packed snacks and a water bottle in my knapsack for the hike.","From German Knappsack, “food bag.”","Knapsack sounds like “snack sack”: a sack on your back full of snacks."],["crimson","KRIM-zuhn","adjective","Deep red.","My cheeks turned crimson when everyone sang to me.","","Crimson sounds like “crimp-red”: the deepest, reddest red."],["bolt","BOHLT","verb","To run away very fast.","The cat will bolt under the bed as soon as the doorbell rings.","","A lightning bolt is super fast. So is a cat that bolts."],["descend","dih-SEND","verb","To go down.","We descended the snowy hill on our sleds.","From Latin descendere, “to climb down.”","Descend: de means down, so you go down."],["dangle","DANG-guhl","verb","To hang and swing loosely.","My legs dangle off the dock and swing above the water.","","Dangle sounds like jangle: keys dangling and swinging on a string."],["confident","KON-fi-duhnt","adjective","Sure that you can do something.","After lots of practice, I felt confident about my spelling test.","From Latin confidere, “to trust fully.”","Confident has “fide” from Latin for trust: you trust yourself."],["juggle","JUG-uhl","verb","To toss and catch several things, keeping them in the air.","My cousin can juggle three oranges and never drop one.","From Latin joculari, “to joke.”","Juggle sounds like jiggle: jiggle, toss, catch, toss, catch."],["dainty","DAYN-tee","adjective","Small, pretty and delicate.","My little sister held her teacup in a dainty little way.","From Latin dignitas, “worth.”","Dainty sounds like “dinky”: tiny, pretty and careful."],["compass","KUHM-puhs","noun","A tool with a needle that always points north.","We used a compass to find our way home on the hike.","","A compass points north so you don’t get lost."],["blurt","BLURT","verb","To say something suddenly without thinking.","I tried to keep the surprise a secret, but I blurted it out by accident!","","Blurt sounds like “burp”: it just pops out before you can stop it."],["dawdle","DAW-duhl","verb","To move slowly and waste time.","Don’t dawdle, or we’ll be late for school!","","Dawdle sounds like doodle: doodling around when you should be moving."],["plump","PLUMP","adjective","Nicely round and full.","The plump puppy waddled over to his food bowl.","","Plump sounds like plum: round and full like a juicy plum."],["avalanche","AV-uh-lanch","noun","A huge amount of snow sliding fast down a mountain.","An avalanche of snow slid off the roof and buried my snowman.","From French avalanche.","Avalanche sounds like “have a lunch”: snow crashes down on your lunch."],["yonder","YON-der","adverb","Over there, a little way off.","See that tree way over yonder? That’s where we buried the time capsule.","","Yonder sounds like “beyond there”: over there, a little ways away."],["tuft","TUFT","noun","A small bunch of hair, grass or feathers.","My little brother has a funny tuft of hair that sticks up every morning.","","Tuft sounds like a puff: a little puff of hair or grass."],["jingle","JING-guhl","verb","To make a light, ringing sound.","The bells on my cat’s collar jingle every time she runs.","","Jingle bells, jingle bells: say it and you hear the ring."],["murky","MUR-kee","adjective","Dark and hard to see through.","After the rain, the puddle was so murky I couldn’t see the bottom.","","Murky sounds like muddy: muddy water is murky water."],["budge","BUHJ","verb","To move just a little bit.","I pushed and pushed, but the heavy door would not budge.","From French bouger, “to stir.”","Budge sounds like “fudge”: stuck like a spoon in thick fudge."],["nestle","NES-uhl","verb","To settle into a warm, cozy spot.","I nestled under my warm blanket while it rained outside.","From Old English nestlian, “to make a nest.”","Nestle has “nest” in it: snuggle in like a bird in its nest."],["nocturnal","nok-TUR-nuhl","adjective","Awake and active at night.","Our hamster is nocturnal, so he runs on his wheel all night.","From Latin nocturnus, “of the night.”","Noct means night, so nocturnal animals party at night."],["lumpy","LUM-pee","adjective","Full of bumps or lumps.","The mashed potatoes were lumpy, full of little bumps.","","Lumpy has “lump” in it: lumps all over, like a bumpy pillow."],["mingle","MING-guhl","verb","To mix with others, often at a party.","At the birthday party, Mom mingled with the other parents by the snacks.","From Old English mengan, “to mix.”","Mingle rhymes with jingle: lots of bells all mixed together, jingling."],["wisp","WISP","noun","A thin, light bit of something.","A wisp of steam rose from my hot chocolate.","","Wisp sounds like a whisper: thin, light and barely there."],["shabby","SHAB-ee","adjective","Old and worn out.","Our shabby old couch has holes, but it’s still the comfiest seat in the house.","","Shabby sounds like flabby and crabby: old, saggy and worn out."],["explore","ik-SPLOR","verb","To look around a new place to learn about it.","On the first day at camp, we ran off to explore every trail and cabin.","From Latin explorare, “to search out.”","Explorers say “Ex-PLORE more!” as they peek around every corner."],["meadow","MED-oh","noun","A wide field of grass and wildflowers.","Our dog ran through the meadow behind school with grass up to her ears.","From Old English mǣd, “a grassy field.”","Picture a happy cow saying “mmm” as it munches in a grassy meadow."],["cling","KLING","verb","To hold on tight.","My baby brother clings to my leg when he’s shy.","From Old English clingan.","Cling sounds like cling wrap: it sticks on tight."],["eager","EE-ger","adjective","Wanting to do something very much.","Max was so eager for his party that he was dressed an hour early.","From Old French aigre, “sharp, keen.”","Eager sounds like eagle: an eagle can’t wait to swoop down and grab it."],["mimic","MIM-ik","verb","To copy the way someone talks or moves.","My little brother likes to mimic everything I say, which drives me nuts.","From Greek mimos, “actor.”","Mimic starts like mime: a mime copies your every move."],["abrupt","uh-BRUHPT","adjective","Very sudden and unexpected.","The game came to an abrupt stop when the rain started pouring.","From Latin abruptus, “broken off.”","Abrupt sounds like “a burp”: sudden and unexpected."],["contagious","kuhn-TAY-juhs","adjective","Easily spread from one person to another.","My giggle was so contagious that the whole class started laughing.","From Latin contingere, “to touch.”","Contagious things go from one person to the next, like yawns."],["cringe","KRINJ","verb","To pull back or make a face because something is awkward or gross.","I cringed when my baby brother tried to eat a worm.","","Cringe sounds like “crunch face”: eww."],["feeble","FEE-buhl","adjective","Weak and without much strength.","The baby bird gave a feeble little chirp, too weak to fly.","From Latin flebilis, “to be wept over.”","Feeble sounds like “feel bull”: feeling the opposite of a strong bull."],["outrageous","owt-RAY-juhs","adjective","Very surprising and over the top.","My friend wore an outrageous hat with a rubber chicken on top.","","Outrageous sounds like “out of range”: way, way past normal."],["barter","BAR-ter","verb","To trade things without using money.","We barter at lunch: my cookies for your chips. No money needed!","","Barter sounds like “better”: trade for something you like better."],["elated","ih-LAY-tid","adjective","Very happy and excited.","Sam was elated when his team won; he jumped and cheered.","From Latin elatus, “lifted up.”","Elated sounds like “elevated”: so happy you float up high."],["antique","an-TEEK","noun","An old object that is valuable because of its age.","Grandma’s rocking chair is an antique from long, long ago.","From Latin antiquus, “old.”","Antique sounds like “ant-teak”: so old even the ants are grandparents."],["fascinate","FAS-uh-nayt","verb","To hold someone’s interest very strongly.","Sharks fascinate my cousin; he reads about them every night.","From Latin fascinare, “to cast a spell on.”","Fascinate sounds like “fast-in-eight”: you get hooked fast."],["investigate","in-VES-tih-gayt","verb","To look into something to find out the truth.","We will investigate who ate the last cookie by checking for crumbs.","From Latin vestigium, “footprint.”","Investigate has “vest” in it: a detective in a vest hunting for clues."],["reveal","rih-VEEL","verb","To show something that was hidden.","Lift the cup to reveal the hidden marble.","From Latin revelare, “to unveil.”","Reveal sounds like “re-veil”: taking off the veil to show what’s under."],["billow","BIL-oh","verb","To puff up and swell out, like a sail in the wind.","The curtains billow like a big balloon when the wind blows in.","From Old Norse bylgja, “wave.”","Billow sounds like “pillow”: puffed up big and soft."],["obvious","OB-vee-uhs","adjective","Easy to see or understand.","It was obvious who ate the cake, because my brother had frosting on his face.","From Latin obvius, “in the way.”","Obvious sounds like “Oh, I see it!”: easy to spot right away."],["blemish","BLEM-ish","noun","A small mark or spot that spoils how something looks.","The apple was perfect except for one small brown blemish.","","Blemish sounds like “blem-ish”: a little blotch-ish spot."],["blustery","BLUHS-ter-ee","adjective","Very windy.","On blustery days, the wind blows my hat right off my head.","","Blustery has “blust” like “blast”: blasts of wind."],["tolerate","TOL-uh-rayt","verb","To put up with something you don’t like.","I can tolerate my little brother’s singing, but only for a minute.","From Latin tolerare, “to bear.”","Tolerate: you let it go on, even if you rate it low."],["aspire","uh-SPIRE","verb","To hope and work to become something.","I aspire to be a great chess player one day.","From Latin aspirare, “to breathe upon.”","Aspire sounds like “a spire”: aim high, like the tip of a tower."],["dedicate","DED-i-kayt","verb","To give your time and effort to something.","She dedicated her whole Saturday to cleaning up the park.","","Dedicate: you give it all your time, like a coach at every practice."],["convince","kuhn-VINS","verb","To get someone to believe or agree with something.","I convinced my parents to let me get a hamster.","","Convince sounds like “win, since”: you win them over."],["vessel","VES-uhl","noun","A ship or a container that holds liquid.","The fishing vessel came back to the dock with a big catch.","","Vessel sounds like “vest-hold”: a boat or cup that holds things."],["lanky","LANG-kee","adjective","Tall and thin, with long arms and legs.","My lanky cousin is tall and skinny with long arms and legs.","","Lanky sounds like “lank-key”: long and skinny like a key."],["exhausted","ig-ZAWS-tid","adjective","Very, very tired.","After soccer practice, I was so exhausted I fell asleep in the car.","From Latin exhaurire, “to draw out, drain.”","Exhausted sounds like “exhaust”: all your gas is used up."],["edible","ED-uh-buhl","adjective","Safe to eat.","The flowers on the cake were edible, so I ate one.","From Latin edere, “to eat.”","Edible sounds like “eat-able”: able to be eaten."],["limber","LIM-ber","adjective","Able to bend and move easily.","We stretch before gym to stay limber and bend easily.","","Limber has “limb” in it: arms and legs that bend easily."],["gradual","GRAJ-oo-uhl","adjective","Happening slowly, a little at a time.","The ice cream melted in a gradual way, one slow drip at a time.","From Latin gradus, “a step.”","Gradual sounds like grade: you go up one grade at a time."],["budget","BUHJ-it","noun","A plan for how to spend money.","I made a budget so I could save five dollars a week for a new skateboard.","From Old French bougette, “a little bag.”","Budget sounds like “budge it”: don’t budge from your money plan."],["bargain","BAR-gin","noun","Something you buy for less than it is worth.","I got a bike at the yard sale for only five dollars. What a bargain!","","Bargain has “gain” in it: you gain a lot for a little money."],["recite","rih-SYT","verb","To say something out loud from memory.","Each of us had to recite a short poem in front of the class.","From Latin recitare, “to read aloud.”","Recite sounds like “re-say it”: saying it again from memory."],["preserve","prih-ZURV","verb","To keep something safe from harm or change.","We put the leftover pie in the fridge to preserve it.","From Latin praeservare, “to guard beforehand.”","Fruit preserves are jam: fruit kept safe in a jar for later."],["assume","uh-SOOM","verb","To think something is true without checking.","I assumed the test would be easy, but I should have checked first.","From Latin assumere, “to take up.”","Assume sounds like “a zoom”: you zoom to an answer without checking."],["plummet","PLUM-it","verb","To fall fast and far.","The kite plummeted to the ground when the wind stopped.","From Old French plommet, “lead weight.”","Plummet sounds like plum: a plum dropping straight down from a tree."],["ajar","uh-JAR","adjective","Slightly open.","The door was ajar, open just a crack, so the cat slipped inside.","","Ajar sounds like “a jar”: a jar lid left a little open."],["evaporate","ih-VAP-uh-rayt","verb","To change from water into a gas and disappear into the air.","After the rain, the puddles began to evaporate and vanish in the hot sun.","From Latin vapor, “steam.”","Evaporate has “vapor” inside: water turns to vapor and floats away."],["lunar","LOO-ner","adjective","Having to do with the moon.","We stayed up late to see the lunar glow from the full moon.","From Latin luna, “moon.”","Lunar sounds like “Luna,” a name for the moon in some languages."],["expand","ik-SPAND","verb","To get bigger.","The balloon will expand bigger and bigger as you blow into it.","From Latin expandere, “to spread out.”","Expand has “pan” in it: dough puffs up and fills the whole pan."],["solitary","SOL-ih-tair-ee","adjective","Alone, or living alone.","A solitary cat sat on the fence, watching the birds alone.","From Latin solus, “alone.”","Sol is like solo: solitary means all alone."],["assure","uh-SHOOR","verb","To tell someone something to take away their worry.","“I assure you the dog is friendly,” said my neighbor, and the dog licked my hand.","","Assure has “sure” in it: you make someone feel sure."],["persevere","pur-suh-VEER","verb","To keep trying even when it is hard.","I kept falling off my bike, but I persevered until I could ride.","From Latin perseverare, “to persist.”","Persevere has “severe” inside: keep going when things get severe."],["bulletin","BUL-ih-tin","noun","A short news report or note.","The school bulletin said the book fair starts on Monday.","","A bulletin board holds notes with the latest news."],["sparse","SPARS","adjective","Thinly spread out, not much of it.","Dad’s hair is getting sparse on top, so he wears a cap.","","Sparse sounds like spare: only a few spare bits here and there."],["identical","eye-DEN-tih-kuhl","adjective","Exactly the same.","The twins wore identical red sweaters, so we couldn’t tell them apart.","From Latin idem, “the same.”","Identical sounds like ID: two ID cards with the exact same face."],["dismay","dis-MAY","noun","A sad, worried feeling when something goes wrong.","To my dismay, my ice cream fell off the cone.","","Dismay: “this may not end well!” Oh no."],["typical","TIP-ih-kuhl","adjective","Normal or usual.","On a typical Saturday, we eat pancakes and watch cartoons.","","Typical has “type” in it: the usual type of thing."],["volunteer","vol-uhn-TEER","verb","To offer to help without being asked or paid.","I volunteer to feed the class hamster every Friday.","","Volunteer: you raise your hand and say “I’ll do it!” for free."],["cower","KOW-er","verb","To crouch down in fear.","The puppy cowered under the table during the thunderstorm.","","Cower sounds like “cower in a tower”: hiding small and scared."],["obedient","oh-BEE-dee-uhnt","adjective","Doing what you are told.","Our obedient dog sits as soon as we say “sit.”","From Latin oboedire, “to obey.”","Obedient sounds like “obey-dient”: someone who obeys."],["nomad","NOH-mad","noun","A person who moves from place to place instead of living in one home.","My aunt lives like a nomad, driving her camper to a new spot every month.","From Greek nomas, “roaming to find pasture.”","Nomad sounds like “no mad”: no fixed home, just happy to roam."],["novel","NOV-uhl","adjective","New and different.","My friend had a novel idea: a lemonade stand that also sells cookies.","From Latin novus, “new.”","Nov means new, so a novel idea is a brand-new one."],["hinder","HIN-der","verb","To get in the way or slow something down.","The muddy path will hinder our bike ride and slow us down.","","Hinder sounds like “hind”: something holding you back from behind."],["sincere","sin-SEER","adjective","Honest and real.","My friend gave me a sincere hug after I lost the race.","From Latin sincerus, “pure, whole.”","Sincere sounds like “sin-sure”: for sure, from the heart."],["urgent","UR-juhnt","adjective","Needing to be done right away.","Mom said it was urgent to get the puppy to the vet right away.","","Urgent sounds like “Hurry, gent!”: do it right now."],["utensil","yoo-TEN-suhl","noun","A tool used for cooking or eating.","A spoon is my favorite eating utensil for ice cream.","From Latin utensilia, “useful things.”","Utensil sounds like “use-tensil”: a tool you use for food."],["swivel","SWIV-uhl","verb","To turn around on one spot.","My desk chair can swivel all the way around.","","Swivel sounds like “swirl”: spin in place without moving away."],["sympathy","SIM-puh-thee","noun","Feeling sorry for someone who is sad or hurt.","I felt sympathy for my friend when her goldfish died.","From Greek sympatheia, “feeling with.”","Sym means together: feeling sad together with someone."],["pursue","per-SOO","verb","To chase or follow something you want.","My dog pursued the ball all the way down the hill.","From Old French poursuir, “to follow.”","Pursue sounds like “purse chase”: chasing a runaway purse down the street."],["probable","PROB-uh-buhl","adjective","Likely to happen.","With these dark clouds, rain is probable.","From Latin probabilis, “provable.”","Probable sounds like probably: it will probably happen."],["mobile","MOH-buhl","adjective","Able to move or be moved easily.","The food truck is mobile, so it drives to a new park each day.","From Latin mobilis, “movable.”","A mobile phone goes everywhere with you because it can move."],["monitor","MON-ih-ter","verb","To watch or check on something over time.","The coach monitored our running times all season.","From Latin monere, “to warn.”","A baby monitor keeps checking on the baby all night long."],["determined","dih-TUR-mind","adjective","Not giving up until you reach your goal.","He was determined to ride his bike without training wheels.","","Determined has “term” like terminal: you won’t stop till the end."],["zoologist","zoh-OL-uh-jist","noun","A scientist who studies animals.","The zoologist told our class how lions sleep up to 20 hours a day.","From Greek zoion, “animal.”","Zoo is in zoologist: a scientist who studies zoo animals and more."],["disrupt","dis-RUHPT","verb","To interrupt or break up something.","The fire drill disrupted our spelling test.","From Latin disrumpere, “to break apart.”","Dis means apart and rupt means break: it breaks things up."],["minimum","MIN-uh-muhm","noun","The least or the smallest amount allowed.","You need a minimum of four players to start the game.","From Latin minimus, “smallest.”","Mini means small: the minimum is the smallest amount."],["fertile","FUR-tuhl","adjective","Good for growing lots of plants.","The fertile soil in Grandma’s garden grows tomatoes as big as softballs.","From Latin fertilis, “bearing fruit.”","Fertile sounds like “fertilizer”: plant food for super-growing soil."],["fiction","FIK-shuhn","noun","Stories that are made up.","My favorite fiction book is about a dragon who bakes pies, which isn’t real.","From Latin fingere, “to shape, make up.”","Fiction sounds like “fake-tion”: a made-up story."],["jubilant","JOO-buh-luhnt","adjective","Full of joy, like after a big win.","The jubilant team cheered and danced after they won the trophy.","From Latin jubilare, “to shout for joy.”","Jubilant sounds like “jump-a-lot”: so happy you jump a lot."],["deserve","dih-ZURV","verb","To earn something because of what you did.","After all that practice, you deserve a rest.","","Deserve has “serve”: you served your time, so you earned it."],["prosper","PROS-per","verb","To do well and grow strong.","Our lemonade stand prospered, and we earned enough for a new ball.","From Latin prosperare, “to make happy.”","Prosper starts with “pro”: you do as well as a pro."],["resourceful","rih-SORS-fuhl","adjective","Good at finding smart ways to solve problems.","The resourceful kids built a robot costume out of cardboard boxes.","","Resourceful: full of resources, finding smart uses for anything around."],["remedy","REM-uh-dee","noun","Something that fixes a problem or helps you heal.","Warm soup is Mom’s favorite remedy for a cold.","From Latin remedium, “cure.”","Remedy sounds like “re-mend-y”: it mends you back up."],["hurdle","HUR-duhl","noun","A bar to jump over in a race, or a problem you have to get past.","Jen jumped over the last hurdle and won the race.","","Hurdle sounds like “hurtle over”: run and leap over the bar."],["foliage","FOH-lee-ij","noun","The leaves of plants and trees.","In the fall, the foliage on the trees turns red, orange and gold.","From Latin folium, “leaf.”","Foliage sounds like “foil-age”: leaves shiny like gold foil in the fall."],["attain","uh-TAYN","verb","To reach or get something after effort.","She practiced all year to attain her swimming badge.","From Latin attingere, “to touch, reach.”","Attain sounds like “a train”: ride the train all the way to your goal."],["inspect","in-SPEKT","verb","To look at something closely and carefully.","The coach will inspect our bike helmets to make sure they are safe.","From Latin inspicere, “to look into.”","Inspect sounds like inspector: a detective with a magnifying glass looks closely."],["confine","kuhn-FYNE","verb","To keep inside a small space.","We confined the puppy to the kitchen while we were out.","","Confine has “fine” like finish line: you can’t go past the edge."],["durable","DUR-uh-buhl","adjective","Strong and able to last a long time.","These durable rain boots lasted through a whole muddy year.","From Latin durare, “to last.”","Durable sounds like “endurable”: tough stuff that lasts and lasts."],["complicated","KOM-pli-kay-tid","adjective","Hard to understand because it has many parts.","The instructions for the toy robot were too complicated to follow.","","Complicated sounds like “complain-icated”: so many parts you want to complain."],["boisterous","BOY-ster-uhs","adjective","Loud, lively, and full of energy.","The boisterous kids yelled and jumped around at the birthday party.","","Boisterous sounds like “boys-ter-ous”: loud kids having a noisy party."],["cultivate","KUHL-tuh-vayt","verb","To grow and care for plants.","We cultivate tomatoes in our backyard garden.","From Latin colere, “to till the soil.”","Cultivate sounds like “cultivator,” a tool that digs up the garden."],["clamor","KLAM-er","noun","A loud, steady noise of many voices.","The clamor of the lunchroom made it hard to hear my friend.","From Latin clamare, “to cry out.”","Clamor sounds like “clam roar”: a crowd of noisy clams all shouting."],["horizon","huh-RY-zuhn","noun","The line where the sky seems to meet the land or sea.","We watched the sun sink down at the horizon, where the sky meets the sea.","From Greek horizon, “bounding circle.”","Horizon sounds like “horizontal”: the long flat line across the sky."],["mascot","MAS-kot","noun","An animal, person or thing that brings a team good luck.","Our school mascot is a big fuzzy bear who dances at games.","From French mascotte, “lucky charm.”","Mascot sounds like “mask-cot”: someone in a costume cheering the team."],["compliment","KOM-pluh-muhnt","noun","Something nice you say about someone.","My friend gave me a compliment on my drawing.","","Compliment sounds like “compli-MINT”: kind words as sweet as mint."],["landmark","LAND-mark","noun","A building or place that is easy to see and helps you know where you are.","The big water tower is a landmark; when we see it, we know we’re almost home.","","Landmark is a mark on the land that helps you find your way."],["barrier","BAIR-ee-er","noun","Something that blocks the way.","The baby gate is a barrier that keeps the puppy out of the kitchen.","","Barrier has “bar” in it: a bar that blocks your way."],["vary","VAIR-ee","verb","To change or be different.","The flavors of jelly beans vary from cherry to lemon.","","Vary sounds like “very different”: things change and aren’t the same."],["strategy","STRAT-uh-jee","noun","A plan to reach a goal.","Our strategy in hide-and-seek was to hide behind the slide.","From Greek strategos, “army leader.”","Strategy sounds like “straight to victory”: a plan to win."],["trivial","TRIV-ee-uhl","adjective","Small and not very important.","Don’t fight over something as trivial as a blue crayon.","","Trivial sounds like trivia: little facts that don’t really matter."],["befriend","bih-FREND","verb","To become a friend to someone.","I tried to befriend the new kid by sharing my snack.","","Befriend has “friend” in it: to be a friend."],["misplace","mis-PLAYS","verb","To put something somewhere and forget where.","I misplaced my homework and later found it in the fridge.","","Mis means wrong: misplace means to put it in the wrong place."],["query","KWEER-ee","noun","A question you ask to find something out.","I had a query for my teacher about the homework.","From Latin quaerere, “to ask.”","Query sounds like “curious”: a curious question."],["erupt","ih-RUPT","verb","To burst out suddenly.","Our baking soda volcano began to erupt with foam all over the table.","From Latin erumpere, “to break out.”","Erupt and rupture share a root meaning burst: it bursts open, kaboom."],["compact","kuhm-PAKT","adjective","Small and packed tightly.","My compact tent folds up small enough to fit in my backpack.","","Compact sounds like “packed”: squished small and tight."],["scheme","SKEEM","noun","A clever plan.","We came up with a scheme to surprise Mom on her birthday.","From Greek skhema, “form, shape.”","Scheme rhymes with dream: a clever plan you dream up."],["interrupt","in-tuh-RUPT","verb","To stop someone while they are talking or doing something.","Please don’t interrupt your friend while she is telling her story.","From Latin interrumpere, “to break apart.”","Inter means between: you jump in between someone’s words."],["ambition","am-BISH-uhn","noun","A strong wish to do or become something.","My ambition is to be a vet so I can help sick animals.","From Latin ambitio, “a going around.”","Ambition sounds like “am-bit-ion”: I am going for it, bit by bit."],["pledge","PLEJ","noun","A serious promise.","I made a pledge to walk the dog every morning.","","Pledge rhymes with edge: a promise you won’t back away from."],["estimate","ES-tuh-mayt","verb","To make a careful guess about an amount.","Can you estimate how many jelly beans are in the jar without counting?","From Latin aestimare, “to value.”","Estimate sounds like “guess-timate”: a smart guess."],["genuine","JEN-yoo-in","adjective","Real and true, not fake.","Her smile was genuine; she really was happy to see the surprise.","From Latin genuinus, “natural, real.”","Genuine sounds like “gen-u-WINE”: the real deal, not a copy."],["lush","LUSH","adjective","Thick, green and full of plants.","After the rain, the yard was lush and green, with grass up to my knees.","","Lush sounds like “mush”: so much green it’s squishy and soft."],["accomplish","uh-KOM-plish","verb","To finish something you set out to do.","I did it! I finished the whole puzzle and accomplished my goal.","From Old French acompliss-, “to complete.”","Accomplish has “complete” inside its roots: you finish the job."],["monotonous","muh-NOT-uh-nuhs","adjective","Boring because it never changes.","Copying the same spelling word fifty times was so monotonous.","From Greek monotonos, “of one tone.”","Mono means one, and tone means sound: one sound over and over."],["summit","SUM-it","noun","The very top of a mountain.","We ate sandwiches when we reached the summit of the hill.","","Summit sounds like “some it”: climb some more and you hit the top."],["dependable","dih-PEN-duh-buhl","adjective","Able to be trusted to do what you said.","Our dependable neighbor feeds our fish every summer.","","Dependable means you can depend on them every time."],["destination","des-tuh-NAY-shuhn","noun","The place you are going to.","Our destination for the field trip was the aquarium.","","Destination sounds like destiny: the place you are headed."],["diligent","DIL-i-juhnt","adjective","Hard-working and careful.","The diligent student checked every math answer twice.","From Latin diligere, “to value.”","Diligent sounds like “dig-it-gent”: someone who digs in and works hard."],["colony","KOL-uh-nee","noun","A group of animals of one kind that live together.","An ant colony lives under our sidewalk.","","A colony is a big ant family all living in one town."],["witty","WIT-ee","adjective","Clever and funny with words.","My witty friend made the whole lunch table laugh with one joke.","","Witty has “wit” in it: quick, clever and funny."],["nuisance","NOO-suhns","noun","Someone or something that is annoying.","The buzzing fly was a nuisance during our picnic.","From Old French nuisance, “harm.”","Nuisance sounds like “noise-ance”: a noise that keeps bugging you."],["nominate","NOM-uh-nayt","verb","To suggest someone for a job or prize.","Our class nominated Sam to be team captain.","From Latin nominare, “to name.”","Nom means name: to nominate is to name someone for a prize."],["reliable","rih-LY-uh-buhl","adjective","Able to be trusted every time.","My dog is reliable: he barks every single time the mail comes.","","Reliable has “rely” in it: something you can rely on."],["ancestor","AN-ses-ter","noun","A family member who lived long before you.","My ancestor came to this country on a big ship long, long ago.","From Latin antecessor, “one who goes before.”","Ancestor has “ance” like “ance-ient”: family from ancient times."],["dwell","DWEL","verb","To live in a place.","Owls dwell in hollow trees.","","Dwell rhymes with shell: a snail dwells in its shell."],["notion","NOH-shuhn","noun","An idea or belief.","My sister had the silly notion that the cat could talk.","From Latin notio, “an idea.”","Notion sounds like note: an idea you jot down in your head."],["duplicate","DOO-pli-kayt","verb","To make an exact copy.","Dad duplicated the house key so I could have my own.","From Latin duplex, “double.”","Du means two, like a duet: one thing becomes two."],["conceal","kuhn-SEEL","verb","To hide.","I concealed the birthday gift under my bed.","From Latin celare, “to hide.”","Conceal sounds like “can’t see it”: it’s hidden."],["glum","GLUM","adjective","Sad and quiet.","Ben looked glum and quiet when the game got rained out.","","Glum sounds like gum stuck under a shoe: sad and stuck."],["brief","BREEF","adjective","Short, not lasting long.","The teacher gave a brief talk, just one minute, before recess.","From Latin brevis, “short.”","Brief sounds like “brief-case”: short and quick, then off you go."],["recede","rih-SEED","verb","To move back or away.","At the beach, we watched the waves recede and leave shells behind.","From Latin recedere, “to go back.”","Re means back: recede means go back, like waves going home."],["considerate","kuhn-SID-er-it","adjective","Thoughtful about other people’s feelings.","It was considerate of you to share your umbrella.","","Considerate means you consider, or think about, others."],["dread","DRED","verb","To feel worried about something that is coming.","I dread cleaning my room on Saturdays.","","Dread sounds like “dead”: you feel so heavy thinking about it."],["devour","dih-VOWR","verb","To eat something quickly and hungrily.","The kids devoured the whole pizza in two minutes.","From Latin devorare, “to swallow down.”","Picture a hungry dragon that devours a whole pizza in one gulp."],["cunning","KUHN-ing","adjective","Clever in a sneaky way.","The cunning fox snuck into the chicken coop.","","Cunning sounds like “conning”: tricking with a sneaky plan."],["awe","AW","noun","A feeling of wonder and amazement.","We stared in awe at the giant fireworks. “Awww!”","","Awe sounds like “aww!”: the sound you make when something amazes you."],["graze","GRAYZ","verb","To eat grass in a field.","The cows graze in the field, munching grass all morning.","From Old English grasian, from græs, “grass.”","Graze has “gra” like grass: animals nibbling on grass."],["flimsy","FLIM-zee","adjective","Thin and weak, easy to break or tear.","The flimsy paper plate bent and my pizza slid onto the floor.","","Flimsy sounds like “flip-sy”: so weak it flips and folds."],["monument","MON-yuh-muhnt","noun","A statue or building made to remember someone or something.","We visited a monument built to remember brave firefighters.","From Latin monumentum, “a reminder.”","Monument sounds like moment: a statue that helps us remember a moment."],["previous","PREE-vee-uhs","adjective","Coming just before.","My previous phone case broke, so I got a new one.","From Latin praevius, “going before.”","Pre means before: previous means the one before."],["bystander","BY-stan-der","noun","A person who is nearby but not taking part.","A bystander on the sidewalk picked up our ball and threw it back.","","Bystander: someone who stands by and watches."],["wince","WINS","verb","To make a quick face because of pain.","I had to wince when I stubbed my toe on the bed.","","Wince sounds like “ow-since”: a quick “ow” face."],["nautical","NAW-tih-kuhl","adjective","Having to do with ships and sailing.","My room has a nautical theme, with boats and anchors on the walls.","From Greek nautes, “sailor.”","Naut means sailor, like an astronaut, a star sailor."],["eclipse","ih-KLIPS","noun","When the moon blocks the sun, or the earth blocks the moon.","During the eclipse, the sun went dark in the middle of the day.","From Greek ekleipsis, “a leaving out, failing.”","Eclipse sounds like “a clip”: the moon clips the sun’s light away."],["neutral","NOO-truhl","adjective","Not taking either side.","When my brother and sister argued, I stayed neutral and ate my toast.","From Latin neuter, “neither.”","A car in neutral goes neither way: no side at all."],["scarce","SKAIRS","adjective","Hard to find because there is not much.","Snacks are scarce in our house the day before grocery shopping.","","Scarce sounds like scared: the snacks are hiding because there are so few."],["triumph","TRY-umf","noun","A big win or success.","Finishing the 1,000-piece puzzle was a triumph for the whole family.","","Triumph sounds like “try and umph!”: you tried hard and won."],["distract","dih-STRAKT","verb","To pull someone’s attention away.","A squirrel outside the window distracted me from my reading.","From Latin distrahere, “to pull apart.”","Dis means apart and tract means pull: your focus gets pulled away."],["appreciate","uh-PREE-shee-ayt","verb","To be thankful for something or someone.","I appreciate my friend sharing her colored pencils with me.","From Latin pretium, “price.”","Appreciate sounds like “a precious state”: you treat it like it’s precious."],["vibrate","VY-brayt","verb","To shake back and forth very fast.","My phone will vibrate on the table when Grandma calls.","From Latin vibrare, “to shake.”","Vibrate sounds like a buzzy bee: bzzzz, shaking fast."],["marine","muh-REEN","adjective","Having to do with the sea.","We saw marine animals like seals and crabs at the aquarium.","From Latin mare, “sea.”","Mar is an old word for sea, and marine animals live in the sea."],["vague","VAYG","adjective","Not clear.","When I asked where my cookies went, my brother gave a vague answer.","","Vague sounds like “vague fog”: fuzzy and not clear."],["adapt","uh-DAPT","verb","To change to fit a new place or situation.","Our new puppy had to adapt to living with two bossy cats.","From Latin adaptare, “to fit to.”","Adapt sounds like “a dab”: change your colors to fit in, like a chameleon."],["desperate","DES-per-it","adjective","Needing something very badly.","The thirsty dog was desperate for a drink of water.","From Latin desperare, “to lose hope.”","Desperate sounds like “des-pair-it”: you need it so badly."],["prompt","PROMPT","adjective","On time, without delay.","Be prompt for practice, or the coach makes you run laps.","From Latin promptus, “ready.”","Be prompt: when the bell rings, you’re already in your seat."],["restless","REST-lis","adjective","Not able to stay still or calm.","The dog gets restless when it hasn’t had a walk.","","Restless means “less rest”: you just can’t sit still."],["blunder","BLUHN-der","noun","A careless or silly mistake.","I wore my shirt inside out to school. What a blunder!","","Blunder sounds like “blooper”: a silly mistake everyone sees."],["temporary","TEM-puh-rair-ee","adjective","Lasting only a short time.","The tattoo from the fair was only temporary and washed off in the bath.","From Latin tempus, “time.”","Temporary sounds like tempo: here for a short beat, then gone."],["pastime","PAS-tym","noun","Something you do for fun.","Drawing comics is my favorite pastime.","","Pastime is “pass time”: something fun to pass the time."],["acquire","uh-KWIRE","verb","To get or gain something.","I hope to acquire a new card for my collection this weekend.","From Latin acquirere, “to get more.”","Acquire sounds like “a choir”: a choir gets new singers to join."],["tedious","TEE-dee-uhs","adjective","Long, slow and boring.","Sorting all the LEGO pieces by color was tedious.","","Tedious sounds like “tea-dious”: waiting forever for tea to cool."],["occasion","uh-KAY-zhuhn","noun","A special time or event.","My birthday is a special occasion, so we eat pancakes for dinner.","From Latin occasio, “opportunity.”","Occasion sounds like “a cake-asion”: a special time with cake."],["constant","KON-stuhnt","adjective","Happening all the time without stopping.","The constant drip of the faucet kept me awake all night.","","Constant sounds like “can’t stop”: it keeps going and going."],["unruly","un-ROO-lee","adjective","Hard to control.","My unruly hair sticks up no matter how much I brush it.","","Unruly means “un-rule-y”: it won’t follow the rules."],["debris","duh-BREE","noun","Scattered pieces of something broken or left behind.","After the storm, we picked up branches and debris in the yard.","From French débris, “broken pieces.”","Debris sounds like “the breeze” left a mess of broken bits."],["precise","prih-SYS","adjective","Exact and careful.","You need precise cuts to make a paper snowflake look nice.","From Latin praecisus, “cut short.”","Precise sounds like “pre-slice”: cutting each slice just right."],["bedraggled","bih-DRAG-uhld","adjective","Wet, messy, and dirty.","The bedraggled dog came in from the rain, wet and covered in mud.","","Bedraggled has “drag” in it: like you were dragged through mud."],["decay","dih-KAY","verb","To slowly rot or break down.","The old pumpkin started to decay and turn mushy.","","Decay sounds like tooth decay: rot that eats things away."],["modest","MOD-ist","adjective","Not bragging about yourself.","Even though she scored three goals, Lila was modest and thanked her team.","From Latin modestus, “keeping within measure.”","A modest kid thanks the team instead of shouting “look at me” loudly."],["patrol","puh-TROHL","verb","To walk around an area to keep it safe.","The crossing guard patrols the street before school.","","Patrol sounds like “pat-roll”: walking the same loop, keeping watch."],["ally","AL-eye","noun","A friend who helps you and is on your side.","My best friend is my ally. She always sticks up for me.","","Ally sounds like “all-y”: someone who is all on your side."],["apparent","uh-PAIR-uhnt","adjective","Easy to see or understand.","The cake crumbs on the dog’s nose made it apparent who ate the cake.","From Latin apparere, “to appear.”","Apparent has “appear” in its root: it appears right in front of you."],["dismal","DIZ-muhl","adjective","Gloomy and sad.","We stayed inside all day because of the dismal gray rain.","From Latin dies mali, “bad days.”","Dismal sounds like “this smells”: gloomy and yucky."],["parallel","PAIR-uh-lel","adjective","Side by side and always the same distance apart.","The two train tracks are parallel and never touch.","From Greek parallelos, “beside one another.”","Parallel has two L’s standing side by side, like parallel lines."],["occupy","OK-yuh-py","verb","To fill a space or time.","Our coats and bags occupied every seat in the car.","From Latin occupare, “to seize.”","“Occupied” on a bathroom door means someone is filling that space."],["thrive","THRYV","verb","To grow strong and healthy.","Our class plant will thrive if we give it sun and water.","","Thrive sounds like “thrive and arrive alive”: growing big and strong."],["maximum","MAK-suh-muhm","noun","The most or the highest amount allowed.","The maximum number of kids on the trampoline is three.","From Latin maximus, “greatest.”","Max means the most: turn the volume all the way to max."],["forecast","FOR-kast","noun","A guess about what will happen, like the weather.","The weather forecast says it will snow on Saturday, so get your sled ready.","","Fore means before: a forecast tells you before it happens."],["tension","TEN-shuhn","noun","A tight, uneasy feeling.","You could feel the tension in the gym before the last free throw.","","Tension sounds like “tense”: everyone holding their breath."],["overcome","oh-ver-KUM","verb","To get past a problem or fear.","I overcame my fear of the high dive and jumped right in.","","Over plus come: you come up and over your problem."],["intense","in-TENS","adjective","Very strong.","The heat from the oven was so intense that I stepped back.","From Latin intensus, “stretched tight.”","Intense sounds like “in tents”: super hot like a tent in the summer sun."],["cram","KRAM","verb","To stuff too much into a small space.","I crammed all my toys into one tiny box.","","Cram sounds like jam: jam it all in."],["proceed","proh-SEED","verb","To go on or keep going.","After lunch, we proceeded to the next stop on our field trip.","From Latin procedere, “to go forward.”","Pro means forward: proceed means to go forward."],["clench","KLENCH","verb","To close or squeeze tightly.","I clenched my fists as the roller coaster climbed.","","Clench sounds like a clamp: squeeze tight."],["detour","DEE-toor","noun","A different, longer way around.","We took a detour because the road was closed.","From French détour, “a turning aside.”","A detour is a tour around the long way."],["capable","KAY-puh-buhl","adjective","Able to do something well.","My little brother is capable of tying his own shoes now.","","Capable has “able” in it: you are able to do it."],["courteous","KUR-tee-uhs","adjective","Polite and kind.","The courteous boy held the door open for everyone.","","Courteous sounds like “curtsy”: polite and kind."],["murmur","MUR-mer","noun","A soft, low sound.","We heard a soft murmur of voices from the kitchen.","From Latin murmur, “a humming sound.”","Murmur says the same soft sound twice: “mur, mur,” like a whisper."],["achieve","uh-CHEEV","verb","To reach a goal by working hard.","I practiced every day to achieve my goal of riding without training wheels.","From Old French achever, “to finish.”","Achieve sounds like “a cheer”: you cheer when you achieve."],["boggle","BOG-uhl","verb","To amaze or confuse the mind.","The number of stars in the sky can boggle your mind.","","Boggle sounds like “goggle”: your eyes goggle when your mind is amazed."],["wholesome","HOHL-suhm","adjective","Good and healthy for your body or mind.","Apple slices and a funny movie made a wholesome night in.","","Wholesome sounds like “whole some”: good for the whole of you."],["potential","puh-TEN-shuhl","noun","What someone or something could become.","Coach says our new player has the potential to be a star.","From Latin potentia, “power.”","Potential sounds like pot: a tiny seed in a pot that could grow huge."],["anticipate","an-TIS-uh-payt","verb","To expect something and get ready for it.","I anticipate a long line at the ice cream truck, so I’ll go early.","From Latin anticipare, “to take before.”","Anticipate sounds like “auntie’s plate”: you know auntie’s plate is coming, so get ready."],["retreat","rih-TREET","verb","To move back or away from something.","The turtle will retreat into its shell if you get too close.","","Re means back: retreat means go back, like a turtle hiding."],["fortunate","FOR-chuh-nit","adjective","Lucky.","We were fortunate to find a twenty-dollar bill on the sidewalk.","From Latin fortuna, “luck.”","Fortunate has “fortune” in it, like a fortune cookie saying good luck."],["valiant","VAL-yuhnt","adjective","Very brave.","The valiant kid in our play saved the village from the dragon.","","Valiant sounds like “valor ant”: a tiny ant that’s super brave."],["dialogue","DY-uh-log","noun","The words people say to each other in a story or play.","I practiced my dialogue for the class play.","From Greek dialogos, “conversation.”","Dia means across: words going back and forth across the stage."],["attentive","uh-TEN-tiv","adjective","Paying close attention.","The attentive student noticed the spelling mistake on the board.","","Attentive has “attent” like “attention”: paying close attention."],["vapor","VAY-per","noun","Tiny drops of water floating in the air.","Vapor rose from my hot cocoa on the cold morning.","","Vapor sounds like “paper” floating up: light, wispy steam."],["practical","PRAK-tih-kuhl","adjective","Useful and sensible.","Rain boots are more practical than sandals on a wet day.","From Greek praktikos, “fit for action.”","Practical sounds like practice: things that really work in real life."],["conclude","kuhn-KLOOD","verb","To come to an end, or finish.","The show concluded with a big bow from the whole cast.","","Conclude sounds like “close the lid”: the end."],["deflate","dih-FLAYT","verb","To let the air out of something.","My pool float deflated overnight and went flat.","","Inflate fills with air; deflate lets it all out. Pfffft."],["migrate","MY-grayt","verb","To move from one place to another, often with the seasons.","Some birds migrate south every fall to find warmer weather.","From Latin migrare, “to move.”","Migrate sounds like “my great” trip: animals taking a big trip each season."],["impressive","im-PRES-iv","adjective","So good that people notice and admire it.","Her block tower was impressive; it was almost as tall as the teacher.","","Impressive has “impress” in it: it makes people say wow."],["rigid","RIJ-id","adjective","Stiff and hard to bend.","The frozen towel was rigid as a board.","From Latin rigidus, “stiff.”","Rigid sounds like “ridge-id”: stiff and straight like a ridge."],["endure","en-DOOR","verb","To keep going through something hard.","We had to endure a long, hot hike, but we didn’t quit.","From Latin indurare, “to make hard.”","Endure has “end” in it: keep going until the end."],["brainstorm","BRAYN-storm","verb","To think up lots of ideas fast.","Let’s brainstorm names for our new goldfish. How about Bubbles?","","Brainstorm: a storm of ideas raining out of your brain."],["accompany","uh-KUHM-puh-nee","verb","To go somewhere with someone.","My big sister will accompany me to the park so I’m not alone.","","Accompany has “company” in it: you keep someone company."],["sensible","SEN-suh-buhl","adjective","Showing good sense.","Wearing boots in the mud is a sensible choice.","","Sensible has “sense” in it: it makes good sense."],["method","METH-uhd","noun","A way of doing something.","My method for cleaning my room is to start with the floor.","From Greek methodos, “a way of going after.”","A method is the “how”: my method for tying shoes is bunny ears."],["expedition","ek-spih-DISH-uhn","noun","A trip taken for a special purpose, like exploring.","We went on an expedition through the woods to find the hidden creek.","From Latin expedire, “to free the feet.”","Expedition sounds like “explore mission”: a trip with a goal."],["adjust","uh-JUHST","verb","To change something a little so it works better.","I had to adjust my bike seat because I grew taller.","","Adjust has “just” in it: change it until it’s just right."],["dormant","DOR-muhnt","adjective","Resting or not active for a while.","The bear stays dormant in its cave all winter.","From Latin dormire, “to sleep.”","Dormant comes from Latin dormire, to sleep, like a dorm full of beds."],["alter","AWL-ter","verb","To change something.","Grandma had to alter my pants because they were too long.","From Latin alter, “other.”","Alter comes from Latin for “other”: you make it into another version."],["conserve","kuhn-SURV","verb","To save something so it is not wasted.","We turn off the lights to conserve energy.","From Latin conservare, “to keep safe.”","Conserve sounds like preserve: save it, don’t waste it."],["navigate","NAV-ih-gayt","verb","To find your way from one place to another.","I navigated us through the corn maze using a little map.","From Latin navigare, “to sail.”","Navigate shares a root with navy, ships that find their way at sea."],["bewildered","bih-WIL-derd","adjective","Very confused.","The puppy looked bewildered when the ball vanished behind my back.","","Bewildered has “wild” in it: your brain goes wild with confusion."],["kin","KIN","noun","Your family and relatives.","All my kin, aunts, uncles and cousins, came to the summer picnic.","From Old English cyn, “family.”","Kin sounds like “kids”: the kids and grown-ups in your family."],["dreary","DREER-ee","adjective","Gray, dull and gloomy.","We played board games inside on the dreary, rainy day.","","Dreary sounds like “drippy and teary”: gray, rainy and sad."],["irritate","IR-ih-tayt","verb","To bother or annoy.","My brother likes to irritate me by poking my arm over and over.","From Latin irritare, “to stir up.”","Irritate sounds like “itch-itate”: like an itch that bugs you."],["bask","BASK","verb","To lie in warm sunshine and enjoy it.","The cat likes to bask in the warm sunny spot on the floor.","","Bask sounds like “basking shark”: lying lazy in the sun."],["flinch","FLINCH","verb","To pull back quickly because of surprise or pain.","When the ball flew at my face, I couldn’t help but flinch.","","Flinch sounds like pinch: if someone pinches you, you jump back."],["suspend","suh-SPEND","verb","To hang something, or to stop it for a while.","We suspend the piñata from a tree branch at my party.","From Latin suspendere, “to hang up.”","Suspend sounds like “suspenders”: they hang your pants up."],["permanent","PUR-muh-nuhnt","adjective","Lasting forever or for a very long time.","The permanent marker would not wash off my hand.","From Latin permanere, “to remain.”","A permanent marker never washes off: it stays and stays."],["contribute","kuhn-TRIB-yoot","verb","To give or add something to help.","Everyone contributed a snack to the class party.","","Contribute: you chip in your part to help the team."],["hibernate","HY-ber-nayt","verb","To sleep through the winter.","Bears hibernate all winter, sleeping in their dens until spring.","From Latin hibernus, “wintry.”","Hibernate sounds like “hi-burr-nate”: when it gets brrr cold, go to sleep."],["boost","BOOST","verb","To lift up or make stronger.","A good breakfast will boost your energy for the whole morning.","","Boost sounds like “boots”: give someone a boost up with your boots."],["swarm","SWORM","noun","A big, busy group of insects or people.","A swarm of bees buzzed around our picnic lemonade.","","Swarm sounds like “swirling storm” of buzzing bugs."],["vital","VY-tuhl","adjective","Very important and needed.","Water is vital for your body, so drink up after recess.","From Latin vita, “life.”","Vital sounds like “vitamins”: things you really need to live."],["cautious","KAW-shuhs","adjective","Careful to stay safe.","Be cautious when you walk on the icy sidewalk.","From Latin cavere, “to take care.”","A caution sign says “Be careful!”, so cautious means careful."],["exaggerate","ig-ZAJ-uh-rayt","verb","To make something sound bigger or better than it really is.","My friend likes to exaggerate; he said his fish was as big as a car.","From Latin exaggerare, “to heap up.”","Exaggerate sounds like “extra great”: making it sound extra big."],["motto","MOT-oh","noun","A short saying that shows what someone believes.","Our family motto is “Never give up.”","From Italian motto, “word.”","A motto is a saying to live by, like “Be kind” on a poster."],["bluff","BLUHF","verb","To pretend you have something or can do something.","He tried to bluff that he had a great card, but he really had a bad one.","","Bluff sounds like “puff”: puff yourself up and pretend."],["vacant","VAY-kuhnt","adjective","Empty, with no one in it.","We played ball in the vacant lot next door.","From Latin vacare, “to be empty.”","Vacant sounds like vacation: everyone left, so it’s empty."],["merge","MURJ","verb","To join together into one.","The two lines of kids merged into one long line at the door.","From Latin mergere, “to dip.”","Merge rhymes with surge: two streams surge together into one river."],["abode","uh-BOHD","noun","The place where someone lives.","The hamster’s cage is his cozy little abode.","","Abode sounds like “a toad”: a toad’s home under a log."],["eavesdrop","EEVZ-drop","verb","To secretly listen to other people talking.","My little brother hid behind the door to eavesdrop on my phone call.","From eavesdrop, the ground where rain drips off a roof’s edge.","People once stood under a house’s eaves to secretly hear talk inside."],["avid","AV-id","adjective","Very eager and interested.","My cousin is an avid reader who finishes a book every week.","From Latin avidus, “eager.”","Avid sounds like “a vid”: so eager, like waiting for a new video."],["compassion","kuhm-PASH-uhn","noun","Caring about someone who is hurting and wanting to help.","She showed compassion by sitting with the new kid at lunch.","From Latin compati, “to suffer with.”","Compassion means “feeling with”: you feel their hurt too."],["collide","kuh-LYDE","verb","To crash into each other.","Two bumper cars collided, and we both laughed.","From Latin collidere, “to strike together.”","Collide sounds like “call it a crash”: bonk."],["terrain","tuh-RAYN","noun","The kind of land in an area.","Our mountain bikes bumped over rocky terrain.","From Latin terra, “earth.”","Terra means earth: terrain is the kind of ground you’re on."],["surplus","SUR-plus","noun","More than you need.","We had a surplus of cupcakes, so we gave some to the neighbors.","","Surplus has “plus” in it: extra, more than you need."],["confess","kuhn-FES","verb","To admit you did something.","I confessed that I ate the last cookie.","","Confess sounds like “come clean, yes!”: I did it."],["dwindle","DWIN-duhl","verb","To get smaller little by little.","Our pile of snacks dwindled during the movie.","","Dwindle sounds like “dwarf” and “little”: getting smaller and smaller."],["ignite","ig-NYTE","verb","To catch fire or start burning.","Dad used a match to ignite the campfire, and the wood started to burn.","From Latin ignis, “fire.”","Ignite has “ign” like ignition, which starts a car with a spark."],["authentic","aw-THEN-tik","adjective","Real and true, not fake.","The baseball was authentic: it really was signed by the whole team.","From Greek authentikos, “original.”","Authentic sounds like “author-tic”: the real author, not a copy."],["commute","kuh-MYOOT","verb","To travel back and forth, like to school or work.","We commute to school by bike every morning.","","Commute: the trip you make again and again, there and back."],["meander","mee-AN-der","verb","To wander slowly with no set path.","We meandered through the park, stopping at every swing and slide.","From Greek Maiandros, a river famous for its twists and turns.","Meander sounds like “me wander”: I wander slowly, no rush."],["imitate","IM-ih-tayt","verb","To copy the way someone acts or sounds.","My parrot can imitate the doorbell so well that we run to answer it.","From Latin imitari, “to copy.”","Imitate sounds like “I’m a mate”: I copy my mate."],["fluent","FLOO-uhnt","adjective","Able to speak a language easily and well.","Rosa is fluent in Spanish; she talks fast and never gets stuck.","From Latin fluere, “to flow.”","Fluent sounds like fluid: words flow out like water."],["knead","NEED","verb","To press and fold dough with your hands.","We knead the bread dough by pressing and folding it with our hands.","","Knead sounds like need: bread needs you to knead it."],["calculate","KAL-kyuh-layt","verb","To work out an answer using math.","We calculated how many cookies each friend gets.","From Latin calculus, “small pebble.”","A calculator helps you calculate the answer."],["tranquil","TRANG-kwil","adjective","Quiet and peaceful.","The house felt tranquil after the baby finally fell asleep.","From Latin tranquillus, “calm.”","Tranquil sounds like “tank-quiet”: a calm fish tank, still and peaceful."],["absurd","ab-SURD","adjective","Silly and not making any sense.","Wearing swim flippers to school would be absurd!","From Latin absurdus, “out of tune.”","Absurd sounds like “a bird”: a bird wearing sneakers is absurd."],["remote","rih-MOHT","adjective","Far away from towns and people.","We camped at a remote lake with no stores and no phone signal.","From Latin remotus, “moved back.”","Remote is like a TV remote: it works from far away."],["befuddled","bih-FUHD-uhld","adjective","Mixed up and unable to think clearly.","The new game rules were so confusing that I felt totally befuddled.","","Befuddled sounds like “be-muddled”: your thoughts are a muddy puddle."],["gravity","GRAV-ih-tee","noun","The force that pulls things down toward the earth.","Gravity makes my ball fall back down every time I throw it up.","From Latin gravis, “heavy.”","Gravity sounds like “gravy-ty”: gravy drips down, pulled by gravity."],["brood","BROOD","verb","To keep thinking about something that makes you unhappy.","Don’t brood about losing the game; we can win next week.","","Brood sounds like “brew-d”: thoughts that brew and stew all day."],["guardian","GAR-dee-uhn","noun","A person who protects or takes care of someone.","My guardian takes care of me and signed my field trip form.","","Guardian has “guard” in it: a person who guards and protects you."],["accustomed","uh-KUHS-tuhmd","adjective","Used to something.","I am accustomed to waking up early for swim practice now.","","Accustomed has “custom” in it: what you’re used to doing."],["motivate","MOH-tih-vayt","verb","To give someone a reason to want to do something.","The promise of pizza motivated us to clean our rooms fast.","From Latin motivus, “moving.”","Motivate and motor share an old word for move: it gets you going."],["beneath","bih-NEETH","adverb","Under or below something.","The cat sleeps beneath the table, right under our feet.","","Beneath has “neath” like “underneath”: down below."],["capsize","KAP-syze","verb","To tip over in the water.","Our canoe capsized, and we swam to shore laughing.","","Capsize: the boat flips so its cap ends up on the bottom."],["glacier","GLAY-sher","noun","A huge sheet of ice that moves very slowly.","In the movie, the kids slid down a giant glacier, a slow river of ice.","From Latin glacies, “ice.”","Glacier sounds like “glass-ier”: a huge sheet of glassy ice."],["pioneer","py-uh-NEER","noun","One of the first people to do or explore something.","My big sister was a pioneer, the first kid on our street to start a dog-walking club.","From Old French peonier, “foot soldier.”","Pioneer sounds like “pie-near”: the first one to find the pie."],["hesitate","HEZ-ih-tayt","verb","To stop for a moment before doing something.","I hesitate for a second at the edge before I jump into the cold pool.","From Latin haesitare, “to get stuck.”","Hesitate sounds like “he’s a-wait-ing”: he stops and waits."],["omit","oh-MIT","verb","To leave something out.","I omitted the onions from my sandwich because I don’t like them.","From Latin omittere, “to let go.”","Omit sounds like “Oh, missed it”: something left out."],["hoist","HOYST","verb","To lift something up, often with ropes.","We hoist the flag up the pole by pulling on the rope.","","Hoist sounds like “hoist-up”: pull, pull, up it goes."],["uneasy","un-EE-zee","adjective","Worried or not comfortable.","I felt uneasy walking into my new class on the first day.","","Uneasy means not easy: your tummy feels all twisty."],["contrast","KON-trast","noun","A big difference between two things.","My white socks were a sharp contrast to my muddy shoes.","","Contrast sounds like “contra,” against: two things that look way different side by side."],["refuge","REF-yooj","noun","A safe place to go.","The cat found refuge under the bed during the thunderstorm.","From Latin refugium, “a place to flee to.”","Refuge sounds like “re-fudge”: safe at home with fudge."],["frequent","FREE-kwuhnt","adjective","Happening often.","We make frequent trips to the ice cream shop, almost every Friday.","From Latin frequens, “crowded, often.”","Frequent sounds like “free-quent”: so often it’s like a free pass."],["dejected","dih-JEK-tid","adjective","Sad and let down.","He looked dejected when the game got rained out.","From Latin deicere, “to throw down.”","Dejected sounds like “rejected”: down in the dumps."],["furious","FYUR-ee-uhs","adjective","Very angry.","The cat was furious when the dog took her bed; she hissed and swatted.","From Latin furia, “rage.”","Furious has “fur” in it: an angry cat with all its fur puffed up."],["chaos","KAY-os","noun","Total mess and confusion.","When the class hamster got loose, the room was total chaos.","From Greek khaos, “empty space.”","Chaos sounds like “KAY-oss”: kids running everywhere, crazy mess."],["crucial","KROO-shuhl","adjective","Very, very important.","Water is crucial; plants die without it.","From Latin crux, “cross.”","Crucial things are must-haves: without them, everything crashes."],["orderly","OR-der-lee","adjective","Neat and in good order.","We walked out in an orderly line during the fire drill.","","Orderly has “order” in it: everything in its right place."],["amateur","AM-uh-cher","noun","Someone who does something for fun, not as a job.","My uncle is an amateur cook. He cooks just for fun, not as a job.","From French amateur, “lover,” from Latin amare, “to love.”","Amateur comes from “amare,” to love: you do it because you love it."],["annual","AN-yoo-uhl","adjective","Happening once every year.","Our annual family picnic happens every June.","From Latin annus, “year.”","Annual has “ann” from “annus,” year: once a year, every year."],["cherish","CHAIR-ish","verb","To love and care for something a lot.","I cherish the bracelet my grandma made for me.","From French cher, “dear.”","Cherish sounds like cherry: something sweet you hold close."],["seldom","SEL-duhm","adverb","Not often.","It seldom snows where we live, so a snow day is a big deal.","","Seldom sounds like “sell some”: you seldom sell your favorite toy."],["fossil","FOS-uhl","noun","The hardened print or bones of a plant or animal from long, long ago.","Jada cracked open a rock and found the shape of a tiny old seashell inside, a real fossil.","From Latin fossilis, “dug up.”","Fossil sounds like “fossil-ized”: old bones turned to stone."],["approximate","uh-PROK-suh-mit","adjective","Close to the exact amount, but not exact.","“About 200 jellybeans” was my approximate guess for the jar.","From Latin proximus, “nearest.”","Approximate sounds like “approx”: close, but not exact."],["legend","LEJ-uhnd","noun","An old story that many people tell, which may not be fully true.","The legend says a giant fish lives in the lake, but nobody has seen it.","From Latin legenda, “things to be read.”","Legend sounds like legendary: a tale so big people keep telling it for years."],["mature","muh-CHUR","adjective","Acting grown up and sensible.","My cousin was mature and helped clean up without being asked.","From Latin maturus, “ripe.”","Mature sounds like “Ma, sure!”: so grown up, Ma is sure you’re ready."],["benefit","BEN-uh-fit","noun","Something good that helps you.","One benefit of walking the dog is that I get exercise too.","From Latin benefactum, “a good deed.”","Bene means “well” in Latin: a benefit does you good."],["commotion","kuh-MOH-shuhn","noun","Noisy, busy confusion.","The loose goat caused a commotion at the fair.","","Commotion has “motion”: everyone moving and shouting at once."],["bungle","BUHNG-guhl","verb","To do something badly or clumsily.","I didn’t want to bungle my lines in the school play, so I practiced.","","Bungle sounds like “bumble”: you bumble and mess it up."],["robust","roh-BUST","adjective","Strong and healthy.","Our robust tomato plants grew taller than me.","From Latin robustus, “strong as oak.”","Robust sounds like “robot bust”: strong as a robot."],["boundary","BOWN-dree","noun","A line that marks the edge of an area.","The fence marks the boundary of our backyard. The ball can’t go past it!","","Boundary has “bound” in it: out of bounds means past the edge."],["affection","uh-FEK-shuhn","noun","A warm feeling of love or liking.","My dog shows affection by licking my face when I get home.","","Affection sounds like “a fluffy action”: hugs, snuggles, and puppy kisses."],["venture","VEN-cher","verb","To go somewhere new that might be risky.","We decided to venture into the dark attic with a flashlight.","","Venture sounds like adventure: going somewhere new and a bit risky."],["portable","POR-tuh-buhl","adjective","Easy to carry.","My portable speaker fits right in my backpack.","From Latin portare, “to carry.”","Port means carry, like a porter carrying bags."],["concentrate","KON-suhn-trayt","verb","To focus all your attention on one thing.","I concentrate best when the room is quiet.","","Concentrate has “center”: put all your thinking in one center spot."],["gnaw","NAW","verb","To keep biting or chewing on something.","Our puppy loves to gnaw on her chew toy for hours.","From Old English gnagan, “to chew.”","Gnaw sounds like “gnash gnash”: keep chomping like a beaver."],["serene","suh-REEN","adjective","Calm and peaceful.","The lake was serene and still early in the morning.","From Latin serenus, “clear, calm.”","Serene sounds like a calm, quiet scene."],["transform","trans-FORM","verb","To change completely.","The caterpillar will transform into a butterfly.","","Trans means across, and form is shape: changing into a new shape."],["embark","em-BARK","verb","To get on a ship or start something new.","We will embark on the boat at noon and start our trip.","From French embarquer, “to put on a boat.”","Embark sounds like “em-BARK”: get on board the barge."],["clarify","KLAIR-uh-fy","verb","To make something easier to understand.","The teacher clarified the rules so we all understood.","From Latin clarus, “clear.”","Clarify has “clar” like clear: making it clear."],["pivot","PIV-uht","verb","To turn on one point.","In basketball, you can pivot on one foot to turn around.","","Pivot sounds like “pin it”: pin one foot down and spin."],["emerge","ih-MURJ","verb","To come out into view.","The chick began to emerge from its egg, one wet head first.","From Latin emergere, “to rise out.”","Emerge sounds like “a merge”: something comes out and joins the world."],["optimist","OP-tuh-mist","noun","Someone who expects good things to happen.","Even in the pouring rain, the optimist on our team said we would win.","From Latin optimus, “best.”","Optimus means best in Latin: an optimist expects the best."],["advise","ad-VIZE","verb","To give someone ideas about what they should do.","My big sister can advise me on which games are the most fun.","","Advise has “vise” like “wise”: wise words to help you."],["decline","dih-KLYNE","verb","To politely say no.","I declined a second slice of cake because I was full.","","Decline sounds like “decide no”: no, thank you."],["humid","HYOO-mid","adjective","Warm and damp, with lots of water in the air.","It was so humid that my shirt felt sticky and my hair puffed up.","From Latin humidus, “moist.”","Humid sounds like “human mist”: wet, misty air that makes you sticky."],["accurate","AK-yer-it","adjective","Exactly right, with no mistakes.","My throw was so accurate that it landed right in the trash can.","From Latin accuratus, “done with care.”","Accurate comes from Latin “cura,” care: done with care, so it’s right."],["energetic","en-er-JET-ik","adjective","Full of energy and moving a lot.","The energetic puppy ran circles around the yard without stopping.","From Greek energeia, “activity.”","Energetic has “energy” right inside it."],["brawny","BRAW-nee","adjective","Big and strong, with lots of muscles.","The brawny farmer carried two heavy hay bales at once.","","Brawny sounds like “brawn” and “brainy”: but brawny means muscles."],["elegant","EL-uh-guhnt","adjective","Graceful and stylish.","My sister looked elegant in her long dress at the wedding.","From Latin elegans, “choosy, fine.”","Elegant sounds like elephant, but picture an elephant dancing ballet gracefully."],["routine","roo-TEEN","noun","The usual way you do things each day.","Brushing my teeth is part of my bedtime routine.","From French route, “road.”","Routine sounds like “route”: the same road you walk every day."],["amend","uh-MEND","verb","To change something to make it better or right.","We had to amend our club rules so everyone could join.","From Latin emendare, “to free from fault.”","Amend has “mend” in it: fix it to make it better."],["overwhelm","oh-ver-WELM","verb","To be too much for someone to handle.","Getting five worksheets at once can overwhelm anyone.","","Overwhelm: like a giant wave crashing over your head."],["peculiar","pih-KYOOL-yer","adjective","Strange or unusual.","The milk tasted peculiar, so we checked the date on the carton.","From Latin peculiaris, “one's own.”","Peculiar sounds like “peek-ular”: something so odd you peek twice."],["possess","puh-ZES","verb","To own or have something.","My little brother possesses more toy cars than anyone I know.","From Latin possidere, “to own.”","Possess is full of S’s, like a kid with so many things."],["demolish","dih-MOL-ish","verb","To tear down completely.","Workers demolished the old shed with a big bulldozer.","","Demolish sounds like “demo”: smash it all down."],["grimace","GRIM-is","noun","A twisted face that shows you dislike something.","I made a grimace, scrunching my face, when I bit into the sour lemon.","","Grimace sounds like “grim face”: a scrunched-up, yucky face."],["enthusiastic","en-thoo-zee-AS-tik","adjective","Very excited and interested.","Our class was so enthusiastic about the field trip that we cheered.","","Enthusiastic sounds like “enthuse-ASTIC”: fantastic excitement."],["wary","WAIR-ee","adjective","Careful because something might go wrong.","Our cat is wary of the vacuum and watches it from far away.","","Wary sounds like worry: careful because you worry something’s wrong."],["criticize","KRIT-uh-syze","verb","To say what you think is wrong with something.","Our coach never criticizes us without showing how to fix it.","From Greek kritikos, “able to judge.”","A critic is someone who says what is good or bad."],["wilderness","WIL-der-nis","noun","Wild land where few people live.","At camp, we hiked into the wilderness, far from any houses.","","Wilderness has “wild” in it: wild land with no towns."],["analyze","AN-uh-lize","verb","To study something closely to understand it.","I analyze my soccer game video to see why I missed the goal.","From Greek analysis, “a breaking up.”","Analyze sounds like “an-a-lies”: look closely to find what’s really there."],["companion","kuhm-PAN-yuhn","noun","A friend who spends time with you.","My dog is my best walking companion.","From Latin com-, “with,” and panis, “bread.”","Com means with, and pan means bread: someone you share bread with."],["resemble","rih-ZEM-buhl","verb","To look like someone or something.","My baby brother is starting to resemble Dad, with the same big ears.","","Resemble has “semble” like same: looking the same."],["mystify","MIS-tuh-fy","verb","To confuse someone completely.","My friend’s card trick mystified the whole class.","","Mystify has “myst” like mystery: it leaves you stuck on a mystery."],["haven","HAY-vuhn","noun","A safe, peaceful place.","My tree house is my haven, a safe and quiet place to read.","From Old English hæfen, “harbor.”","Haven sounds like heaven: a calm, safe place."],["pact","PAKT","noun","A promise or deal between people.","My best friend and I made a pact to always share our snacks.","From Latin pactum, “agreement.”","Pact rhymes with fact: a promise you both stick to."],["obtain","uhb-TAYN","verb","To get something.","I saved my allowance to obtain the new game.","From Latin obtinere, “to hold.”","Obtain sounds like “got-tain”: you got it in your hands."],["outspoken","owt-SPOH-kuhn","adjective","Saying what you think in a clear, bold way.","My outspoken friend told the coach the rule was unfair.","","Outspoken: your thoughts are spoken out loud for all to hear."],["reckless","REK-lis","adjective","Not careful about danger.","Riding your bike down the hill with no helmet is reckless.","","Reckless sounds like “wreck-less”: careless enough to cause a wreck."],["generous","JEN-er-uhs","adjective","Happy to share and give.","Leo was generous and gave half his cookie to his little sister.","From Latin generosus, “of noble birth.”","Generous sounds like “gen-ER-us”: giving to everyone around us."],["locate","LOH-kayt","verb","To find where something is.","Can you locate the remote? It’s somewhere in the couch.","From Latin locus, “place.”","Locate has “loc” like location: find the spot where it is."],["dabble","DAB-uhl","verb","To try something a little, just for fun.","I like to dabble in painting on rainy weekends.","","Dabble sounds like a duck dabbling: just dipping its beak in a little."],["ascend","uh-SEND","verb","To go up or climb.","We watched the balloon ascend up, up, up into the sky.","From Latin ascendere, “to climb up.”","Ascend sounds like “a send”: send the balloon up, up, up."],["abandon","uh-BAN-duhn","verb","To leave something behind and not come back for it.","We had to abandon our sandcastle when the waves came in.","From Old French abandoner, “to give up.”","Abandon sounds like “a band gone”: the whole band left and never came back."],["construct","kuhn-STRUHKT","verb","To build.","We constructed a birdhouse out of wood and nails.","From Latin construere, “to pile up.”","Construction workers construct, or build, houses."],["habitat","HAB-ih-tat","noun","The natural home of a plant or animal.","The pond is a habitat where frogs and ducks live, eat and sleep.","From Latin habitat, “it lives.”","Habitat sounds like “have-it-at”: where an animal has its home."],["curfew","KUR-fyoo","noun","A time you must be home or in bed.","My curfew at sleepovers is ten o’clock, lights out.","From French couvre-feu, “cover the fire.”","Curfew comes from French “cover the fire”: time to shut down for the night."],["reluctant","rih-LUK-tuhnt","adjective","Not wanting to do something.","I was reluctant to jump into the cold pool, so I just dipped my toe.","From Latin reluctari, “to struggle against.”","Reluctant sounds like “really can’t”: your feet won’t move."],["consequence","KON-si-kwens","noun","Something that happens because of something else.","If I stay up too late, the consequence is feeling grumpy.","","A consequence is what comes next because of what you did."],["appeal","uh-PEEL","verb","To be interesting or pleasing to someone.","Stories about dragons really appeal to my brother. He loves them!","","Appeal sounds like “a peel”: a banana peel’s yummy inside appeals to monkeys."],["fidget","FIJ-it","verb","To move around because you cannot sit still.","I fidget and wiggle in my chair when I’m waiting for lunch.","","Fidget sounds like fidget spinner: a toy for hands that can’t sit still."],["scavenge","SKAV-inj","verb","To search for useful things others threw away.","Raccoons scavenge for snacks in our trash cans at night.","","Scavenge sounds like a scavenger hunt: searching for bits and pieces."],["obstacle","OB-stuh-kuhl","noun","Something that blocks your way.","The fallen tree was an obstacle on our bike path.","From Latin obstaculum, “a hindrance.”","An obstacle course is full of things blocking your way."],["opponent","uh-POH-nuhnt","noun","The person or team you play against.","My opponent in the chess game was my grandpa.","From Latin opponere, “to set against.”","Opponent and opposite share a root: the one on the other side."],["immense","ih-MENS","adjective","Huge and very large.","The whale was immense, way bigger than our little boat.","From Latin immensus, “not measured.”","Immense sounds like “I’m mega”: mega huge."],["bountiful","BOWN-tih-fuhl","adjective","Plenty of something, more than enough.","Our garden was bountiful, with more tomatoes than we could ever eat.","","Bountiful sounds like “bounty full”: full of more than enough."],["perplexed","per-PLEKST","adjective","Very confused.","I was perplexed when my math answer was wrong even after I checked it twice.","From Latin perplexus, “tangled.”","Perplexed sounds like “puzzle-plexed”: puzzled times ten."],["spectacle","SPEK-tuh-kuhl","noun","An amazing thing to see.","The fireworks show was a real spectacle that lit up the sky.","From Latin spectare, “to watch.”","Spectacle sounds like spectacles: something you put your glasses on to watch."],["drastic","DRAS-tik","adjective","Big and sudden.","Cutting my long hair really short was a drastic change.","","Drastic sounds like “dramatic”: a big, sudden switch."],["rival","RY-vuhl","noun","Someone you compete against.","Our soccer team’s biggest rival is the school across town.","From Latin rivalis, “one who shares a stream.”","Rival sounds like arrival: when your rival arrives, it’s game on."],["stern","STURN","adjective","Strict and serious.","The coach gave us a stern look when we showed up late.","","Stern rhymes with “learn your lesson”: a strict, serious face."],["chide","CHYDE","verb","To gently scold.","Mom chided me for leaving my shoes on the stairs.","","Chide sounds like “child”: a gentle “tsk, tsk” telling-off."],["burden","BUR-duhn","noun","A heavy load to carry.","Carrying my brother’s heavy backpack too felt like a burden.","","Burden sounds like “bird-en”: a bird carrying a load that’s way too heavy."],["numerous","NOO-mer-uhs","adjective","Many; a lot.","There were numerous ants crawling on our picnic blanket.","From Latin numerus, “number.”","Numerous has “numer” like number: a big number of things."],["yield","YEELD","verb","To let others go first, or to make or grow something.","At the crosswalk, cars must yield to kids crossing the street.","","A yield sign on the road means: slow down and let others go first."],["arid","AIR-id","adjective","Very dry, with little rain.","The desert is so arid that it hardly ever rains.","From Latin aridus, “dry.”","Arid sounds like “a rid”: rid of all water, dry as dust."],["haul","HAWL","verb","To pull or carry something heavy.","We had to haul the heavy wagon full of pumpkins up the hill.","","Haul sounds like “hall”: pulling a heavy box down the long hall."],["persuade","per-SWAYD","verb","To get someone to agree with you.","I tried to persuade Mom to let us have pizza for dinner.","From Latin persuadere, “to urge.”","Persuade sounds like “purr sweet”: sweet-talk someone into agreeing."],["dispute","dih-SPYOOT","noun","An argument or disagreement.","We settled our dispute over the swing by taking turns.","","Dispute sounds like “this beaut”: two kids fighting over the best toy."],["majestic","muh-JES-tik","adjective","Grand and beautiful in a way that makes you feel small.","The majestic mountain looked so tall it touched the clouds.","From Latin majestas, “greatness.”","Majestic sounds like Majesty, a name for a king: grand and royal."],["browse","BROWZ","verb","To look through things in a relaxed way.","I like to browse the library shelves until a cool book catches my eye.","","Browse sounds like “brows”: lift your brows as you look around."],["extinct","ik-STINGKT","adjective","No longer living anywhere on Earth.","Dinosaurs are extinct, so you will never see a live one.","From Latin exstinguere, “to put out.”","Extinct sounds like “ex-stinct”: the last one is gone, no more."],["realm","RELM","noun","A kingdom, or an area of something.","In our video game, a dragon guards the snowy realm.","","Realm sounds like “real home” for a king or queen: their kingdom."],["outskirts","OWT-skurts","noun","The edges of a town.","My cousin lives on the outskirts of town, near the farms.","","Outskirts sounds like the outside of a skirt: the very edge."],["flexible","FLEK-suh-buhl","adjective","Able to bend easily, or able to change plans.","My friend is so flexible she can touch her toes and bend into a bridge.","From Latin flectere, “to bend.”","Flex your arm: flexible things bend and stretch easily."],["thorough","THUR-oh","adjective","Careful and complete.","Dad did a thorough job cleaning my room, even under the bed.","","Thorough sounds like “through”: you go through every bit."],["devise","dih-VYZE","verb","To think up a plan or idea.","We devised a secret plan to surprise Mom on her birthday.","","Devise sounds like “device”: you invent a clever plan."],["muster","MUS-ter","verb","To gather up, like courage or strength.","I mustered all my courage to raise my hand in class.","From Latin monstrare, “to show.”","Muster sounds like mustard: squeeze out every last bit of courage."],["plead","PLEED","verb","To beg for something.","My brother pleaded with Mom for five more minutes of video games.","","Plead rhymes with need: begging for something you really need."],["bleary","BLEER-ee","adjective","Tired and blurry, like sleepy eyes.","My eyes were bleary when I woke up too early.","","Bleary sounds like “blurry”: tired eyes that see fuzzy."],["visible","VIZ-uh-buhl","adjective","Able to be seen.","The cat thought it was hiding, but its tail was still visible behind the curtain.","From Latin videre, “to see.”","Vis means see, like vision: visible means you can see it."],["baffle","BAF-uhl","verb","To confuse someone completely.","This riddle will baffle you: what has hands but can’t clap? A clock!","","Baffle sounds like “waffle”: your brain gets as mixed up as waffle squares."],["quaint","KWAYNT","adjective","Old-fashioned in a cute, charming way.","Grandma’s quaint little house has lace curtains and a cuckoo clock.","","Quaint sounds like paint: old, cute and freshly painted like a storybook."],["aloof","uh-LOOF","adjective","Not friendly, keeping to yourself.","Our new cat stayed aloof, sitting alone and ignoring us all day.","","Aloof sounds like “a roof”: someone sitting far away on the roof."],["wisdom","WIZ-duhm","noun","Good sense that comes from knowing a lot.","Grandma’s wisdom helped me fix the fight with my best friend.","","Wisdom has “wis” like wise: a wise owl’s good sense."],["prior","PRY-er","adjective","Coming before.","Prior to the game, we stretched our legs.","From Latin prior, “earlier.”","Prior sounds like pre, which means before: prior means before."],["neglect","nih-GLEKT","verb","To not take care of something.","I neglected my plant for a month, and it turned brown.","From Latin neglegere, “to not pick up.”","Neglect sounds like “neck-left”: you left it alone and forgot it."],["banter","BAN-ter","noun","Friendly, joking talk.","My friends and I trade silly jokes and banter all through lunch.","","Banter sounds like “bounce-ter”: jokes bouncing back and forth."],["improvise","IM-pruh-vyze","verb","To make something up on the spot.","When we lost the ball, we had to improvise and use a rolled-up sock.","From Latin improvisus, “not seen ahead.”","Improvise sounds like “improve-ise”: make it up and make it work."],["pedestrian","puh-DES-tree-uhn","noun","A person walking, not riding.","The car stopped to let a pedestrian cross the street.","From Latin pedester, “on foot.”","Ped means foot, like pedal: a pedestrian goes on foot."],["cuisine","kwi-ZEEN","noun","A style of cooking.","Our class tasted foods from many different cuisines at the culture fair.","From French cuisine, “kitchen.”","Cuisine is the French word for kitchen: a style of cooking."],["paraphrase","PAIR-uh-frayz","verb","To say something again in your own words.","Can you paraphrase the story so your little brother understands it?","From Greek paraphrasis, “telling in other words.”","Picture a parrot that repeats your phrase, but in its own words."],["segment","SEG-muhnt","noun","One part of something.","I ate one segment of the orange and gave my sister the rest.","From Latin segmentum, “a piece cut off.”","Segment sounds like “section”: one piece of the whole."],["noteworthy","NOHT-wur-thee","adjective","Important enough to notice.","Scoring three goals in one game was noteworthy.","","Noteworthy means worth a note: write it in your notebook."],["mythical","MITH-ih-kuhl","adjective","Found only in old stories, not real life.","A unicorn is a mythical animal you only find in stories.","From Greek mythos, “story.”","Mythical has “myth” in it: from a story, not real life."],["dissolve","dih-ZOLV","verb","To mix into a liquid and disappear.","Sugar dissolves quickly in hot cocoa.","From Latin dissolvere, “to loosen apart.”","Dissolve: the sugar disappears like a magic trick in your drink."],["coarse","KORS","adjective","Rough and bumpy to touch.","The coarse sand scratched my bare feet.","","Coarse sounds like horse: rough like a horse’s scratchy brush."],["abridge","uh-BRIJ","verb","To make a book or story shorter.","The library has an abridged version of the long book that’s shorter for kids.","","Abridge sounds like “a bridge”: a shortcut across the long story."],["valid","VAL-id","adjective","True, fair or acceptable.","Being sick is a valid reason to miss practice.","From Latin validus, “strong.”","Valid sounds like “value-d”: it has value, so it counts."],["labyrinth","LAB-uh-rinth","noun","A maze with many winding paths.","We got lost for an hour in the corn labyrinth at the farm.","From Greek labyrinthos, “maze.”","Labyrinth sounds like “lab-maze”: a twisty maze you wander in."],["pristine","pris-TEEN","adjective","Perfectly clean, like new.","My new white sneakers stayed pristine for exactly one day.","From Latin pristinus, “former.”","Pristine sounds like “pretty clean”: spotless and brand-new."],["prevail","prih-VAYL","verb","To win or succeed in the end.","After three tries, our team prevailed and won the tug-of-war.","From Latin praevalere, “to be stronger.”","Prevail sounds like “pray we’ll win”: and in the end, you do."],["distort","dih-STORT","verb","To twist something out of its normal shape.","The funhouse mirror distorted my face into a long noodle.","From Latin distorquere, “to twist apart.”","Distort sounds like “twist-tort”: twisted out of shape."],["plausible","PLAW-zuh-buhl","adjective","Possible and easy to believe.","“The dog ate my homework” isn’t a plausible excuse, since we don’t have a dog.","From Latin plausibilis, “worthy of applause.”","Plausible and applause share a root: an idea worth clapping for."],["adorn","uh-DORN","verb","To decorate with pretty things.","We adorn the tree with paper stars every winter.","From Latin adornare, “to decorate.”","Adorn sounds like “a horn”: put a decoration on like a unicorn horn."],["stagnant","STAG-nuhnt","adjective","Not moving or flowing, and often smelly.","The water in the old bucket was stagnant and smelled awful.","From Latin stagnum, “pool.”","Stagnant sounds like “stuck-nant”: stuck, still and stinky."],["tactic","TAK-tik","noun","A move or method used to reach a goal.","My tactic in tag is to run in circles around the slide.","From Greek taktikos, “of arranging.”","Tactic sounds like “tick-tock tactic”: a smart move to win."],["ovation","oh-VAY-shuhn","noun","Loud clapping from a crowd.","The crowd gave our class play a standing ovation.","From Latin ovatio, “rejoicing.”","Ovation sounds like “Oh, wow!”: everyone clapping and cheering."],["gauge","GAYJ","verb","To measure or judge something.","We used a stick to gauge how deep the puddle was.","","A gas gauge measures how much gas is in the tank."],["evade","ih-VAYD","verb","To get away from or avoid.","The rabbit zigzagged to evade the dog and got away.","From Latin evadere, “to get away.”","Evade sounds like “escape-ade”: dodge and escape."],["detach","dih-TACH","verb","To separate or unhook.","I detached the trailer from my toy truck.","","Detach is the opposite of attach: unhook it."],["contrary","KON-trer-ee","adjective","Completely opposite.","Contrary to what I expected, the test was easy.","From Latin contra, “against.”","Contrary sounds like “contra,” Latin for against: the opposite way."],["buffer","BUHF-er","noun","Something that protects by coming between two things.","The bushes are a buffer between our yard and the busy road.","","Buffer sounds like “bumper”: it bumps away danger."],["colleague","KOL-eeg","noun","A person you work with.","Mom had lunch with a colleague from her office.","","Colleague has “league”: people on your work team."],["restore","rih-STOR","verb","To bring something back to how it was.","Dad used glue to restore my broken toy car.","From Latin restaurare, “to rebuild.”","Restore sounds like “re-store”: put it back in the store, good as new."],["metamorphosis","met-uh-MOR-fuh-sis","noun","A big change in form, like a caterpillar becoming a butterfly.","My messy room went through a total metamorphosis after I cleaned it.","From Greek metamorphosis, “change of shape.”","Morph means shape: a metamorphosis changes the whole shape."],["mirage","mih-RAHZH","noun","Something you think you see that is not really there.","On the hot road, I saw what looked like a puddle, but it was a mirage.","From French mirer, “to look at.”","Mirage sounds like mirror: it tricks your eyes like a fun-house mirror."],["amenity","uh-MEN-ih-tee","noun","Something that makes a place more pleasant or comfy.","The hotel’s best amenity was the pool with a big slide.","From Latin amoenus, “pleasant.”","Amenity sounds like “a-menu-ty”: extra nice things on the menu."],["legible","LEJ-uh-buhl","adjective","Clear enough to read.","Please write neatly so your homework is legible for the teacher.","From Latin legere, “to read.”","Legible sounds like “read-able”: leg- comes from a Latin word meaning to read."],["orient","OR-ee-ent","verb","To figure out where you are or which way to go.","When we got lost in the mall, I used the map to orient myself.","From Latin oriens, “rising,” as in the rising sun in the east.","Orient sounds like “Oh, right!”: you figure out which way is right."],["flourish","FLUR-ish","verb","To grow strong and healthy.","With sun and water, our classroom plants started to flourish.","From Latin florere, “to bloom.”","Flourish sounds like flower: flowers flourish in the sun."],["bestow","bih-STOH","verb","To give something as an honor or gift.","The principal will bestow a gold medal on the spelling bee winner.","","Bestow sounds like “best-owe”: give the best to someone."],["distinct","dih-STINGKT","adjective","Clearly different or easy to notice.","Each zebra has a distinct pattern of stripes.","","Distinct sounds like “this thing”: you can point to exactly which one."],["defiant","dih-FY-uhnt","adjective","Boldly refusing to obey.","The defiant cat would not get off the kitchen table.","","Defiant sounds like “defy-ant”: a tiny ant stomping and refusing."],["deft","DEFT","adjective","Quick and skillful with your hands.","With deft fingers, she folded a paper crane in one minute.","","Deft sounds like left: quick and skillful with either hand."],["annex","AN-eks","noun","An extra building added on or nearby.","Art class is held in the school annex, the small building next door.","From Latin annectere, “to tie to.”","Annex sounds like “an extra”: an extra building."],["oblivious","uh-BLIV-ee-uhs","adjective","Not noticing what is going on around you.","Dad was oblivious to the toilet paper stuck on his shoe.","From Latin oblivisci, “to forget.”","Oblivious sounds like “oh, blind to it”: you don’t notice a thing."],["dubious","DOO-bee-uhs","adjective","Unsure or doubtful.","I was dubious about the green smoothie, but it was tasty.","From Latin dubius, “doubtful.”","Dubious sounds like “doubt-ious”: full of doubt."],["calamity","kuh-LAM-i-tee","noun","A sudden disaster or terrible event.","Dropping the birthday cake on the floor felt like a calamity.","","Calamity sounds like “clam-in-a-city”: a total disaster."],["gourmet","goor-MAY","adjective","Fancy and made with great care, often about food.","Dad made gourmet pancakes with berries, cream and fancy syrup.","From French gourmet, “wine taster.”","Gourmet is a French word: picture a fancy chef in a tall white hat."],["inquire","in-KWYRE","verb","To ask about something.","Mom called the pool to inquire about swim lessons and their prices.","From Latin inquirere, “to seek into.”","Inquire sounds like “inquiry”: a question you ask out loud."],["debate","dih-BAYT","noun","A formal talk where people argue different sides.","Our class held a debate about whether recess should be longer.","","Debate sounds like “the bait”: both sides try to hook you with reasons."],["dispatch","dih-SPACH","verb","To send off quickly.","The coach dispatched a runner to grab the extra balls.","","Dispatch sounds like “this patch”: send someone off fast to fix it."],["apparel","uh-PAIR-uhl","noun","Clothing.","The store sells sports apparel like jerseys, shorts, and socks.","","Apparel sounds like “a pair-el”: a pair of shorts, a pair of socks."],["comprehend","kom-pri-HEND","verb","To understand.","I read the page twice to comprehend it.","From Latin comprehendere, “to grasp.”","Comprehend sounds like “come, I get it”: you understand."],["mediocre","mee-dee-OH-ker","adjective","Just okay; not very good.","The pizza was mediocre, not bad but not great either.","From Latin mediocris, “middling.”","Medi means middle: mediocre is stuck right in the middle."],["sufficient","suh-FISH-uhnt","adjective","Enough.","Two slices of pizza were sufficient to fill me up.","","Sufficient sounds like “suffish-ent”: enough-ish, just right."],["obsolete","ob-suh-LEET","adjective","No longer used because something better came along.","My old flip phone is obsolete now that everyone has smartphones.","From Latin obsoletus, “worn out.”","Obsolete sounds like “oh, so late”: too late, nobody uses it now."],["diminish","dih-MIN-ish","verb","To become smaller or less.","The puddle diminished in the hot sun until it was gone.","","Diminish sounds like “dim and finish”: fading smaller till it’s gone."],["mandatory","MAN-duh-tor-ee","adjective","Something you must do; not a choice.","Wearing a helmet is mandatory at the skate park.","From Latin mandare, “to order.”","Mandatory sounds like “man, it’s a must”: you have to do it."],["feasible","FEE-zuh-buhl","adjective","Possible to do.","Is it feasible to build a sled from a cardboard box? Let’s try!","From French faisable, “doable.”","Feasible sounds like “easy-ble”: easy enough that it can be done."],["exceed","ik-SEED","verb","To go beyond a limit or amount.","If we sell ten more muffins, our bake sale will exceed its goal.","From Latin excedere, “to go beyond.”","Exceed sounds like “extra speed”: go past the line."],["gullible","GUL-uh-buhl","adjective","Easy to fool.","My gullible brother believed me when I said the moon was cheese.","From gull, an old word meaning “to trick.”","Gullible sounds like “gull”: a seagull will grab any trick bait."],["deter","dih-TUR","verb","To stop someone from doing something by making them worry.","The scarecrow deters birds from eating the corn.","From Latin deterrere, “to frighten off.”","Deter sounds like “the terror”: something scary that keeps you away."],["kinetic","kih-NET-ik","adjective","Having to do with motion.","The rolling skateboard has kinetic energy because it is moving.","From Greek kinein, “to move.”","Kinetic sounds like “kick-netic”: kick a ball and it moves."],["partial","PAR-shuhl","adjective","Only part, not the whole.","I only got partial credit because I forgot to show my work.","From Latin pars, “part.”","Partial has “part” in it: only part, not the whole."],["abstain","ab-STAYN","verb","To choose not to do or have something.","I will abstain from candy until after dinner, even though it’s hard.","From Latin abstinere, “to hold back.”","Abstain sounds like “ab-stain”: stay away so you don’t get a stain."],["anecdote","AN-ik-doht","noun","A short, interesting story about something that happened.","Grandpa told a funny anecdote about falling off his first bike.","From Greek anekdota, “things not published.”","Anecdote sounds like “an egg dote”: a short, funny story about an egg."],["quell","KWEL","verb","To calm or stop something.","The teacher clapped her hands to quell the noisy class.","","Quell sounds like “quiet, well”: making things quiet."],["prominent","PROM-uh-nuhnt","adjective","Easy to see or well known.","The giant slide is the most prominent thing at our playground.","From Latin prominere, “to stick out.”","Prominent things stick out, like a mountain on a flat field."],["spontaneous","spon-TAY-nee-uhs","adjective","Done all of a sudden, without a plan.","We took a spontaneous trip for ice cream after school.","From Latin sponte, “of one’s own free will.”","Spontaneous sounds like “spot on now”: done right now, no plan."],["insight","IN-syte","noun","A clear, deep understanding.","Grandpa had an insight: the bread was flat because we forgot the yeast.","","Insight is sight on the inside: seeing what is really going on."],["frugal","FROO-guhl","adjective","Careful not to waste money or things.","Being frugal, Grandpa reuses old jars and never wastes a thing.","From Latin frugalis, “thrifty.”","Frugal sounds like “free-gull”: a seagull that never wastes a crumb."],["advocate","AD-vuh-kuht","noun","A person who speaks up for someone or something.","My aunt is an advocate for animal shelters. She speaks up for pets.","From Latin advocatus, “one called to help.”","Advocate has “voc” like “vocal”: be vocal for someone."],["coincide","koh-in-SYDE","verb","To happen at the same time.","This year my birthday coincides with the first day of spring.","","Coincide sounds like coincidence: two things at the same time."],["barren","BAIR-uhn","adjective","Empty, with few or no plants.","The hill was barren, with only rocks and dirt and no plants.","","Barren sounds like “bare-en”: bare and empty land."],["elaborate","ih-LAB-er-it","adjective","Having lots of careful parts and details.","Mia built an elaborate fort with tunnels, flags and a secret door.","From Latin elaborare, “to work out.”","Elaborate has “labor” in it: lots of work and lots of parts."],["whim","WIM","noun","A sudden wish or idea.","On a whim, we decided to have breakfast for dinner.","","Whim sounds like “whee!”: a sudden fun idea."],["resilient","rih-ZIL-yuhnt","adjective","Able to bounce back after something hard.","My friend was resilient and kept trying after falling off her skateboard.","From Latin resilire, “to spring back.”","Resilient sounds like a rubber ball: it bounces right back."],["belittle","bih-LIT-uhl","verb","To make someone or something seem unimportant.","Good teammates never belittle each other’s mistakes.","","Belittle has “little” in it: make someone feel little."],["terminate","TUR-muh-nayt","verb","To bring something to an end.","The ref had to terminate the game because of lightning.","From Latin terminus, “end, boundary.”","Terminate sounds like terminal: the last stop where everything ends."],["profound","pruh-FOWND","adjective","Very deep or strong in meaning.","Grandma said something profound: “Being kind is always the right choice.”","From Latin profundus, “deep.”","Profound has “found” in it: a big idea found deep down."],["superb","soo-PURB","adjective","Excellent, of the best kind.","The chef made a superb cake with three layers of chocolate.","From Latin superbus, “proud, grand.”","Superb has “super” in it: super good."],["vulnerable","VUL-ner-uh-buhl","adjective","Easy to hurt or harm.","A baby bird is vulnerable when it falls out of its nest.","From Latin vulnus, “wound.”","Vulnerable sounds like “very fumble-able”: easy to get hurt."],["prohibit","proh-HIB-it","verb","To not allow something by a rule.","Our pool rules prohibit running on the deck.","From Latin prohibere, “to hold back.”","Picture a big red sign that says “No running”: running is prohibited."],["erode","ih-ROHD","verb","To wear away slowly.","Each wave helps to erode our sandcastle until it’s flat.","From Latin erodere, “to gnaw away.”","Erode sounds like “ear-rode”: water rode over it until it wore away."],["narrate","NAIR-ayt","verb","To tell a story out loud.","Grandpa narrated the story of the time his dog ate his shoe.","From Latin narrare, “to tell.”","A narrator is the voice that narrates, or tells, the story."],["abyss","uh-BIS","noun","A very deep hole or gap that seems to have no bottom.","The cave looked like a dark abyss with no bottom.","From Greek abyssos, “bottomless.”","Abyss sounds like “a bye-ss”: wave bye as you fall in forever."],["rebuke","rih-BYOOK","verb","To tell someone sharply that they did wrong.","The coach had to rebuke the player who pushed during the game.","","Rebuke sounds like “rule book”: the coach throws the rule book at you."],["astute","uh-STOOT","adjective","Smart and quick to notice things.","The astute detective in my book spotted the tiny clue first.","From Latin astutus, “crafty.”","Astute sounds like “a-stew”: smart enough to know what’s in the stew."],["acquaint","uh-KWAYNT","verb","To make yourself or someone familiar with something.","The tour will acquaint new students with the school.","","Acquaint sounds like “a-quaint-ance”: meeting something new."],["accumulate","uh-KYOO-myuh-layt","verb","To gather or pile up over time.","Dust will accumulate under the bed if we don’t sweep.","From Latin cumulus, “heap.”","Accumulate sounds like “a cumulus cloud”: it piles up bigger and bigger."],["loiter","LOY-ter","verb","To stand around without a real reason.","Kids loiter around the snack table even when there’s nothing left.","","Loiter sounds like “lazy-loafer”: hanging around for no reason."],["infinite","IN-fuh-nit","adjective","Without any end.","Counting the stars feels infinite; you could never reach the end.","From Latin infinitus, “without end.”","In-finite means not finite: no finish line, ever."],["pinnacle","PIN-uh-kuhl","noun","The highest point.","Winning the spelling bee was the pinnacle of my school year.","From Latin pinnaculum, “little peak.”","Pinnacle sounds like “pin on top”: a pin stuck on the very top."],["parody","PAIR-uh-dee","noun","A funny copy of something to make people laugh.","We made a parody of our favorite song with words about homework.","From Greek parodia, “a mocking song.”","Parody sounds like “parrot-y”: copying something in a silly, funny way."],["diagnose","DY-uhg-nohs","verb","To find out what is causing a problem.","The vet diagnosed my cat’s sore paw.","","Diagnose has “nose”: the doctor sniffs out what is wrong."],["tangible","TAN-juh-buhl","adjective","Real enough to touch or see.","A trophy is a tangible prize you can hold in your hands.","From Latin tangere, “to touch.”","Tang comes from a Latin word for touch: tangible means you can touch it."],["propel","pruh-PEL","verb","To push something forward.","I pushed with my foot to propel my scooter down the sidewalk.","From Latin propellere, “to drive forward.”","Propel sounds like propeller: it pushes a plane forward."],["plight","PLYT","noun","A hard or sad situation.","We felt bad about the plight of the lost kitten out in the rain.","","Plight rhymes with fright: a scary, sad spot to be in."],["blight","BLITE","noun","A disease that makes plants dry up and die.","A blight made all the tomato plants in our garden turn brown and die.","","Blight sounds like “bite”: a sickness that bites plants."],["deduce","dih-DOOS","verb","To figure out from clues.","I deduced the dog ate my sandwich from the crumbs on his nose.","From Latin deducere, “to lead down.”","Detectives deduce: clue plus clue equals the answer."],["unkempt","un-KEMPT","adjective","Messy and not cared for.","After the camping trip, my hair was totally unkempt.","","Unkempt means not kept: no one kept it neat."],["aspect","AS-pekt","noun","One part or side of something.","My favorite aspect of camp is swimming in the lake.","From Latin aspicere, “to look at.”","Aspect has “spect” like “inspect”: one part you look at."],["secluded","sih-KLOO-did","adjective","Hidden away from other people.","We found a secluded spot in the park where no one could find us.","","Secluded sounds like “secret” and “excluded”: hidden away."],["suppress","suh-PRES","verb","To hold something back.","I tried to suppress a giggle during the quiet test.","From Latin supprimere, “to press down.”","Suppress has “press” in it: pressing it down to hold it in."],["adjacent","uh-JAY-suhnt","adjective","Right next to something.","My room is adjacent to my brother’s room. Our walls touch!","From Latin adiacere, “to lie near.”","Adjacent sounds like “a jay sent”: a bird sent next door."],["proximity","prok-SIM-ih-tee","noun","Being close to something.","Our house is in close proximity to the park, so we walk there.","From Latin proximus, “nearest.”","Proximus means nearest in Latin: proximity means close by."],["eccentric","ik-SEN-trik","adjective","Odd or unusual in a fun way.","Our eccentric neighbor walks his cat on a leash and wears a top hat.","From Greek ekkentros, “out of the center.”","Eccentric means “off center”: a little bit off the usual path."],["amass","uh-MAS","verb","To collect a large amount of something.","Over the years, she managed to amass a huge rock collection.","","Amass has “mass” in it: gather a big mass of stuff."],["grueling","GROO-uh-ling","adjective","Very hard and tiring.","The grueling hike up the steep hill took two hours.","","Grueling sounds like gruel: as hard as eating cold, lumpy oatmeal."],["culprit","KUHL-prit","noun","The one who did something wrong.","The culprit who ate the cupcakes had frosting on his whiskers.","","Culprit sounds like “cul-PRIT, who did it?”: the one to blame."],["adversary","AD-ver-sair-ee","noun","Someone you compete against.","In the chess match, my adversary was my best friend.","","Adversary has “vers” like “versus”: the one you go versus."],["prodigy","PROD-uh-jee","noun","A young person with amazing talent.","The six-year-old prodigy could play hard songs on the piano.","From Latin prodigium, “a marvel.”","Prodigy sounds like “pro kiddie”: a kid who plays like a pro."],["naive","ny-EEV","adjective","Trusting too easily, without much experience.","My naive little brother believed me when I said carrots grow on trees.","From French naïf, “natural.”","Naive sounds like “nah, I’ll believe”: trusting way too fast."],["berate","bih-RAYT","verb","To scold someone loudly and angrily.","The coach did not berate us for losing. He told us to keep trying.","","Berate sounds like “be irate”: yell at someone angrily."],["pseudonym","SOO-duh-nim","noun","A made-up name a writer uses.","My sister signs her comics with a pseudonym, Captain Pickle.","From Greek pseudonymos, “falsely named.”","Pseudo means fake, and nym means name: a fake name."],["bizarre","bih-ZAR","adjective","Very strange or odd.","My dog has a bizarre habit of sleeping in the bathtub.","","Bizarre sounds like “bazaar”: a market full of strange things."],["strenuous","STREN-yoo-uhs","adjective","Needing lots of hard work or effort.","Carrying all the groceries up the stairs was strenuous.","","Strenuous sounds like “strain-uous”: it makes you strain and sweat."],["amiable","AY-mee-uh-buhl","adjective","Friendly and pleasant.","The amiable bus driver smiles and waves to every kid.","From Latin amicabilis, “friendly.”","Ami means “friend” in French: amiable means friendly."],["malfunction","mal-FUNGK-shuhn","noun","A problem that stops a machine from working right.","The snack machine had a malfunction and kept my coins.","","Mal means bad: a malfunction is a machine working badly."],["avert","uh-VURT","verb","To turn away or to stop something bad from happening.","I turned my head to avert my eyes from the scary part of the movie.","From Latin avertere, “to turn away.”","Avert comes from Latin for “turn away”: turn away from trouble."],["perspective","per-SPEK-tiv","noun","A way of seeing or thinking about something.","From my little brother’s perspective, bedtime is way too early.","From Latin perspicere, “to look through.”","Spect means look, like spectacles: perspective is how you look at things."],["conspicuous","kuhn-SPIK-yoo-uhs","adjective","Easy to see or notice.","My bright orange hat was conspicuous in the crowd.","From Latin conspicere, “to look at.”","Conspicuous sounds like “spot it”: easy to spy."],["miniature","MIN-ee-uh-cher","adjective","Very small; a tiny copy of something bigger.","My sister’s dollhouse has miniature chairs smaller than my thumb.","From Italian miniatura, a small painting in an old book.","Miniature starts with mini: a tiny version of something."],["chronic","KRON-ik","adjective","Lasting a long time or happening again and again.","My brother’s chronic sniffles come back every spring.","From Greek khronos, “time.”","Chronos is Greek for time: chronic lasts a long time."],["lenient","LEE-nee-uhnt","adjective","Not strict, more forgiving.","The lenient teacher let us have an extra day to finish the project.","From Latin lenis, “soft, gentle.”","Lenient sounds like “lean-y”: leaning toward letting you off easy."],["abundant","uh-BUHN-duhnt","adjective","More than enough; plenty.","There were so many blackberries that we filled two buckets. They were abundant!","From Latin abundare, “to overflow.”","Abundant sounds like “a bun dance”: so many buns they’re dancing everywhere."],["zeal","ZEEL","noun","Great eagerness for something.","My dog chases tennis balls with great zeal.","From Greek zelos, “eager rivalry.”","Zeal sounds like “zeal-ous seal”: a seal clapping with excitement."],["assert","uh-SURT","verb","To say something firmly and with confidence.","She asserted that her paper airplane would fly the farthest.","From Latin asserere, “to claim.”","Assert sounds like “a shirt” that says I AM RIGHT in big letters."],["retrieve","rih-TREEV","verb","To go get something and bring it back.","The dog loves to retrieve the ball and drop it at my feet.","","Retrieve sounds like retriever: the dog that goes and brings it back."],["rotate","ROH-tayt","verb","To turn around a center point.","The ceiling fan will rotate around and around all night.","From Latin rota, “wheel.”","Rotate sounds like “roll-tate”: rolling round and round."],["mesmerize","MEZ-muh-ryz","verb","To hold someone's attention so they can't look away.","The floating bubbles mesmerized my baby sister.","","Mesmerize sounds like “mess my eyes”: so cool your eyes can’t look away."],["pertinent","PUR-tuh-nuhnt","adjective","Connected to what you are talking about.","Your favorite color isn’t pertinent to a math question.","From Latin pertinere, “to relate to.”","Pertinent sounds like “pertains to it”: it has to do with it."],["apprehensive","ap-rih-HEN-siv","adjective","Worried that something bad might happen.","I felt apprehensive about my first day at a new school.","","Apprehensive sounds like “a pretend-sive”: you pretend you’re fine, but you’re worried."],["idiom","ID-ee-uhm","noun","A phrase that means something different from its words.","“It’s raining cats and dogs” is an idiom; it just means it’s raining hard.","From Greek idioma, “a special way of speaking.”","An idiom is a saying where the words play pretend, like piece of cake."],["designate","DEZ-ig-nayt","verb","To choose someone or something for a job.","The teacher designated me as line leader today.","","Designate has “sign”: you put a sign on who gets the job."],["commence","kuh-MENS","verb","To begin.","The race will commence when the whistle blows.","","Commence sounds like “commencement,” the day graduates begin a new start."],["onset","ON-set","noun","The beginning of something.","At the onset of the storm, we ran inside.","","Onset is when things are set and on, like the start of a race."],["cumbersome","KUHM-ber-suhm","adjective","Big, heavy and hard to carry.","The cumbersome cello case barely fit in the car.","","Cumbersome sounds like “cumber-some”: like carrying a giant cucumber."],["diverse","dih-VURS","adjective","Made up of many different kinds.","Our class is diverse, with kids who speak five different languages.","","Diverse: like a box of crayons with every color."],["acknowledge","ak-NOL-ij","verb","To admit or show that you notice something.","She waved to acknowledge her friends in the crowd.","","Acknowledge has “know” in it: show that you know they’re there."],["taut","TAWT","adjective","Pulled tight.","Pull the jump rope taut so it doesn’t sag in the middle.","","Taut sounds like taught: the rope was taught to stand up straight."],["lavish","LAV-ish","adjective","Rich, fancy and generous.","The birthday party was lavish, with a bounce house and three cakes.","","Lavish sounds like “lava-ish”: so much fancy stuff it overflows like lava."],["balk","BAWK","verb","To stop and refuse to go on.","My dog will balk at the vet’s door and refuse to go in.","","Balk sounds like “stalk”: stop dead and refuse to move."],["proficient","pruh-FISH-uhnt","adjective","Very good at something from practice.","After a summer of practice, I’m proficient at doing cartwheels.","From Latin proficere, “to make progress.”","Proficient starts with pro: good at it like a pro."],["deceive","dih-SEEV","verb","To trick someone into believing something false.","The stick bug deceives birds by looking like a twig.","From Latin decipere, “to catch, trick.”","Deceive sounds like “the sleeve”: a trick hidden up your sleeve."],["punctual","PUNGK-choo-uhl","adjective","Always on time.","My friend is so punctual that she gets to class before the bell every day.","From Latin punctum, “point.”","Punct means point: punctual means right on the dot."],["quota","KWOH-tuh","noun","A set amount you must reach or can’t go over.","Mom’s quota is two cookies after dinner, no more.","From Latin quota pars, “how great a part.”","Quota sounds like “quote a number”: a set amount."],["loathe","LOHTH","verb","To dislike something very much.","I loathe wet socks; they make me want to scream.","From Old English lāthian, “to hate.”","Loathe sounds like “loads of hate”: really, really dislike."],["intricate","IN-trih-kit","adjective","Having many small parts that fit together.","The spider spun an intricate web with hundreds of tiny threads.","From Latin intricare, “to tangle.”","Intricate sounds like “in-tricky”: tricky with lots of tiny parts."],["competent","KOM-pi-tuhnt","adjective","Having the skill to do something well.","She is a competent swimmer, so she can go in the deep end.","","Competent sounds like “can compete”: good enough to play."],["convey","kuhn-VAY","verb","To share or get an idea across.","Her big smile conveyed how happy she was.","","Convey sounds like conveyor belt: it carries a message across."],["burnish","BUR-nish","verb","To rub something until it is smooth and shiny.","We burnish the old coins with a soft cloth until they shine.","","Burnish sounds like “burn-ish”: rub so hard it shines like fire."],["elusive","ih-LOO-siv","adjective","Hard to find or catch.","The elusive cat hid every time we tried to catch her for her bath.","From Latin eludere, “to escape by tricks.”","Elusive sounds like “he’s loose”: always getting away."],["bias","BY-uhs","noun","Unfairly favoring one side over another.","The judge tried hard to have no bias and pick the best cake fairly.","","Bias sounds like “by us”: picking “us” just because it’s us."],["discard","dih-SKARD","verb","To throw away.","We discarded the broken crayons into the trash.","","Discard sounds like “toss a card”: throw it away."],["maneuver","muh-NOO-ver","verb","To move skillfully around things.","I maneuvered my bike around all the puddles.","From French manœuvre, “handwork.”","Maneuver sounds like “man, move her”: steer carefully around things."],["aquatic","uh-KWAT-ik","adjective","Living or growing in water.","Frogs, fish, and turtles are aquatic animals that live in water.","From Latin aqua, “water.”","Aqua means water: aquatic animals live in the water."],["decipher","dih-SY-fer","verb","To figure out something hard to read.","We deciphered the secret code in the treasure hunt.","","A cipher is a secret code; decipher means crack it."],["wane","WAYN","verb","To get smaller or weaker little by little.","My energy starts to wane by the end of a long school day.","","Wane sounds like “wane, wane, go away”: getting smaller bit by bit."],["depict","dih-PIKT","verb","To show in a picture or in words.","My drawing depicts my family at the beach.","From Latin depingere, “to paint.”","Depict sounds like “the picture”: it shows it."],["excavate","EK-skuh-vayt","verb","To dig out and uncover.","We used shovels to excavate the sandbox and find my lost toy car.","From Latin excavare, “to hollow out.”","Excavate has “cave” in it: dig out a cave."],["modify","MOD-uh-fy","verb","To change something a little.","I modified my paper airplane by folding the wings up.","From Latin modificare, “to limit.”","Modify sounds like “mod it”: change it up a little."],["pandemonium","pan-duh-MOH-nee-uhm","noun","Wild noise and confusion.","When the class hamster got loose, there was pandemonium in the room.","","Pandemonium sounds like “pans and drums”: everyone banging at once."],["antagonist","an-TAG-uh-nist","noun","The character who works against the hero in a story.","The antagonist in the movie was a sneaky fox who tried to stop the hero.","From Greek antagōnistēs, “opponent.”","Antagonist has “anti” in it: against the hero."],["skeptical","SKEP-tih-kuhl","adjective","Not quick to believe something.","I was skeptical when my brother said he saw a ghost.","From Greek skeptikos, “thoughtful, inquiring.”","Skeptical sounds like “skip it”: you skip believing it right away."],["morale","muh-RAL","noun","How hopeful and cheerful a group feels.","Our team’s morale went way up after we won the first game.","From French moral, “spirit.”","Morale sounds like “more rally”: the team feels ready to rally."],["proclaim","proh-KLAYM","verb","To announce something loudly and proudly.","My sister proclaimed that she was the best dancer in the family.","From Latin proclamare, “to cry out.”","Pro plus claim: you claim it loud and out in front."],["solemn","SOL-uhm","adjective","Very serious and quiet.","Everyone was solemn and quiet during the moment of silence.","","Solemn sounds like “so calm”: serious and still."],["altitude","AL-tih-tood","noun","How high something is above the ground or sea.","The plane flew at a very high altitude, way above the clouds.","From Latin altus, “high.”","Altitude comes from Latin “altus,” meaning high: how high up you are."],["abrasive","uh-BRAY-siv","adjective","Rough enough to scrape, or harsh in how you act.","Sandpaper is abrasive. It feels rough and scratchy.","From Latin abradere, “to scrape off.”","Abrasive sounds like “abrasion”: a scrape on your knee."],["tamper","TAM-per","verb","To mess with something you should not touch.","Don’t tamper with Dad’s fishing gear or the lines will tangle.","","Tamper sounds like temper: mess with it and someone loses their temper."],["surmount","ser-MOWNT","verb","To get past a problem.","I had to surmount my fear of heights to climb the rock wall.","","Surmount has “mount” in it: climbing up and over a mountain."],["finicky","FIN-ih-kee","adjective","Very fussy about what you like.","My finicky little brother won’t eat anything green on his plate.","","Finicky sounds like “picky”: very fussy."],["drought","DROWT","noun","A long time with little or no rain.","During the drought, the grass turned brown and crunchy.","","Drought sounds like “dried out”: no rain for a long time."],["relish","REL-ish","verb","To enjoy something a lot.","I relish the first day of summer break.","","Relish is also a yummy sauce: enjoy it like hot dogs with relish."],["credible","KRED-uh-buhl","adjective","Able to be believed or trusted.","We used credible websites for our school report.","From Latin credere, “to believe.”","Incredible means hard to believe, so credible means you can believe it."],["swindle","SWIN-duhl","verb","To cheat someone out of money or things.","The kid tried to swindle me by trading a fake card for my real one.","","Swindle sounds like “swiped and swindled”: tricked out of your stuff."],["contaminate","kuhn-TAM-uh-nayt","verb","To make something dirty or unsafe.","A fly landed on my sandwich and contaminated it, so I threw it out.","","Contaminate: yucky germs got in and spoiled it."],["arduous","AR-joo-uhs","adjective","Very hard and tiring.","Climbing the steep hill was an arduous task. We were so tired!","From Latin arduus, “steep.”","Arduous sounds like “hard-uous”: very hard work."],["accessible","ak-SES-uh-buhl","adjective","Easy to reach, get, or use.","The new ramp makes the playground accessible to everyone.","","Accessible has “access” in it: easy to get to."],["contemplate","KON-tuhm-playt","verb","To think deeply about something.","I sat on the swing and contemplated which book to read next.","","Contemplate: sit still like a thinker statue and think it over."],["interval","IN-ter-vuhl","noun","The time or space between two things.","We took a water break at short intervals during practice, every ten minutes.","From Latin intervallum, “space between walls.”","Inter means between: the time in between."],["component","kuhm-POH-nuhnt","noun","One part of a bigger thing.","The battery is one key component of my remote control car.","From Latin componere, “to put together.”","Component: one piece of the puzzle that makes the whole."],["vex","VEKS","verb","To bother or annoy.","It will vex my sister if I keep humming the same song.","From Latin vexare, “to shake, trouble.”","Vex sounds like hex: a little bothering spell on someone."],["wield","WEELD","verb","To hold and use a tool well.","My sister can wield a hockey stick like a pro.","","Wield sounds like shield: holding your tool like a knight’s shield."],["opaque","oh-PAYK","adjective","Not letting light through.","The bathroom window is opaque, so nobody can see inside.","From Latin opacus, “shaded.”","Opaque sounds like “oh, packed”: packed so thick light can’t get through."],["animated","AN-uh-may-tid","adjective","Lively and full of energy.","The kids had an animated chat about their favorite video games.","From Latin anima, “breath, life.”","Animated like cartoons: lively, bouncy, and full of energy."],["hypothesis","hy-POTH-uh-sis","noun","A guess you can test in an experiment.","My hypothesis is that the paper plane with bigger wings will fly farther, so let’s test it.","From Greek hypothesis, “a base, supposition.”","Hypothesis sounds like “high-pot-thesis”: a guess you test like a recipe in a pot."],["gallant","GAL-uhnt","adjective","Brave and polite.","In a gallant move, Theo held the door for everyone in the rain.","","Gallant sounds like “gal-ant”: a brave knight being polite."],["collaborate","kuh-LAB-uh-rayt","verb","To work with others on a project.","We collaborated with our friends to make a recycling poster.","From Latin com-, “with,” and laborare, “to work.”","Co means together, and labor means work: working together."],["mishap","MIS-hap","noun","A small accident.","We had a small mishap when the milk spilled all over the table.","","Mis means wrong, and hap is an old word for luck: bad luck."],["camaraderie","kah-muh-RAH-duh-ree","noun","A warm, friendly feeling among a group of friends or teammates.","The team’s camaraderie grew with every pizza party and practice.","From French camarade, “comrade.”","Camaraderie sounds like “comrade”: best buddies sticking together."],["heritage","HAIR-ih-tij","noun","Traditions and things passed down from your family or the past.","Grandma’s dumpling recipe is part of our family heritage; she learned it from her mom.","From Latin heres, “heir.”","Heritage sounds like “inherit”: things passed down to you."],["articulate","ar-TIK-yuh-lit","adjective","Able to say things clearly and well.","The articulate student explained her science project clearly to the judges.","","Articulate sounds like “art-tic-you-late”: words neat as art."],["domestic","duh-MES-tik","adjective","Kept by people at home, not wild.","Dogs and cats are domestic animals that live with people.","From Latin domus, “house.”","Domestic comes from Latin domus, home: animals that live at home."],["compromise","KOM-pruh-myze","noun","An agreement where each side gives up a little.","Our compromise was pizza tonight and tacos tomorrow.","","In a compromise, everyone gives a little and everyone gets a little."],["attribute","AT-ruh-byoot","noun","Something special about someone, like being kind or funny.","Kindness is my grandma’s best attribute.","","Attribute sounds like “a tribute”: a good quality we honor."],["conscience","KON-shuhns","noun","The little voice inside that tells you right from wrong.","My conscience told me to give back the extra change.","From Latin conscientia, “knowledge within oneself.”","Conscience has “science” in it: your inner lab that tests right and wrong."],["overt","oh-VURT","adjective","Done openly, not hidden.","Her big eye roll was an overt sign she didn’t like the joke.","From Old French overt, “open.”","Overt is the opposite of covert: nothing is under cover."],["ailment","AYL-muhnt","noun","A small illness.","A cold is a common winter ailment.","","Ailment sounds like “ail-ment”: what ails you, makes you feel sick."],["multitude","MUL-tih-tood","noun","A very large number of things or people.","A multitude of kids rushed to the ice cream truck.","From Latin multitudo, “a great number.”","Multi means many: a multitude is a huge crowd."],["incentive","in-SEN-tiv","noun","Something that makes you want to try harder.","A pizza party is a great incentive to read more books.","From Latin incentivus, “setting the tune.”","Incentive sounds like “in-cent”: a reward that makes you want to try."],["sustain","suh-STAYN","verb","To keep something going.","We need to keep feeding the fire to sustain it.","From Latin sustinere, “to hold up.”","Sustain: keep it going, like holding one long note in a song."],["innovate","IN-uh-vayt","verb","To come up with new ideas or ways of doing things.","Our class tried to innovate a faster way to line up for lunch.","From Latin novus, “new.”","Innovate has “nov” like novel and new: make something new."],["empathy","EM-puh-thee","noun","Understanding and sharing how another person feels.","Nia showed empathy when she saw the new kid alone and sat with him.","From Greek pathos, “feeling.”","Empathy: feel what they feel, like walking in their shoes."],["mutual","MYOO-choo-uhl","adjective","Shared by two or more people.","My best friend and I have a mutual love of pancakes.","From Latin mutuus, “exchanged.”","Mutual sounds like “mu-two-al”: shared by the two of you."],["simulate","SIM-yuh-layt","verb","To copy how something real looks or works.","The video game can simulate driving a real race car.","","Simulate sounds like similar: making something similar to the real thing."],["integrity","in-TEG-rih-tee","noun","Being honest and doing the right thing.","Ali showed integrity when he gave back the extra change the cashier gave him.","From Latin integer, “whole.”","Integrity sounds like “integer,” a whole number: being whole and honest."],["amplify","AM-pluh-fy","verb","To make something louder or stronger.","A microphone will amplify your voice so everyone can hear.","From Latin amplus, “large.”","Amplify sounds like “amp”: a guitar amp makes it LOUD."],["bolster","BOHL-ster","verb","To support or make stronger.","Practicing every day will bolster your confidence.","From Old English bolster, “a long pillow.”","A bolster is a big pillow: it props you up and supports you."],["glimpse","GLIMPS","noun","A quick look.","I caught a glimpse of a shooting star before it vanished.","","Glimpse sounds like “glance-quick”: just a peek."],["perceive","per-SEEV","verb","To notice or understand something.","I could perceive a funny smell coming from my brother’s backpack.","From Latin percipere, “to seize wholly.”","Perceive rhymes with receive: your eyes and ears receive the news."],["epic","EP-ik","adjective","Very big, long or impressive.","We had an epic snowball fight that lasted all afternoon.","From Greek epos, “word, story.”","Epic: think of an epic movie, big, long and full of action."],["endeavor","en-DEV-er","noun","A serious try at doing something.","Building a giant cardboard castle was our biggest endeavor this year.","","Endeavor sounds like “end-ever”: try hard till the end."],["boon","BOON","noun","Something helpful or a blessing.","The new bike lane is a boon for kids who ride to school.","From Old Norse bón, “prayer, request.”","Boon sounds like “boom!”: a big burst of good luck."],["dynamic","dy-NAM-ik","adjective","Full of energy and always changing.","Our dynamic coach makes every practice fun and full of energy.","From Greek dynamis, “power.”","Dynamic sounds like dynamite: full of bang and energy."],["awry","uh-RY","adverb","Wrong, or not the way it was planned.","Our cake went awry when we forgot to add the eggs.","","Awry rhymes with “oh my!”: what you say when plans go wrong."],["casual","KAZH-oo-uhl","adjective","Relaxed and not fancy.","Friday is casual day, so I wore my comfy hoodie.","","Casual sounds like “cozy-all”: comfy clothes, nothing fancy."],["broach","BROHCH","verb","To bring up a topic to talk about.","I waited until dinner to broach the idea of getting a puppy.","","Broach sounds like “approach”: bring up a topic."],["encounter","en-KOWN-ter","noun","A meeting, often by surprise.","We had a surprise encounter with a deer on the bike path.","","Encounter has “counter”: you run into someone face to face."],["gesture","JES-cher","noun","A movement of the hands or body that shows a meaning.","A thumbs-up is a gesture that means “good job.”","From Latin gerere, “to carry, act.”","Gesture sounds like “jest-sure”: a hand move that says something."],["assess","uh-SES","verb","To judge or figure out how good or big something is.","The teacher will assess our reading to see how much we’ve grown.","","Assess sounds like “a-test”: test it to see how good it is."],["vigorous","VIG-er-uhs","adjective","Strong and full of energy.","We did vigorous jumping jacks in gym class until we were out of breath.","","Vigorous sounds like a tiger: strong, fast and full of energy."],["prolong","pruh-LONG","verb","To make something last longer.","We prolonged our sleepover by asking to stay for breakfast.","From Latin prolongare, “to lengthen.”","Prolong has “long” in it: making fun things last long."],["utilize","YOO-tuh-lyz","verb","To use something in a helpful way.","We can utilize old boxes to build a cardboard castle.","","Utilize sounds like utility: finding a use for something."],["deplete","dih-PLEET","verb","To use up.","We depleted our supply of glue sticks by Friday.","","Deplete sounds like “de-plate”: all the food on the plate is gone."],["compile","kuhm-PYLE","verb","To gather things into one list or collection.","We compiled our favorite recipes into one class cookbook.","","Compile has “pile”: put it all into one pile."],["latitude","LAT-ih-tood","noun","How far north or south a place is from the middle of the earth.","On the globe, we traced the latitude lines that run side to side like ladder steps.","From Latin latitudo, “width.”","Latitude sounds like “ladder-tude”: lines like ladder rungs going up and down the globe."],["blatant","BLAY-tuhnt","adjective","Very obvious, done openly.","Peeking at my cards was a blatant way to cheat.","","Blatant sounds like “blaring”: loud and obvious."],["array","uh-RAY","noun","A large, neat group of things.","The bakery had a colorful array of cupcakes in neat rows.","","Array sounds like “a ray”: a ray of colors all lined up."],["correspond","kor-uh-SPOND","verb","To write letters to each other.","I correspond with a pen pal by writing letters.","","Correspond has “respond”: you write back and forth."],["virtual","VUR-choo-uhl","adjective","Made by a computer, not real.","We took a virtual tour of a zoo on the class computer.","","Virtual sounds like “virtually there”: on a screen, not really there."],["delve","DELV","verb","To dig deep into a subject.","We delved into books about volcanoes for our project.","","Delve sounds like “dig a shelf”: digging deep to find more."],["disperse","dih-SPURS","verb","To spread out in different directions.","The pigeons dispersed when my dog ran at them.","From Latin dispergere, “to scatter.”","Disperse: dis means apart, so everyone scatters apart."],["steadfast","STED-fast","adjective","Loyal and not changing.","My dog is steadfast and waits by the door every day for me.","","Steadfast: steady and stuck fast, never moving away."],["justify","JUS-tuh-fy","verb","To give a good reason for something.","Can you justify why we need a second hamster? Give me a good reason.","From Latin justus, “fair, right.”","Justify has “just” in it: show it’s fair and right."],["versatile","VUR-suh-tuhl","adjective","Able to do many different things.","A pocket knife is versatile because it has lots of tools.","","Versatile sounds like “very tile”: one tile that fits everywhere."],["discreet","dih-SKREET","adjective","Careful not to draw attention or share secrets.","Be discreet about the surprise party, so Mom doesn’t find out.","","Discreet sounds like “this secret”: keep it quiet."],["upheaval","up-HEE-vuhl","noun","A big, sudden change.","Moving to a new house was a big upheaval for our family.","","Upheaval has “heave” in it: everything heaved up and turned over."],["prototype","PROH-tuh-typ","noun","The first model of something new.","My first prototype of a paper boat sank, so I made a better one.","From Greek prototypos, “first form.”","Proto means first: a prototype is the very first try."],["compensate","KOM-puhn-sayt","verb","To make up for something.","I compensated for my late start by running faster.","","Compensate: you make up for it, like paying back what you owe."],["circumstance","SUR-kuhm-stans","noun","A fact or event that affects what happens.","Because of the rainy circumstances, we moved the picnic inside.","From Latin circumstare, “to stand around.”","Circum means around: the things around what happens."],["curtail","ker-TAYL","verb","To cut short or limit.","Rain curtailed our soccer game, so we went home early.","","Curtail has “tail”: cut the tail off short."],["bonanza","buh-NAN-zuh","noun","A big pile of good luck or good stuff.","The yard sale was a bonanza of cheap comic books.","From Spanish bonanza, “fair weather.”","Bonanza sounds like “bonus”: a big bonus of good stuff."],["agenda","uh-JEN-duh","noun","A list of things to do or talk about.","The first thing on our club agenda was picking a cool name.","From Latin agenda, “things to be done.”","Agenda is Latin for “things to be done”: your to-do list."],["uphold","up-HOHLD","verb","To support or keep something going.","We all promise to uphold the class rules.","","Uphold means hold up: keep it standing strong."],["immerse","ih-MURS","verb","To put fully into something, like water or a task.","I immerse myself in a good book and forget everything else.","From Latin immergere, “to dip in.”","Immerse sounds like “in mermaid”: dive fully in like a mermaid."],["presume","prih-ZOOM","verb","To think something is true without being sure.","I presumed the cookies were for us, but they were for the bake sale.","From Latin praesumere, “to take beforehand.”","Presume sounds like “pre-assume”: you guess before you really know."],["illuminate","ih-LOO-mih-nayt","verb","To light up.","Fireflies illuminate the backyard with tiny blinking lights.","From Latin lumen, “light.”","Illuminate has “lumin” like “luminous”: full of light."],["transparent","trans-PAIR-uhnt","adjective","Clear enough to see through.","The fish tank is transparent, so we can see the fish swim.","","Trans means through: you can see right through it."],["adhere","ad-HEER","verb","To stick to something, or to follow a rule closely.","Stickers adhere to my notebook, and they won’t come off.","From Latin adhaerere, “to stick to.”","Adhere sounds like “add here”: stick it right here."],["appease","uh-PEEZ","verb","To calm someone by giving them what they want.","We gave the barking puppy a toy to appease him, and he got quiet.","From Old French apaisier, “to bring to peace.”","Appease has “peace” in it: give them something to make peace."],["jostle","JOS-uhl","verb","To bump and push in a crowd.","Kids jostle each other in the lunch line to get to the front.","","Jostle sounds like “joust-le”: bumping like knights jousting."],["orator","OR-uh-ter","noun","A skilled public speaker.","The best orator in our class gave a speech that made everyone cheer.","From Latin orator, “speaker.”","Orare is Latin for speak, like oral: an orator speaks well."],["candid","KAN-did","adjective","Honest and open about what you think.","My friend gave me a candid answer about my new haircut.","From Latin candidus, “white, pure.”","A candid camera catches the real you, no pretending."],["meager","MEE-ger","adjective","Too small in amount; not enough.","My meager allowance wasn’t enough to buy even one comic.","From Latin macer, “thin.”","Meager sounds like “meh, eager”: you were eager but got only a little."],["perimeter","puh-RIM-ih-ter","noun","The distance around the edge of a shape.","We jogged the perimeter of the soccer field to warm up.","From Greek perimetros, “measure around.”","Peri means around, and meter means measure: measure around."],["throng","THRONG","noun","A big crowd of people.","A throng of kids rushed out when the bell rang.","","Throng sounds like “thrown”: a crowd thrown together."],["thwart","THWORT","verb","To stop someone from doing something.","The goalie jumped to thwart my shot.","","Thwart sounds like “thwack!”: blocking someone’s move."],["vintage","VIN-tij","adjective","Old but of good quality.","Grandpa’s vintage bike from when he was a kid still rides well.","","Vintage sounds like “vint-age”: old age, but still good."],["evident","EV-ih-duhnt","adjective","Easy to see or understand.","With mud on his paws, it was evident the puppy had been outside.","From Latin evidens, “clear to see.”","Evident sounds like evidence: the clues make it plain to see."],["embellish","em-BEL-ish","verb","To decorate, or to add extra details to a story.","We embellish our notebooks with stickers, glitter and doodles.","From Old French embellir, “to make beautiful.”","Embellish sounds like “em-BELL-ish”: add bells and bows on top."],["devote","dih-VOHT","verb","To give lots of time or love to something.","He devotes every recess to practicing his kickflips.","","Devote sounds like “the vote”: you vote all your time for it."],["acute","uh-KYOOT","adjective","Sharp or very strong.","Owls have acute hearing. They can hear a mouse squeak from far away.","From Latin acutus, “sharpened.”","Acute comes from Latin for needle: sharp like a needle point."],["eloquent","EL-uh-kwuhnt","adjective","Able to speak or write in a clear, moving way.","Jamal gave an eloquent speech that made the whole class cheer.","From Latin eloqui, “to speak out.”","Eloquent sounds like “elegant”: words that sound smooth and beautiful."],["accord","uh-KORD","noun","Agreement.","The two teams were in accord about the rules, so there was no fighting.","From Latin cor, “heart.”","Accord sounds like “a chord”: everyone in tune together."],["exhilarate","ig-ZIL-uh-rayt","verb","To make someone feel very happy and excited.","Going down the giant water slide will exhilarate you.","From Latin hilaris, “cheerful.”","Exhilarate sounds like “hilarious”: so fun you scream and laugh."],["anonymous","uh-NON-uh-muhs","adjective","Having a name that is not known.","An anonymous person left a nice note, but nobody knows who wrote it.","From Greek anōnymos, “nameless.”","Anonymous sounds like “a no-name-ous”: someone with no name."],["enigma","ih-NIG-muh","noun","A puzzle or mystery that is hard to figure out.","Where my socks disappear to is a total enigma.","From Greek ainigma, “riddle.”","Enigma sounds like “a mystery-ma”: a puzzle no one can solve."],["adamant","AD-uh-muhnt","adjective","Firm and not willing to change your mind.","My brother was adamant that pizza is a breakfast food. He would not change his mind!","","Adamant sounds like “a dam”: firm and won’t budge."],["fathom","FATH-uhm","verb","To understand something hard.","I can’t fathom how a tiny ant carries a crumb bigger than itself.","From Old English fæthm, “outstretched arms.”","A fathom measures how deep water is: understand something deep."],["prospect","PROS-pekt","noun","Something that might happen in the future.","The prospect of a snow day made us all cheer.","From Latin prospectus, “view.”","Pro means forward and spect means look: something you look forward to."],["cartographer","kar-TOG-ruh-fer","noun","A person who makes maps.","The cartographer drew a map of all the hiking trails in our town.","","Carto means map, and graph means draw or write: a map drawer."],["niche","NICH","noun","A place or job that fits someone just right.","My friend found her niche on the team as the goalie.","From French niche, “a nook in a wall.”","Niche sounds like nitch: a little nook that fits you perfectly."],["significant","sig-NIF-ih-kuhnt","adjective","Important or large enough to notice.","Losing my front tooth was a significant event in first grade.","","Significant has “sign” in it: big enough to be a sign."],["renowned","rih-NOWND","adjective","Famous and admired.","The pizza place downtown is renowned for its giant slices.","","Renowned has “known” in it: known by everyone."],["recount","rih-KOWNT","verb","To tell the story of what happened.","Can you recount what happened at the sleepover?","","Re means again: recount means tell the story again."],["preliminary","prih-LIM-uh-nair-ee","adjective","Coming first, to get ready for the main thing.","Before the big race, we ran a preliminary lap to warm up.","From Latin limen, “threshold.”","Pre means before: preliminary things come before the main event."],["initiative","ih-NISH-uh-tiv","noun","The will to start something without being told.","Ella took the initiative and cleaned the art table without being asked.","From Latin initium, “a beginning.”","Initiative has “initial” in it: you make the first move."],["contradict","kon-truh-DIKT","verb","To say the opposite of what someone else said.","My sister contradicted me about whose turn it was.","From Latin contra, “against,” and dicere, “to say.”","Contra means against and dict means say: saying the opposite."],["affable","AF-uh-buhl","adjective","Friendly and easy to talk to.","The affable librarian always smiles and helps us find good books.","From Latin affabilis, “easy to speak to.”","Affable sounds like “laugh-able”: easy to laugh and talk with."],["toxic","TOK-sik","adjective","Poisonous and harmful.","Never eat berries you find outside, because some are toxic.","","Toxic sounds like “toxic yuck”: poison that makes you sick."],["reinforce","ree-in-FORS","verb","To make something stronger.","We used extra tape to reinforce our cardboard fort.","","Reinforce has “force” in it: adding more force to make it strong."],["enhance","en-HANS","verb","To make something better.","Sprinkles enhance a cupcake and make it even better.","","Enhance sounds like “enhanced powers”: a superhero upgrade that makes things better."],["alleviate","uh-LEE-vee-ayt","verb","To make something less painful or hard.","A cold cloth can alleviate the itch of a bug bite.","From Latin levis, “light.”","Alleviate sounds like “a lift-iate”: lift the pain away."],["mentor","MEN-tor","noun","A wise person who helps and guides you.","My older cousin is my mentor, and she taught me how to shoot hoops.","From Greek Mentor, a wise teacher in an old Greek story.","Mentor was the name of a wise helper in an old Greek story."],["barometer","buh-ROM-ih-ter","noun","A tool that measures air pressure to help predict weather.","The barometer showed that a storm was coming, so we stayed inside.","From Greek baros, “weight.”","Barometer has “meter” in it: a meter measures the air."],["vocal","VOH-kuhl","adjective","Speaking up loudly and often.","My little brother is very vocal about hating broccoli.","From Latin vox, “voice.”","Vocal sounds like voice: using your voice a lot."],["jovial","JOH-vee-uhl","adjective","Cheerful and friendly.","The jovial bus driver sang silly songs to us every morning.","","Jovial sounds like “jolly-al”: jolly and cheerful."],["stamina","STAM-uh-nuh","noun","The energy to keep going a long time.","Runners need stamina to finish the whole race.","","Stamina sounds like “stay-mina”: you can stay going a long time."],["jargon","JAR-guhn","noun","Special words used by one group that others may not understand.","The gamers used so much jargon that Grandma had no idea what they meant.","From Old French jargon, “chatter of birds.”","Jargon sounds like “jar of gone”: words that go right over your head."],["predicament","prih-DIK-uh-muhnt","noun","A tricky problem that is hard to get out of.","I was in a predicament when I got locked out with my ice cream melting.","","Predicament sounds like “pretty sticky moment”: you’re stuck in a pickle."],["tentative","TEN-tuh-tiv","adjective","Not sure or not final yet.","Our picnic plans are tentative because it might rain.","","Tentative sounds like a tent: you can pack it up and change plans."],["undermine","un-der-MYN","verb","To weaken something bit by bit.","Eating candy all day will undermine your healthy dinner.","","Undermine: digging a mine under a wall until it falls."],["hoard","HORD","verb","To collect and hide a large supply of something.","Squirrels hoard acorns, hiding piles of them for the winter.","From Old English hord, “treasure.”","Hoard sounds like “hold more”: keep grabbing and holding more."],["ultimate","UL-tuh-mit","adjective","The greatest or the last.","The ultimate prize at the fair was a giant stuffed bear.","From Latin ultimus, “last.”","Ultimate sounds like “ultra”: the most, the last, the top."],["confront","kuhn-FRUHNT","verb","To face something hard head-on.","I confronted my fear and jumped off the high dive.","","Confront has “front”: you face it head-on, front to front."],["obscure","uhb-SKYUR","adjective","Not well known or hard to see.","The band was so obscure that nobody at school had heard of it.","From Latin obscurus, “dark.”","Obscure sounds like “oh, so secure”: hidden where no one finds it."],["essential","ih-SEN-shuhl","adjective","Very much needed.","A water bottle is essential for a long, hot bike ride.","From Latin essentia, “being.”","Essential sounds like “a-must-ial”: something you must have."],["phenomenon","fih-NOM-uh-non","noun","Something that happens that people can see and wonder about.","A double rainbow after a storm is an amazing phenomenon.","From Greek phainomenon, “thing seen.”","Phenomenon sounds like phenomenal: something amazing you see happen."],["outlook","OWT-luk","noun","The way you see things or what you expect.","Even after losing, Jada kept a happy outlook on the season.","","Outlook: how you look out at the world."],["formidable","FOR-mih-duh-buhl","adjective","Very strong and hard to beat.","The other team was formidable; they had never lost a game.","From Latin formido, “fear.”","Formidable sounds like “for-mighty-able”: mighty and tough to beat."],["adequate","AD-ih-kwit","adjective","Good enough for what is needed.","Two sandwiches were adequate for our short hike. We didn’t need more.","From Latin adaequare, “to make equal.”","Adequate has “equ” like “equal”: equal to what you need."],["momentum","moh-MEN-tuhm","noun","The force that keeps something moving.","Once our sled got momentum, it zoomed all the way down the hill.","From Latin momentum, “movement.”","Momentum sounds like “more motion”: more and more speed."],["convenient","kuhn-VEEN-yuhnt","adjective","Easy and handy to use or reach.","The library is convenient because it is right next to our school.","","Convenient sounds like “convenience store”: quick and handy."],["obstinate","OB-stuh-nit","adjective","Stubborn; refusing to change your mind.","My obstinate cat refused to get off my homework.","From Latin obstinatus, “determined.”","Obstinate sounds like obstacle: someone who won’t move out of the way."],["indignant","in-DIG-nuhnt","adjective","Upset because something seems unfair.","Kai was indignant when he got blamed for a spill his brother made.","From Latin indignari, “to think unworthy.”","Indignant sounds like “in-dig-nant”: you dig in your heels because it’s unfair."],["verdict","VUR-dikt","noun","A final decision.","The judges gave their verdict: our volcano won the science fair.","From Latin vere dictum, “truly said.”","Verdict has “ver” like very true: the final, true decision."],["trait","TRAYT","noun","A quality that makes someone who they are.","Kindness is my best friend’s best trait.","","Trait rhymes with great: a great thing that makes you you."],["brazen","BRAY-zuhn","adjective","Bold in a rude way, without shame.","The brazen seagull grabbed a fry right off my plate!","From Old English bræsen, “made of brass.”","Brazen sounds like “brass-en”: bold as brass."],["novice","NOV-is","noun","A beginner.","I’m a novice at skateboarding, so I still fall a lot.","From Latin novicius, “new.”","Nov means new: a novice is new at something."],["symmetry","SIM-ih-tree","noun","When two halves match like a mirror.","A butterfly has symmetry because both wings match.","From Greek symmetria, “due proportion.”","Sym means together, and metry means measure: both sides measure the same."],["deliberate","dih-LIB-er-it","adjective","Done on purpose and with care.","She took slow, deliberate steps across the log bridge.","","Deliberate sounds like “delivered right”: done on purpose, with care."],["detest","dih-TEST","verb","To dislike very much.","I detest the sound of chalk squeaking on the board.","","Detest sounds like “the test”: something you really dislike."],["capacity","kuh-PAS-i-tee","noun","The most that something can hold.","The bus has a capacity of forty kids, so no more can fit.","From Latin capax, “able to hold.”","Capacity sounds like “cup-acity”: how much the cup can hold."],["waver","WAY-ver","verb","To be unsure between choices.","I waver between chocolate and strawberry every time we get ice cream.","","Waver sounds like a waving flag: flapping one way, then the other."],["extravagant","ik-STRAV-uh-guhnt","adjective","Spending or using much more than needed.","The cat’s extravagant birthday party had three cakes and a pony.","From Latin extra, “outside,” and vagari, “to wander.”","Extravagant starts with “extra”: way, way extra."],["outlandish","owt-LAN-dish","adjective","Very strange or odd.","My brother had an outlandish plan to build a slide from his bedroom window.","","Outlandish: so odd it seems to come from an outer land."],["refine","rih-FYN","verb","To make something better with small changes.","I refine my drawing by erasing and fixing little parts.","","Refine has “fine” in it: making it even finer."],["compel","kuhm-PEL","verb","To force or push someone to do something.","The cold rain compelled us to go inside.","From Latin compellere, “to drive together.”","Compel has “pel,” like propel: something pushes you to do it."],["cease","SEES","verb","To stop.","The rain finally ceased, and we ran outside to play.","From Latin cessare, “to stop.”","Cease sounds like “seize”: freeze, stop."],["fatigue","fuh-TEEG","noun","A feeling of being very tired.","After the long swim meet, fatigue set in and my arms felt heavy.","From Latin fatigare, “to tire out.”","Fatigue sounds like “fat leg”: legs so tired they feel heavy."],["usher","USH-er","verb","To lead someone to a place.","The usher showed us to our seats at the movie theater.","","Usher sounds like “Us here!”: they lead us here to our seats."],["haphazard","hap-HAZ-erd","adjective","Done without a plan, in a messy way.","My haphazard packing, just throwing stuff in, left my socks at home.","From hap, “luck,” and hazard, “chance.”","Hap means luck, as in “happen”: done by luck, with no plan."],["impartial","im-PAR-shuhl","adjective","Fair to everyone, not taking sides.","The referee must be impartial and not cheer for either team.","","Im-partial means not partial: not on anyone’s part or side."],["falter","FAWL-ter","verb","To stumble or lose strength.","Her voice did not falter when she sang the solo in front of everyone.","","Falter sounds like “fall-ter”: you almost fall or wobble."],["affluent","AF-loo-uhnt","adjective","Having a lot of money.","The affluent family had a huge house and three fancy cars.","From Latin affluere, “to flow toward.”","Affluent sounds like “a flow-ent”: money flowing in."],["subtle","SUT-uhl","adjective","Small and hard to notice.","There was a subtle hint of mint in the brownies.","","Subtle sounds like “sub-tle”: hidden under, hard to spot."],["prudent","PROO-duhnt","adjective","Careful and wise about the future.","It’s prudent to bring a jacket when the sky looks cloudy.","From Latin prudens, “wise.”","Prudent sounds like “prepared student”: careful and thinking ahead."],["audible","AW-duh-buhl","adjective","Loud enough to be heard.","My whisper was so quiet that it was barely audible.","From Latin audire, “to hear.”","Audible has “audi” like “audio”: sound you can hear."],["oversight","OH-ver-syt","noun","A mistake made by not noticing something.","Forgetting the forks for our picnic was a silly oversight.","","Oversight: your sight went right over it, so you missed it."],["banish","BAN-ish","verb","To send someone or something away for good.","Mom said she would banish the noisy toy to the closet forever.","","Banish sounds like “vanish”: send it away for good."],["jeopardy","JEP-er-dee","noun","Danger of losing something.","Our picnic was in jeopardy when dark storm clouds rolled in.","From Old French jeu parti, “a split game.”","Jeopardy sounds like “jump-pardy”: everything might jump into danger."],["dilemma","dih-LEM-uh","noun","A hard choice between two things.","My dilemma was choosing between the zoo and the water park.","","Di means two: a dilemma is two choices and you can only pick one."],["futile","FYOO-tuhl","adjective","Having no chance of working.","Trying to keep the cat off the couch was futile; she always jumped back up.","From Latin futilis, “leaky.”","Futile sounds like “few-tile”: you try and try, but it’s no use."],["curator","kyoo-RAY-ter","noun","A person in charge of a museum collection.","The curator at the museum showed us the dinosaur bones.","From Latin curare, “to take care of.”","Curator sounds like “cure-ator”: someone who takes care of treasures."],["concise","kuhn-SYSE","adjective","Short and clear, with no extra words.","Her concise answer fit on one line.","From Latin concisus, “cut up.”","Concise sounds like “cut size”: cut down short."],["procrastinate","proh-KRAS-tuh-nayt","verb","To put off doing something.","I procrastinated on my project until the night before it was due.","From Latin crastinus, “of tomorrow.”","Cras is Latin for tomorrow, so procrastinate means leave it for tomorrow."],["irate","eye-RAYT","adjective","Very angry.","The irate goose honked and chased us away from its babies.","From Latin ira, “anger.”","Irate has “ire,” an old word for anger: very angry."],["replenish","rih-PLEN-ish","verb","To fill something up again.","After the party, we had to replenish the snack bowls.","From Old French replenir, “to fill again.”","Re means again: replenish means fill it up again."],["ardent","AR-duhnt","adjective","Full of strong feeling; very eager.","He is an ardent fan of his soccer team and never misses a game.","From Latin ardere, “to burn.”","Ardent sounds like “ar-dent”: so eager you’d put a dent in the wall cheering."],["literal","LIT-er-uhl","adjective","Meaning exactly what the words say.","My little sister took “it’s raining cats and dogs” in a literal way and looked out for pets.","From Latin littera, “letter.”","Literal has “letter” inside: exactly what the letters say."],["savvy","SAV-ee","adjective","Smart and knowing a lot about something.","My savvy cousin knows every shortcut in the video game.","","Savvy sounds like “saw it”: you’ve seen it all, so you know it."],["voyage","VOY-ij","noun","A long trip, often by sea.","The pirate ship in our story went on a long voyage across the sea.","","Voyage sounds like “Bon voyage!” what people shout as a ship sails off."],["derive","dih-RYVE","verb","To get something from a source.","Many English words are derived from Latin.","From Latin derivare, “to draw off water.”","Derive sounds like “the river”: words flow from a source."],["rigorous","RIG-er-uhs","adjective","Very strict and careful.","Our coach has a rigorous practice with lots of laps.","","Rigorous sounds like “rigid”: strict and no slacking."],["impulse","IM-puls","noun","A sudden wish to do something.","On an impulse, I jumped into the leaf pile without even thinking.","From Latin impellere, “to push on.”","Impulse has “pulse” in it: a quick heart jump that says do it now."],["fabricate","FAB-rih-kayt","verb","To make something, or to make up a false story.","Don’t fabricate an excuse that the dog ate your homework.","From Latin fabrica, “workshop.”","Fabricate sounds like “fib-ricate”: making up a fib."],["abolish","uh-BOL-ish","verb","To end a rule or habit for good.","Our class voted to abolish homework on birthdays. Hooray!","From Latin abolere, “to destroy.”","Abolish sounds like “a polish”: wipe the rule away clean."],["remnant","REM-nuhnt","noun","A small piece left over.","A remnant of birthday cake was left in the fridge.","","Remnant sounds like “remain”: the bit that remains."],["dismantle","dis-MAN-tuhl","verb","To take apart piece by piece.","We dismantled the tent after our camping trip.","","Dismantle: take apart, piece by piece, like a LEGO castle."],["sluggish","SLUG-ish","adjective","Slow and low on energy.","I feel sluggish on Monday mornings after a late weekend.","","Sluggish has “slug” in it: slow like a slug."],["allure","uh-LOOR","noun","The power to attract or tempt.","The allure of the ice cream truck made everyone run outside.","","Allure has “lure” in it: a fishing lure pulls fish in."],["conifer","KON-uh-fer","noun","A tree that has cones and needles instead of flat leaves.","We decorated the conifer in our yard with pinecone ornaments.","From Latin conifer, “cone-bearing.”","Conifer starts with “cone”: a tree that grows pinecones."],["monologue","MON-uh-log","noun","A long speech by one person.","My uncle gave a long monologue about his fishing trip while we ate.","From Greek monologos, “speaking alone.”","Mono means one: a monologue is just one person talking."],["docile","DOS-uhl","adjective","Calm and easy to handle.","The docile pony let every kid pet it.","From Latin docilis, “easy to teach.”","Docile sounds like “dozy”: calm and gentle, never wild."],["persistent","per-SIS-tuhnt","adjective","Not giving up; keeping on.","The persistent puppy kept scratching at the door until we let him in.","From Latin persistere, “to continue steadily.”","Persistent sounds like “pest-tent”: it keeps on and on, never quitting."],["exasperate","ig-ZAS-puh-rayt","verb","To annoy someone a lot.","The jammed zipper started to exasperate me after ten tries.","From Latin exasperare, “to make rough.”","Exasperate sounds like “exhaust-perate”: so annoyed you sigh out loud."],["almanac","AWL-muh-nak","noun","A yearly book of facts, dates, and weather.","We checked the almanac to see when the full moon would be.","","Almanac sounds like “all-man-act”: all the facts in one book."],["allocate","AL-uh-kayt","verb","To give out a share for a certain use.","We allocate ten minutes of class time for silent reading.","From Latin locus, “place.”","Allocate sounds like “all-o-cate”: give a share to all."],["zenith","ZEE-nith","noun","The highest point.","At the zenith of the roller coaster, we could see the whole park.","","Zenith sounds like “zoom to the top”: the highest point."],["shrewd","SHROOD","adjective","Clever at making good choices.","My shrewd sister traded one sticker for three better ones.","","Shrewd sounds like shrew: a quick, clever little animal."],["uncanny","un-KAN-ee","adjective","Strange in a way that is hard to explain.","My cousin and I look so alike, it’s uncanny.","","Uncanny sounds like “can’t explain-y”: weird in a way you can’t explain."],["adept","uh-DEPT","adjective","Very good at doing something.","My sister is so adept at video games that she beats every level.","From Latin adeptus, “having reached.”","Adept sounds like “a deft”: quick, skilled hands."],["placid","PLAS-id","adjective","Calm and peaceful.","The lake was so placid it looked like a sheet of glass.","From Latin placidus, “gentle.”","Placid sounds like “place it”: set it down calm, nothing moves."],["turmoil","TUR-moyl","noun","A time of big upset and mess.","The house was in turmoil when the puppy got loose during dinner.","","Turmoil sounds like “stir and boil”: everything bubbling over."],["ordeal","or-DEEL","noun","A hard or painful experience.","Getting my tangled kite out of the tree was a real ordeal.","","Ordeal sounds like “hard deal”: a tough thing to go through."],["misconception","mis-kuhn-SEP-shuhn","noun","A wrong idea about something.","My little brother had the misconception that the moon follows our car.","","Mis means wrong, and conception means idea: a wrong idea."],["accommodate","uh-KOM-uh-dayt","verb","To have room for someone, or to help with what they need.","The big van can accommodate our whole soccer team, with room to spare.","","Accommodate sounds like “a comfy mate”: make room for a comfy friend."],["consistent","kuhn-SIS-tuhnt","adjective","Always acting or happening the same way.","Her consistent practice every day made her a great piano player.","","Consistent: the same, same, same, every single time."],["emphasize","EM-fuh-syze","verb","To show that something is very important.","The teacher said the word slowly and loudly to emphasize it.","From Greek emphasis, “a showing.”","Emphasize sounds like “empha-SIZE”: make it a bigger size."],["feign","FAYN","verb","To pretend.","My cat will feign sleep, then pounce on my toes.","From Latin fingere, “to shape, pretend.”","Feign sounds like “fake”: pretend."],["garble","GAR-buhl","verb","To mix up words so they are hard to understand.","The bad phone signal would garble every word Grandma said.","","Garble sounds like gargle: words gargled into a mess."],["repercussion","ree-per-KUSH-uhn","noun","An effect that comes later from an action.","Staying up late had a repercussion: I fell asleep in class.","","Repercussion has “percussion” in it: a drum beat that echoes later."],["levity","LEV-ih-tee","noun","Lightness and humor in a serious moment.","His silly joke brought some levity to the long, boring wait.","From Latin levis, “light.”","Levity sounds like levitate: it makes a heavy mood float up."],["heed","HEED","verb","To pay attention to advice or a warning.","Heed the sign that says “Wet Floor,” or you might slip.","From Old English hēdan, “to guard.”","Heed sounds like “hear it”: listen and do what it says."],["callous","KAL-uhs","adjective","Not caring about other people’s feelings.","Laughing when he fell was a callous thing to do.","","A callus is hard, tough skin; a callous heart is hard and uncaring."],["agrarian","uh-GRAIR-ee-uhn","adjective","Having to do with farms and farming.","Long ago, most towns had an agrarian life: everyone farmed.","From Latin ager, “field.”","Agrarian sounds like “a grain-ian”: all about growing grain."],["melancholy","MEL-uhn-kol-ee","adjective","Quietly sad in a thoughtful way.","The rainy day made me feel melancholy, so I hugged my dog.","From Greek melankholia, “black bile,” once thought to cause sadness.","Melancholy sounds like “melon collie”: a sad collie dog with no melon."],["delineate","dih-LIN-ee-ayt","verb","To describe or mark out clearly.","Chalk lines delineate the edges of the soccer field.","From Latin linea, “line.”","Delineate has “line”: you draw the lines."],["palatable","PAL-uh-tuh-buhl","adjective","Pleasant to taste, or okay enough to accept.","Adding cheese made the broccoli palatable to my little sister.","From Latin palatum, “roof of the mouth.”","Your palate is the roof of your mouth: palatable food pleases it."],["reconcile","REK-uhn-syl","verb","To become friends again after a fight.","After our fight, my best friend and I decided to reconcile.","From Latin reconciliare, “to bring together again.”","Reconcile: re means again, so friends again."],["fortify","FOR-tuh-fy","verb","To make something stronger.","We packed more snow on the walls to fortify our snow fort.","From Latin fortis, “strong.”","Fortify has “fort” in it: make it strong like a fort."],["wistful","WIST-fuhl","adjective","Softly sad and wishing for something.","I felt wistful looking at old photos from summer camp.","","Wistful sounds like “wish-ful”: full of wishes."],["exert","ig-ZURT","verb","To use strength or effort.","You have to exert a lot of muscle to open this tight pickle jar.","From Latin exserere, “to put forth.”","Exert sounds like “exercise”: put in effort and strength."],["resolute","REZ-uh-loot","adjective","Firm and set on a goal.","She was resolute about finishing the race even with a sore foot.","","Resolute sounds like “resolve”: firm and set."],["opulent","OP-yuh-luhnt","adjective","Rich and fancy.","The hotel had an opulent lobby with gold chairs and a giant fountain.","From Latin opulentus, “wealthy.”","Opulent sounds like “oodles of plenty”: rich, fancy and shiny."],["forfeit","FOR-fit","verb","To lose or give up something, often as a penalty.","The team had to forfeit the game when only four players showed up.","","Forfeit sounds like “four-fit”: only four fit, so you lose."],["stupendous","stoo-PEN-duhs","adjective","Amazing and huge.","The magic show was stupendous, with a rabbit and a disappearing car.","From Latin stupere, “to be amazed.”","Stupendous rhymes with tremendous: big and amazing."],["commodity","kuh-MOD-i-tee","noun","Something that is bought and sold.","On the old farm, wheat was the main commodity sold at market.","","Commodity sounds like “common item”: stuff people buy and sell."],["latent","LAY-tuhnt","adjective","Hidden but able to come out later.","She had a latent talent for drawing that showed up when she tried comics.","From Latin latere, “to lie hidden.”","Latent sounds like “later”: hidden now, showing up later."],["benevolent","buh-NEV-uh-luhnt","adjective","Kind and wanting to help others.","The benevolent neighbor fixed every kid’s bike for free.","From Latin bene, “well,” and volens, “wishing.”","Bene means “well” in Latin: wishing others well."],["sheepish","SHEE-pish","adjective","A bit embarrassed about a mistake.","I gave a sheepish smile when I got caught eating the last cookie.","","Sheepish has “sheep” in it: shy and a little embarrassed."],["rapport","ra-POR","noun","A friendly, easy connection with someone.","I have great rapport with my new teacher, so class is fun.","From French rapporter, “to bring back.”","Rapport sounds like “rap partner”: you two just click."],["apathy","AP-uh-thee","noun","Not caring or feeling interested.","The class showed apathy about the boring movie. Nobody cared.","From Greek apatheia, “without feeling.”","A means “without,” pathy means “feeling”: no feeling, no caring."],["prolific","pruh-LIF-ik","adjective","Making a lot of something.","My friend is a prolific artist who draws ten pictures a day.","","Prolific sounds like “pro lots”: a pro who makes lots and lots."],["accrue","uh-KROO","verb","To build up little by little over time.","Money will accrue in my piggy bank if I save a little each week.","From Latin accrescere, “to grow.”","Accrue sounds like “a crew”: a crew that keeps growing."],["nebulous","NEB-yuh-luhs","adjective","Fuzzy and unclear.","My weekend plans are still nebulous, so I don’t know what we’ll do.","From Latin nebula, “mist.”","Nebula means cloud in Latin: nebulous ideas are cloudy and fuzzy."],["contemporary","kuhn-TEM-puh-rer-ee","adjective","Modern, or living at the same time.","The museum has a room full of contemporary art made this year.","From Latin com-, “with,” and tempus, “time.”","Con means with, tempor means time: from the same time, or now."],["flamboyant","flam-BOY-uhnt","adjective","Bold, bright and showy.","The parrot was flamboyant, with bright red, green and blue feathers.","From French flamboyer, “to flame.”","Flamboyant has “flam” like flame: bright and showy as fire."],["eradicate","ih-RAD-ih-kayt","verb","To get rid of something completely.","We pulled every last weed to eradicate them from the garden.","From Latin radix, “root.”","Eradicate has “radic,” Latin for root: pull it out by the root."],["admonish","ad-MON-ish","verb","To warn or scold someone gently.","The librarian had to admonish us for giggling too loudly.","From Latin admonere, “to remind.”","Admonish sounds like “a mom-ish” warning: a gentle scold."],["trite","TRYT","adjective","Used so often it is no longer fresh.","“Have a nice day” felt trite after hearing it fifty times.","","Trite rhymes with bite: a gum that’s been chewed too long."],["deteriorate","dih-TEER-ee-uh-rayt","verb","To get worse over time.","The old treehouse began to deteriorate in the rain.","From Latin deterior, “worse.”","Deteriorate sounds like “deter-rot”: slowly rotting and getting worse."],["stoic","STOH-ik","adjective","Calm and not showing feelings, even when things are hard.","He was stoic and didn’t cry when he got his shot.","","Stoic sounds like “stone-ic”: calm like a stone."],["genre","ZHAHN-ruh","noun","A kind or type of book, movie or music.","Mystery is my favorite book genre, then comedy.","From French genre, “kind.”","Genre is a French word for kind or type, said ZHON-ruh."],["laud","LAWD","verb","To praise highly.","The teachers laud the kids who cleaned up the park, saying “Great job!”","From Latin laus, “praise.”","Laud sounds like loud: praise someone out loud."],["altruism","AL-troo-iz-uhm","noun","Caring about others and helping them without wanting a reward.","Shoveling the neighbor’s driveway for free was an act of altruism.","From French altruisme, from Latin alter, “other.”","Altruism sounds like “all true”: true helping with no reward."],["subdue","suhb-DOO","verb","To bring under control or make quieter.","Mom used a lullaby to subdue the crying baby.","","Sub means under: subdue means bring things under control."],["adulation","aj-uh-LAY-shuhn","noun","Very great praise or admiration.","The young singer loved the adulation of her cheering fans.","","Adulation sounds like “adoration”: lots of loving praise."],["acclimate","AK-luh-mayt","verb","To get used to a new place or new weather.","It took a few days to acclimate to the cold mountain air.","","Acclimate has “climate” in it: get used to the climate."],["paltry","PAWL-tree","adjective","Very small and not worth much.","My little brother offered a paltry two cents for my bike.","","Paltry sounds like poultry: one tiny chicken wing, not much at all."],["pompous","POM-puhs","adjective","Acting too important and proud.","The pompous kid bragged that he was too good for kickball.","From Latin pompa, “parade.”","Pompous sounds like pom-pom: waving fluffy pom-poms for yourself."],["languid","LANG-gwid","adjective","Slow and relaxed, with little energy.","The cat gave a slow, languid stretch in the warm sun.","From Latin languere, “to be faint.”","Languid sounds like “lounge-id”: lounging around slowly."],["emulate","EM-yuh-layt","verb","To copy someone because you admire them.","Little kids emulate the big kids’ soccer moves because they think they’re cool.","From Latin aemulari, “to try to equal.”","Emulate sounds like “emu-late”: an emu copying a cool bird it admires."],["disseminate","dih-SEM-uh-nayt","verb","To spread information widely.","We disseminated flyers about the bake sale all over town.","From Latin semen, “seed.”","Disseminate has “semin,” Latin for seed: scatter it like seeds."],["precarious","prih-KAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Shaky and likely to fall or fail.","The stack of plates looked precarious, so I moved it away from the edge.","From Latin precarius, “obtained by asking.”","Precarious sounds like “pre-scary-ous”: so wobbly it’s scary."],["rejuvenate","rih-JOO-vuh-nayt","verb","To make someone feel young and fresh again.","A nap on the couch will rejuvenate me after school.","From Latin juvenis, “young.”","Juven means young, like juvenile: making you feel young again."],["omnipresent","om-nih-PREZ-uhnt","adjective","Seeming to be everywhere at once.","In our house, my little sister’s toys are omnipresent.","From Latin omnis, “all,” and praesens, “present.”","Omni means all, and present means here: here, there, everywhere."],["debilitate","dih-BIL-i-tayt","verb","To make weak.","The flu debilitated me for a whole week.","From Latin debilis, “weak.”","Debilitate sounds like “de-ability”: your strength gets taken away."],["irony","EYE-ruh-nee","noun","When what happens is the opposite of what you expect.","The irony was that the fire station’s birthday cake caught fire.","From Greek eirōneia, “pretend ignorance.”","Irony: a fire station cake catching fire, the opposite of what you expect."],["solace","SOL-is","noun","Comfort when you are sad.","My dog gave me solace when I was sad about my lost game.","From Latin solacium, “comfort.”","Solace sounds like “so less sad”: something that comforts you."],["bewitch","bih-WICH","verb","To charm or delight someone completely.","The snowy morning seemed to bewitch everyone. We all stared in wonder.","","Bewitch has “witch” in it: like a witch cast a happy spell."],["append","uh-PEND","verb","To add something to the end.","Please append your name to the end of the list.","From Latin appendere, “to hang on.”","Append sounds like “a pend-ant”: hang it on the end."],["nuance","NOO-ahns","noun","A tiny difference that is easy to miss.","Mom can hear the nuance between my real laugh and my fake laugh.","From French nuance, “shade of color.”","Nuance sounds like “new ants”: tiny differences, small as ants."],["haughty","HAW-tee","adjective","Acting like you are better than others.","The haughty cat stuck its nose in the air and ignored everyone.","From Old French haut, “high.”","Haughty sounds like “high-ty”: nose way up high."],["tenacious","tuh-NAY-shuhs","adjective","Holding on tight and not giving up.","The tenacious puppy would not let go of the rope toy.","From Latin tenax, “holding fast.”","Tenacious sounds like “ten nails”: holding on with all ten fingers."],["succinct","suhk-SINGKT","adjective","Short and clear.","The coach gave a succinct speech: “Play hard and have fun.”","","Succinct sounds like “suck it in”: squeeze it short."],["bemoan","bih-MOHN","verb","To complain or feel sad about something.","There’s no use to bemoan the rain; let’s play a board game.","","Bemoan has “moan” in it: moaning and complaining."],["contrive","kuhn-TRYVE","verb","To cleverly plan or invent something.","We contrived a way to reach the kite stuck in the tree.","","Contrive sounds like “contraption”: inventing a clever gadget or plan."],["mediate","MEE-dee-ayt","verb","To help two sides settle a disagreement.","When my friends argued over the ball, I mediated and helped them take turns.","From Latin mediare, “to be in the middle.”","Medi means middle: you stand in the middle to help both sides."],["affirm","uh-FURM","verb","To say firmly that something is true.","The coach will affirm that practice starts at four. It’s true!","From Latin affirmare, “to make firm.”","Affirm has “firm” in it: say it firmly."],["propensity","pruh-PEN-sih-tee","noun","A natural habit or leaning toward something.","My cat has a propensity to nap in the laundry basket.","From Latin propensus, “inclined.”","Propensity sounds like “prone to”: you just tend to do it."],["convoluted","KON-vuh-loo-tid","adjective","Very twisty and hard to follow.","The game had convoluted rules nobody understood.","","Convoluted sounds like curly noodles: twisty and tangled."],["contour","KON-toor","noun","The outline or shape of something.","I traced the contour of my hand on paper to make a turkey.","","Contour sounds like “con-tour”: a tour around the outside edge."],["superfluous","soo-PUR-floo-uhs","adjective","More than is needed.","Three bottles of ketchup at one picnic is superfluous.","From Latin superfluus, “overflowing.”","Super means over: superfluous means overflowing, too much."],["circumvent","sur-kuhm-VENT","verb","To find a way around a problem.","We circumvented the long lunch line by bringing our own food.","From Latin circumvenire, “to come around.”","Circum means around: you go around the problem."],["sullen","SUL-uhn","adjective","Quiet and grumpy.","My brother was sullen after he lost his phone for a week.","","Sullen sounds like sulking: grumpy and silent."],["meticulous","muh-TIK-yuh-luhs","adjective","Very careful about every small detail.","My sister is meticulous and colors inside every single line.","","Meticulous sounds like “meet tick-ulous”: checking every tiny tick mark."],["nostalgia","no-STAL-juh","noun","A warm, happy-sad feeling when you miss the past.","Looking at my baby photos filled me with nostalgia.","From Greek nostos, “return home,” and algos, “pain.”","Nostos means going home in Greek: missing home or old times."],["coherent","koh-HEER-uhnt","adjective","Clear and making sense.","When I was sleepy, my answers were not very coherent.","From Latin cohaerere, “to stick together.”","Coherent sounds like “co-here”: all the ideas stick together."],["transient","TRAN-zee-uhnt","adjective","Lasting only a short time.","The rainbow was transient and gone in five minutes.","From Latin transire, “to go across.”","Transient sounds like transit: just passing through."],["conjecture","kuhn-JEK-cher","noun","A guess made without full proof.","My conjecture is that the missing sock is behind the dryer.","","Conjecture sounds like “guess-ture”: just a guess."],["adage","AD-ij","noun","An old saying that many people believe is true.","My grandpa’s favorite adage is “Practice makes perfect.”","","Adage sounds like “add age”: a saying that got old with age."],["ubiquitous","yoo-BIK-wih-tuhs","adjective","Found everywhere.","Glitter from the craft project was ubiquitous: it was on the couch, the dog and my socks.","From Latin ubique, “everywhere.”","Ubiquitous sounds like “you be quick to see it”: it’s everywhere."],["permeate","PUR-mee-ayt","verb","To spread through every part of something.","The smell of popcorn permeated the whole house.","From Latin permeare, “to pass through.”","Permeate sounds like “per-meet”: it meets every part of the room."],["prowess","PROW-is","noun","Great skill.","Her prowess at jump rope amazed the whole playground.","From Old French proesse, “bravery.”","Prowess starts like pro: skill like a pro."],["plethora","PLETH-er-uh","noun","Far more than enough.","Our pantry has a plethora of cereal boxes.","From Greek plethore, “fullness.”","Plethora sounds like “plenty more”: way more than you need."],["rectify","REK-tuh-fy","verb","To fix something that is wrong.","I had to rectify my spelling mistake before turning in my paper.","From Latin rectus, “right, straight.”","Rect means right, like rectangle’s right angles: making it right."],["deluge","DEL-yooj","noun","A huge flood or rush of something.","After we posted the flyer, we got a deluge of cookie orders.","","Deluge sounds like “the huge”: a huge flood pouring in."],["mortified","MOR-tuh-fyd","adjective","Very embarrassed.","I was mortified when I waved back at someone who was waving at the kid behind me.","","Mortified sounds like “more tomato”: your face turns tomato red."],["aplomb","uh-PLOM","noun","Calm confidence, even when things are hard.","She answered the tricky question with aplomb, calm and cool.","From French à plomb, “straight down, like a plumb line.”","Aplomb comes from French for “straight up and down”: standing tall and calm."],["elicit","ih-LIS-it","verb","To draw out a reaction or answer.","The clown’s silly hat would elicit giggles from every kid.","From Latin elicere, “to draw out.”","Elicit sounds like “e-licit”: it lets the laughter out."],["confound","kuhn-FOWND","verb","To confuse or puzzle.","The magic trick confounded everyone at the party.","","Confound sounds like “confused, found nothing”: totally puzzled."],["denounce","dih-NOWNS","verb","To say out loud that something is wrong.","Our class denounced littering with a big poster.","","Denounce has “announce”: you announce loudly that it’s wrong."],["calibrate","KAL-uh-brayt","verb","To adjust a tool so it measures correctly.","We calibrated the scale to zero before weighing our pumpkins.","","Calibrate: getting it just right, like tuning a guitar."],["substantial","suhb-STAN-shuhl","adjective","Large in size or amount.","We ate a substantial breakfast before the long hike.","","Substantial sounds like “substance”: lots of it, big and filling."],["akin","uh-KIN","adjective","Similar or like something.","Learning to skate is akin to learning to ride a bike.","","Akin has “kin” in it: kin means family, alike."],["voracious","vaw-RAY-shuhs","adjective","Wanting a lot of something, especially food or books.","My sister is a voracious reader and finishes a book every day.","From Latin vorare, “to swallow up.”","Voracious sounds like “roar-acious”: a lion roaring for more food."],["animosity","an-uh-MOS-ih-tee","noun","Strong dislike between people.","There was no animosity between the teams. They shook hands after the game.","","Animosity sounds like “enemy-osity”: feeling like enemies."],["peruse","puh-ROOZ","verb","To read or look through something carefully.","I perused the menu for a long time before ordering pancakes.","","Peruse sounds like “pair of shoes”: you look over every pair carefully."],["maverick","MAV-er-ik","noun","Someone who thinks and acts in their own way.","Our coach is a maverick who tries plays no other team uses.","From Samuel Maverick, a rancher who did not brand his cattle.","Maverick sounds like “my own trick”: doing things your own way."],["wry","RY","adjective","Dry and a little funny in a quiet way.","Dad gave a wry smile when I said I’d cleaned my room.","","Wry rhymes with dry: a dry, funny little smile."],["aesthetic","es-THET-ik","adjective","About beauty and how things look.","She arranged her bookshelf by color because it looked nice. It was an aesthetic choice.","From Greek aisthētikos, “perceptive.”","Aesthetic sounds like “ess-thetic”: makes it look nice."],["profuse","pruh-FYOOS","adjective","Pouring out in large amounts.","He gave profuse apologies after knocking over my juice.","From Latin profusus, “poured forth.”","Profuse comes from a Latin word for pour: pouring out lots."],["obstruct","uhb-STRUKT","verb","To block the way.","A big pile of snow obstructed our front door.","From Latin obstruere, “to build against.”","Obstruct sounds like obstacle: something that blocks the way."],["endorse","en-DORS","verb","To publicly support or approve something.","The student council will endorse the idea of a garden club and help it start.","From Latin dorsum, “back,” as in signing the back of a paper.","Endorse sounds like “and yes”: putting your yes behind it."],["allay","uh-LAY","verb","To calm or ease a fear or worry.","The vet helped allay my worry about my sick hamster.","","Allay sounds like “a lay”: lay your worry down."],["deficient","dih-FISH-uhnt","adjective","Not having enough of something needed.","My plant was deficient in sunlight, so it turned pale.","","Deficient sounds like “deficit”: there’s not enough."],["abhor","ab-HOR","verb","To hate something very much.","I abhor the sound of nails on a chalkboard. Yuck!","From Latin abhorrere, “to shrink back from.”","Abhor has “horror” in it: you feel horror at it."],["boorish","BOOR-ish","adjective","Rude and having bad manners.","Talking loudly during the movie was boorish.","","Boorish sounds like “boar-ish”: rude like a grunting boar."],["chagrin","shuh-GRIN","noun","Embarrassment or annoyance.","To my chagrin, I wore two different shoes to school.","From French chagrin, “sorrow.”","Chagrin sounds like “sheepish grin”: an embarrassed smile."],["accentuate","ak-SEN-choo-ayt","verb","To make something stand out more.","The bright frame will accentuate the colors in my painting.","","Accentuate has “accent” in it: make it stand out."],["beguile","bih-GILE","verb","To charm or trick someone.","The cat’s cute purring can beguile you into giving it extra treats.","","Beguile has “guile” in it, which means sneaky tricks: it charms you."],["facilitate","fuh-SIL-ih-tayt","verb","To make something easier to do.","A map will facilitate our treasure hunt and make it easier.","From Latin facilis, “easy.”","Facilitate has “facil,” from the Latin for easy: make it easy."],["pragmatic","prag-MAT-ik","adjective","Practical and focused on what works.","Since it was raining, the pragmatic choice was to play board games inside.","From Greek pragma, “deed.”","Pragmatic sounds like “practical magic”: smart choices that really work."],["prosaic","proh-ZAY-ik","adjective","Plain and ordinary, not exciting.","My lunch was prosaic, just a plain cheese sandwich.","From Latin prosa, “prose.”","Prosaic sounds like “plain mosaic”: no colors, just plain gray tiles."],["intact","in-TAKT","adjective","Whole and not damaged.","Our egg stayed intact after we dropped it in its padded box.","From Latin intactus, “untouched.”","Intact has “tact,” as in touch: untouched and in one piece."],["brusque","BRUHSK","adjective","Short and rude in how you speak.","The busy clerk gave a brusque answer and turned away.","","Brusque sounds like “brisk”: short, fast, and a little rude."],["benchmark","BENCH-mark","noun","Something you use to compare and measure other things against.","My best race time last year is the benchmark I want to beat.","","Benchmark: a mark on the bench to measure against."],["impeccable","im-PEK-uh-buhl","adjective","Perfect, with no mistakes.","Her room was impeccable before the guests came, not one sock on the floor.","","Impeccable sounds like “un-peck-able”: not even one bird peck of a mistake."],["pervasive","per-VAY-siv","adjective","Spread everywhere.","The smell of wet dog was pervasive in the car.","From Latin pervadere, “to go through.”","Pervasive sounds like invasive: it invades every corner."],["inherent","in-HAIR-uhnt","adjective","Being a natural, built-in part of something.","There is an inherent risk of getting wet when you go canoeing.","From Latin inhaerere, “to stick in.”","Inherent sounds like “in-here”: it’s already in here, built in."],["antithesis","an-TITH-uh-sis","noun","The exact opposite of something.","My messy room is the antithesis of my sister’s super neat room.","From Greek antithesis, “a setting against.”","Anti means “against”: the exact opposite."],["incessant","in-SES-uhnt","adjective","Never stopping.","The puppy’s incessant barking went on all night without stopping.","From Latin incessans, “not stopping.”","Incessant sounds like “in-cease-ant”: it never ceases or stops."],["lull","LUL","noun","A short time of quiet or calm.","During a lull in the storm, when it got quiet, we ran to the car.","","Lull sounds like lullaby: soft and quiet for a little while."],["cursory","KUR-suh-ree","adjective","Quick and not careful.","A cursory look at my homework missed three mistakes.","","Cursory sounds like “cursor”: zipping fast across the screen without stopping."],["supplement","SUP-luh-muhnt","noun","Something added to make a thing complete.","I eat fruit to supplement my lunch.","","Supplement sounds like “supply more”: adding to make it full."],["impede","im-PEED","verb","To block or slow progress.","Fallen branches can impede the path and slow down our bikes.","From Latin impedire, “to shackle the feet.”","Impede has “ped,” Latin for foot: something in the way of your feet."],["torrent","TOR-uhnt","noun","A fast, strong rush of water or something else.","A torrent of water came down the gutter during the storm.","","Torrent sounds like “torn rain”: rain pouring like the sky tore open."],["abstract","AB-strakt","adjective","Based on ideas, not on real things you can touch.","Fairness is an abstract idea: you can’t touch it, but you can feel it.","From Latin abstractus, “drawn away.”","Abstract art is about ideas, not real things you can touch."],["disparity","dih-SPAIR-i-tee","noun","A big difference.","There was a big disparity between the two teams’ heights.","","Disparity: dis means not, par means equal: not equal."],["intermittent","in-ter-MIT-uhnt","adjective","Stopping and starting again.","The rain was intermittent: it rained, stopped, then rained again all day.","From Latin intermittere, “to leave off for a while.”","Inter means between: on, then a gap in between, then on again."],["euphoria","yoo-FOR-ee-uh","noun","A feeling of great happiness.","We felt pure euphoria when the last-second shot went in.","From Greek euphoros, “bearing well.”","Euphoria sounds like “you-for-ya”: a big cheer of joy just for you."],["digress","dy-GRES","verb","To wander off the main topic.","Grandpa often digresses into stories about his old dog.","From Latin digredi, “to step away.”","Digress: di means away, gress means step: stepping away from the topic."],["refute","rih-FYOOT","verb","To prove that something is wrong.","I could refute my brother’s claim because I had the cookie wrapper as proof.","From Latin refutare, “to drive back.”","Refute sounds like “re-shoot”: shooting the idea down."],["incognito","in-kog-NEE-toh","adverb","In disguise so no one knows who you are.","Dad went to the party incognito, dressed as a giant banana.","From Italian incognito, “unknown.”","Incognito sounds like “in-cog-NO-know”: no one knows who you are."],["deplore","dih-PLOR","verb","To strongly dislike or disapprove of.","I deplore soggy cereal.","","Deplore sounds like “the pour”: you hate the milk pour that makes it soggy."],["banal","buh-NAL","adjective","Boring and ordinary, with nothing new.","The movie had a banal ending that we all saw coming.","","Banal sounds like “ba-nap”: so boring you take a nap."],["resonate","REZ-uh-nayt","verb","To ring out with sound, or to feel meaningful.","The bell will resonate through the whole gym.","From Latin resonare, “to sound again.”","Resonate sounds like “re-sound”: the sound rings again and again."],["corroborate","kuh-ROB-uh-rayt","verb","To back up a story with proof.","My friend corroborated my story about the giant fish.","","Corroborate: a friend backs you up, saying “I saw it too.”"],["complacent","kuhm-PLAY-suhnt","adjective","Too pleased with yourself to keep trying hard.","After winning twice, the team got complacent and lost.","","Complacent sounds like “complain-less”: too comfy to try hard."],["trepidation","trep-ih-DAY-shuhn","noun","A feeling of fear about what might happen.","I opened my report card with trepidation.","From Latin trepidare, “to tremble, hurry.”","Trepidation sounds like “trembling”: shaky and scared."],["engross","en-GROHS","verb","To hold all of someone’s attention.","A good video game can engross me so much I don’t hear Mom calling.","","Engross sounds like “in gross”: so stuck in it, you don’t notice anything."],["malleable","MAL-ee-uh-buhl","adjective","Easy to shape or change.","Warm play dough is malleable, so you can shape it into anything.","From Latin malleus, “hammer.”","Malleable sounds like mallet: tap it with a mallet and it changes shape."],["discern","dih-SURN","verb","To notice or tell the difference.","Can you discern the two different bird calls?","","Discern sounds like “this or that?”: telling them apart."],["comply","kuhm-PLY","verb","To follow a rule or request.","Every swimmer must comply with the lifeguard’s rules.","","Comply sounds like “come, I’ll obey”: you follow the rule."],["stringent","STRIN-juhnt","adjective","Very strict.","Our library has stringent rules about returning books on time.","From Latin stringere, “to draw tight.”","Stringent sounds like “string tight”: rules pulled very tight."],["negate","nih-GAYT","verb","To cancel the effect of something.","Eating a whole bag of candy would negate my healthy lunch.","From Latin negare, “to deny.”","Negate sounds like negative: it cancels the good part out."],["vacillate","VAS-uh-layt","verb","To keep changing your mind.","I vacillate between pizza and tacos every Friday night.","From Latin vacillare, “to sway.”","Vacillate sounds like oscillate, like a fan: turning back and forth."],["intrepid","in-TREP-id","adjective","Very brave and bold.","The intrepid kids crossed the wobbly rope bridge without looking back.","From Latin intrepidus, “not alarmed.”","In-trepid means not trembling: no shaking, just brave."],["vicinity","vih-SIN-ih-tee","noun","The area nearby.","Is there a pizza place in the vicinity of the park?","From Latin vicinus, “neighbor.”","Vicinity sounds like “vicinity, nearby city”: the area close by."],["adroit","uh-DROYT","adjective","Clever and skillful, especially with your hands.","The adroit juggler kept five balls in the air without dropping one.","From French à droit, “rightly.”","Adroit has “droit,” French for “right”: right-handed, skilled."],["reiterate","ree-IT-uh-rayt","verb","To say something again to make it clear.","The teacher had to reiterate the rules three times.","From Latin iterum, “again.”","Reiterate sounds like “re-repeat”: saying it again."],["arable","AIR-uh-buhl","adjective","Good for growing crops.","The land by the river is arable, so farmers grow corn there.","From Latin arare, “to plow.”","Arable sounds like “a-row-able”: you can plant rows of crops."],["entice","en-TYSE","verb","To tempt someone with something good.","The smell of popcorn will entice everyone into the kitchen.","","Entice sounds like “in-nice”: something nice that pulls you in."],["nonchalant","non-shuh-LAHNT","adjective","Calm and relaxed, as if you don't care.","He stayed nonchalant even when the ball flew right at his face.","From French nonchalant, “not caring.”","Nonchalant sounds like “no challenge”: acting like nothing bothers you."],["capitulate","kuh-PICH-uh-layt","verb","To give in after holding out.","After an hour of begging, Dad capitulated and made pancakes.","","Capitulate sounds like “cap it, I’m late”: I give up."],["caustic","KAW-stik","adjective","Harsh and stinging, in words or in chemicals.","Her caustic remark about my drawing really stung.","From Greek kaustikos, “burning.”","Caustic sounds like “cost-ick”: words that sting and burn."],["fickle","FIK-uhl","adjective","Changing your mind often.","My fickle friend wanted pizza, then tacos, then pizza again.","From Old English ficol, “tricky.”","Fickle sounds like pickle: can’t pick, keeps switching."],["mundane","mun-DAYN","adjective","Ordinary and a bit dull.","Folding laundry is a mundane chore.","From Latin mundanus, “of the world.”","Mundane sounds like Monday: plain, ordinary and a bit boring."],["connoisseur","kon-uh-SUR","noun","An expert who knows a lot about something fine.","My uncle is a connoisseur of hot sauces; he can name them all.","From old French conoistre, “to know.”","Connoisseur sounds like “know-it-sir”: an expert who knows the best."],["condone","kuhn-DOHN","verb","To allow or accept something wrong.","Our teacher will not condone cheating.","","Condone sounds like “okay, done”: saying a wrong thing is okay."],["threshold","THRESH-hohld","noun","A doorway, or the point where something begins.","The cat stopped at the threshold and refused to come inside.","","Threshold has “hold” in it: the doorway spot that holds you before you enter."],["frivolous","FRIV-uh-luhs","adjective","Silly and not important.","Buying a tiny hat for the goldfish was a frivolous idea.","From Latin frivolus, “silly, worthless.”","Frivolous sounds like “frilly-less”: frilly and not important."],["analogy","uh-NAL-uh-jee","noun","A comparison that explains one thing by showing how it is like another.","Our teacher used an analogy: the heart is like a pump.","From Greek analogia, “proportion.”","Analogy has “logy” like “logic”: compare one thing to another."],["audacious","aw-DAY-shuhs","adjective","Very bold and daring.","She made an audacious plan to read 100 books in one summer!","From Latin audax, “bold.”","Audacious sounds like “odd-acious”: so bold it seems odd."],["ingenious","in-JEEN-yuhs","adjective","Very clever and inventive.","Leo had an ingenious plan to reach the cookie jar using a broom.","From Latin ingenium, “natural talent.”","Ingenious sounds like genius: a genius idea."],["incorporate","in-KOR-puh-rayt","verb","To include something as part of a whole.","We will incorporate photos into our class report to make it better.","From Latin corpus, “body.”","Incorporate has “corp,” Latin for body: put it into the body of the work."],["wily","WY-lee","adjective","Clever and tricky.","The wily cat figured out how to open the treat cupboard.","","Wily rhymes with smiley: a sly fox smiling at its clever trick."],["decree","dih-KREE","noun","An official order.","By royal decree, the queen in our class play gave everyone a holiday.","","Decree sounds like a king yelling an order: pizza for all."],["affinity","uh-FIN-ih-tee","noun","A natural liking for something.","My cousin has an affinity for drawing animals. She loves it.","From Latin affinis, “bordering on.”","Affinity sounds like “a-fin-ity”: a fish has an affinity for water."],["pretentious","prih-TEN-shuhs","adjective","Trying to seem more important or smart than you are.","The pretentious kid said he only eats “fancy” chicken nuggets.","","Pretentious sounds like pretend: pretending to be fancier than you are."],["infuse","in-FYOOZ","verb","To fill something with a quality or flavor.","Mom likes to infuse her water with lemon slices for flavor.","From Latin infundere, “to pour in.”","Infuse sounds like “in-fuse”: put flavor right into it."],["burgeon","BUR-juhn","verb","To grow or spread quickly.","Our class garden began to burgeon in spring, with sprouts everywhere.","From Old French borjon, “bud.”","Burgeon sounds like “burst-geon”: bursting with growth."],["deride","dih-RYDE","verb","To make fun of in a mean way.","Some kids derided his idea, but it won the science fair.","From Latin deridere, “to laugh at.”","Deride sounds like “the ride”: taking someone for a mean ride of jokes."],["abate","uh-BAYT","verb","To become less strong.","We waited for the wind to abate so we could fly our kite.","","Abate sounds like “a bit”: the storm calms down a bit."],["remorse","rih-MORS","noun","A deep feeling of being sorry for doing wrong.","I felt remorse after I broke my friend’s toy.","From Latin remordere, “to bite again.”","Remorse sounds like “more sorry”: feeling more and more sorry."],["minuscule","MIN-uh-skyool","adjective","Extremely tiny.","There was one minuscule crumb left on my plate.","From Latin minusculus, “rather small.”","Minuscule starts with “minus”: so small it’s almost nothing."],["haggle","HAG-uhl","verb","To argue about the price of something.","At the yard sale, Dad tried to haggle the price of the bike down to five dollars.","","Haggle sounds like wiggle: you wiggle the price up and down."],["apex","AY-peks","noun","The highest point of something.","We stood at the apex of the hill and saw the whole town.","From Latin apex, “peak.”","Apex sounds like “a peak”: the very top."],["overbearing","oh-ver-BAIR-ing","adjective","Bossy in a way that is hard to be around.","The overbearing kid told everyone exactly how to build their sandcastles.","","Overbearing: like a big bear standing over you, giving orders."],["salient","SAY-lee-uhnt","adjective","Most important or easy to notice.","The salient part of the recipe is: don’t forget the chocolate chips.","From Latin salire, “to leap.”","Salient sounds like “sail-ient”: it sticks up like a sail."],["reminisce","rem-uh-NIS","verb","To talk or think about happy past times.","Grandma and I love to reminisce about our trip to the beach.","","Reminisce has “remin” like remind: reminding each other of good times."],["erratic","ih-RAT-ik","adjective","Not steady or regular, hard to predict.","The fly’s erratic zigzag path made it hard to swat.","From Latin errare, “to wander.”","Erratic sounds like “error-tic”: all over the place, full of errors."],["palpable","PAL-puh-buhl","adjective","So strong you can almost touch it.","The excitement was palpable on the last day of school.","From Latin palpare, “to touch gently.”","Palpable sounds like “pal-able”: so real you can high-five it like a pal."],["culminate","KUHL-muh-nayt","verb","To reach the highest point or final moment.","The week of practice culminated in our big concert.","From Latin culmen, “top.”","Culminate: you climb to the very top, the big finish."],["verve","VURV","noun","Energy and style.","She danced with verve at the school talent show.","","Verve sounds like nerve and swerve: bold moves with style."],["gist","JIST","noun","The main point of something.","I missed part of the movie, but I got the gist: the dog saves the day.","From Old French gist, “it lies,” meaning the main point.","Gist sounds like “just”: just the main idea."],["vehement","VEE-uh-muhnt","adjective","Showing very strong feelings.","My sister was vehement that she did not take my hoodie.","","Vehement sounds like “very mean it”: you mean it very strongly."],["oblique","oh-BLEEK","adjective","Slanted, or said in a sideways, hidden way.","My sister gave an oblique hint about her birthday by leaving toy pictures on my bed.","From Latin obliquus, “slanting.”","Oblique rhymes with sneak: it slants sideways instead of going straight."],["commend","kuh-MEND","verb","To praise in public.","The principal commended our class for its kindness.","","Commend sounds like “comment”: a nice comment out loud."],["embody","em-BOD-ee","verb","To be a perfect example of an idea or quality.","Our team captain seems to embody kindness; she is kind to everyone.","","Embody has “body” in it: an idea in a real body."],["ascertain","as-er-TAYN","verb","To find out for sure.","We checked the map to ascertain which trail led to the lake.","","Ascertain has “certain” in it: find out for certain."],["lethargic","luh-THAR-jik","adjective","Very sleepy and slow.","I felt lethargic after the big holiday lunch and just wanted the couch.","From Greek lēthargos, “forgetful.”","Lethargic sounds like “let-her-nap”: too sleepy to move."],["converge","kuhn-VURJ","verb","To come together at one point.","The two trails converge at the waterfall.","","Converge: lines come together like slices meeting in a pizza’s middle."],["explicit","ik-SPLIS-it","adjective","Stated very clearly, with nothing left out.","The teacher gave explicit steps: “Write your name, then answer number one.”","From Latin explicitus, “unfolded.”","Explicit sounds like “explain it”: spelled out clearly."],["cajole","kuh-JOHL","verb","To gently talk someone into something with sweet words.","I cajoled my brother into playing my favorite game by sharing my candy.","","Cajole sounds like “cookie-jar”: sweet-talking with treats."],["dissuade","dih-SWAYD","verb","To talk someone out of doing something.","My friend dissuaded me from eating a whole bag of candy.","From Latin dissuadere, “to advise against.”","Persuade means talk into; dissuade means talk out of."],["disdain","dis-DAYN","noun","A feeling that something is not worth your respect.","The cat looked at the cheap toy with disdain.","","Disdain sounds like “this stain”: looking at it like a dirty stain."],["qualm","KWAHM","noun","A small worry that something is not right.","I had a qualm about borrowing my sister’s sweater without asking.","","Qualm sounds like calm: a little worry that won’t let you feel calm."],["vestige","VES-tij","noun","A small trace of something that is gone.","A vestige of glitter stayed on the rug months after the party.","From Latin vestigium, “footprint.”","Vestige sounds like “vest left”: a little bit left behind."],["destitute","DES-ti-toot","adjective","Very poor, without food, home or other basic things.","Our food drive helps destitute families in town.","","Destitute sounds like “destroyed suit”: no money, not even nice clothes."],["copious","KOH-pee-uhs","adjective","Large in amount; lots and lots.","I took copious notes, filling ten whole pages.","From Latin copia, “plenty.”","Copious sounds like “copy-us”: so much it looks copied over and over."],["demean","dih-MEEN","verb","To make someone feel less important.","Good teammates never demean each other.","","Demean sounds like “be mean”: making someone feel small."],["bode","BOHD","verb","To be a sign of what will happen.","Dark clouds bode badly for our picnic.","","Bode rhymes with “road”: a sign of what’s coming down the road."],["unscathed","un-SKAYTHD","adjective","Not hurt at all.","I fell off the swing but came out unscathed.","","Unscathed sounds like “un-scratched”: not a scratch on you."],["peripheral","puh-RIF-er-uhl","adjective","At the edge, not the main part.","I saw the ball coming out of the corner of my eye, in my peripheral vision.","From Greek periphereia, “circumference.”","Peri means around: peripheral things sit around the edges."],["terse","TURS","adjective","Using very few words, sometimes in a rude way.","When I asked if he was mad, his terse reply was “No.”","","Terse sounds like “terse, curse, worse”: short and snappy."],["misgiving","mis-GIV-ing","noun","A feeling of doubt or worry.","I had misgivings about jumping off the high rock into the lake.","","Misgiving: your tummy says “maybe miss this one” right before you jump."],["surmise","ser-MYZ","verb","To guess based on clues.","From the crumbs, I could surmise who ate the cookies.","","Surmise sounds like “sure, my eyes”: guessing from what you see."],["jaunty","JAWN-tee","adjective","Cheerful and full of confidence.","He walked to school in a jaunty way, whistling and swinging his bag.","From French gentil, “noble, nice.”","Jaunty sounds like “jump-ty”: a happy little bounce in your step."],["dearth","DURTH","noun","Not enough of something.","There was a dearth of snow, so no sledding this year.","","Dearth sounds like “death” of supply: almost none left."],["eclectic","ih-KLEK-tik","adjective","Made from many different kinds of things.","Jo has an eclectic playlist with cartoon songs, rock and old banjo tunes.","From Greek eklektikos, “picking out.”","Eclectic sounds like electric: an electric mix of all kinds of stuff."],["bastion","BAS-chuhn","noun","A place or group that strongly protects something.","Our quiet library is a bastion of calm in our noisy town.","","A bastion is a strong part of a castle wall: it guards what’s inside."],["coerce","koh-URS","verb","To force someone to do something.","You can’t coerce a cat into taking a bath.","","Coerce sounds like “co-force”: force them to do it."],["pacify","PAS-uh-fy","verb","To calm or soothe.","I pacified the crying baby with her favorite toy.","From Latin pax, “peace.”","A pacifier calms a baby, and pacify means to calm."],["squander","SKWON-der","verb","To waste something, like money or time.","Don’t squander your allowance on candy you won’t eat.","","Squander sounds like “squash and wander”: money wandering away."],["misnomer","mis-NOH-mer","noun","A name that does not fit the thing.","Calling our tiny dog “Tank” is a funny misnomer.","From Old French mesnommer, “to misname.”","Mis means wrong, and nomer comes from name: the wrong name."],["panacea","pan-uh-SEE-uh","noun","Something people think will fix every problem.","Mom thinks hot soup is a panacea for every sickness.","From Greek panakeia, “cure-all.”","Pan means all in Greek: a panacea is a cure for all."],["virtuoso","vur-choo-OH-soh","noun","A person with amazing skill, often in music.","My cousin is a virtuoso on the drums and plays super fast.","From Italian virtuoso, “skilled.”","Virtuoso sounds like “virtually a pro”: amazing skill."],["absolve","ab-ZOLV","verb","To free someone from blame.","The video absolved my brother. It showed the cat broke the vase.","From Latin absolvere, “to set free.”","Absolve has “solve” in it: problem solved, no blame."],["beseech","bih-SEECH","verb","To beg someone in an eager way.","“I beseech you, please let me stay up late!” I begged my parents.","","Beseech sounds like “beseeching”: begging on your knees."],["vindicate","VIN-dih-kayt","verb","To show someone was right or not to blame.","The video vindicated me: I really did score the goal.","","Vindicate sounds like “win-dicate”: you win because you were right."],["precocious","prih-KOH-shuhs","adjective","Smart or skilled very early in life.","My precocious little cousin could read chapter books at age four.","From Latin praecox, “ripe early.”","Precocious means “cooked early” in Latin: ready before others."],["legacy","LEG-uh-see","noun","Something handed down or left behind for others.","The class garden is our legacy, something we leave for next year’s kids.","","Legacy sounds like “leave-a-see”: what you leave behind for others to see."],["infer","in-FUR","verb","To figure something out from clues.","From the muddy paw prints, I can infer the dog went outside.","From Latin inferre, “to bring in.”","Infer sounds like “in-fur”: clues in the fur tell you where the dog went."],["arbitrary","AR-bih-trair-ee","adjective","Picked for no real reason.","Picking teams by shoe color felt arbitrary. There was no good reason.","From Latin arbitrarius, “uncertain.”","Arbitrary sounds like “a bit random”: no real reason."],["finesse","fih-NES","noun","Skill and grace in handling something.","She frosted the cupcakes with finesse, making each one perfect.","From French fin, “fine.”","Finesse sounds like “fine-ness”: doing it fine and smooth."],["superficial","soo-per-FISH-uhl","adjective","Only on the surface, not deep.","The scratch on my knee was only superficial, just on the skin.","From Latin superficies, “surface.”","Super means above: superficial is only on top."],["rampant","RAM-puhnt","adjective","Spreading fast and out of control.","Weeds were rampant in the garden after summer break.","","Rampant sounds like “ramp ant”: ants racing up a ramp, everywhere."],["delegate","DEL-i-gayt","verb","To give part of a job to someone else.","The team captain delegated a job to each player.","","Delegate sounds like “delete-a-task”: hand it off to someone else."],["vantage","VAN-tij","noun","A spot that gives a good view.","From my vantage point on the bleachers, I saw the whole game.","","Vantage sounds like advantage: a better spot to see."],["metaphor","MET-uh-for","noun","Saying one thing is another thing to show how they are alike.","“My bedroom is a zoo” is a metaphor for how messy it is.","From Greek metaphora, “a carrying over.”","A metaphor says one thing is another, like calling a messy room a zoo."],["affront","uh-FRUHNT","noun","An insult that hurts someone’s pride.","Being picked last for the team felt like an affront.","","Affront has “front” in it: an insult right to your front."],["anthology","an-THOL-uh-jee","noun","A book with many stories or poems by different writers.","Our class made an anthology of our best poems, all in one book.","From Greek anthologia, “a gathering of flowers.”","Anthology sounds like “ant-ology”: lots of stories like a busy ant hill."],["zephyr","ZEF-er","noun","A soft, gentle breeze.","A gentle zephyr cooled us off at the beach.","From Greek Zephyros, “the west wind.”","Zephyr sounds like a soft “zzff” of a breeze."],["charisma","kuh-RIZ-muh","noun","A special charm that draws people to you.","Her charisma made the whole class want to join her club.","From Greek kharisma, “gift.”","Charisma sounds like “charm-isma”: full of charm."],["archaic","ar-KAY-ik","adjective","Very old and no longer used.","“Thou” is an archaic word for “you.” Nobody says it now.","From Greek arkhaios, “ancient.”","Archaic sounds like “arch-ancient”: very, very old."],["overture","OH-ver-cher","noun","A first step to start a friendship or a deal; also the opening music of a show.","Sharing my chips with the new kid was an overture of friendship.","From Old French overture, “opening.”","Overture sounds like “open door”: the first move that opens things up."],["conform","kuhn-FORM","verb","To follow the rules or act like everyone else.","Our costumes must conform to the school’s dress code.","","Conform: con means with, form means shape: fitting the same shape."],["anomaly","uh-NOM-uh-lee","noun","Something different from what is normal or expected.","A snowy day in summer would be an anomaly.","From Greek anōmalia, “unevenness.”","Anomaly sounds like “a-no-maly”: no, that’s not normal."],["myriad","MEER-ee-uhd","adjective","Too many to count.","On a clear night at camp, we saw myriad stars.","From Greek myrias, “ten thousand.”","Myriad sounds like “many-ad”: so many you can’t count them all."],["relinquish","rih-LING-kwish","verb","To give up or let go of something.","I had to relinquish the TV remote to my sister.","From Latin relinquere, “to leave behind.”","Relinquish sounds like “release”: you let it go."],["lucrative","LOO-kruh-tiv","adjective","Making a lot of money.","Our lemonade stand was lucrative on the hottest day; we made fifty dollars.","From Latin lucrum, “profit.”","Lucrative sounds like “lucky-rative”: lucky with lots of money."],["contrite","kuhn-TRYTE","adjective","Truly sorry for doing something wrong.","The contrite puppy brought back the stolen sock.","","Contrite sounds like “cry”: so sorry you could cry."],["aggregate","AG-rih-git","noun","A total made by adding things together.","Our team’s aggregate score was the highest in the league.","From Latin aggregare, “to add to a flock.”","Aggregate sounds like “add-gregate”: add it all up."],["facade","fuh-SAHD","noun","The front of a building, or a fake outside look.","His happy facade hid that he was nervous about the test.","From French façade, “front.”","Facade has “face” in it: the face a building or person shows."],["scrupulous","SKROO-pyuh-luhs","adjective","Very careful to do what is right.","My friend is scrupulous and always gives back extra change.","","Scrupulous sounds like “super careful-ous”: careful to do right."],["mitigate","MIT-ih-gayt","verb","To make something less bad.","Wearing a helmet can mitigate how much you get hurt if you fall off your bike.","From Latin mitigare, “to soften.”","Mitigate sounds like mitten: mittens make the cold hurt less."],["concede","kuhn-SEED","verb","To admit something is true, often unwillingly.","I conceded that my sister’s sandcastle was better than mine.","","Concede: you shake hands and admit the other side won."],["scintillating","SIN-tuh-lay-ting","adjective","Sparkling, or very lively and clever.","The fireworks were scintillating, sparkling all over the sky.","From Latin scintilla, “spark.”","Scintillating sounds like “sparkle-lating”: twinkling and lively."],["grievance","GREE-vuhns","noun","A complaint about something unfair.","The class wrote down their grievance about recess being too short.","","Grievance has “grieve” in it: something that makes you upset."],["opportune","op-er-TOON","adjective","Happening at just the right time.","Mom was in a great mood, so it was an opportune time to ask for a puppy.","From Latin opportunus, “toward the port,” as in a helpful wind.","Opportune sounds like opportunity: just the right moment."],["synthesize","SIN-thuh-syz","verb","To combine parts to make something new.","We will synthesize our notes into one poster.","From Greek synthesis, “putting together.”","Syn means together: putting parts together into something new."],["exemplary","ig-ZEM-pluh-ree","adjective","So good that others should copy it.","Her exemplary behavior on the trip earned her a gold star.","From Latin exemplum, “example.”","Exemplary has “example” in it: such a good example others copy it."],["counterfeit","KOWN-ter-fit","adjective","Fake and made to look real.","The counterfeit trading card had blurry printing.","","Counterfeit is a copycat: it looks real, but it’s fake."],["amenable","uh-MEE-nuh-buhl","adjective","Willing to go along with an idea.","Dad was amenable to pancakes for dinner. He said yes!","","Amenable sounds like “a-men”: someone happy to say yes."],["conundrum","kuh-NUHN-druhm","noun","A confusing problem or riddle.","Fitting the giant pizza box in the fridge was a conundrum.","","Conundrum sounds like a funny drum: a puzzle that bangs on your brain."],["tenuous","TEN-yoo-uhs","adjective","Very weak or thin.","Our plan to stay up all night was tenuous once Mom got home.","From Latin tenuis, “thin.”","Tenuous sounds like “thin-uous”: thin and ready to snap."],["canny","KAN-ee","adjective","Smart and careful, especially with money or choices.","The canny shopper saved her allowance for a whole month.","","Canny sounds like “can-do”: smart and careful."],["allot","uh-LOT","verb","To give out a share of something.","Mom will allot each of us three cookies after dinner.","","Allot has “lot” in it: give each a lot, a share."],["contend","kuhn-TEND","verb","To compete or struggle.","Five teams will contend for the trophy.","","A contender is someone trying to win: they contend."],["manifest","MAN-uh-fest","verb","To show clearly.","His excitement manifested in a big happy dance across the kitchen.","From Latin manifestus, “clear.”","Mani means hand: manifest means as clear as if it’s in your hand."],["gregarious","grih-GAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Liking to be with other people.","Our gregarious dog runs to say hello to every person at the park.","From Latin grex, “flock.”","Greg-arious: friendly Greg greets everyone in the group."],["inundate","IN-uhn-dayt","verb","To flood, or to give too much of something.","After my birthday post, friends began to inundate me with messages.","From Latin unda, “wave.”","Inundate has “und,” Latin for wave: waves and waves pour over you."],["cynical","SIN-i-kuhl","adjective","Believing people only care about themselves.","Don’t be so cynical; most people really want to help.","","Cynical sounds like “sigh-nical”: always sighing that nobody is nice."],["oust","OWST","verb","To push someone out of a place or job.","The new kid ousted me from the top spot on the game leaderboard.","","Oust sounds like out: push someone out."],["hone","HOHN","verb","To sharpen or make better through practice.","She practices every day to hone her piano skills.","From Old English hān, “stone.”","Hone rhymes with stone: you sharpen a knife on a hone stone."],["provisional","pruh-VIZH-uh-nuhl","adjective","Set up for now, maybe changed later.","We made a provisional plan to go to the beach if it’s sunny.","","Provisional sounds like “pro, for a while”: good for now, not forever."],["outmoded","owt-MOH-did","adjective","Out of date; no longer in style.","Dad’s outmoded phone still flips open like a clam.","","Outmoded: out of the mode, or style, that’s in now."],["sporadic","spuh-RAD-ik","adjective","Happening now and then, not on a regular basis.","We had sporadic rain all day, just a sprinkle now and then.","From Greek sporadikos, “scattered.”","Sporadic sounds like “spotty”: here, then not, then here again."],["perpetual","per-PECH-oo-uhl","adjective","Going on forever, without stopping.","My little brother seems to be in perpetual motion and never sits still.","From Latin perpetuus, “continuous.”","Perpetual sounds like “pure, continual”: going and going forever."],["cognitive","KOG-ni-tiv","adjective","About thinking, learning and remembering.","Puzzles give your cognitive skills a workout.","From Latin cognoscere, “to get to know.”","Cognitive sounds like “cog”: the gears turning in your brain."],["annotate","AN-oh-tayt","verb","To add notes to explain something you are reading.","We annotate our books with sticky notes about our favorite parts.","From Latin nota, “mark.”","Annotate has “note” in it: write notes on it."],["jaded","JAY-did","adjective","Bored or tired from having too much of something.","After ten rides, my brother was jaded, and even the roller coaster bored him.","","Jaded sounds like “faded”: the fun has faded away."],["moot","MOOT","adjective","No longer worth arguing about.","Arguing about the picnic is moot, because it’s raining anyway.","","Moot sounds like mute: the argument goes silent because it doesn’t matter."],["accolade","AK-uh-layd","noun","An award or words of high praise.","Winning the science fair was the biggest accolade of her year.","","Accolade sounds like “a cool aid”: a cool prize for a job well done."],["autonomy","aw-TON-uh-mee","noun","The freedom to make your own choices.","Getting my own bus pass gave me more autonomy to go places.","From Greek autonomia, “self-rule.”","Auto means “self”: you choose for yourself."],["avarice","AV-er-is","noun","Wanting money and things way too much.","The greedy dragon’s avarice made it hoard every coin in the land.","From Latin avarus, “greedy.”","Avarice sounds like “have-a-rice”: wanting all the rice, never sharing."],["consolidate","kuhn-SOL-i-dayt","verb","To combine into one stronger thing.","We consolidated our two toy bins into one big bin.","","Consolidate has “solid”: many pieces packed into one solid thing."],["ludicrous","LOO-dih-kruhs","adjective","So silly it is funny.","A dog wearing sunglasses and a tie looks ludicrous.","From Latin ludus, “play, game.”","Ludicrous sounds like “ludi-CRAZY”: so silly it’s crazy."],["glib","GLIB","adjective","Smooth and quick in speaking, but not careful or deep.","His glib excuse, said too quickly, did not fool the teacher.","","Glib sounds like “slick lip”: smooth talk that slides out too fast."],["cadence","KAY-duhns","noun","The rhythm and rise and fall of sounds.","The cadence of the poem made it easy to memorize.","From Latin cadere, “to fall.”","Cadence sounds like “dance”: words with a beat you can move to."],["hierarchy","HY-uh-rar-kee","noun","A system that ranks people or things from top to bottom.","In our chicken coop, there is a hierarchy: the big hen eats first.","","Hierarchy sounds like “higher-archy”: who is higher and who is lower."],["advent","AD-vent","noun","The arrival or start of something important.","The advent of spring brought tulips to our yard.","From Latin adventus, “arrival.”","Advent sounds like “adventure”: the start of something new."],["pessimist","PES-uh-mist","noun","Someone who expects things to go wrong.","The pessimist on our team was sure we’d lose before the game even started.","From Latin pessimus, “worst.”","Pessimus means worst in Latin: a pessimist expects the worst."],["comprise","kuhm-PRYZE","verb","To be made up of.","Our team comprises six players and a coach.","","Comprise: a pizza comprises crust, sauce and cheese."],["volatile","VOL-uh-tuhl","adjective","Likely to change quickly, often in a wild way.","The weather was volatile, sunny one minute and stormy the next.","From Latin volare, “to fly.”","Volatile sounds like a volcano: it could blow up any time."],["hiatus","hy-AY-tuhs","noun","A break or pause.","Our art club took a hiatus during test week and started again after.","From Latin hiatus, “an opening, gap.”","Hiatus sounds like “hi, pause”: say hi, take a break, then come back."],["redundant","rih-DUN-duhnt","adjective","Not needed because it repeats something.","Wearing two hats at once is redundant.","From Latin redundare, “to overflow.”","Redundant sounds like “re-done”: doing it again when once was enough."],["oscillate","OS-uh-layt","verb","To swing back and forth.","The fan oscillates back and forth to cool the whole room.","From Latin oscillare, “to swing.”","Oscillate sounds like “a swing-late”: swinging back and forth like a swing."],["bravado","bruh-VAH-doh","noun","Showing off how brave you are, often to hide fear.","With great bravado, he said he wasn’t scared of the dark. Then he jumped at a noise!","From Spanish bravada.","Bravado has “brave” in it: acting brave, maybe pretending."],["attest","uh-TEST","verb","To show or say that something is true.","Everyone who tried my pie can attest that it was yummy.","From Latin attestari, “to bear witness.”","Attest has “test” in it: you tested it, so you can say it’s true."],["blanch","BLANCH","verb","To turn pale, often from shock.","He seemed to blanch, turning pale, when he saw the giant spider.","From French blanc, “white.”","Blanch sounds like “blank”: your face goes blank and white."],["daunting","DAWN-ting","adjective","Seeming hard or scary to start.","The huge stack of library books looked daunting.","","Daunting sounds like “haunting”: scary to start."],["ravenous","RAV-uh-nuhs","adjective","Very, very hungry.","After swimming all day, I was ravenous and ate three sandwiches.","","Ravenous sounds like raven: a hungry bird gobbling up everything."],["adjourn","uh-JURN","verb","To stop a meeting and continue it later.","The club meeting will adjourn now and start again on Friday.","From Old French ajorner, “to set a day.”","Adjourn has “jour,” French for “day”: continue another day."],["procure","proh-KYUR","verb","To get something, often with effort.","We had to procure more marshmallows before the campfire.","From Latin procurare, “to take care of.”","Procure sounds like “pro cure”: a pro goes out and gets what you need."],["abscond","ab-SKOND","verb","To leave secretly and quickly, often with something.","The puppy tried to abscond with my sock and run off!","From Latin abscondere, “to hide away.”","Abscond sounds like “ab-scoot”: sneak off quick."],["epitome","ih-PIT-uh-mee","noun","A perfect example of something.","My grandma’s kitchen, warm and smelling of cookies, is the epitome of cozy.","From Greek epitome, “a cutting short.”","Epitome sounds like “the tip-top-me”: the very top example."],["meddle","MED-uhl","verb","To get involved in something that is not your business.","Please don’t meddle in our argument. We can sort it out ourselves.","From Old French medler, “to mix.”","Meddle sounds like middle: getting in the middle where you don’t belong."],["subsequent","SUB-sih-kwuhnt","adjective","Coming after something else.","The first game was hard, but subsequent games were easier.","From Latin subsequi, “to follow close after.”","Subsequent sounds like sequence: coming next in line."],["indifferent","in-DIF-er-uhnt","adjective","Not caring one way or the other.","The cat was indifferent to its new toy and just went back to sleep.","","In-different: it makes no difference to you."],["mosaic","moh-ZAY-ik","noun","A picture made from small colored pieces.","We glued bits of colored paper together to make a mosaic of a fish.","","Mosaic sounds like “more tiles”: lots of tiny pieces making one picture."],["uproar","UP-ror","noun","Loud noise and confusion.","There was an uproar in the gym when our team scored.","","Uproar has “roar” in it: a big, loud roar going up."],["onerous","OH-ner-uhs","adjective","Heavy, hard and tiring to do.","Cleaning out the garage was an onerous job that took all day.","From Latin onus, “burden.”","Onus means heavy load in Latin: an onerous job weighs you down."],["scrutinize","SKROO-tuh-nyz","verb","To look at something very closely.","The teacher will scrutinize our math homework for mistakes.","","Scrutinize sounds like “screw your eyes”: look very close."],["dissent","dih-SENT","noun","Disagreement with the group.","There was some dissent when the class voted on the field trip.","","Dissent: dis means apart, sent means feel: feeling differently."],["rudimentary","roo-duh-MEN-tuh-ree","adjective","Very basic, at the starting level.","I only know rudimentary Spanish, like “hola” and “gracias.”","From Latin rudimentum, “first attempt.”","Rudimentary sounds like “root-imentary”: just the roots, the basics."],["relentless","rih-LENT-lis","adjective","Not stopping or giving up.","The rain was relentless all weekend, so we stayed inside.","","Relentless means “less relenting”: it never takes a break."],["innate","ih-NAYT","adjective","Something you are born with.","Birds have an innate sense of when to fly south; nobody teaches them.","From Latin innatus, “inborn.”","Innate sounds like “in-nate”: in you from birth, like the word natal."],["pensive","PEN-siv","adjective","Deep in quiet thought.","My friend looked pensive while staring out the bus window.","From French pensif, “thoughtful.”","Pensive sounds like pencil: chewing your pencil, deep in thought."],["deviate","DEE-vee-ayt","verb","To go off the usual path.","We deviated from the trail to watch a deer.","From Latin de-, “away from,” and via, “road.”","Deviate: via means road, de means off: off the road."],["quandary","KWON-duh-ree","noun","A state of not knowing what to do.","I was in a quandary about which friend’s party to go to.","","Quandary sounds like “wander-y”: wandering because you can’t decide."],["paradox","PAIR-uh-doks","noun","Something that sounds impossible but may still be true.","It’s a paradox that I feel so tired after a day of doing nothing.","From Greek paradoxos, “contrary to belief.”","Paradox sounds like “pair of docs”: two doctors saying opposite things, both right."],["paramount","PAIR-uh-mownt","adjective","Most important of all.","Safety is paramount when you ride your bike near the street.","From Old French par amont, “above.”","Paramount has “mount” like mountain: at the very top, most important."],["parched","PARCHT","adjective","Very dry or very thirsty.","After soccer practice in the sun, I was parched and drank two bottles of water.","","Parched sounds like “parked in the sun”: dry, hot and thirsty."],["unprecedented","un-PRES-ih-den-tid","adjective","Never done or seen before.","Our class read an unprecedented 500 books this year, a school record.","","Un means not: unprecedented means nothing came before it."],["allege","uh-LEJ","verb","To say something is true without proof.","My sister alleges that I ate the last cookie, but she can’t prove it.","","Allege sounds like “a ledge”: a claim standing on a shaky ledge, no proof."],["noxious","NOK-shuhs","adjective","Harmful or very unpleasant.","The noxious smell from the garbage truck made us hold our noses.","From Latin noxius, “harmful.”","Noxious sounds like “nose-ious”: a smell so bad your nose runs away."],["cumulative","KYOO-myuh-luh-tiv","adjective","Growing by adding up over time.","The cumulative effect of reading every night was huge.","From Latin cumulus, “heap.”","Cumulative sounds like “accumulate”: it piles up like a snowball."],["nullify","NUL-uh-fy","verb","To cancel out or make worthless.","My brother peeked at my cards, which nullified his win.","From Latin nullus, “none.”","Null means zero: nullify turns something into zero."],["bevy","BEV-ee","noun","A group of people or animals.","A bevy of ducks swam across the pond together.","","Bevy sounds like “heavy”: a heavy crowd of birds."],["attune","uh-TOON","verb","To help someone notice and understand something better.","After a week with our new puppy, I was attuned to every little whine.","","Attune has “tune” in it: get in tune, like tuning a guitar."],["placate","PLAY-kayt","verb","To make someone less angry.","I placated my angry sister by letting her pick the movie.","From Latin placare, “to calm.”","Placate sounds like “plate cake”: hand over a plate of cake to calm someone."],["mar","MAR","verb","To spoil or damage.","One muddy footprint marred our clean kitchen floor.","From Old English merran, “to hinder.”","Mar sounds like scar: it leaves an ugly mark."],["imminent","IM-uh-nuhnt","adjective","About to happen very soon.","With thunder rumbling, rain was imminent, so we ran inside.","From Latin imminere, “to hang over.”","Imminent sounds like “in a minute”: it’s about to happen."],["imperative","im-PAIR-uh-tiv","adjective","Very important and needing to be done.","It is imperative that we feed the fish before the trip, or they’ll go hungry.","From Latin imperare, “to command.”","Imperative sounds like emperor: when the emperor says do it, you must."],["marginal","MAR-juh-nuhl","adjective","Small and not very important.","Adding sprinkles made only a marginal difference to the cake.","From Latin margo, “edge.”","A margin is a page’s edge: marginal stuff sits off to the side."],["catalyst","KAT-uh-list","noun","Something that causes a change to happen.","The science fair was the catalyst for my love of robots.","","Catalyst sounds like “cat-a-list”: the cat that starts the whole chase."],["desolate","DES-uh-lit","adjective","Empty and lonely.","The beach looked empty and desolate in the winter.","From Latin desolare, “to leave alone.”","Desolate sounds like “so late”: everyone has gone home."],["fallacy","FAL-uh-see","noun","A false idea that many people believe.","It is a fallacy that you lose most of your heat through your head.","From Latin fallere, “to deceive.”","Fallacy sounds like “false-acy”: something false people believe."],["constrain","kuhn-STRAYN","verb","To hold back or limit.","Our tiny yard constrains how far we can kick the ball.","","Constrain sounds like “chain”: it holds you back."],["antidote","AN-tih-doht","noun","A cure or a fix for something bad.","Laughing with friends is the best antidote to a bad day.","From Greek antidoton, “given against.”","Antidote has “anti” in it: against the bad stuff."],["blasé","blah-ZAY","adjective","Not impressed or excited because you have seen it before.","My older brother acts blasé about roller coasters now. He just yawns.","From French blasé, “worn out with pleasure.”","Blasé sounds like “blah-zay”: blah, been there, done that."],["corrode","kuh-ROHD","verb","To slowly wear away, like metal rusting.","Salt water corroded the old bike chain until it rusted.","From Latin corrodere, “to gnaw to pieces.”","Corrode sounds like “corroded road”: slowly eaten away."],["defer","dih-FUR","verb","To put off until later.","We deferred the picnic until the rain stopped.","","Defer sounds like “the far”: push it far off until later."],["dissipate","DIS-uh-payt","verb","To fade away or scatter.","The morning fog dissipated by lunch.","","Dissipate: like steam from cocoa, it spreads out and disappears."],["bygone","BY-gon","adjective","From a time long past.","Grandma loves telling stories about bygone days when she was a kid.","","Bygone: days gone by."],["augment","awg-MENT","verb","To make bigger or add to.","We augment our lemonade stand money by selling cookies too.","From Latin augere, “to increase.”","Augment sounds like “add-ment”: add more to make it bigger."],["garner","GAR-ner","verb","To gather or earn something.","Her funny video began to garner lots of laughs at school.","From Latin granarium, “granary.”","Garner sounds like “gather”: gather up a lot."],["serendipity","ser-uhn-DIP-ih-tee","noun","Finding something good by happy chance.","By serendipity, I found a five-dollar bill in my old jacket.","","Serendipity sounds like “surprise-dipity”: a happy surprise."],["tirade","TY-rayd","noun","A long, angry speech.","My brother went on a tirade when his team lost the video game.","","Tirade sounds like “tire-raid”: yelling until everyone is tired."],["hamper","HAM-per","verb","To slow down or get in the way.","Heavy rain can hamper our soccer game and make us slip.","","A laundry hamper full of clothes blocks the door and slows you down."],["fervent","FUR-vuhnt","adjective","Full of strong, warm feeling.","He is a fervent fan of his sister’s soccer team and cheers loudest of all.","From Latin fervere, “to boil.”","Fervent sounds like fever: hot, burning feeling."],["protagonist","proh-TAG-uh-nist","noun","The main character in a story.","In my comic, the protagonist is a brave hamster named Pip.","From Greek protagonistes, “first actor.”","Protos means first in Greek: the protagonist is the first, or main, character."],["dissect","dy-SEKT","verb","To cut apart carefully to study.","In science class we carefully cut open a flower to dissect it and see its parts.","From Latin dissecare, “to cut apart.”","Dis means apart and sect means cut: cut apart to study."],["concoct","kuhn-KOKT","verb","To mix up or make up something new.","We concocted a smoothie with every fruit in the fridge.","From Latin concoquere, “to cook together.”","Concoct sounds like a wizard mixing a crazy potion."],["verbose","ver-BOHS","adjective","Using more words than needed.","My verbose friend took ten minutes to explain a two-minute game.","From Latin verbum, “word.”","Verb is a word: verbose means too many words."],["homage","OM-ij","noun","Something done to show respect or honor.","Our mural pays homage to the first teacher at our school.","","Homage sounds like “home-age”: honoring someone like family at home."],["ambivalent","am-BIV-uh-luhnt","adjective","Having mixed feelings about something.","I felt ambivalent about moving: excited, but sad to leave my friends.","","Ambi means “both”: you feel both ways at once."],["brash","BRASH","adjective","Bold and confident in a pushy way.","The brash new player bragged he would score ten goals.","","Brash sounds like “brag” plus “rash”: bragging without thinking."],["allude","uh-LOOD","verb","To hint at something without saying it directly.","Mom alluded to a surprise trip but would not tell us where.","From Latin alludere, “to play with.”","Allude sounds like “a lude”: a hint, not the whole thing."],["rebuttal","rih-BUT-uhl","noun","An answer that argues against what someone said.","My rebuttal was that cats are better because they clean themselves.","","Rebuttal sounds like “re-but”: “But here’s why you’re wrong,” you say."],["covert","KOH-vert","adjective","Secret or hidden.","We planned a covert mission to hide Mom’s present.","","Covert has “cover”: done under cover, in secret."],["ebb","EB","verb","To slowly flow away or get weaker.","We waited for the tide to ebb and go back out so we could find shells.","From Old English ebba, “low tide.”","Ebb sounds like “ebb away”: the tide slowly slides back out."],["askew","uh-SKYOO","adjective","Crooked; not straight.","The picture on the wall was askew after the door slammed.","","Askew sounds like “a skew”: tilted and crooked."],["penchant","PEN-chuhnt","noun","A strong liking for something.","My dog has a penchant for stealing socks.","From French pencher, “to lean.”","Penchant sounds like “pen chant”: loving pens so much you sing about them."],["ornate","or-NAYT","adjective","Covered with fancy decoration.","Grandma’s mirror has an ornate gold frame covered in swirls.","From Latin ornatus, “decorated.”","Ornate sounds like “more on it”: more and more decoration."],["blithe","BLITHE","adjective","Happy and carefree.","She sang in a blithe voice as she skipped down the hall.","From Old English blīthe, “joyful.”","Blithe sounds like “bliss”: happy and carefree."],["iconic","eye-KON-ik","adjective","Very well known and easy to recognize.","The giant red slide is iconic; everyone knows our park by it.","From Greek eikōn, “image.”","Iconic has “icon” in it, like an app icon you spot instantly."],["lackluster","LAK-lus-ter","adjective","Dull and not exciting.","The magic show was lackluster because the rabbit never came out of the hat.","","Lack-luster means lacking luster, or shine: no sparkle at all."],["plagiarize","PLAY-juh-ryz","verb","To copy someone else's work and say it is yours.","Copying your friend’s story and turning it in as yours is plagiarizing.","From Latin plagiarius, “kidnapper.”","Plagiarius means kidnapper in Latin: stealing someone’s words."],["commemorate","kuh-MEM-uh-rayt","verb","To do something special to remember a person or event.","We planted a tree to commemorate our school’s 100th birthday.","From Latin memorare, “to remember.”","Commemorate has “memor” like memory: you do it to remember."],["muse","MYOOZ","verb","To think about something in a slow, dreamy way.","I mused about what I would do if I won a million dollars.","","Muse sounds like amuse: drifting in a fun, dreamy daydream."],["riveting","RIV-it-ing","adjective","So interesting you can’t look away.","The last minute of the game was so riveting we didn’t blink.","","Riveting has “rivet,” a metal pin: it pins your eyes in place."],["mollify","MOL-uh-fy","verb","To calm someone who is upset.","I mollified my upset sister by giving her my last gummy bear.","From Latin mollis, “soft.”","Mollis means soft in Latin: mollify means soften someone’s mood."],["replicate","REP-lih-kayt","verb","To copy or do something again the same way.","I tried to replicate Mom’s pancakes, but mine came out flat.","","Replicate sounds like replica: making a copy."],["dexterous","DEK-struhs","adjective","Skillful with the hands.","The dexterous juggler kept five balls in the air.","From Latin dexter, “right-handed, skillful.”","Dexterous has “dexter,” Latin for right hand: handy and skillful."],["preposterous","prih-POS-ter-uhs","adjective","Silly and hard to believe.","My brother’s preposterous story said a squirrel stole his homework.","From Latin praeposterus, “reversed,” as in “before behind.”","Pre means before and post means after: backwards and silly."],["ambiguous","am-BIG-yoo-uhs","adjective","Having more than one possible meaning; unclear.","The riddle was ambiguous, so we argued about the answer.","From Latin ambiguus, “doubtful.”","Ambi means “both”: two ways to read it."],["acclaim","uh-KLAYM","noun","Loud praise and approval.","The class movie won acclaim, and everyone clapped and cheered.","From Latin acclamare, “to shout at.”","Acclaim sounds like “a claim to fame”: everyone cheers for you."],["auspicious","aw-SPISH-uhs","adjective","Showing signs of future success.","A sunny morning felt like an auspicious start to our camping trip.","","Auspicious sounds like “awesome-picious”: a sign something awesome is coming."],["satire","SAT-yr","noun","Writing or acting that uses humor to poke fun at something.","The class play was a satire that poked fun at long school lunch lines.","From Latin satura, “a mixed dish.”","Satire sounds like “sat on a tire”: silly on purpose."],["escalate","ES-kuh-layt","verb","To grow bigger or more serious step by step.","A small pillow fight can quickly escalate into a giant one.","From Latin scala, “ladder.”","Escalate sounds like escalator: it goes up, up, up."],["lassitude","LAS-ih-tood","noun","A tired, weak feeling.","A heavy lassitude came over us after the long, hot beach day.","From Latin lassus, “weary.”","Lassitude sounds like “lazy-tude”: a lazy, tired attitude."],["rancor","RANG-ker","noun","Bitter, long-lasting anger.","She held rancor for weeks after he broke her toy.","From Latin rancor, “rankness, grudge.”","Rancor sounds like “rank”: anger that goes sour and smelly."],["abstemious","ab-STEE-mee-uhs","adjective","Careful not to eat or drink too much.","My abstemious uncle only ever takes one small piece of cake.","","Abstemious sounds like “abstain”: hold back from too much."],["cerebral","suh-REE-bruhl","adjective","Thinking deeply rather than going by feelings.","My cerebral friend loves chess and logic puzzles.","From Latin cerebrum, “brain.”","Cerebral comes from cerebrum, the thinking part of your brain."],["redoubtable","rih-DOW-tuh-buhl","adjective","Strong enough to earn respect, even a little fear.","Our redoubtable goalie blocked every single shot.","","Redoubtable has “doubt” in it: no one doubts how strong they are."],["winsome","WIN-suhm","adjective","Sweet and charming.","The puppy’s winsome face won everyone over.","","Winsome sounds like “win some”: it wins hearts."],["strident","STRY-duhnt","adjective","Loud and harsh.","The strident alarm clock made everyone jump.","From Latin stridere, “to creak.”","Strident sounds like “screechy-dent”: loud and harsh."],["peremptory","puh-REMP-tuh-ree","adjective","Bossy and expecting to be obeyed at once.","The coach gave a peremptory whistle, and we all ran right over.","","Peremptory sounds like “pre-empty”: empty the room right now, no questions."],["convalesce","kon-vuh-LES","verb","To rest and get better after being sick.","I convalesced on the couch with soup and comic books after the flu.","From Latin valere, “to be strong.”","Convalesce sounds like “couch and rest”: getting better slowly."],["guile","GYLE","noun","Clever, tricky behavior.","The fox in the story used guile and tricks to get the grapes.","","Guile sounds like “sly”: clever and tricky."],["susceptible","suh-SEP-tuh-buhl","adjective","Easily affected or harmed by something.","I’m susceptible to colds when I don’t sleep enough.","","Susceptible sounds like “sus-catchable”: easy to catch things."],["machination","mak-uh-NAY-shuhn","noun","A sneaky plan or scheme.","My sister’s sneaky machinations to get the front seat never work.","From Latin machinari, “to contrive.”","Machination has “machine” in it: a sneaky plan with lots of moving parts."],["ossify","OS-uh-fy","verb","To become hard, fixed and unable to change.","If you never try new foods, your tastes can ossify.","From Latin os, “bone.”","Os means bone in Latin: ossify means to harden like bone."],["axiom","AK-see-uhm","noun","A statement accepted as true without needing proof.","It’s an axiom in our house: whoever cooks does not wash the dishes.","From Greek axiōma, “what is thought fitting.”","Axiom sounds like “ax-iom”: a rule that’s solid as an ax."],["desultory","DES-uhl-tor-ee","adjective","Without a plan; jumping from one thing to another.","Our desultory chat bounced from dogs to space to pancakes.","","Desultory sounds like “dessert story”: hopping around with no plan."],["insipid","in-SIP-id","adjective","Dull, without flavor or interest.","The soup was insipid, with no taste, until we added salt.","From Latin sapidus, “tasty,” so “not tasty.”","Insipid sounds like “in-sip-id”: one sip and it tastes like nothing."],["querulous","KWER-uh-luhs","adjective","Always complaining in a whiny way.","My querulous little cousin whined about every single thing.","From Latin queri, “to complain.”","Querulous sounds like “query-ous”: always asking “why can’t I?” all day."],["umbrage","UM-brij","noun","A feeling of being offended.","She took umbrage when I said her drawing looked like a potato.","From Latin umbra, “shade.”","Umbrage sounds like “um, rage”: a little rage from feeling hurt."],["magnanimous","mag-NAN-uh-muhs","adjective","Generous and forgiving, even to someone who lost or did wrong.","The winning team was magnanimous and helped the other team pick up the cones.","From Latin magnanimus, “great-souled.”","Magna means big and animus means spirit: a big-hearted spirit."],["germane","jer-MAYN","adjective","Closely connected to the topic.","Keep your questions germane to the lesson, not about lunch.","From Latin germanus, “of the same parents.”","Germane sounds like “germ-main”: stick to the main point."],["juxtapose","JUK-stuh-pohz","verb","To place things side by side to compare them.","The art teacher had us juxtapose warm and cool colors side by side.","From Latin juxta, “next to.”","Juxtapose has “pose”: pose two things side by side."],["harangue","huh-RANG","noun","A long, loud speech that scolds.","The coach gave a long harangue about leaving water bottles on the field.","","Harangue sounds like “harang-harang”: a long, loud nagging speech."],["assay","AS-ay","noun","A test to find out what something is made of.","An assay showed the shiny rock was not real gold.","","Assay sounds like “a test”: test what it’s made of."],["ballyhoo","BAL-ee-hoo","noun","Noisy excitement or attention-getting fuss.","After all the ballyhoo, the new ride was only five seconds long.","","Ballyhoo sounds like “ballyhoo-hoo-hoo!”: noisy fuss."],["fastidious","fa-STID-ee-uhs","adjective","Very careful about details and neatness.","My fastidious cousin lines up his pencils by color every morning.","From Latin fastidium, “disgust.”","Fastidious sounds like “fussy-tidious”: super fussy and tidy."],["obsequious","uhb-SEE-kwee-uhs","adjective","Too eager to please or obey.","The obsequious kid laughed way too hard at every one of the teacher’s jokes.","From Latin obsequi, “to comply.”","Obsequious sounds like “oh, seek you”: always seeking to please you."],["bonhomie","bon-uh-MEE","noun","Cheerful friendliness.","The bonhomie at the block party made everyone feel welcome.","From French bonhomie, “good nature.”","Bonhomie is French for “good man”: cheerful friendliness."],["abeyance","uh-BAY-uhns","noun","A state of being put on hold for a while.","Our beach trip plans are in abeyance until the rain stops.","","Abeyance sounds like “a bay-ance”: plans sitting on hold in the bay."],["abrogate","AB-ruh-gayt","verb","To officially end a rule or agreement.","The club voted to abrogate the old rule about secret handshakes.","From Latin abrogare, “to repeal.”","Abrogate sounds like “a-bro-gate”: closing the gate on a rule."],["assonance","AS-uh-nuhns","noun","The repeating of vowel sounds in nearby words, as in poems.","“Go slow, Joe” repeats the long O sound. That’s assonance!","From Latin assonare, “to sound in answer.”","Assonance sounds like “a song-ance”: vowel sounds that sing together."],["bathos","BAY-thos","noun","A sudden drop from a serious moment to a silly one.","The serious play turned into bathos when the hero tripped over a cat.","From Greek bathos, “depth.”","Bathos sounds like “bath-os”: a serious scene falls into the bath."],["hubris","HYOO-bris","noun","Too much pride.","The rabbit’s hubris made him nap during the race, and he lost.","From Greek hybris, “pride.”","Hubris sounds like “huge-bris”: a huge head full of pride."],["efface","ih-FAYS","verb","To rub out or make hard to see.","Rain began to efface our chalk drawing on the sidewalk until it faded away.","From French effacer, “to wipe out.”","Efface has “face” in it: wipe the face off, so it fades."],["ebullient","ih-BUL-yuhnt","adjective","Bubbling over with cheer and energy.","Our ebullient coach high-fived every player and cheered the whole game.","From Latin ebullire, “to boil over.”","Ebullient has “bull,” from the Latin for boil: bubbling over with joy."],["largesse","lar-ZHES","noun","Generous giving of gifts or money.","Thanks to a neighbor’s largesse, our school got a hundred new books.","From Latin largus, “generous.”","Largesse has “large” in it: large, generous giving."],["eschew","es-CHOO","verb","To stay away from on purpose.","Before a race, runners eschew candy and soda.","","Eschew sounds like “a-choo!”: sneeze it away and don’t touch it."],["tantamount","TAN-tuh-mownt","adjective","Pretty much the same as.","Skipping practice the day before the game is tantamount to giving up.","","Tantamount sounds like “amount the same”: it adds up to the same thing."],["reverie","REV-uh-ree","noun","A happy daydream.","I was lost in a reverie about summer vacation during math class.","From French rêverie, “dreaming.”","Reverie sounds like “rêve,” French for dream: a daydream."],["misanthrope","MIS-uhn-throhp","noun","Someone who dislikes people in general.","In the movie, the grumpy misanthrope hated parties, people and even puppies.","From Greek misanthropos, “hating people.”","Mis means hate and anthrope means human: someone who dislikes people."],["assuage","uh-SWAYJ","verb","To ease or calm a bad feeling.","A hug from Mom helped assuage my disappointment.","","Assuage sounds like “a sway”: sway the pain away."],["conciliatory","kuhn-SIL-ee-uh-tor-ee","adjective","Trying to make peace.","My conciliatory offer to share the swing ended our fight.","","Conciliatory sounds like “council”: trying to make peace."],["inexorable","in-EK-ser-uh-buhl","adjective","Impossible to stop.","The inexorable tide kept coming in and washed our sandcastle away.","From Latin exorare, “to plead successfully.”","Inexorable sounds like “never-stop-able”: like the tide, nothing can stop it."],["bilious","BIL-yuhs","adjective","Grumpy and bad-tempered.","After missing the bus, he was in a bilious mood all day.","From Latin bilis, “bile.”","Bilious sounds like “bee-lious”: grumpy as a stinging bee."],["disquiet","dis-KWY-it","noun","A feeling of worry.","A small feeling of disquiet grew as the bus ran later and later.","","Disquiet: not quiet inside; your tummy feels worried."],["mendacious","men-DAY-shuhs","adjective","Not telling the truth.","The mendacious kid said he never ate candy, with chocolate on his chin.","From Latin mendax, “lying.”","Mendacious sounds like “mend the truth”: patching it up with lies."],["crass","KRAS","adjective","Rude and without good taste.","Bragging about the prize in front of everyone was crass.","","Crass sounds like “crash”: rude, loud manners."],["acerbic","uh-SUR-bik","adjective","Sharp and biting in how you speak.","When I tracked mud inside, my sister said, “Wow, great job,” in an acerbic voice.","From Latin acerbus, “sour, bitter.”","Acerbic sounds like “acid”: sharp and sour words."],["lackadaisical","lak-uh-DAY-zih-kuhl","adjective","Lazy and not caring much.","His lackadaisical sweeping left crumbs all over the floor.","","Lackadaisical sounds like “lacking daisy”: lazy, lying in the daisies."],["nascent","NAY-suhnt","adjective","Just starting to grow or form.","Our nascent band only knows one song so far.","From Latin nasci, “to be born.”","Nasci means to be born in Latin: nascent things are just born."],["consummate","KON-suh-mit","adjective","Showing great skill; complete and perfect.","The magician at the party was a consummate showman.","","Consummate sounds like “sum”: the total, complete best."],["taciturn","TAS-ih-turn","adjective","Quiet and not saying much.","Our taciturn neighbor only nods and waves.","From Latin tacitus, “silent.”","Tacit means silent: a taciturn kid stays quiet."],["commiserate","kuh-MIZ-uh-rayt","verb","To share sympathy with someone who is sad.","We commiserated about our canceled field trip.","From Latin miser, “wretched.”","Com means with, and miser is Latin for sad: being sad with someone."],["pellucid","puh-LOO-sid","adjective","Very clear, easy to see through or understand.","The pool water was so pellucid I could see my toes.","From Latin pellucidus, “transparent.”","Pellucid has “lucid” in it, which means clear: crystal clear."],["trenchant","TREN-chuhnt","adjective","Sharp, clear and to the point.","My friend made a trenchant point: we can’t play outside without a ball.","","Trenchant sounds like trench, cut sharp and deep."],["avow","uh-VOW","verb","To say something openly and firmly.","He avowed that he would finish the race no matter what.","","Avow sounds like “a vow”: a promise said out loud."],["lithe","LYTHE","adjective","Thin, strong and able to bend easily.","The lithe cat slipped easily through the narrow gap in the fence.","From Old English līthe, “gentle, soft.”","Lithe sounds like “light”: light, thin and bendy."],["incisive","in-SY-siv","adjective","Sharp and clear in thinking.","Her incisive question got right to the heart of the problem.","From Latin incidere, “to cut into.”","Incisive sounds like incisor, your sharp front tooth: sharp thinking."],["derelict","DAIR-uh-likt","adjective","Left behind and falling apart.","A derelict rowboat sat rotting at the edge of the lake.","","Derelict sounds like “there it’s left”: left behind and falling apart."],["benighted","bih-NY-tid","adjective","Lacking knowledge or understanding.","I was benighted about baseball rules until my friend explained them.","","Benighted has “night” in it: in the dark."],["forlorn","fer-LORN","adjective","Lonely and sad.","A forlorn mitten lay alone in the snow.","From Old English forloren, “lost.”","Forlorn sounds like “for-lone”: lonely and left behind."],["reprehensible","rep-rih-HEN-suh-buhl","adjective","Very wrong and deserving blame.","Cheating on a test is reprehensible.","","Reprehensible sounds like “reprimand-able”: so wrong you get scolded."],["debacle","dih-BAH-kuhl","noun","A sudden, total failure.","The cake that sank in the middle was a total debacle.","","Debacle sounds like “the buckle broke”: everything falls apart."],["equanimity","ee-kwuh-NIM-ih-tee","noun","Staying calm, even when things go wrong.","When paint spilled, she stayed calm with equanimity and grabbed a towel.","From Latin aequus, “even,” and animus, “mind.”","Equanimity starts like equal: your mood stays even and level."],["proverbial","pruh-VUR-bee-uhl","adjective","Well known, as in a common saying.","Finding my earring at the beach was like finding the proverbial needle in a haystack.","From Latin proverbium, “a saying.”","Proverbial comes from proverb, a well-known saying."],["provincial","pruh-VIN-shuhl","adjective","Narrow in thinking, not open to the wider world.","My provincial uncle thinks any food he hasn’t tried must be gross.","From Latin provincia, “province.”","A province is a far-off region: provincial thinking never leaves its small town."],["cacophony","kuh-KOF-uh-nee","noun","A harsh mix of loud, clashing sounds.","The band warming up made a cacophony of honks and squeaks.","From Greek kakos, “bad,” and phone, “sound.”","Caco is Greek for bad, and phon means sound: bad sound."],["allegory","AL-uh-gor-ee","noun","A story with a hidden meaning or lesson.","The tortoise and the hare is an allegory about patience.","From Greek allēgoria, “speaking otherwise.”","Allegory sounds like “all-a-story”: a story with a lesson."],["opprobrium","uh-PROH-bree-uhm","noun","Harsh blame and shame from lots of people.","The player who cheated faced opprobrium from the whole school.","From Latin opprobrium, “reproach.”","Opprobrium sounds like “oh, problem”: everyone says you’re the problem."],["aphorism","AF-uh-riz-uhm","noun","A short, wise saying.","“Well begun is half done” is my coach’s favorite aphorism.","From Greek aphorismos, “definition.”","Aphorism sounds like “a four-ism”: wise words in about four words."],["neophyte","NEE-uh-fyt","noun","A beginner at something.","As a neophyte at chess, I lost my queen in four moves.","From Greek neophytos, “newly planted.”","Neo means new: a neophyte is new at something."],["divulge","dih-VUHLJ","verb","To share a secret.","She would not divulge the ending of the book.","","Divulge sounds like “bulge”: a secret bulging to come out."],["platitude","PLAT-ih-tood","noun","A saying used so often that it means little.","“Everything happens for a reason” is a platitude people say all the time.","From French plat, “flat.”","Plat means flat in French: a platitude is a flat, tired saying."],["anodyne","AN-uh-dine","adjective","Harmless and unlikely to upset anyone, often a bit dull.","My birthday card just said “Have a nice day.” It was so anodyne!","From Greek anōdynos, “without pain.”","Anodyne sounds like “a nod-dine”: so plain everyone nods off."],["convivial","kuhn-VIV-ee-uhl","adjective","Friendly, lively and fun, like a good party.","The family reunion had a convivial mood with games and laughing.","From Latin convivium, “a feast.”","Viv means live, like vivid: a party full of life."],["remiss","rih-MIS","adjective","Careless about doing what you should.","It would be remiss of me to forget to feed the cat.","","Remiss has “miss” in it: you missed doing your job."],["salubrious","suh-LOO-bree-uhs","adjective","Good for your health.","A walk outside is salubrious after sitting all day.","From Latin salus, “health.”","Salubrious sounds like “salad-brious”: healthy like a salad."],["propagate","PROP-uh-gayt","verb","To spread or grow more of something.","We propagated our plant by putting a cut piece in a glass of water.","From Latin propagare, “to plant cuttings.”","Propagate sounds like “pro-copy-gate”: making copies that spread out."],["cloying","KLOY-ing","adjective","So sweet it becomes too much.","The frosting was cloying after the second cupcake.","","Cloying sounds like “clogging”: so sweet it clogs your mouth."],["paucity","PAW-sih-tee","noun","A small amount; not enough.","There was a paucity of cookies, so we each only got half.","From Latin paucus, “few.”","Paucus means few in Latin: a paucity is too few."],["vitriolic","vit-ree-OL-ik","adjective","Very bitter and harsh.","The angry note was vitriolic and full of mean words.","","Vitriolic sounds like “vitamin acid”: words that burn."],["approbation","ap-ruh-BAY-shuhn","noun","Approval or praise.","The young chef won the approbation of the whole family.","From Latin approbare, “to approve.”","Approbation has “approve” in it: approval and praise."],["bibliophile","BIB-lee-uh-file","noun","A person who loves books.","As a true bibliophile, she has books stacked in every room.","From Greek biblion, “book,” and philos, “loving.”","Biblio means “book” and phile means “lover”: book lover."],["rapacious","ruh-PAY-shuhs","adjective","Greedy and grabbing for more.","The rapacious seagulls grabbed all our chips.","From Latin rapere, “to seize.”","Rapacious sounds like “raptor”: a bird snatching everything."],["scathing","SKAY-thing","adjective","Very harsh and critical.","The coach gave a scathing speech after we lost by 20.","","Scathing sounds like scalding: words that burn."],["agog","uh-GOG","adjective","Very eager and excited.","The kids were agog as the baby chicks hatched.","","Agog sounds like “a-goggle”: eyes wide with excitement."],["intransigent","in-TRAN-sih-juhnt","adjective","Refusing to change your mind.","The intransigent toddler would only wear his rain boots, no matter what.","From Spanish intransigente, “not compromising.”","Intransigent sounds like “in trench”: dug in a trench and won’t budge."],["venerable","VEN-er-uh-buhl","adjective","Respected because of age or wisdom.","The venerable oak in the park is over 200 years old.","","Venerable sounds like “very honorable”: respected for being old and wise."],["alliteration","uh-lit-uh-RAY-shuhn","noun","Using the same first sound in words close together.","“Silly snakes slither slowly” is full of alliteration.","From Latin littera, “letter.”","Alliteration sounds like “all-letter”: all same letter sounds."],["besmirch","bih-SMURCH","verb","To damage someone’s good name.","Telling fibs about my friend would besmirch her good name.","","Besmirch sounds like “be smear”: smear mud on someone’s name."],["impervious","im-PUR-vee-uhs","adjective","Not let through, or not bothered by something.","My new raincoat is impervious to water; I stayed totally dry.","From Latin impervius, “not passable.”","Impervious sounds like a superhero shield: nothing gets through."],["bellwether","BEL-weth-er","noun","Something that shows where a trend is heading.","The first warm day is a bellwether of summer.","From Middle English, a lead sheep that wore a bell.","Bellwether sounds like “bell weather”: a bell telling what weather is coming."],["maudlin","MAWD-lin","adjective","Sad and teary in an overdone way.","My brother got maudlin when he found out the last slice of pizza was gone.","","Maudlin sounds like “mad and weepy”: crying way too much over small stuff."],["celerity","suh-LER-i-tee","noun","Great speed.","The hummingbird moved with amazing celerity.","From Latin celer, “swift.”","Celerity sounds like “accelerate”: zoom, super fast."],["coalesce","koh-uh-LES","verb","To come together to form one whole.","Our scattered ideas coalesced into one great science project.","","Coalesce sounds like “coal”: little pieces pressed into one lump."],["spurious","SPYOOR-ee-uhs","adjective","False, even if it looks real.","My brother’s spurious excuse was that the dog ate his socks.","From Latin spurius, “false.”","Spurious sounds like “spoof-ious”: fake that looks real."],["perfidious","per-FID-ee-uhs","adjective","Not loyal; breaking promises.","The perfidious friend in the story told everyone her secret.","From Latin perfidia, “faithlessness.”","Fides means faith in Latin: perfidious means breaking faith."],["presage","PRES-ij","verb","To be a sign of what is coming.","Dark gray clouds often presage a storm.","From Latin praesagire, “to sense beforehand.”","Pre means before: to presage is to give a sign before it comes."],["unflappable","un-FLAP-uh-buhl","adjective","Always calm, even when things go wrong.","Our unflappable teacher stayed calm when the fire alarm went off.","","Unflappable means no flapping: calm, not flapping around like a bird."],["pernicious","per-NISH-uhs","adjective","Harmful in a slow, sneaky way.","Staying up late every night can have a pernicious effect on your grades.","From Latin pernicies, “ruin.”","Pernicious sounds like “pretty vicious”: hurting slowly and quietly."],["extol","ik-STOHL","verb","To praise very highly.","The judges extol her drawing, saying it’s the best in the show.","From Latin extollere, “to raise up.”","Extol sounds like “extra-tall”: praise someone up extra tall."],["manifold","MAN-uh-fohld","adjective","Many and of different kinds.","The new playground offers manifold ways to have fun.","From Old English manigfeald, “many-fold.”","Manifold means “many fold”: folded into lots of different kinds."],["insatiable","in-SAY-shuh-buhl","adjective","Never satisfied, always wanting more.","My little brother has an insatiable hunger for dinosaur facts.","From Latin satiare, “to fill.”","In-satiable means can’t be satisfied: always want more."],["aspersion","uh-SPUR-zhuhn","noun","An unkind remark that makes people think badly of someone.","She would never cast aspersions on a teammate.","","Aspersion sounds like “a person”: insulting a person."],["apropos","ap-ruh-POH","adjective","Fitting for the moment.","Her joke about snow was apropos on the first snowy day.","From French à propos, “to the purpose.”","Apropos sounds like “a pro-pose”: it fits just right."],["apoplectic","ap-uh-PLEK-tik","adjective","So angry you can barely speak.","The coach was nearly apoplectic when the bus left without the team.","","Apoplectic sounds like “a-pop-electric”: so mad you pop."],["inane","ih-NAYN","adjective","Silly and without sense.","We giggled at the inane jokes, like “Why did the chicken cross the road?”","From Latin inanis, “empty.”","Inane sounds like “insane”: silly with no sense."],["defunct","dih-FUHNGKT","adjective","No longer working or existing.","We found a defunct robot toy in the attic that wouldn’t turn on.","","Defunct sounds like “de-function”: it doesn’t function anymore."],["exhort","ig-ZORT","verb","To strongly urge someone to do something.","The coach would exhort us to run one more lap, yelling “You can do it!”","From Latin exhortari, “to encourage.”","Exhort sounds like “extra shout”: cheer someone on hard."],["stolid","STOL-id","adjective","Calm and showing little feeling.","The stolid guard didn’t smile even when we made funny faces.","","Stolid sounds like “solid”: like a solid rock, no feelings showing."],["itinerant","eye-TIN-er-uhnt","adjective","Moving from place to place.","An itinerant ice cream truck moves from street to street all summer.","From Latin iter, “journey.”","Itinerant sounds like itinerary: a travel plan, always on the move."],["didactic","dy-DAK-tik","adjective","Meant to teach a lesson.","The book was a little too didactic, with a lesson on every page.","From Greek didaskein, “to teach.”","Didactic sounds like “did you get it?”: always teaching a lesson."],["amalgamate","uh-MAL-guh-mayt","verb","To combine into one.","The two small clubs chose to amalgamate into one big club.","","Amalgamate sounds like “a mall gang mate”: everyone mixed into one big group."],["circuitous","ser-KYOO-i-tuhs","adjective","Taking a long, winding route.","We took a circuitous path through the woods.","","Circuitous sounds like circuit: going round and round."],["demure","dih-MYOOR","adjective","Quiet, shy and modest.","The demure kitten hid shyly behind its mother.","","Demure sounds like “the mouse”: quiet and shy."],["evanescent","ev-uh-NES-uhnt","adjective","Fading away quickly.","The evanescent bubbles popped before we could count them.","From Latin evanescere, “to vanish.”","Evanescent has “vanish” sounds inside: e-VAN-escent, it vanishes."],["jocular","JOK-yuh-ler","adjective","Joking and playful.","Uncle Sam’s jocular stories kept us laughing all night.","From Latin jocus, “joke.”","Jocular sounds like “joke-ular”: full of jokes."],["zeitgeist","TSYT-gyst","noun","The mood and ideas of a certain time.","Fidget spinners were part of the zeitgeist when my sister was in school.","From German Zeit, “time,” and Geist, “spirit.”","Zeit means time and Geist means spirit in German: the spirit of a time."],["perturb","per-TURB","verb","To make someone worried or upset.","The loud thunder perturbed my dog, so he hid under the bed.","From Latin perturbare, “to throw into confusion.”","Perturb sounds like disturb: shaking up your calm."],["sonorous","SON-er-uhs","adjective","Deep, full and rich in sound.","The singer’s sonorous voice filled the whole hall.","From Latin sonor, “sound.”","Son comes from a Latin word for sound: a big, deep, booming sound."],["badger","BAJ-er","verb","To keep asking someone in an annoying way.","My little sister will badger me until I play with her.","","A badger digs and digs, never stopping."],["nexus","NEK-suhs","noun","A connection or central link.","The kitchen is the nexus of our house, where everyone meets.","From Latin nexus, “a binding.”","Nexus sounds like “next to us”: the spot that links everyone together."],["torpid","TOR-pid","adjective","Slow and sluggish, without energy.","The cat was torpid on the hot summer afternoon.","From Latin torpere, “to be numb.”","Torpid sounds like “torpedo stuck”: slow and sleepy."],["amorphous","uh-MOR-fuhs","adjective","Having no clear shape.","An amorphous blob of slime oozed across the table.","From Greek amorphos, “shapeless.”","A means “without,” morph means “shape”: no shape."],["beholden","bih-HOHL-duhn","adjective","Owing someone a favor.","I felt beholden to my neighbor for finding my lost cat.","","Beholden has “hold” in it: held by a favor you owe."],["cryptic","KRIP-tik","adjective","Mysterious and hard to understand.","My brother left a cryptic note: “Look under the third step.”","From Greek kryptos, “hidden.”","Cryptic sounds like crypt: dark and mysterious, like a secret code."],["quintessential","kwin-tuh-SEN-shuhl","adjective","The most perfect example of something.","Pizza and a movie is the quintessential Friday night.","From Latin quinta essentia, “fifth essence.”","Quint means five, like quintuplets: the fifth, purest part of something."],["nadir","NAY-der","noun","The lowest point.","Losing my shoe in the mud was the nadir of my day.","From Arabic nazir, “opposite.”","Nadir sounds like nada: the very bottom, where there’s nothing left."],["surreptitious","sur-uhp-TISH-uhs","adjective","Done in a secret, sneaky way.","I took a surreptitious bite of cake before the party started.","","Surreptitious sounds like “sneaky-titious”: done in secret."],["officious","uh-FISH-uhs","adjective","Bossy in a meddling way, giving orders no one asked for.","The officious hall monitor gave orders to everyone, even the teachers.","From Latin officiosus, “obliging.”","Officious sounds like “office boss”: bossing people who didn’t ask."],["behoove","bih-HOOV","verb","To be right or useful for someone to do.","It would behoove you to study before the spelling test.","","Behoove sounds like “be-hoof”: a horse should use its hooves."],["anachronism","uh-NAK-ruh-niz-uhm","noun","Something placed in the wrong time period.","A knight with a cell phone would be an anachronism.","From Greek anachronismos, “wrong timing.”","Chrono means “time”: an anachronism is in the wrong time."],["exacerbate","ig-ZAS-er-bayt","verb","To make a problem worse.","Scratching your mosquito bite will only exacerbate the itch.","From Latin acerbus, “harsh, bitter.”","Exacerbate sounds like “extra-bait”: adding extra to make it worse."],["inscrutable","in-SKROO-tuh-buhl","adjective","Impossible to understand or read.","My cat stared at me with an inscrutable look; I had no idea what she wanted.","From Latin scrutari, “to search.”","In-scrutable: you can scrutinize it all day and still not figure it out."],["plaudit","PLAW-dit","noun","Praise or applause.","Our class play earned plaudits from all the parents.","From Latin plaudite, “applaud!”","Plaudit sounds like “applaud it”: clap, clap, cheer."],["deprecate","DEP-ri-kayt","verb","To show you disapprove of something.","Mom deprecates leaving dirty dishes in the sink.","","Deprecate sounds like “the wreck”: you frown on a mess."],["incandescent","in-kuhn-DES-uhnt","adjective","Glowing with heat, or full of strong feeling.","The incandescent bulb glowed so hot I couldn’t touch it.","From Latin candere, “to glow.”","Incandescent sounds like “candle-scent”: glowing like a candle."],["dulcet","DUHL-sit","adjective","Sweet and pleasant to hear.","The dulcet sound of the flute filled the room.","From Latin dulcis, “sweet.”","Dulce means sweet in Spanish, like dulce de leche."],["sinuous","SIN-yoo-uhs","adjective","Curvy and winding.","The sinuous river curved through the valley like a ribbon.","From Latin sinus, “a curve.”","Sinuous sounds like “snake-uous”: curvy like a slithering snake."],["indolent","IN-duh-luhnt","adjective","Lazy and avoiding work.","The indolent cat lay on the couch and let the mouse walk right by.","From Latin dolere, “to feel pain.”","Indolent sounds like “in-dull-ent”: dull, lazy and doing nothing."],["evince","ih-VINS","verb","To show or reveal a feeling or quality.","His wide grin seemed to evince pure joy.","From Latin evincere, “to prove.”","Evince sounds like “evidence”: it shows the clue for all to see."],["dichotomy","dy-KOT-uh-mee","noun","A split between two opposite things.","The story shows a dichotomy between city life and farm life.","From Greek dikha, “in two,” and temnein, “to cut.”","Dicho is Greek for in two: split in two."],["effulgent","ih-FUL-juhnt","adjective","Shining brightly.","The effulgent full moon lit up our whole walk home.","From Latin effulgere, “to shine out.”","Effulgent sounds like “full-gent”: full of bright light."],["inimical","ih-NIM-ih-kuhl","adjective","Harmful or working against something.","Too much screen time can be inimical to good sleep.","From Latin inimicus, “enemy.”","Inimical sounds like “enemy-cal”: acting like an enemy."],["chicanery","shi-KAY-nuh-ree","noun","Clever tricks used to fool people.","The card game ended when we spotted my cousin’s chicanery.","","Chicanery sounds like “chicken-ery”: sneaky tricks from a sly chicken."],["translucent","trans-LOO-suhnt","adjective","Letting some light through, but not clear.","The shower curtain is translucent, so light comes through but you can’t see in.","From Latin translucere, “to shine through.”","Trans means through, and lucent means light: some light shines through."],["undulate","UN-juh-layt","verb","To move in smooth waves.","The jump rope seemed to undulate like a snake.","From Latin unda, “wave.”","Undulate sounds like “undo-late”: waving up and down."],["conjure","KON-jer","verb","To bring something to mind, or make it appear as if by magic.","The smell of pine conjures memories of camping.","","A magician conjures a rabbit out of a hat."],["begrudge","bih-GRUHJ","verb","To feel unhappy that someone has something.","I don’t begrudge my friend her new bike; she saved for it.","","Begrudge has “grudge” in it: feeling unhappy at someone."],["pithy","PITH-ee","adjective","Short but full of meaning.","My grandpa’s advice is short and pithy: “Work hard, be kind.”","","Pithy sounds like pit: small like a fruit pit, but packed with life."],["curmudgeon","ker-MUHJ-uhn","noun","A grumpy, cranky person.","Our neighbor acts like a curmudgeon, but he waves at us every morning.","","Curmudgeon sounds like “grumpy dungeon”: a cranky grump."],["adumbrate","AD-uhm-brayt","verb","To give a rough outline or hint of something.","Dark clouds adumbrate the storm that’s on its way.","From Latin umbra, “shadow.”","Adumbrate has “umbra” in it, which means shadow: a shadow of what’s coming."],["diaphanous","dy-AF-uh-nuhs","adjective","So thin and light you can almost see through it.","The fairy costume had diaphanous wings you could see through.","","Diaphanous: dia means through: thin enough to see through."],["cogent","KOH-juhnt","adjective","Clear and convincing.","She made a cogent case for a longer recess, and the teacher agreed.","","Cogent sounds like “cog”: every gear of the argument fits."],["innocuous","ih-NOK-yoo-uhs","adjective","Harmless.","The spider looked scary but was innocuous; it couldn’t hurt anyone.","From Latin nocuus, “harmful,” so “not harmful.”","Innocuous sounds like innocent: it does no harm."],["lexicon","LEK-sih-kon","noun","All the words used in a language or by a group.","Skateboarders have their own lexicon of words for tricks.","From Greek lexis, “word.”","Lexicon sounds like “lexi-dictionary”: a big list of words."],["insular","IN-suh-ler","adjective","Cut off from others and not open to new ideas.","The club felt insular, only for its few members, until it invited new kids.","From Latin insula, “island.”","Insular comes from the Latin for island: cut off like an island."],["resurgence","rih-SUR-juhns","noun","A rise again after a quiet time.","There was a resurgence of yo-yos at school this spring.","From Latin resurgere, “to rise again.”","Resurgence has “surge” in it: surging back up again."],["cavalier","kav-uh-LEER","adjective","Not caring enough about something important.","His cavalier attitude toward homework hurt his grade.","","A cavalier rides high on a horse, too cool to care."],["parsimonious","par-suh-MOH-nee-uhs","adjective","Very unwilling to spend money.","Our parsimonious neighbor reuses the same paper bag for years.","From Latin parsimonia, “thrift.”","Parsimonious sounds like “parse money”: counting every single penny."],["subterfuge","SUB-ter-fyooj","noun","A trick used to hide what you are doing.","My brother used subterfuge to sneak a cookie out of the jar.","","Sub means under: a trick done under the table."],["enervate","EN-er-vayt","verb","To drain someone’s energy.","The hot, sticky afternoon seemed to enervate the whole team.","From Latin nervus, “sinew, strength.”","Enervate sounds like “energy-gone”: it drains your energy."],["whet","WET","verb","To sharpen, or to make a feeling stronger.","The smell of pizza will whet my appetite.","","Whet sounds like wet: wet your appetite so it wants more."],["supercilious","soo-per-SIL-ee-uhs","adjective","Acting as if you are better than others.","The supercilious kid said his drawing was way better than everyone else’s.","From Latin supercilium, “eyebrow.”","Super means above: acting like you’re above everyone else."],["atrophy","AT-ruh-fee","verb","To waste away or get weaker from lack of use.","If you never practice, your piano skills will atrophy.","From Greek atrophia, “lack of food.”","Atrophy sounds like “a trophy”: unused skills get dusty like an old trophy."],["urbane","ur-BAYN","adjective","Polite, smooth and at ease with people.","The urbane host made every guest feel welcome at the party.","From Latin urbanus, “of the city.”","Urbane sounds like urban, the city: smooth and polite like a city host."],["assiduous","uh-SIJ-oo-uhs","adjective","Working with steady care and effort.","Her assiduous practice made her the best piano player in the class.","From Latin assiduus, “sitting down to, busy.”","Assiduous sounds like “a sit-down”: sit down and work hard."],["baleful","BAYL-fuhl","adjective","Threatening or full of bad feeling.","The cat gave the vacuum cleaner a baleful stare.","","Baleful sounds like “bail-full”: full of bad feelings."],["harbinger","HAR-bin-jer","noun","A sign of something that is coming.","The first robin is a harbinger that spring is coming soon.","","Harbinger sounds like “herald-bringer”: it brings news of what’s coming."],["feckless","FEK-lis","adjective","Not trying hard and not getting things done.","The feckless plan to clean the garage never got past one box.","From Scots feck, “effect, value.”","Feck is an old Scottish word for effect: feckless means no effect."],["compunction","kuhm-PUHNGK-shuhn","noun","A feeling of guilt that stops you from doing something.","He had no compunction about eating the last slice, since it was his turn.","","Compunction is that guilty pinch inside that says “Don’t do it.”"],["sublime","suh-BLYM","adjective","So beautiful or great it fills you with awe.","The view from the top of the hill was sublime.","From Latin sublimis, “lofty.”","Sublime sounds like “sub-lime”: so amazing it’s like sweet lime pie."],["puerile","PYOOR-uhl","adjective","Silly and childish.","My brother’s puerile burping jokes made Grandma sigh.","From Latin puer, “boy.”","Puer means boy in Latin: puerile means childish."],["morass","muh-RAS","noun","A messy situation that is hard to get out of.","My messy closet turned into a morass of clothes and toys.","From Dutch moeras, “marsh.”","Morass sounds like “more mess”: a muddy mess you get stuck in."],["munificent","myoo-NIF-ih-suhnt","adjective","Very, very generous.","Our munificent neighbor gave every kid on the street a full-size candy bar.","From Latin munificus, “generous.”","Munificent sounds like “money-ficent”: giving away lots of money and gifts."],["abstruse","ab-STROOS","adjective","Very hard to understand.","The instructions were so abstruse that I read them three times.","From Latin abstrusus, “hidden.”","Abstruse sounds like “obtuse” and “confuse”: very hard to get."],["deign","DAYN","verb","To do something you think is beneath you.","The cat finally deigned to come when I called.","","Deign sounds like “dainty”: acting too fancy to bother."],["jettison","JET-ih-suhn","verb","To throw away something you do not need.","We had to jettison some toys to fit the tent in the car.","From Old French getaison, “a throwing.”","Jettison sounds like jet: toss it out the jet to make it lighter."],["incongruous","in-KONG-groo-uhs","adjective","Out of place or not fitting in.","A snowman looked incongruous on the hot, sunny beach.","From Latin congruus, “fitting.”","In-congruous: not congruent, not matching, so it looks out of place."],["staid","STAYD","adjective","Serious, proper and a bit dull.","Grandpa’s staid shoes are all black and brown.","","Staid sounds like stayed: it stayed the same, plain and serious."],["ineffable","in-EF-uh-buhl","adjective","Too great to describe in words.","The view from the top of the mountain was ineffable; I had no words.","From Latin effabilis, “able to be spoken.”","Ineffable means you can’t say it: no words could ever be enough."],["decorous","DEK-er-uhs","adjective","Polite and proper in behavior.","The class was decorous and quiet during the guest speaker’s talk.","","Decorous sounds like “decorum”: proper manners."],["felicity","fih-LIS-ih-tee","noun","Great happiness, or a well-chosen way of saying something.","We felt pure felicity on the first day of summer vacation.","From Latin felix, “happy, lucky.”","Felicity sounds like “feline-city”: happy as a cat in a city of yarn."],["denizen","DEN-i-zuhn","noun","A creature or person that lives in a certain place.","Crabs are denizens of the tide pools.","","Denizen sounds like “den citizen”: someone who lives there."],["churlish","CHUR-lish","adjective","Rude and grumpy.","It would be churlish not to thank Grandma for the sweater.","","Churlish sounds like “churl,” an old word for a rude, rough person."],["dogged","DAW-gid","adjective","Stubborn and never giving up.","Her dogged practice finally paid off at the recital.","","Dogged: like a dog with a bone, it never lets go."],["bulwark","BUL-werk","noun","A strong wall or something that protects.","Good friends are a bulwark against loneliness.","","Bulwark sounds like “bull-wall”: a strong wall."],["malaise","ma-LAYZ","noun","A fuzzy feeling of being unwell or uneasy.","A gray malaise hung over me on the last day of summer break.","From French malaise, “discomfort.”","Mal means bad and aise means ease in French: an uneasy feeling."],["cantankerous","kan-TANG-ker-uhs","adjective","Grumpy and quick to argue.","Our cantankerous old goat headbutts the fence.","","Cantankerous sounds like “can’t, angry!”: grumpy and ready to argue."],["sardonic","sar-DON-ik","adjective","Making fun in a dry, mean way.","He gave a sardonic laugh when I said I’d finish homework early.","","Sardonic sounds like “sardine-ic”: salty and sharp."],["wheedle","WEE-duhl","verb","To use sweet talk to get what you want.","My sister tried to wheedle an extra cookie from Grandma.","","Wheedle sounds like a wheel: rolling sweet words to get your way."],["aegis","EE-jis","noun","Protection or support from someone.","The little kids crossed the busy street safely under the aegis of the crossing guard.","From Greek aigis, “goatskin shield.”","Aegis was the magic shield in Greek myths: it means protection."],["propitious","pruh-PISH-uhs","adjective","Showing good signs for success.","A sunny, breezy day was propitious for flying our kites.","From Latin propitius, “favorable.”","Propitious sounds like “pro-picnic-ous”: a perfect day for a picnic."],["paragon","PAIR-uh-gon","noun","A perfect example of something.","My big sister is a paragon of patience with our baby brother.","","Paragon sounds like “pair of gold”: a gold-star example."],["prodigal","PROD-ih-guhl","adjective","Spending money or things wastefully.","The prodigal kid spent all his birthday money in one afternoon.","From Latin prodigus, “wasteful.”","Prodigal sounds like “prod it all”: poking through all your money fast."],["deleterious","del-i-TEER-ee-uhs","adjective","Harmful.","Too much screen time can be deleterious to good sleep.","","Deleterious sounds like “delete”: harmful, wiping out the good."],["capricious","kuh-PRISH-uhs","adjective","Changing moods or plans suddenly, for no reason.","The capricious weather went from sunny to hail in one hour.","","Capricious sounds like a Capri goat: hopping one way, then another."],["ostentatious","os-ten-TAY-shuhs","adjective","Showy, trying to impress people.","His ostentatious sneakers had flashing lights and gold laces.","From Latin ostentare, “to show off.”","Ostentatious sounds like ostrich: a big bird puffing up its feathers to show off."],["reticent","RET-ih-suhnt","adjective","Not willing to share your thoughts or feelings.","He was reticent about his surprise and wouldn’t say a word.","From Latin reticere, “to keep silent.”","Reticent sounds like “resist sent”: resisting sending your words out."],["alacrity","uh-LAK-rih-tee","noun","Cheerful eagerness and speed.","He said yes to the pool party with alacrity.","From Latin alacer, “lively.”","Alacrity sounds like “a rocket-y”: zoom, eager and quick."],["impetuous","im-PECH-oo-uhs","adjective","Acting quickly without thinking.","In an impetuous moment, he jumped in the pool with his shoes on.","From Latin impetus, “rush, attack.”","Impetuous sounds like “impatient”: doing it right now without thinking."],["disconcert","dis-kuhn-SURT","verb","To unsettle or confuse.","The silent crowd disconcerted the young singer.","","Disconcert: like a concert going wrong, it throws you off."],["wizened","WIZ-uhnd","adjective","Dry and wrinkled with age.","The wizened old man in the story had a long white beard.","","Wizened sounds like wizard: old and wrinkly like a wizard."],["protean","PROH-tee-uhn","adjective","Able to change into many forms or do many things.","My protean friend can sing, paint, juggle and fix bikes.","From Greek Proteus, a sea god in old myths who could change shape.","Proteus was a sea god in Greek myths who could change his shape."],["ameliorate","uh-MEEL-yuh-rayt","verb","To make a bad situation better.","Planting trees helped ameliorate the heat on the playground.","From Latin melior, “better.”","Ameliorate has “meli” like “mellow”: make it better."],["sycophant","SIK-uh-fuhnt","noun","Someone who says too-nice things to get something.","The sycophant told the coach every idea was brilliant to get more playing time.","","Sycophant sounds like “sick of fans”: too much fake praise."],["boondoggle","BOON-dog-uhl","noun","A project that wastes time and money.","The giant robot costume became a boondoggle that we never finished.","","Boondoggle sounds like “boo-boo doggle”: a big flop that wastes time."],["desiccate","DES-i-kayt","verb","To dry out completely.","The hot sun desiccated the leftover orange slice into a hard chip.","From Latin siccus, “dry.”","Desiccate sounds like “desert”: dried out like sand."],["ruminate","ROO-muh-nayt","verb","To think deeply about something for a long time.","I lay in bed to ruminate about what to name my new puppy.","From Latin ruminari, “to chew the cud.”","Ruminate: cows chew their food over and over, like thoughts."],["mawkish","MAW-kish","adjective","Too sweet and mushy in a way that feels fake.","The card had a mawkish poem with way too many hearts and kisses.","","Mawkish sounds like “mock kiss”: a fake, over-sweet smooch."],["aggrandize","uh-GRAN-dize","verb","To make something seem bigger or more important.","He tried to aggrandize his part in our group project, but he only drew one picture.","From French agrandir, “to make larger.”","Aggrandize has “grand” in it: make it seem grand."],["voluble","VOL-yuh-buhl","adjective","Talking a lot, easily and fast.","My voluble aunt talks all through dinner.","","Voluble sounds like volume: words at full volume, nonstop."],["peccadillo","pek-uh-DIL-oh","noun","A small, unimportant fault.","Eating one cookie before dinner is a peccadillo, not a crime.","From Spanish pecadillo, “little sin.”","Peccadillo sounds like armadillo: a tiny critter, a tiny fault."],["aberration","ab-uh-RAY-shuhn","noun","Something that is not normal or usual.","Getting a low grade was an aberration for such a strong student.","From Latin aberrare, “to wander away.”","Aberration sounds like “a bear-ation”: a bear at school is not normal."],["quiescent","kwee-ES-uhnt","adjective","Quiet and still, not active for now.","The puppy was quiescent at last, asleep in its bed.","From Latin quiescere, “to rest.”","Quiescent sounds like quiet: still and quiet for now."],["ignominy","IG-nuh-min-ee","noun","Public embarrassment or shame.","The team faced the ignominy of a 10 to 0 loss but still shook hands.","From Latin ignominia, “loss of good name.”","Ignominy has “nomin” like name: your name brought low in public."],["clandestine","klan-DES-tin","adjective","Done in secret.","We held a clandestine meeting to plan Dad’s surprise party.","From Latin clam, “secretly.”","Clandestine sounds like “clan in hiding”: a secret club meeting."],["vicarious","vy-KAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Felt by watching or hearing about someone else.","I got vicarious fun watching my brother ride the roller coaster.","From Latin vicarius, “substitute.”","Vicarious sounds like “via carrier”: you feel it via someone else."],["veneer","vuh-NEER","noun","A thin outer layer that hides what is under it.","The table had a wood veneer over cheap plastic.","","Veneer sounds like “veneer near”: a thin skin near the surface."],["perennial","puh-REN-ee-uhl","adjective","Lasting a long time or coming back year after year.","Pizza night is a perennial favorite in our house.","From Latin perennis, “lasting through the years.”","Per means through and annus means year: lasting year after year."],["gossamer","GOS-uh-mer","adjective","Very light, thin and delicate.","Dew sparkled on the gossamer threads of the spider web.","","Gossamer sounds like “goose summer”: as light as a fluffy goose feather."],["cathartic","kuh-THAR-tik","adjective","Making you feel better by letting out strong feelings.","Having a good cry after a rough day felt cathartic.","From Greek katharos, “pure.”","Cathartic: like letting steam out of a kettle, you feel lighter."],["myopic","my-OP-ik","adjective","Only thinking about now, not later; also not able to see far away.","Spending all my allowance on candy in one day was a myopic choice.","From Greek myops, “short-sighted.”","Myopic sounds like “my optic”: my eyes only see what’s up close."],["chary","CHAIR-ee","adjective","Cautious and slow to trust.","The stray cat was chary of strangers.","","Chary sounds like scary: careful because things seem scary."],["arcane","ar-KAYN","adjective","Known or understood by only a few people.","My cousin knows arcane facts about old video game glitches.","From Latin arcanus, “secret.”","Arcane sounds like “a cane”: secret like a wizard’s cane."],["acrimony","AK-ruh-moh-nee","noun","Bitter, angry feelings between people.","The friends settled their argument without any acrimony.","From Latin acrimonia, “sharpness.”","Acrimony sounds like “acid money”: bitter, sour feelings."],["countenance","KOWN-tuh-nuhns","noun","A person’s face or expression.","Her calm countenance helped the nervous kids relax.","","Countenance sounds like “count on her face”: a face’s look."],["vilify","VIL-uh-fy","verb","To say very bad, unfair things about someone.","It isn’t fair to vilify a kid for one mistake.","From Latin vilis, “cheap, worthless.”","Vilify has “vil” like villain: making someone look like a villain."],["commensurate","kuh-MEN-sher-it","adjective","Matching in size or amount.","Our reward was commensurate with how hard we worked.","","Commensurate has “measure”: they measure the same."],["fatuous","FACH-oo-uhs","adjective","Silly and not thought through.","The fatuous idea to sled down the stairs was quickly dropped.","From Latin fatuus, “foolish.”","Fatuous sounds like “fat-chance-us”: a silly idea with a fat chance of working."],["profligate","PROF-lih-git","adjective","Wildly wasteful.","My profligate cousin buys new sneakers every single week.","","Profligate sounds like “profit-gate”: all your money pours out the gate."],["recapitulate","ree-kuh-PICH-uh-layt","verb","To sum up the main points again.","At the end of class, the teacher will recapitulate the lesson.","From Latin capitulum, “chapter, heading.”","Recap is short for recapitulate: going over the main points."],["equivocal","ih-KWIV-uh-kuhl","adjective","Unclear, with more than one possible meaning.","His equivocal answer, “Maybe, maybe not,” left us unsure if he’d come.","From Latin aequus, “equal,” and vox, “voice.”","Equivocal starts like equal: two answers, equally possible."],["debase","dih-BAYS","verb","To lower the quality or value of something.","Cheating would debase our win and make it worth nothing.","","Debase: de means down: knocked down to the base."],["abjure","ab-JOOR","verb","To give up a belief or habit for good.","He decided to abjure video games during test week.","From Latin abjurare, “to deny on oath.”","Abjure sounds like “a jury”: promise the jury you’ll give it up for good."],["obfuscate","OB-fuh-skayt","verb","To make something confusing on purpose.","My brother tried to obfuscate who broke the lamp by talking really fast.","From Latin obfuscare, “to darken.”","Obfuscate sounds like “oh, fuzzy-cate”: making things fuzzy on purpose."],["pedantic","puh-DAN-tik","adjective","Too focused on tiny rules and details.","My pedantic friend corrected my grammar in the middle of a game of tag.","","Pedantic sounds like “pedant-tick”: ticking off every tiny mistake."],["avuncular","uh-VUHNG-kyuh-ler","adjective","Kind and friendly like an uncle.","The avuncular coach always had a joke and a kind word for us.","From Latin avunculus, “mother’s brother.”","Avuncular has “uncle” inside: kind like an uncle."],["aver","uh-VUR","verb","To state something as true with confidence.","My brother will aver that he saw a shooting star.","From Latin verus, “true.”","Aver sounds like “a very”: you say it’s very, very true."],["milieu","meel-YOO","noun","The place and people all around someone.","A noisy milieu of friends and music is where my sister feels happiest.","From French milieu, “middle.”","In French, milieu means middle place: what’s all around you."],["tractable","TRAK-tuh-buhl","adjective","Easy to manage or control.","The tractable puppy learned to sit after one try.","From Latin tractare, “to handle.”","Tractable sounds like tractor: easy to steer."],["implacable","im-PLAK-uh-buhl","adjective","Impossible to calm or change.","The hungry baby goat was implacable until it got its bottle.","From Latin placare, “to calm.”","Im-placable means can’t be placated: nothing can calm it down."],["exorbitant","ig-ZOR-bih-tuhnt","adjective","Much too high in price or amount.","The snack stand charged an exorbitant price: ten dollars for popcorn!","From Latin exorbitare, “to go off the track.”","Exorbitant sounds like “ex-orbit”: a price so high it’s out of orbit."],["flippant","FLIP-uhnt","adjective","Not serious when you should be.","Her flippant reply, “Who cares?”, made the class gasp.","","Flippant sounds like flip: flipping off something serious with a shrug."],["idyllic","eye-DIL-ik","adjective","Peaceful and perfect, like a picture.","The cabin by the lake, with birds singing and calm water, was idyllic.","From Greek eidyllion, “little picture.”","Idyllic sounds like “ideal”: a perfect, peaceful picture."],["garrulous","GAIR-uh-luhs","adjective","Talking a lot, often about small things.","The garrulous parrot chatted all through dinner and never stopped.","From Latin garrulus, “chattering.”","Garrulous sounds like “gargle-us”: words gargling out nonstop."],["ostensible","o-STEN-suh-buhl","adjective","Seeming true on the outside, but maybe not the real reason.","The ostensible reason for his visit was homework help, but he really came for snacks.","From Latin ostendere, “to show.”","Ostendere means to show in Latin: what’s shown, not what’s real."],["prescient","PRESH-uhnt","adjective","Seeming to know what will happen before it does.","My prescient friend said it would rain, and she brought an umbrella.","From Latin praescire, “to know beforehand.”","Pre means before and sci means know, like science: knowing before."],["sedentary","SED-n-tair-ee","adjective","Sitting a lot and not moving much.","Playing video games all day is very sedentary.","From Latin sedere, “to sit.”","Sedentary sounds like “sit-entary”: sitting, sitting, sitting."],["castigate","KAS-ti-gayt","verb","To scold someone harshly.","The coach castigated us for leaving the gym a mess.","","Castigate sounds like “cast at the gate”: a big scolding."],["repudiate","rih-PYOO-dee-ayt","verb","To firmly say no to something.","The team captain will repudiate the rumor that she’s quitting.","","Repudiate sounds like “re-pooh-pooh it”: you wave it away."],["wrought","RAWT","adjective","Made or shaped with care, often by hand.","The gate was wrought from iron by hand.","From Old English, an old past form of “work.”","Wrought is an old form of “worked”: made or shaped by hand."],["histrionic","his-tree-ON-ik","adjective","Overly dramatic, like an actor.","My brother gave a histrionic groan, falling to the floor, when asked to set the table.","From Latin histrio, “actor.”","Histrionic sounds like “history-on-stage”: drama like a play."],["debonair","deb-uh-NAIR","adjective","Stylish, charming and confident.","My grandpa looked debonair in his bow tie.","From French de bon air, “of good manner.”","Debonair sounds like “the bonny air”: cool, stylish and charming."],["unfathomable","un-FATH-uh-muh-buhl","adjective","Too deep or strange to understand.","The number of stars in the sky is unfathomable.","","A fathom measures sea depth: unfathomable is too deep to measure."],["ascribe","uh-SKRIBE","verb","To say that something is caused by or belongs to someone.","We ascribe the missing cookies to the dog.","From Latin ascribere, “to write in addition.”","Ascribe has “scribe” in it: write the blame on someone."],["covet","KUHV-it","verb","To want something that belongs to someone else.","I covet my friend’s glow-in-the-dark sneakers.","","Covet sounds like “love it”: wanting what someone else has."],["piquant","PEE-kuhnt","adjective","Pleasantly sharp in taste, or interesting.","The salsa was piquant, with just enough spice to make my lips tingle.","From French piquer, “to prick.”","Piquer means to prick in French: a flavor that pricks your tongue."],["erudite","AIR-yuh-dyte","adjective","Having a lot of knowledge from study.","The erudite tour guide knew the name of every bird in the woods.","From Latin erudire, “to teach, polish.”","Erudite sounds like “a-rude-bright”: not rude, just very bright and smart."],["tumultuous","too-MUL-choo-uhs","adjective","Loud, wild and full of confusion.","The last minute of the game was tumultuous, with everyone screaming.","From Latin tumultus, “uproar.”","Tumultuous sounds like tumble: everything tumbling wild."],["maelstrom","MAYL-struhm","noun","A wild, swirling mess, like a whirlpool.","The kitchen became a maelstrom of flour and eggs while we baked.","From early Dutch maelstroom, “grinding stream.”","Maelstrom ends with a storm sound: a wild, swirling storm."],["furtive","FUR-tiv","adjective","Sneaky and trying not to be noticed.","The dog gave a furtive look at the cake, then crept closer.","From Latin furtivus, “stolen, secret.”","Furtive sounds like “fur-tive”: a furry sneak tiptoeing quietly."],["phlegmatic","fleg-MAT-ik","adjective","Calm and hard to excite.","Our phlegmatic dog didn’t even blink during the thunderstorm.","From Greek phlegma, “phlegm,” once thought to make people calm.","Phlegmatic sounds like “flag-matic”: a flag on a windless day, totally calm."],["coddle","KOD-uhl","verb","To treat someone too gently.","Grandpa coddles the dog with extra treats and belly rubs.","","Coddle sounds like cuddle: too much gentle care."],["esoteric","es-uh-TAIR-ik","adjective","Understood by only a small group.","The game had esoteric rules that only the older kids understood.","From Greek esōterikos, “inner.”","Esoteric sounds like “so-secret-ic”: secret stuff only a few know."],["brackish","BRAK-ish","adjective","Slightly salty, like water where a river meets the sea.","Some fish live in brackish water near the coast.","","Brackish sounds like “salty-ish”: a little salty."],["cosset","KOS-it","verb","To pamper and protect.","We cosseted the sick puppy with soft blankets.","","Cosset sounds like “cozy set”: pampered and snug."],["consternation","kon-ster-NAY-shuhn","noun","Shock and worry about something unexpected.","To our consternation, the pool was closed on the hottest day.","","Consternation sounds like “constant frustration”: shocked and upset."],["recalcitrant","rih-KAL-sih-truhnt","adjective","Stubborn and refusing to obey.","The recalcitrant puppy would not come inside, no matter what.","From Latin recalcitrare, “to kick back.”","Recalcitrant sounds like “re-kick-it”: kicking back and refusing."],["raucous","RAW-kuhs","adjective","Loud and rough-sounding.","The raucous crowd cheered at the basketball game.","From Latin raucus, “hoarse.”","Raucous sounds like “rock-us”: loud, wild rock music."],["dour","DOOR","adjective","Stern and gloomy.","The dour museum guard finally cracked a smile.","","Dour sounds like “sour”: a sour, stern face."],["congenial","kuhn-JEEN-yuhl","adjective","Pleasant, friendly and easy to be around.","Our congenial camp counselor made everyone feel welcome.","","Congenial sounds like “genial genie”: a friendly, cheerful helper."],["euphemism","YOO-fuh-miz-uhm","noun","A gentle word used in place of a harsh one.","Saying the goldfish “went to sleep” was a euphemism to make it sound gentle.","From Greek euphēmismos, “speaking well.”","Eu means good in Greek: a good, gentle way to say something hard."],["bumptious","BUHMP-shuhs","adjective","Loud and too sure of yourself in an annoying way.","The bumptious new kid told everyone how to play the game.","","Bumptious sounds like “bump-tious”: bumping into everyone with bossiness."],["rhetoric","RET-er-ik","noun","The skill of using words to win people over.","My sister used clever rhetoric to convince Mom to get a puppy.","From Greek rhetor, “public speaker.”","Rhetoric sounds like “red-trick”: a word trick to win people over."],["redolent","RED-uh-luhnt","adjective","Full of a strong, pleasant smell, or bringing a memory to mind.","The kitchen was redolent of fresh cookies.","From Latin redolere, “to give off a smell.”","Redolent sounds like “red-smell-ent”: smelling bold and rich."],["byword","BY-wurd","noun","A person or thing known as the best example of something.","Our librarian is a byword for patience.","","Byword: the word people use for the best example."],["astringent","uh-STRIN-juhnt","adjective","Sharp or harsh in taste or tone.","Lemons have an astringent taste that makes your mouth pucker.","From Latin astringere, “to bind tight.”","Astringent sounds like “a string-ent”: tight and sharp."],["hackneyed","HAK-need","adjective","Used so often it feels stale.","“Once upon a time” felt hackneyed, so Zoe started her story with a bang.","From hackney, a horse anyone could rent.","Hackneyed sounds like “hacked”: used so much it’s worn out."],["facetious","fuh-SEE-shuhs","adjective","Joking at a time that calls for being serious.","His facetious answer, “I did my homework on the moon,” made the teacher sigh.","From Latin facetus, “witty.”","Facetious sounds like “face-tease-us”: a joking face when you should be serious."],["listless","LIST-lis","adjective","Having no energy or interest.","The heat made the dogs listless; they just lay there all day.","","List-less: no list of things you want to do."],["circumlocution","sur-kuhm-loh-KYOO-shuhn","noun","Using many words when a few would do.","His circumlocution made a simple yes take five minutes.","From Latin circum, “around,” and loqui, “to speak.”","Circum means around and locution means speaking: talking around and around."],["proclivity","proh-KLIV-ih-tee","noun","A natural leaning toward doing something.","My little sister has a proclivity for climbing on furniture.","From Latin proclivis, “sloping.”","Clivus means slope in Latin: you lean toward it, like a hill."],["circumspect","SUR-kuhm-spekt","adjective","Careful to think about risks before acting.","Be circumspect about what you share online.","From Latin circumspicere, “to look around.”","Circum means around and spect means look: look all around first."],["bombastic","bom-BAS-tik","adjective","Using big, showy words to sound important.","The villain in the play gave a bombastic speech about how great he was.","","Bombastic has “bomb” in it: big, explosive words."],["apposite","AP-uh-zit","adjective","Very fitting and to the point.","When we got lost, my friend asked the apposite question: “Who has the map?”","From Latin apponere, “to put near.”","Apposite sounds like “opposite”, but it means just right."],["perfunctory","per-FUNGK-tuh-ree","adjective","Done quickly, without care or interest.","He gave a perfunctory wave without even looking up from his game.","From Latin perfunctorius, “careless.”","Perfunctory sounds like “perfect, done”: just get it done, no care."],["abnegation","ab-nih-GAY-shuhn","noun","Giving up something you want, often for others.","Giving up the last slice so her brother could have it was an act of abnegation.","From Latin abnegare, “to deny.”","Abnegation has “neg” like “negative”: saying no to what you want."],["halcyon","HAL-see-uhn","adjective","Calm, happy and peaceful, often about the past.","Grandpa smiles when he talks about the halcyon summers of his childhood.","From Greek halkyōn, “kingfisher.”","The halcyon was a bird in old stories that kept the sea calm."],["droll","DROHL","adjective","Funny in an odd or dry way.","Grandma’s droll jokes make us laugh at dinner.","","Droll sounds like “troll”: a funny, odd little joke."],["verdant","VUR-duhnt","adjective","Green with lots of plants.","Our backyard is verdant after the spring rains.","","Verd is like vert, French for green: verdant means very green."],["mettle","MET-uhl","noun","Courage and strength to face something hard.","The tough hike tested our mettle, but we made it to the top.","","Mettle sounds like metal: strong and tough as steel inside."],["behest","bih-HEST","noun","A person’s order or request.","At my grandma’s behest, we all wore matching shirts.","","Behest sounds like “request”: an order or ask."],["diatribe","DY-uh-tryb","noun","A long, angry speech against something.","My brother went on a diatribe about broccoli.","","Diatribe sounds like “dia-tribe”: an angry speech to the whole tribe."],["vapid","VAP-id","adjective","Dull and empty of ideas.","The vapid TV show had no story at all.","From Latin vapidus, “flat, spoiled.”","Vapid sounds like vapor: thin and empty like air."],["transcend","tran-SEND","verb","To go beyond the usual limits.","Great music can transcend language, so everyone loves it.","From Latin transcendere, “to climb over.”","Trans means beyond, and scend means climb: climbing beyond limits."],["callow","KAL-oh","adjective","Young and without much experience.","As a callow first-time camper, I forgot my flashlight.","","Callow sounds like “shallow”: not much experience yet."],["discomfit","dis-KUHM-fit","verb","To make someone feel uneasy or embarrassed.","The surprise question discomfited the quiz show host.","","Discomfit sounds like “discomfort”: uneasy and embarrassed."],["decry","dih-KRY","verb","To speak out strongly against something.","The students decried the plan to cancel art class.","","Decry has “cry”: you cry out against it."],["sanguine","SANG-gwin","adjective","Cheerful and hopeful.","I’m sanguine that we’ll win the game tomorrow.","From Latin sanguis, “blood.”","Sanguine sounds like “sunny-guine”: sunny and hopeful."],["mellifluous","muh-LIF-loo-uhs","adjective","Sweet and smooth to hear.","The singer’s mellifluous voice made the whole room go quiet.","From Latin mel, “honey,” and fluere, “to flow.”","Mel means honey in Latin: a mellifluous voice flows like honey."],["acquiesce","ak-wee-ES","verb","To agree to something without arguing, even if you don’t love it.","My parents finally acquiesced and let us camp in the backyard.","From Latin acquiescere, “to rest, to be at peace.”","Acquiesce sounds like “a-quiet-yes”: a quiet yes."],["penury","PEN-yuh-ree","noun","Being very poor.","The family in the story lived in penury and could barely buy bread.","From Latin penuria, “need.”","Penury sounds like penny: down to your very last penny."],["recondite","REK-uhn-dyt","adjective","Hard to understand and known by few people.","My grandpa knows recondite facts about old coins that no one else knows.","From Latin reconditus, “hidden away.”","Recondite sounds like “recon”: hidden facts you have to scout for."],["ephemeral","ih-FEM-er-uhl","adjective","Lasting only a short time.","A soap bubble is ephemeral; it’s here, then pop, it’s gone.","From Greek ephēmeros, “lasting a day.”","Ephemeral comes from Greek for “lasting a day”: here today, gone tomorrow."],["perspicacious","pur-spih-KAY-shuhs","adjective","Quick to notice and understand things.","The perspicacious detective in my book noticed the muddy footprint right away.","From Latin perspicax, “sharp-sighted.”","Perspicacious has “spic” like spectacles: eyes that see everything."],["attenuate","uh-TEN-yoo-ayt","verb","To make thinner or weaker.","Thick curtains attenuate the noise from the street.","From Latin tenuis, “thin.”","Attenuate sounds like “a thin-uate”: make thinner."],["engender","en-JEN-der","verb","To cause or bring about a feeling or situation.","Sharing snacks can engender friendship on a long bus ride.","From Latin generare, “to create.”","Engender sounds like “and generate”: it generates a feeling."],["baroque","buh-ROHK","adjective","Very fancy and full of detail.","The cake had baroque swirls of frosting all over it.","","Baroque sounds like “bar-rock”: fancy swirls like a rock castle."],["laconic","luh-KON-ik","adjective","Using very few words.","When I asked how school was, my laconic brother just said, “Fine.”","From Greek Lakōnikos, “from Laconia,” where people spoke few words.","Laconic sounds like “lack-onic”: lacking words."],["apprise","uh-PRIZE","verb","To tell or inform someone about something.","Please apprise me of any changes to the practice schedule.","","Apprise sounds like “a prize”: give someone the news as a prize."],["solicitous","suh-LIS-ih-tuhs","adjective","Showing care and concern for someone.","The solicitous nurse kept checking if I was comfortable.","","Solicitous sounds like “so-lizzy-shus”: so caring and kind."],["dissemble","dih-SEM-buhl","verb","To hide your real feelings or thoughts.","She tried to dissemble her excitement, but she kept grinning.","","Dissemble sounds like “disguise”: hiding your real feelings."],["precipitous","prih-SIP-ih-tuhs","adjective","Very steep, or very sudden.","The trail had a precipitous drop, so we held on to the railing.","","Precipitous sounds like precipice, a cliff edge: steep and sudden."],["nefarious","nuh-FAIR-ee-uhs","adjective","Wicked or very bad.","The cartoon villain had a nefarious plan to steal all the cookies.","From Latin nefarius, “wicked.”","Nefarious sounds like “never fair”: a villain who never plays fair."],["hyperbole","hy-PUR-buh-lee","noun","A big exaggeration used for effect.","“I’m so hungry I could eat a horse” is hyperbole.","From Greek hyperbolē, “a throwing beyond.”","Hyper means over: way over the top, like a hyper kid."],["malinger","muh-LING-ger","verb","To pretend to be sick to get out of work.","My brother tried to malinger with a fake cough to skip his chores.","From French malingre, “sickly.”","Malinger sounds like “mall lingerer”: faking sick to hang out at the mall."],["badinage","bad-n-AHZH","noun","Playful, witty talk.","The badinage between the two friends made the long car trip fun.","From French badinage, “joking.”","Badinage sounds a bit like “banter”: both mean playful, joking talk."],["obdurate","OB-duh-rit","adjective","Stubborn and not willing to change.","My obdurate little brother would not eat even one pea.","From Latin obduratus, “hardened.”","Obdurate has “dur” like durable: hard and tough, won’t budge."],["rescind","rih-SIND","verb","To take back or cancel.","Mom will rescind my phone time if I don’t finish my chores.","From Latin rescindere, “to cut off.”","Rescind sounds like “re-send back”: taking it back."],["stalwart","STAWL-wert","adjective","Loyal, strong and dependable.","My stalwart best friend sticks with me no matter what.","","Stalwart sounds like “stall-wall”: standing solid like a wall."],["lugubrious","luh-GOO-bree-uhs","adjective","Looking or sounding sad in a gloomy, heavy way.","The droopy dog gave a lugubrious sigh when we left for school.","From Latin lugere, “to mourn.”","Lugubrious sounds like “lug-gloomy-us”: lugging around a heavy, sad face."],["censure","SEN-sher","noun","Strong, official disapproval.","The referee’s censure of the rough play quieted the field.","","Censure sounds like “censor”: a strong, official “not okay.”"],["pusillanimous","pyoo-suh-LAN-uh-muhs","adjective","Lacking courage; timid.","The pusillanimous knight in our story hid behind a tree.","From Latin pusillus, “very small,” and animus, “spirit.”","Pusillus means tiny in Latin: a person with only tiny courage."],["disheveled","dih-SHEV-uhld","adjective","Messy and untidy.","I arrived disheveled after the windy bike ride.","","Disheveled sounds like “dish-shoveled”: hair messy like a shoveled pile."],["doldrums","DOHL-druhmz","noun","A spell of feeling bored, sad or stuck.","A rainy week put us all in the doldrums.","","Doldrums sounds like “dull drums”: a slow, boring beat."],["disingenuous","dis-in-JEN-yoo-uhs","adjective","Pretending to be honest when you are not.","Saying “I didn’t see it” was disingenuous, since he was standing right there.","","Disingenuous: dis means not, ingenuous means honest: not honest."],["soporific","sop-uh-RIF-ik","adjective","Making you sleepy.","The boring movie was so soporific that Dad fell asleep.","From Latin sopor, “deep sleep.”","Soporific sounds like “sleepy-rific”: it makes you snooze."],["panache","puh-NASH","noun","Bold, confident style.","She did her cartwheel with such panache that everyone clapped.","From French panache, “plume of feathers.”","Panache sounds like mustache: twirling it with bold style."],["predilection","pred-uh-LEK-shuhn","noun","A special liking for something.","My cat has a predilection for sleeping in shoeboxes.","","Predilection sounds like “pre-delight”: something you delight in."],["dilatory","DIL-uh-tor-ee","adjective","Slow and causing delay.","My dilatory start on the project meant a busy weekend.","","Dilatory sounds like “delay-tory”: slow and late."],["dither","DITH-er","verb","To be unable to decide.","I dithered so long over ice cream flavors that the line grew.","","Dither sounds like “this or that?” over and over."],["dissonant","DIS-uh-nuhnt","adjective","Sounding harsh and not in harmony.","The two recorders played dissonant notes that hurt my ears.","From Latin dissonare, “to sound different.”","Dissonant: dis means apart, son means sound: sounds that clash."],["dapper","DAP-er","adjective","Neat and stylish.","My dog looked dapper in his tiny bow tie.","","Dapper sounds like “dap”: so stylish you get a fist bump."],["superlative","suh-PUR-luh-tiv","adjective","Of the very highest quality.","The pizza at that place is superlative.","","Superlative has “super” in it: the super, very best."],["proscribe","proh-SKRYB","verb","To forbid by a rule.","Our school proscribes phones during class.","From Latin proscribere, “to publish in writing.”","Proscribe has “scribe,” to write: a written rule that says no."],["austere","aw-STEER","adjective","Very plain and simple, with no extras.","The cabin was austere, with just a bed, a table, and a stove.","From Greek austēros, “harsh, dry.”","Austere sounds like “a stern”: plain and simple, no extras."],["bifurcate","BY-fer-kayt","verb","To split into two branches.","The trail will bifurcate at the big oak tree.","From Latin bifurcus, “two-forked.”","Bi means “two”: split into two."],["unctuous","UNGK-choo-uhs","adjective","Too sweet and smooth to seem honest.","The unctuous salesman kept saying, “You look amazing in that jacket.”","From Latin unctus, “oiled.”","Unctuous sounds like “ointment”: smooth and slippery."],["palliate","PAL-ee-ayt","verb","To make something hurt less without fixing it.","An ice pack helped palliate my bumped knee, but it still hurt.","From Latin palliare, “to cloak.”","Palliate sounds like pillow: a soft pillow makes pain feel softer."],["prevaricate","prih-VAIR-ih-kayt","verb","To avoid telling the whole truth.","When Mom asked about the broken vase, my brother prevaricated.","From Latin praevaricari, “to walk crookedly.”","Prevaricate comes from Latin for walking crooked: twisting around the truth."],["nondescript","non-dih-SKRIPT","adjective","Plain and not easy to describe or notice.","My lost backpack was so nondescript that ten others looked just like it.","","Non means not: nondescript means not easy to describe."],["sagacious","suh-GAY-shuhs","adjective","Wise and showing good judgment.","Our sagacious grandma always knows how to settle an argument.","From Latin sagax, “keen, wise.”","Sagacious sounds like sage, a wise old person."],["peripatetic","pair-uh-puh-TET-ik","adjective","Traveling from place to place.","My peripatetic aunt sends postcards from a new city every month.","From Greek peripatein, “to walk around.”","Peri means around: a peripatetic person walks around everywhere."],["accoutrements","uh-KOO-truh-muhnts","noun","Extra items or gear used for something.","She packed all the accoutrements for camping: lantern, cooler, and hammock.","","Accoutrements sounds like “a cool tree mints”: cool gear for the trip."],["obstreperous","uhb-STREP-er-uhs","adjective","Noisy and hard to control.","The obstreperous puppy barked and jumped at every visitor.","From Latin obstrepere, “to make a noise against.”","Obstreperous sounds like “obstacle-roarous”: loud, wild and in your way."],["doleful","DOHL-fuhl","adjective","Very sad.","The puppy gave me a doleful look when I left for school.","","Doleful sounds like “dull-full”: full of sadness."],["gauche","GOHSH","adjective","Awkward and clumsy in social ways.","It felt gauche to grab seconds before everyone else had eaten.","From French gauche, “left, clumsy.”","Gauche is French for left: like a clumsy left hand at the table."],["unassuming","un-uh-SOO-ming","adjective","Modest and not showing off.","The unassuming new kid turned out to be the best at chess.","","Unassuming: not assuming you’re special, so you stay humble."],["temerity","tuh-MER-ih-tee","noun","Bold daring that may be too much.","He had the temerity to eat my fries right in front of me.","From Latin temeritas, “rashness.”","Temerity sounds like “dare-ity”: daring, maybe too much."],["sumptuous","SUMP-choo-uhs","adjective","Rich, fancy and grand.","Grandma made a sumptuous feast for the holidays.","From Latin sumptus, “cost, expense.”","Sumptuous sounds like “sum of yum”: a big pile of yummy food."],["impudent","IM-pyuh-duhnt","adjective","Rude and not showing respect.","The impudent squirrel stole a chip right off my plate and stared at me.","From Latin pudere, “to be ashamed.”","Impudent sounds like “im-rude-ent”: bold and rude."],["abase","uh-BAYS","verb","To make yourself seem lower or less important.","He refused to abase himself by begging for a better seat.","","Abase has “base” in it: go down low."],["ennui","ahn-WEE","noun","A tired feeling of boredom.","After a week of rain and no screens, ennui filled the whole house.","From French ennui, “boredom.”","Ennui is French and sounds like “on-WEE”: no whee, just bored."],["beleaguered","bih-LEE-gerd","adjective","Worn down by lots of problems.","The beleaguered teacher had three fire drills and a broken projector in one day.","","Beleaguered once meant surrounded by an army: problems on every side."],["propriety","pruh-PRY-ih-tee","noun","Polite and proper behavior.","Grandma expects propriety at her table: napkins on laps and no burping.","From Latin proprietas, “ownership, fitness.”","Propriety sounds like proper: doing the polite, proper thing."],["bucolic","byoo-KOL-ik","adjective","Having to do with the peaceful countryside.","The farm was a bucolic scene of cows, sheep, and green hills.","From Greek boukolos, “cowherd.”","Bucolic sounds like “bu-cow-lic”: cows in the peaceful countryside."],["dilapidated","dih-LAP-i-day-tid","adjective","Old, broken and falling apart.","We fixed up the dilapidated shed and made it a clubhouse.","","Lapis is Latin for stone: like stones falling off an old wall."],["nonplussed","non-PLUST","adjective","So surprised that you don't know what to do or say.","I was nonplussed when my teacher showed up at my birthday party.","From Latin non plus, “no more.”","Non plus means no more in Latin: too stunned to say more."],["foible","FOY-buhl","noun","A small, harmless weakness or habit.","Dad’s funny foible is humming every time he cooks.","From French foible, “weak.”","Foible sounds like “feeble”: a small, harmless weak spot."],["brouhaha","BROO-hah-hah","noun","A noisy fuss over something small.","There was a big brouhaha when the class hamster got loose.","From French brouhaha.","Brouhaha sounds like “brew-ha-ha”: a noisy fuss."],["mercurial","mer-KYUR-ee-uhl","adjective","Changing moods quickly and often.","My baby sister is mercurial, happy one second and crying the next.","From Latin Mercurius, the quick messenger god of Roman myths.","Mercury is the fastest planet: mercurial moods zip around fast."],["bereft","bih-REFT","adjective","Lacking something or left without it.","The garden was bereft of flowers after the frost.","","Bereft sounds like “be left”: left without."],["effervescent","ef-er-VES-uhnt","adjective","Bubbly, or lively and full of fun.","Her effervescent laugh made the whole class smile.","From Latin effervescere, “to boil up.”","Effervescent sounds like “fizz-ervescent”: bubbly like soda."],["petulant","PECH-uh-luhnt","adjective","Grumpy and sulky like a spoiled child.","My petulant brother stomped off when he lost at checkers.","From Latin petulans, “impudent.”","Petulant sounds like “pet ant”: a tiny ant stomping its feet in a huff."],["artifice","AR-tuh-fis","noun","Clever tricks used to fool people.","The magician’s artifice kept us guessing all night.","From Latin artificium, “craft.”","Artifice has “art” in it: the art of tricks."]],"urdu":[["کام","KAAM","noun","Work or a task.","Aaj mujhe bohat kaam hai. (I have a lot of work today.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","Stay CALM and finish your work."],["سبق","SA-baq","noun","A lesson.","Aaj ka sabaq yaad kar lo. (Learn today’s lesson by heart.)","From Arabic.","Put the lesson in your SACK."],["رشک","RASHK","noun","Admiring envy, wishing you had what someone has.","Unki mehnat par rashk aata hai. (I admire and envy their hard work.)","From Persian.",""],["ڈاک خانہ","DAAK-khaa-na","noun","A post office.","Yeh khat daak khaane mein daal do. (Drop this letter at the post office.)","","Daak means mail, khaana means house: the mail house."],["محنت","MEH-nat","noun","Hard work.","Mehnat ka phal meetha hota hai. (The fruit of hard work is sweet.)","From Arabic.",""],["سادگی","SAA-da-gi","noun","Simplicity.","Unki saadgi sab ko pasand thi. (Everyone loved her simplicity.)","From Persian.","Saada means plain: simple and sweet."],["امتحان","im-ti-HAAN","noun","An exam or test.","Agle hafte mera imtihaan hai. (I have an exam next week.)","From Arabic.",""],["بجلی","BIJ-li","noun","Lightning, or electricity.","Raat ko bijli chamki. (Lightning flashed at night.)","","BIG LEE-ghtning in the sky."],["محبت","mo-HAB-bat","noun","Deep love.","Maa ki mohabbat sab se badi hai. (A mother’s love is the greatest.)","From Arabic.","“More hug, but…” there is always room for more love."],["خواب","KHWAAB","noun","A dream, while sleeping or a hope for the future.","Mera khwaab hai ke main doctor banoon. (My dream is to become a doctor.)","From Persian.",""],["چمچ","CHAM-mach","noun","A spoon.","Chamach se khao. (Eat with a spoon.)","From Persian.","“Chum-much” soup with a spoon."],["سورج","SOO-raj","noun","The sun.","Sooraj subah jaldi nikal aaya. (The sun came out early this morning.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","The sun SOARS up every morning."],["فکر","FIKR","noun","Worry; also thought.","Fikr na karo, main hoon na. (Don’t worry, I am here.)","From Arabic.",""],["دانت","DAANT","noun","A tooth.","Sone se pehle daant saaf karo. (Brush your teeth before bed.)","From Sanskrit.","Sounds like “dent”: do not dent your teeth."],["جہاز","ja-HAAZ","noun","A ship, or an airplane.","Hum jahaaz se Lahore gaye. (We went to Lahore by plane.)","From Arabic, through Persian.","JAZZ music playing on the airplane."],["پھپھو","PHUP-po","noun","Aunt; your father’s sister.","Phuppo ke ghar daawat hai. (There is a dinner party at Auntie’s house.)","","Phuppo, Papa’s sister: both start with a P sound."],["طوطا","TO-ta","noun","A parrot.","Hamara tota baatein karta hai. (Our parrot talks.)","","A parrot is TOTALLY chatty."],["اداسی","u-DAA-si","noun","Sadness, a low feeling.","Uski aankhon mein udaasi thi. (There was sadness in her eyes.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.",""],["معصوم","maa-SOOM","adjective","Innocent and pure.","Bachche ki masoom hansi pyaari thi. (The child’s innocent laugh was lovely.)","From Arabic.",""],["چین","CHAIN","noun","Ease and peace of mind.","Tumhari awaaz sun kar chain aa gaya. (Hearing your voice put me at ease.)","","Sounds like “chain”: a calm mind, not in chains."],["نیکی","NE-ki","noun","A good deed, goodness.","Neki kar, darya mein daal. (Do good and forget about it.)","From Persian.",""],["گرمی","GAR-mi","noun","Heat, or the hot season.","Is saal garmi bohat hai. (It is very hot this year.)","From Persian.","Too much heat makes you GRUMPY."],["رنگ","RANG","noun","A color.","Tumhara pasandeeda rang kaunsa hai? (What is your favorite color?)","From Persian.","The bell RANG in every color."],["مٹھائی","mi-THAA-ee","noun","Sweets, the kind shared at happy times.","Khushkhabri par mithaai baanti gayi. (Sweets were shared for the good news.)","From Sanskrit.","Mitha means sweet; mithaai is the box of sweets."],["ایمان","ee-MAAN","noun","Faith.","Imaan sachcha ho to dar nahin lagta. (With true faith, there is no fear.)","From Arabic.",""],["عزیز","a-ZEEZ","adjective","Dear and beloved.","Tum mujhe bohat azeez ho. (You are very dear to me.)","From Arabic.",""],["شادی","SHAA-di","noun","A wedding or marriage.","Agle mahine khala ki beti ki shaadi hai. (My cousin’s wedding is next month.)","From Persian.","Shaadi comes from a word for joy: a happy day."],["صابن","SAA-bun","noun","Soap.","Saabun se haath dhona. (Wash your hands with soap.)","From Arabic.","Soap “bun” in your hand."],["عشق","ISHQ","noun","Deep, passionate love.","Shayar ko apni zubaan se ishq tha. (The poet was in love with his language.)","From Arabic.",""],["گوشت","GOSHT","noun","Meat.","Eid par gosht pakta hai. (Meat is cooked on Eid.)","From Persian.","Sounds like “ghost”: the meat vanished from the plate."],["رات","RAAT","noun","Night.","Raat ko taare chamakte hain. (Stars shine at night.)","From Sanskrit.","A “rat” comes out at night."],["بھائی","bha-EE","noun","A brother; also a friendly word for any man.","Mera bhai mujh se bada hai. (My brother is older than me.)","From Sanskrit.","Ask your brother to “buy” you a treat."],["بھولنا","BHOOL-na","verb","To forget.","Apna bag mat bhoolna. (Do not forget your bag.)","","Forget the BULL? Never."],["کالا","KAA-la","adjective","Black.","Raat ko aasmaan kaala hota hai. (The sky is black at night.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A black COLA drink."],["بیٹی","BAY-ti","noun","Daughter.","Meri beti school jaati hai. (My daughter goes to school.)","From Sanskrit.","Beti, the “betty” of the house."],["دوپٹہ","du-PAT-ta","noun","A long light scarf worn with a shalwar kameez.","Ammi ka dupatta rang barangi hai. (Mom’s scarf is colorful.)","From Sanskrit.","Do means two: a scarf with two ends."],["پیٹ","PAYT","noun","The stomach or belly.","Mera pet bhar gaya. (My tummy is full.)","From Sanskrit.","Pat your “pet” belly after a big meal."],["دیوانہ","dee-WAA-na","adjective","Crazy, madly in love with someone or something.","Woh gaano ka deewana hai. (He is crazy about songs.)","From Persian.",""],["آئینہ","AA-ee-na","noun","A mirror.","Aaine mein apni shakal dekho. (Look at your face in the mirror.)","From Persian.","“I-na” mirror, I see me."],["باغ","BAAGH","noun","A garden.","Baagh mein bohat se phool hain. (There are many flowers in the garden.)","From Persian.","Fill a BAG with garden flowers."],["عزت","IZ-zat","noun","Respect and honor.","Bado ki izzat karo. (Respect your elders.)","From Arabic.","Izzat is what elders “is” owed: respect."],["جاگنا","JAAG-na","verb","To wake up or stay awake.","Subah jaldi jaagna achha hai. (Waking up early is good.)","From Sanskrit.","“Jog” after you wake up."],["بھوک","BHOOK","noun","Hunger.","Mujhe bahut bhook lagi hai. (I am very hungry.)","From Sanskrit.","Hungry? Read a cook “book.”"],["چائے","CHAA-e","noun","Tea, usually with milk and sugar.","Aao, chai pi lo. (Come, have some tea.)","From Chinese, through Persian.","Chai: the cup that brings everyone to the table."],["سستا","SAS-ta","adjective","Cheap or inexpensive.","Yeh dukaan bohat sasti hai. (This shop is very cheap.)","","SASSY shoppers love cheap deals."],["تسلی","ta-SAL-li","noun","Comfort and reassurance.","Ammi ki baat se tasalli hui. (Mom’s words comforted me.)","From Arabic.",""],["منزل","MAN-zil","noun","A destination. Also a floor of a building.","Hum apni manzil par pahunch gaye. (We reached our destination.)","From Arabic.","The MAN finally reached his goal."],["گاؤں","GAA-on","noun","A village.","Daada jaan ka gaaon bohat khoobsurat hai. (Grandpa’s village is very beautiful.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A village where cows GO ON grazing."],["ماشاءاللہ","maa-shaa-AL-lah","phrase","What God has willed; said to praise something good.","Mashallah, kitna pyaara ghar hai. (Mashallah, what a lovely home.)","From Arabic.",""],["سردی","SAR-di","noun","Cold, or the cold season.","Sardi mein garam kapre pehno. (Wear warm clothes in the cold.)","From Persian.","SURE-dee is cold, grab your sweater."],["ان شاء اللہ","in-SHAA-al-lah","phrase","If God wills; said about future plans.","Kal milenge, inshallah. (We will meet tomorrow, God willing.)","From Arabic.","Said for every plan, big or small."],["آداب","aa-DAAB","phrase","A polite greeting with respect.","Aadaab, Daadi jaan. (Respectful greetings, dear Grandma.)","From Arabic.","Aadaab: a little bow with your hand."],["خوشبو","khush-BOO","noun","A sweet smell or fragrance.","Ammi ke khaane ki khushboo aa rahi hai. (I can smell Ammi’s cooking.)","From Persian.","Khush means happy, boo means smell: a happy smell."],["سلام","sa-LAAM","noun","A greeting of peace.","Ghar aate hi sab ko salaam karo. (Greet everyone when you come home.)","From Arabic.",""],["بسم اللہ","bis-mil-LAH","phrase","In the name of God; said before starting something.","Bismillah karke khana shuru karo. (Say Bismillah and start eating.)","From Arabic.","The first word before the first bite."],["ہنسنا","HANS-na","verb","To laugh.","Bachche kahaani sun kar hans pade. (The children laughed at the story.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","HANS can not stop laughing."],["مسکراہٹ","mus-ku-RAA-hat","noun","A smile.","Tumhari muskurahat se din ban gaya. (Your smile made my day.)","","Muskurahat: a “musk” of a smile, spreading sweetness."],["ہاتھی","HAA-thi","noun","An elephant.","Chiriya ghar mein bara sa haathi tha. (There was a huge elephant at the zoo.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","An elephant is HAUGHTY and huge."],["پتھر","PATH-thar","noun","A stone or rock.","Darya mein chikne patthar hain. (There are smooth stones in the river.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A stone PATH in the garden."],["ملاقات","mu-laa-QAAT","noun","A meeting, a visit with someone.","Aapse mil kar achhi mulaqaat rahi. (It was a lovely meeting with you.)","From Arabic.",""],["ہمت","HIM-mat","noun","Courage and the will to try.","Himmat na haaro, phir koshish karo. (Don’t lose courage, try again.)","From Arabic.",""],["پھول","PHOOL","noun","A flower.","Baagh mein laal phool khile hain. (Red flowers have bloomed in the garden.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A flower is a pool of color."],["آرزو","aar-ZOO","noun","A heartfelt wish or desire.","Uski aarzoo poori ho gayi. (Her wish came true.)","From Persian.","I wish to go to the zoo: aar-ZOO."],["رحم","REHM","noun","Mercy and compassion.","Jaanwaron par rehm karo. (Be kind to animals.)","From Arabic.",""],["شام","SHAAM","noun","Evening.","Shaam ko park chalte hain. (Let’s go to the park in the evening.)","From Persian.","At “shaam,” the sky turns calm."],["تمیز","ta-MEEZ","noun","Good manners.","Bachon ko tameez sikhaao. (Teach the children good manners.)","From Arabic.","Good manners never “tease.”"],["بال","BAAL","noun","Hair.","Ammi mere baal bana rahi hain. (Mom is doing my hair.)","From Sanskrit.","Hair in a “ball” bun."],["سکون","su-KOON","noun","Peace and calm of the heart.","Namaaz ke baad dil ko sukoon milta hai. (The heart finds peace after prayer.)","From Arabic.","Like a cocoon: warm, safe and calm."],["آدھا","AA-dha","adjective","Half.","Roti aadhi aadhi baant lo. (Split the roti in half.)","From Sanskrit.",""],["چڑیا","CHI-ri-ya","noun","A small bird, like a sparrow.","Khirki par ek chiriya baithi hai. (A little bird is sitting at the window.)","","CHIRP-ia: the little bird that chirps."],["دیکھنا","DEKH-na","verb","To see or to look.","Dekho, aasmaan mein sitaare! (Look, stars in the sky!)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","DECK the halls, then look."],["کپڑے","KAP-re","noun","Clothes.","Eid ke naye kapre aa gaye. (The new Eid clothes have arrived.)","From Sanskrit.","Clothes in a “cupboard.”"],["ذائقہ","ZAA-i-qa","noun","Taste or flavor.","Naani ke khaane ka zaiqa laajawaab hai. (Grandma’s food tastes amazing.)","From Arabic.","Zaiqa: a “zing” on your tongue."],["غصہ","GHUS-sa","noun","Anger.","Ghussa thanda karo aur baat karo. (Calm your anger and talk.)","From Arabic.","Ghussa sounds like “gusher”: anger gushing out."],["کرم","ka-RAM","noun","Grace, a generous kindness or blessing.","Allah ka karam hai ke sab khairiyat se hain. (By God’s grace, everyone is well.)","From Arabic.",""],["مور","MOR","noun","A peacock.","Mor ne apne rangeen par phailaaye. (The peacock spread its colorful feathers.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A peacock always wants MORE attention."],["شلوار","shal-WAAR","noun","Loose trousers worn with a qameez.","Shalwar qameez pehen lo. (Put on your shalwar kameez.)","From Persian.","Comfy trousers, no “war” with tight jeans."],["گھوڑا","GHO-ra","noun","A horse.","Ghora tez daurta hai. (The horse runs fast.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","GO-ra! Giddy up, horse."],["وعدہ","WAA-da","noun","A promise.","Waada karo, jaldi wapas aaoge. (Promise you will come back soon.)","From Arabic.",""],["احساس","eh-SAAS","noun","A feeling or awareness.","Mujhe apni ghalti ka ehsaas hai. (I realize my mistake.)","From Arabic.",""],["جان","JAAN","noun","Life or soul; also a loving word for a dear one.","Meri jaan, khana kha lo. (My dear, please eat.)","From Persian.","Call your dear one Jaan: “my life”."],["پیسہ","PAI-sa","noun","Money. Also a small coin.","Is kitaab ke liye paise jama karo. (Save money for this book.)","","PAY-sa: you pay with money."],["بہار","ba-HAAR","noun","Spring, the season of flowers.","Bahaar mein har taraf phool khilte hain. (In spring, flowers bloom everywhere.)","From Persian.","Spring flowers come back BY HER door."],["حیا","ha-YAA","noun","Modesty and a sense of decency.","Haya insaan ka zevar hai. (Modesty is a person’s jewel.)","From Arabic.",""],["بہن","be-HEN","noun","A sister.","Meri behen bahut pyaari hai. (My sister is very sweet.)","From Sanskrit.","A sister clucks over you like a mother hen."],["تشریف","tash-REEF","noun","A polite word for someone’s presence, used to welcome or seat a guest.","Tashreef rakhiye. (Please have a seat.)","From Arabic.",""],["نزاکت","na-ZAA-kat","noun","Delicate grace and gentleness.","Uski baaton mein nazaakat thi. (There was a gentle grace in her words.)","From Persian.",""],["چاول","CHAA-wal","noun","Rice.","Chawal pak gaye hain. (The rice is cooked.)","From Sanskrit.","“Chow all” the rice."],["جنگل","JAN-gal","noun","A forest or jungle.","Jangal mein bohat se jaanwar rehte hain. (Many animals live in the forest.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","Jangal sounds just like jungle."],["پیالہ","pya-LAA","noun","A bowl or cup.","Ek pyaala kheer de do. (Give me a bowl of rice pudding.)","From Persian.","“Pal-a” bowl to share with your pal."],["فخر","FAKHR","noun","Pride, in a good way.","Mujhe apne khandaan par fakhr hai. (I am proud of my family.)","From Arabic.",""],["قمیض","qa-MEEZ","noun","A long shirt or tunic.","Yeh qameez bahut sundar hai. (This shirt is very pretty.)","From Arabic.","Sounds like “chemise,” another word for shirt."],["استاد","us-TAAD","noun","A teacher, or a master of a skill.","Hamare ustaad bohat mehnati hain. (Our teacher works very hard.)","From Persian.","A teacher who can STUD-y anything."],["بالٹی","BAAL-ti","noun","A bucket.","Baalti mein paani bharo. (Fill the bucket with water.)","From Portuguese.","A bucket with a “bolt” handle."],["ادا","a-DAA","noun","A charming style or graceful way of doing something.","Nani ki har ada pyaari hai. (Everything Grandma does is charming.)","From Arabic.",""],["دوستی","dos-TI","noun","Friendship.","Hamari dosti bachpan se hai. (Our friendship goes back to childhood.)","From Persian.",""],["موسم","MAU-sam","noun","Weather or season.","Aaj mausam bohat achha hai. (The weather is very nice today.)","From Arabic.","Awesome weather, MAU-SAM style."],["سسرال","sus-RAAL","noun","Your in-laws’ home or family.","Woh Eid par sasuraal gayi. (She went to her in-laws for Eid.)","From Sanskrit.","Sasur is father-in-law; sasuraal is his whole household."],["بستر","BIS-tar","noun","A bed or bedding.","Bachay bistar mein ghus gaye. (The kids snuggled into bed.)","From Persian.","Lie in bed and look at the “star.”"],["بھروسہ","bha-RO-sa","noun","Trust.","Mujhe tum par poora bharosa hai. (I trust you completely.)","",""],["پرندہ","pa-RIN-da","noun","A bird.","Subah parinde gaana gaate hain. (Birds sing in the morning.)","From Persian.","A bird flies PAR (over) the RIND of the earth."],["ہزار","ha-ZAAR","noun","A thousand.","Main ne hazaar baar kaha! (I said it a thousand times!)","From Persian.","Hazaar sounds like “bazaar,” full of a thousand things."],["یقین","ya-QEEN","noun","Belief and certainty.","Mujhe yaqeen hai tum kar loge. (I am sure you will do it.)","From Arabic.",""],["احترام","eh-te-RAAM","noun","Respect and honor.","Baron ka ehtiraam karo. (Respect your elders.)","From Arabic.",""],["عیدی","EE-di","noun","Money or gifts given to children on Eid.","Naana ne sab bachon ko eidi di. (Grandpa gave Eid money to all the kids.)","From Arabic.","Eid plus a little extra: eidi."],["ملن","mi-LAN","noun","A meeting or coming together.","Eid par sab ka milan hota hai. (Everyone comes together on Eid.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.",""],["چھت","CHHAT","noun","A roof or a flat rooftop.","Garmiyon mein hum chhat par sote the. (In summer we slept on the rooftop.)","From Sanskrit.","“Chat” with cousins on the rooftop at night."],["سفر","sa-FAR","noun","A journey or trip.","Hamara safar bohat mazedaar tha. (Our trip was so much fun.)","From Arabic.","A safari is a journey SO FAR away."],["وقار","wa-QAAR","noun","Dignity and graceful bearing.","Dadi ke chehre par waqaar tha. (There was dignity in Grandma’s face.)","From Arabic.",""],["بولنا","BOL-na","verb","To speak or to say.","Zara Urdu mein bolo. (Say it in Urdu, please.)","","BOLD people speak up."],["کھڑکی","KHIR-ki","noun","A window.","Khirki se thandi hawa aa rahi hai. (A cool breeze comes through the window.)","","Look out the window and “kick” up your feet."],["دوائی","da-WAA-ee","noun","Medicine.","Waqt par dawaai le lo. (Take your medicine on time.)","From Arabic.","Dawaai helps you “do well.”"],["کتاب","ki-TAAB","noun","A book.","Yeh kitaab bohat dilchasp hai. (This book is very interesting.)","From Arabic.","KEEP a TAB in your book."],["زمین","za-MEEN","noun","The ground, land, or the Earth.","Patte zameen par gire hue hain. (The leaves are lying on the ground.)","From Persian.","The ground is where the ZEBRA MEETS its feet."],["ارمان","ar-MAAN","noun","A cherished hope or dream.","Beti ki shaadi maa ka armaan tha. (Her daughter’s wedding was the mother’s dream.)","From Persian.",""],["بادل","BAA-dal","noun","A cloud.","Aasmaan par kaale baadal hain. (There are dark clouds in the sky.)","","A big BOTTLE of rain floating up high."],["چینی","CHEE-ni","noun","Sugar.","Chai mein kitni cheeni? (How much sugar in your tea?)","","Cheeni sounds like “cheeky”: sweet and a little naughty."],["خاموشی","khaa-MO-shi","noun","Silence, quietness.","Kabhi kabhi khamoshi bhi jawaab hoti hai. (Sometimes silence is also an answer.)","From Persian.",""],["خواہش","KHWAA-hish","noun","A wish or desire.","Tumhari kya khwahish hai? (What is your wish?)","From Persian.",""],["نیند","NEEND","noun","Sleep.","Mujhe neend aa rahi hai. (I am feeling sleepy.)","From Sanskrit.","You “need” your sleep."],["مہمان نوازی","meh-MAAN na-WAA-zi","phrase","Hospitality, warmly welcoming guests.","Unki mehmaan nawaazi mashhoor hai. (Their hospitality is famous.)","From Persian.",""],["خریدنا","kha-REED-na","verb","To buy.","Hum bazaar se kitaabein khareedenge. (We will buy books at the market.)","From Persian.","CAR REED: buy a car indeed."],["گننا","GIN-na","verb","To count.","Chalo, das tak ginte hain. (Come, let us count to ten.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","BEGIN-na counting: one, two, three."],["درخت","da-RAKHT","noun","A tree.","Is darakht par aam lagte hain. (Mangoes grow on this tree.)","From Persian.","The tree has a RACK of branches."],["تقدیر","taq-DEER","noun","Destiny, the divine plan.","Mehnat se taqdeer badalti hai. (Hard work changes destiny.)","From Arabic.",""],["قیمت","QEE-mat","noun","Price or value.","Is jacket ki qeemat kya hai? (What is the price of this jacket?)","From Arabic.","KEEP it MAT-ching the price tag."],["تولیہ","TAU-li-ya","noun","A towel.","Nahaane ke baad tauliya lo. (Take a towel after your bath.)","From Portuguese.","Tauliya sounds like “towel-ya.”"],["کھیلنا","KHEL-na","verb","To play.","Bachay baahar khel rahe hain. (The kids are playing outside.)","From Sanskrit.",""],["سننا","SUN-na","verb","To listen or to hear.","Dhyaan se suno. (Listen carefully.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","SOON-a, listen to what I say."],["سوال","sa-WAAL","noun","A question.","Kya main ek sawaal pooch sakta hoon? (May I ask a question?)","From Arabic.","SO, WALL-y, what is your question?"],["دروازہ","dar-WAA-za","noun","A door.","Darwaaza khol do, mehmaan aaye hain. (Open the door, guests are here.)","From Persian.","“Door-waaza”: it starts with the same sound as door."],["ڈھونڈنا","DHOONDH-na","verb","To search or look for.","Main apni chaabi dhoondh rahi hoon. (I am looking for my key.)","","Search the DUNES for treasure."],["شوخی","SHO-khi","noun","Playfulness and cheeky charm.","Uski aankhon mein shokhi thi. (There was a playful sparkle in her eyes.)","From Persian.",""],["ناراضگی","naa-RAA-zi-gi","noun","Being upset or annoyed with someone.","Choti si naraazgi ko dil mein mat rakho. (Don’t hold a small hurt in your heart.)","From Persian.",""],["آپ","AAP","noun","The polite, respectful word for you.","Aap kaise hain? (How are you?)","","Use Aap for elders: the polite “you”."],["غرور","gha-ROOR","noun","Pride or arrogance.","Ghuroor achhi baat nahin. (Arrogance is not a good thing.)","From Arabic.","Sounds like a lion’s “roar”: proud and loud."],["شکریہ","SHUK-ri-ya","phrase","Thank you.","Chai ke liye shukriya. (Thank you for the tea.)","From Arabic.","Shukriya, “sugar” for the person who helped you."],["آنکھ","AANKH","noun","An eye.","Uski aankhein bahut khoobsurat hain. (Her eyes are very beautiful.)","From Sanskrit.","“Ankh”: an eye that sees everything."],["دسترخوان","das-tar-KHWAAN","noun","A cloth spread for a meal; the family meal itself.","Sab dastarkhwaan par baith jaao. (Everyone, sit down at the meal.)","From Persian.","The cloth where the whole family sits to eat."],["گائے","GAA-ye","noun","A cow.","Gaaye khet mein ghaas kha rahi hai. (The cow is eating grass in the field.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A cow GUY eating grass."],["ہاتھ","HAATH","noun","A hand.","Khaane se pehle haath dho lo. (Wash your hands before eating.)","From Sanskrit.","“Hot” hands from a warm cup of chai."],["ایمانداری","ee-maan-DAA-ri","noun","Honesty.","Imaandaari sab se achhi policy hai. (Honesty is the best policy.)","From Arabic and Persian.",""],["شوق","SHAUQ","noun","A hobby, keen interest or eagerness.","Mujhe kitaabein parhne ka shauq hai. (I love reading books.)","From Arabic.","Shauq sounds like “shock”: so eager it shocks you."],["مہربانی","meh-er-BAA-ni","noun","Kindness; also used to say please or thank you kindly.","Aap ki bahut meherbaani. (That is very kind of you.)","From Persian.","Meher means kindness, like the warmth of the sun."],["دفتر","DAF-tar","noun","An office.","Abbu daftar se der se aaye. (Dad came home late from the office.)","From Persian.","DAFT to be late to the office."],["شان","SHAAN","noun","Grandeur, pride and glory.","Mehmaan hamare ghar ki shaan hain. (Guests are the pride of our home.)","From Arabic.",""],["برکت","BAR-kat","noun","Blessing and abundance that keeps growing.","Is ghar mein bahut barkat hai. (This home is full of blessings.)","From Arabic.","A bar of gold that never runs out."],["نصیب","na-SEEB","noun","Fate, what is written for you.","Jo naseeb mein hai, woh mil kar rahega. (What is meant for you will come to you.)","From Arabic.",""],["لکھنا","LIKH-na","verb","To write.","Apna naam yahaan likho. (Write your name here.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","I LIKE to write."],["پچھتاوا","pach-TAA-wa","noun","Regret.","Baad mein pachtaawa na ho, abhi parh lo. (Study now so you don’t regret it later.)","From Hindi.",""],["گزارش","gu-ZAA-rish","noun","A polite request.","Meri guzaarish hai ke aap zaroor aayein. (I humbly request that you please come.)","From Persian.",""],["وقت","WAQT","noun","Time.","Khaane ka waqt ho gaya. (It is time to eat.)","From Arabic.","Waqt to “walk,” waqt to talk."],["بخار","bu-KHAAR","noun","A fever.","Bache ko bukhaar hai. (The child has a fever.)","From Arabic, through Persian.","“Boo! Hot!” A fever makes you burn."],["جذبہ","JAZ-ba","noun","A strong feeling or passion.","Unke jazbe ko salaam. (Hats off to their spirit.)","From Arabic.",""],["ناز","NAAZ","noun","Pride and fond delight; also loving fuss.","Maa ko apni beti par naaz hai. (Mom is proud of her daughter.)","From Persian.",""],["گلابی","gu-LAA-bi","adjective","Pink.","Bahaar mein gulaabi phool khilte hain. (Pink flowers bloom in spring.)","From Persian.","Pink like a GULAB, a rose."],["حساب","hi-SAAB","noun","Math, or an account of money.","Mujhe hisaab ka sabaq pasand hai. (I like the math lesson.)","From Arabic.",""],["مہینہ","ma-HEE-na","noun","A month.","Ramzan ka maheena aa raha hai. (The month of Ramzan is coming.)","From Persian.","The moon (maah) gives us the month."],["تہذیب","teh-ZEEB","noun","Culture, good manners and refined ways of living.","Hamari tehzeeb mein baron ki izzat hai. (Respecting elders is part of our culture.)","From Arabic.",""],["قلم","QA-lam","noun","A pen.","Kya main tumhara qalam le sakti hoon? (May I borrow your pen?)","From Arabic.","Write CALM words with your pen."],["چچا","CHA-cha","noun","Uncle; your father’s younger brother.","Chacha jaan cricket khelte hain. (Uncle plays cricket.)","From Sanskrit.","Chacha loves to dance the cha-cha."],["الفت","UL-fat","noun","Affection and fondness.","Dono mein gehri ulfat thi. (There was deep affection between the two.)","From Arabic.",""],["شہر","SHE-har","noun","A city.","Karachi ek bara shehar hai. (Karachi is a big city.)","From Persian.","SHARE a big city with millions."],["نمک","na-MAK","noun","Salt.","Daal mein thoda namak daalo. (Add a little salt to the lentils.)","From Persian.","“No muck”: just a pinch of clean white salt."],["پوچھنا","POOCH-na","verb","To ask.","Raasta kisi se pooch lo. (Ask someone for directions.)","","Ask the POOCH where the ball went."],["نیلا","NEE-la","adjective","Blue.","Aasmaan neela hai. (The sky is blue.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","The NILE river is blue."],["سال","SAAL","noun","A year.","Naya saal mubarak! (Happy New Year!)","From Persian.","A whole year to “sail” around the sun."],["پہاڑ","pa-HAAR","noun","A mountain.","Hum garmiyon mein pahaar par gaye. (We went to the mountains in summer.)","","PA climbs HARD up the mountain."],["گھڑی","GHA-ri","noun","A watch or clock.","Meri ghari paanch minute aage hai. (My watch is five minutes fast.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A clock in a GARRY's pocket."],["ہمدردی","ham-DAR-di","noun","Sympathy, sharing someone’s pain.","Usne bohat hamdardi se baat suni. (She listened with great sympathy.)","From Persian.","Ham means same, dard means pain: same pain."],["لال","LAAL","adjective","Red.","Us ne laal kapre pehne hain. (She is wearing red clothes.)","From Persian.","LA LA, red like a ruby."],["صبر","SABR","noun","Patience, staying calm while you wait or suffer.","Sabr karo, sab theek ho jayega. (Be patient, everything will be fine.)","From Arabic.","Sabr sounds like “sober”: stay calm and steady."],["امی","AM-mi","noun","Mom; a loving word for mother.","Ammi, mujhe bhook lagi hai. (Mom, I am hungry.)","","Sounds like “Mommy” without the M at the front."],["الماری","al-MAA-ri","noun","A cupboard or wardrobe.","Kapre almaari mein rakh do. (Put the clothes in the wardrobe.)","From Portuguese.","Almaari sounds like “armoire,” a tall cupboard."],["اعتبار","ai-te-BAAR","noun","Trust and faith in someone’s word.","Ek baar aitbaar toot jaye to mushkil hai. (Once trust breaks, it is hard.)","From Arabic.",""],["ملنا","MIL-na","verb","To meet or to get.","Kal naani se milne jaayenge. (We will go meet Grandma tomorrow.)","From Sanskrit.","Families “mill” around when they meet."],["مبارک","mu-BAA-rak","adjective","Blessed; used to say congratulations.","Eid mubarak! (Happy Eid!)","From Arabic.","Mubarak brings a “bar” of blessings."],["خاندان","khaan-DAAN","noun","A family, including the wider family.","Eid par saara khandaan jama hota hai. (The whole family gathers on Eid.)","From Persian.","Khan, Dan and everyone else: the whole family."],["ستارہ","si-TAA-ra","noun","A star.","Raat ko aasmaan mein bohat se sitaare the. (There were many stars in the sky at night.)","From Persian.","A star plays the SITAR in the sky."],["پکانا","pa-KAA-na","verb","To cook.","Aaj main khana pakaaungi. (Today I will cook.)","From Sanskrit.","“Pack” the pot with spices and cook."],["ٹوپی","TO-pi","noun","A cap or hat.","Abbu ne namaaz ki topi pehni. (Dad put on his prayer cap.)","","Topi sits on “top” of your head."],["اجازت","i-JAA-zat","noun","Permission.","Kya mujhe jaane ki ijaazat hai? (May I be excused?)","From Arabic.",""],["چراغ","chi-RAAGH","noun","An oil lamp; a light.","Chiraagh ki roshni mein kahaani suni. (We heard a story by lamplight.)","From Persian.","Aladdin rubbed a chiraagh."],["التجا","il-ti-JAA","noun","A humble plea or appeal.","Uski iltija sun kar sab ka dil pighal gaya. (Hearing his plea, everyone’s heart melted.)","From Arabic.",""],["چلنا","CHAL-na","verb","To walk, or to go.","Chalo, park tak paidal chalte hain. (Come, let us walk to the park.)","","CHAL-o! Let us go."],["نقشہ","NAQ-sha","noun","A map.","Naqshe mein dekho, shehar kahaan hai. (Look on the map to see where the city is.)","From Arabic, through Persian.","KNOCK on the map to find your way."],["لفظ","LAFZ","noun","A word.","Aaj ka lafz yaad karo. (Remember today’s word.)","From Arabic.","A funny word makes you LAUGH-z."],["معافی","maa-FI","noun","Forgiveness, or an apology.","Main maafi chahta hoon. (I am sorry, please forgive me.)","From Arabic.",""],["پڑھنا","PARH-na","verb","To read or to study.","Roz thoda parhna chahiye. (You should read a little every day.)","From Sanskrit.","Read in the “park.”"],["کتب خانہ","KU-tub-khaa-na","noun","A library.","Main har hafte kutub khaane jaati hoon. (I go to the library every week.)","From Arabic and Persian.","Kutub means books, khaana means house: a house of books."],["دن","DIN","noun","A day, or daytime.","Aaj ka din bohat achha guzra. (Today was a very good day.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","After a long day, it is DIN-ner time."],["دل","DIL","noun","The heart.","Mera dil khush ho gaya. (My heart became happy.)","From Persian.","A “dill” pickle with a heart shape."],["گھر","GHAR","noun","A home or a house.","Shaam ko sab ghar aa jaate hain. (Everyone comes home in the evening.)","From Sanskrit.","Picture a car parked at home in the “gar-age.”"],["شیر","SHER","noun","A lion or tiger.","Sher jangal ka baadshah hai. (The lion is king of the jungle.)","From Persian.","Sher Khan, the tiger in The Jungle Book."],["دلہن","dul-HAN","noun","A bride.","Dulhan laal jode mein bahut khoobsurat lag rahi thi. (The bride looked beautiful in red.)","","The dulhan holds the groom’s “hand.”"],["نوکری","NAU-kri","noun","A job.","Bhai ko nayi naukri mil gayi. (Brother got a new job.)","From Persian.","KNOCK-ree on the door for a new job."],["نانی","NAA-ni","noun","Grandmother on your mother’s side.","Naani kahaani sunaati hain. (Grandma tells stories.)","From Sanskrit.","Sounds like “Nanny,” the one who looks after everyone."],["نہانا","na-HAA-na","verb","To take a bath or shower.","Jaldi nahaa lo, der ho rahi hai. (Bathe quickly, it is getting late.)","From Sanskrit.","“Nah! Ha!” says the child who does not want a bath."],["انسانیت","in-saa-NI-yat","noun","Humanity and human kindness.","Insaaniyat sab se bara mazhab hai. (Humanity is the greatest faith.)","From Arabic.",""],["درد","DARD","noun","Pain or ache.","Ghutne mein dard hai. (My knee hurts.)","From Persian.","A “dart” of pain."],["خوش آمدید","khush-aam-DEED","phrase","Welcome.","Hamare ghar mein khush aamdeed. (Welcome to our home.)","From Persian.",""],["تنخواہ","tan-KHWAAH","noun","Salary or wages.","Pehli tankhwaah par sab ko mithaai khilaayi. (She treated everyone to sweets with her first salary.)","From Persian.",""],["انکساری","in-ki-SAA-ri","noun","Humility, not thinking too highly of yourself.","Bare log inkisaari se baat karte hain. (Great people speak with humility.)","From Arabic.",""],["ادب","A-dab","noun","Respect and good manners; also literature.","Bachon ko adab se baat karni chahiye. (Children should speak with respect.)","From Arabic.",""],["سونا","SO-na","verb","To sleep.","Ab so jaao, raat ho gayi. (Go to sleep now, it is night.)","From Sanskrit.","“So, nap” time: go to sleep."],["حسین","ha-SEEN","adjective","Lovely and beautiful.","Kya haseen shaam hai. (What a lovely evening.)","From Arabic.","Most beautiful thing I have “seen”: ha-SEEN."],["جی","JEE","phrase","Yes, said with respect; also added to show respect.","Jee Ammi, abhi aata hoon. (Yes Mom, coming right now.)","","Gee, yes! Always polite."],["باورچی","BAA-war-chi","noun","A cook or chef.","Hamare baawarchi ki biryani mashhoor hai. (Our cook’s biryani is famous.)","Turkish origin.","Baawarchi khaana is the kitchen: the cook’s place."],["خوبصورت","khoob-SOO-rat","adjective","Beautiful.","Aaj tum bohat khoobsurat lag rahi ho. (You look very beautiful today.)","From Persian.","Khoob means very, surat means face: a very lovely face."],["خلوص","khu-LOOS","noun","Sincerity, a pure and honest heart.","Usne poore khuloos se madad ki. (She helped with complete sincerity.)","From Arabic.",""],["جدائی","ju-DAA-i","noun","Separation from a loved one.","Bhai ki judaai mushkil thi. (Being apart from my brother was hard.)","From Persian.",""],["شاگرد","shaa-GIRD","noun","A student or pupil.","Ustaad ne shaagirdon ko kahaani sunaai. (The teacher told the students a story.)","From Persian.","A student must GIRD up to learn."],["اطمینان","it-mee-NAAN","noun","Reassurance and peace of mind.","Report dekh kar itminaan hua. (Seeing the report gave me peace of mind.)","From Arabic.",""],["روٹی","RO-ti","noun","Flat bread made on a griddle.","Ammi garam garam roti bana rahi hain. (Mom is making hot rotis.)","From Sanskrit.","Roti goes round and round, like a wheel that rolls."],["رشتہ","RISH-ta","noun","A relationship or family tie; also a marriage proposal.","Hamara rishta bahut purana hai. (Our bond is very old.)","From Persian.","Rishta is a thread that ties two families together."],["سبزی","SAB-zi","noun","Vegetables, or a cooked vegetable dish.","Aaj khaane mein sabzi hai. (We have vegetables for dinner today.)","From Persian.","Sabz means green: sabzi is the green food."],["سفید","sa-FAID","adjective","White.","Pahaaron par safaid barf hai. (There is white snow on the mountains.)","From Persian.","White paint SAFE and clean."],["خدا حافظ","khu-DA HAA-fiz","phrase","Goodbye; may God keep you safe.","Khuda haafiz, phir milenge. (Goodbye, see you again.)","From Persian.","Haafiz means protector: may God protect you on the way."],["گاڑی","GAA-ri","noun","A car, or any vehicle.","Gaari mein baitho, hum chal rahe hain. (Get in the car, we are leaving.)","","A car parked in the GARAGE."],["یاد","YAAD","noun","Memory; missing someone.","Mujhe aap ki bahut yaad aati hai. (I miss you very much.)","From Persian.","Yaad: you think of someone a whole “yard” away."],["مصالحہ","ma-SAA-la","noun","A mix of spices used in cooking.","Is saalan mein masala tez hai. (This curry has strong spices.)","From Arabic.","Masala makes every dish dance."],["برف","BARF","noun","Snow or ice.","Pahaaron par barf giri hai. (Snow has fallen on the mountains.)","From Persian.","Picture a white BARF-i sweet made of snow."],["کھانا","KHAA-na","noun","Food or a meal; also used to mean to eat.","Khana tayyar hai! (Food is ready!)","From Sanskrit.","“Kha” sounds like a happy gasp when food arrives."],["جھاڑو","JHAA-roo","noun","A broom.","Daadi subah jhaaroo lagaati hain. (Grandma sweeps every morning.)","","“Jar-oo”: sweep the dust into a jar."],["زندگی","ZIN-da-gi","noun","Life.","Zindagi ek khoobsurat safar hai. (Life is a beautiful journey.)","From Persian.",""],["سڑک","SA-rak","noun","A road or street.","Sarak paar karte waqt dono taraf dekho. (Look both ways when crossing the road.)","","A road is a long TRACK."],["ماموں","MAA-mu","noun","Uncle; your mother’s brother.","Mamu hamesha tohfe laate hain. (Uncle always brings gifts.)","","Mama’s brother is Mamu."],["آرام","aa-RAAM","noun","Rest and comfort.","Thoda aaraam kar lo. (Rest for a bit.)","From Persian.","Lie down, “arm” behind your head, and rest."],["پیار","PYAAR","noun","Love and affection.","Naani sab ko bahut pyaar karti hain. (Grandma loves everyone very much.)","From Sanskrit.","Pyaar sounds like “pure”: pure love."],["پانی","PAA-ni","noun","Water.","Ek glass paani dena. (Please give me a glass of water.)","From Sanskrit.","A “panny” full of water on the stove."],["خدمت","KHID-mat","noun","Service, caring for others.","Waldain ki khidmat sab se bari neki hai. (Serving your parents is the greatest good deed.)","From Arabic.",""],["پیلا","PEE-la","adjective","Yellow.","Kele peele hote hain. (Bananas are yellow.)","","PEEL a yellow banana."],["اپنائیت","ap-NAA-i-yat","noun","A warm feeling of belonging, like family.","Unke ghar mein bohat apnaiyat thi. (Their home felt so warm and welcoming.)","From Hindi.","Apna means “mine”: feeling at home."],["راستہ","RAAS-ta","noun","A path or way.","Mujhe school ka raasta maaloom hai. (I know the way to school.)","From Persian.","RASTA: the road that leads the way."],["خزاں","khi-ZAAN","noun","Autumn, when leaves fall.","Khizaan mein patte peele ho jaate hain. (In autumn the leaves turn yellow.)","From Persian.","Autumn leaves go KISSING the ground."],["خودداری","khud-DAA-ri","noun","Self-respect and dignity.","Unki khuddaari ne madad lene na di. (Her self-respect would not let her take help.)","From Persian.",""],["ہوا","ha-WA","noun","Wind or air.","Aaj thandi hawa chal rahi hai. (A cool wind is blowing today.)","From Arabic.","HA! WOW, what a windy day."],["پل","PUL","noun","A bridge.","Hum ne darya par pul paar kiya. (We crossed the bridge over the river.)","From Persian.","PULL yourself across the bridge."],["پیاس","PYAAS","noun","Thirst.","Garmi mein bahut pyaas lagti hai. (You get very thirsty in the heat.)","From Sanskrit.","“Pass” me the water, I am thirsty."],["بھورا","BHOO-ra","adjective","Brown.","Hamari billi bhoori hai. (Our cat is brown.)","","A brown BURRO, a little donkey."],["امید","um-MEED","noun","Hope.","Kal behtar hoga, yahi umeed hai. (Tomorrow will be better, that is the hope.)","From Persian.","Hope to “meet” you: u-MEED."],["کشتی","KASH-ti","noun","A boat.","Hum ne jheel mein kashti chalaayi. (We rowed a boat on the lake.)","From Persian.","Pay CASH to ride the boat."],["مہندی","MEHN-di","noun","Henna, and the party with henna before a wedding.","Chaand raat par mehndi lagaai. (We put on henna the night before Eid.)","From Sanskrit.","Mehndi “mends” your hands into art."],["طوفان","too-FAAN","noun","A storm.","Kal raat zor ka toofaan aaya. (A big storm came last night.)","From Arabic.","Too FAN-cy a wind: it is a storm."],["حسرت","HAS-rat","noun","A longing that was never fulfilled.","Unhein gaon jaane ki hasrat rahi. (He always longed to go back to the village.)","From Arabic.",""],["جنون","ju-NOON","noun","A wild passion, a near-madness for something.","Usse cricket ka junoon hai. (He is crazy about cricket.)","From Arabic.","At “noon” he is still at it: ju-NOON."],["ہفتہ","HAF-ta","noun","A week; also Saturday.","Agle hafte hum naani ke ghar jaayenge. (Next week we will go to Grandma’s.)","From Persian.","Haft means seven: seven days."],["دادی","DAA-di","noun","Grandmother on your father’s side.","Daadi ne mere liye sweater buna. (Grandma knitted me a sweater.)","From Sanskrit.","Dadi belongs to Dad’s side of the family."],["ابو","AB-bu","noun","Dad; a loving word for father.","Abbu daftar se aa gaye. (Dad is back from the office.)","","Sounds like “a boo”: Dad pretending to scare you."],["سایہ","SAA-ya","noun","Shade or a shadow.","Chalo, darakht ke saaye mein baithte hain. (Come, let us sit in the shade of the tree.)","From Persian.","SIGH-ya, finally some cool shade."],["سر","SAR","noun","The head.","Mere sar mein dard hai. (I have a headache.)","From Persian.","“Sir” is the head of the house."],["تمنا","ta-MAN-na","noun","A deep wish or longing.","Meri tamanna hai ke hum Hajj karein. (My wish is that we go on Hajj.)","From Arabic.",""],["ہنسی","han-SI","noun","Laughter.","Ghar mein bachon ki hansi goonj rahi thi. (Children’s laughter filled the house.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.",""],["کتا","KUT-ta","noun","A dog.","Hamare parosi ka kutta bohat pyaara hai. (Our neighbor’s dog is very sweet.)","","A dog that likes to CUT-ta through the yard."],["چاہت","CHAA-hat","noun","Love and longing for someone.","Uski chaahat sachchi thi. (His love was true.)","From Hindi.",""],["بندر","BAN-dar","noun","A monkey.","Bandar darakht par chadh gaya. (The monkey climbed the tree.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A monkey swinging on a BANNER."],["حوصلہ","HAUS-la","noun","Spirit, encouragement, the heart to keep going.","Abbu ne mera hausla barhaya. (Dad lifted my spirits.)","From Arabic.",""],["شکایت","shi-KAA-yat","noun","A complaint.","Mujhe tumse koi shikayat nahin. (I have no complaint against you.)","From Arabic.",""],["نخرہ","NAKH-ra","noun","Fussiness, playful showing off.","Itne nakhre mat karo, khana kha lo. (Don’t be so fussy, just eat.)","From Persian.",""],["بیٹا","BAY-ta","noun","Son; elders also say it lovingly to any child.","Beta, khana kha lo. (Dear child, come eat.)","From Sanskrit.","“Bay-ta” sounds like “better”: come here, my better half."],["غم","GHAM","noun","Sorrow or grief.","Dost ke saath gham aadha ho jata hai. (Sorrow is halved with a friend.)","From Arabic.","Gham sounds like “gum”: sadness that sticks."],["دلاسا","di-LAA-sa","noun","Words of comfort and consolation.","Dost ne mujhe dilaasa diya. (My friend comforted me.)","From Persian.",""],["تکیہ","TAK-ya","noun","A pillow.","Mera takiya bahut naram hai. (My pillow is very soft.)","From Persian.","“Tuck” your head into the pillow."],["ہرا","HA-ra","adjective","Green.","Baarish ke baad sab kuch hara ho gaya. (After the rain everything turned green.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","HURRAH, the grass is green again."],["خوشی","khu-SHI","noun","Happiness and joy.","Tumhein dekh kar bohat khushi hui. (I was so happy to see you.)","From Persian.","Khushi sounds like “cushy”: a cushy, happy feeling."],["مٹی","MIT-ti","noun","Soil, earth, or dust.","Baarish ke baad mitti ki khushboo aati hai. (After rain comes the smell of the soil.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","Muddy MITTENS full of soil."],["صحت","SEH-hat","noun","Health.","Sehat sab se badi daulat hai. (Health is the greatest wealth.)","From Arabic.","“Say hat”: wear one in the sun for good health."],["پھل","PHAL","noun","Fruit.","Roz ek phal khaana chahiye. (You should eat one fruit a day.)","From Sanskrit.","Fruit “falls” from the tree."],["دودھ","DOODH","noun","Milk.","Bachon ko doodh pilaao. (Give the children milk.)","From Sanskrit.","Doodh, the white drink that makes you “dude” strong."],["دریا","dar-YA","noun","A river.","Dariya ka paani bohat thanda tha. (The river water was very cold.)","From Persian.","A river flows through the AREA."],["انتظار کرنا","in-ti-ZAAR KAR-na","verb","To wait.","Bus stop par mera intezaar karo. (Wait for me at the bus stop.)","From Arabic, with an Urdu verb.","IN the BAZAAR, I wait for you."],["میدان","mai-DAAN","noun","An open field or ground.","Bachche maidaan mein cricket khel rahe hain. (The children are playing cricket in the field.)","From Persian.","MY DAWN run across the open field."],["انتظار","in-te-ZAAR","noun","Waiting; waiting for someone.","Hum aap ka intezaar kar rahe the. (We were waiting for you.)","From Arabic.",""],["مچھلی","MACH-li","noun","A fish.","Talaab mein rangeen machhliyan hain. (There are colorful fish in the pond.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A fish swims at MACH speed."],["دھند","DHUND","noun","Fog or mist.","Subah itni dhund thi ke kuch nazar nahin aaya. (It was so foggy this morning we could see nothing.)","","Fog makes the world look DUNNED and dim."],["انڈا","AN-da","noun","An egg.","Naashte mein anda khaao. (Eat an egg for breakfast.)","From Sanskrit.","An egg, “and a” piece of toast."],["طبیعت","ta-BEE-at","noun","How someone is feeling; health or mood.","Aap ki tabiyat kaisi hai? (How are you feeling?)","From Arabic.","Ask about someone’s tabiyat to show you care."],["صبح","su-BAH","noun","Morning.","Subah ki chai sab se achhi lagti hai. (Morning tea tastes the best.)","From Arabic.",""],["اتوار","it-WAAR","noun","Sunday.","Itwaar ko chhutti hoti hai. (Sunday is a day off.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","IT WAS Sunday, so no school."],["شرم","SHA-ram","noun","Shyness or shame.","Woh sharam se laal ho gayi. (She turned red with shyness.)","From Persian.",""],["شکر","SHUKR","noun","Gratitude, being thankful.","Har haal mein shukr karo. (Be thankful in every situation.)","From Arabic.","Shukr sounds like “sugar”: gratitude is sweet."],["دعا","du-AA","noun","A prayer or a blessing you ask for someone.","Naani ki dua hamesha saath hai. (Grandma’s prayers are always with us.)","From Arabic.","“Do ah”: hands raised, ask with a sigh."],["جمعہ","JU-ma","noun","Friday.","Jumma ko sab mil kar khaana khaate hain. (On Friday everyone eats together.)","From Arabic.","Friday means everyone gathers together."],["جواب","ja-WAAB","noun","An answer or reply.","Sahi jawaab kya hai? (What is the right answer?)","From Arabic.","Your JAW moves when you answer."],["پڑوسی","pa-RO-si","noun","A neighbor.","Hamare padosi bahut achhe hain. (Our neighbors are very nice.)","From Sanskrit.","The neighbor “pops over” with a plate of food."],["ہانڈی","HAAN-di","noun","A cooking pot, often clay or metal.","Handi mein saalan pak raha hai. (The curry is cooking in the pot.)","From Sanskrit.","A “handy” pot for every curry."],["شرارت","sha-RAA-rat","noun","Mischief, playful naughtiness.","Chhote bhai ki shararat ne sab ko hansa diya. (Little brother’s mischief made everyone laugh.)","From Arabic.",""],["قسم","QA-sam","noun","An oath, a solemn promise.","Qasam se, maine nahin khaya. (I swear, I didn’t eat it.)","From Arabic.",""],["تنہائی","tan-HAA-i","noun","Loneliness or solitude.","Kitaab tanhaai ki achhi saathi hai. (A book is good company when alone.)","From Persian.",""],["آسمان","aas-MAAN","noun","The sky.","Aaj aasmaan bilkul saaf hai. (The sky is totally clear today.)","From Persian.","Ask a MAN to look up at the sky."],["چاند","CHAAND","noun","The moon.","Dekho, aaj chaand kitna bara hai! (Look, how big the moon is tonight!)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","Chance to see the moon? Look up at night."],["پتّا","PAT-ta","noun","A leaf.","Hawa se ek patta gir gaya. (A leaf fell in the wind.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","PAT the little green leaf."],["آج","AAJ","noun","Today.","Aaj mausam bahut achha hai. (The weather is very nice today.)","From Sanskrit.","“Adj-ust” your plans for today."],["دلچسپ","dil-CHASP","adjective","Interesting.","Dada ki kahaani bohat dilchasp thi. (Grandpa’s story was very interesting.)","From Persian.","Dil plus chasp means it sticks to the heart."],["پریشانی","pa-re-SHAA-ni","noun","Worry and trouble.","Koi pareshaani ho to batana. (Tell me if anything is troubling you.)","From Persian.",""],["مہمان","meh-MAAN","noun","A guest who visits your home.","Ghar mein mehmaan aa gaye. (Guests have arrived at the house.)","From Persian.","“May-MAN” stay for dinner? The guest always says yes."],["ذوق","ZAUQ","noun","Good taste and a love for fine things.","Unhein shaayari ka bohat zauq hai. (He has a fine taste for poetry.)","From Arabic.",""],["لگن","la-GAN","noun","Dedication and devotion to a task.","Usne poori lagan se kaam kiya. (She worked with full dedication.)","From Hindi.",""],["جوتا","JOO-ta","noun","A shoe.","Andar aane se pehle joote utaaro. (Take off your shoes before coming in.)","","“Shoe-ta”: just swap the sh for a j."],["سیکھنا","SEEKH-na","verb","To learn.","Main Urdu seekh rahi hoon. (I am learning Urdu.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","SEEK knowledge to learn."],["قسمت","QIS-mat","noun","Luck or destiny.","Aaj qismat ne saath diya. (Luck was on our side today.)","From Arabic.",""],["شرمندگی","shar-min-DA-gi","noun","Embarrassment.","Ghalti par sharmindagi hui. (I felt embarrassed about my mistake.)","From Persian.",""],["بیچنا","BECH-na","verb","To sell.","Woh bazaar mein phal bechta hai. (He sells fruit at the market.)","","Sell shells on the BEACH."],["وفا","wa-FAA","noun","Loyalty and faithfulness.","Kutta wafa ki misaal hai. (A dog is an example of loyalty.)","From Arabic.",""],["مسافر","mu-SAA-fir","noun","A traveler or passenger.","Train mein bohat musaafir the. (There were many passengers on the train.)","From Arabic.","A traveler goes SO FAR on a safar."],["تتلی","TIT-li","noun","A butterfly.","Phool par ek peeli titli baithi hai. (A yellow butterfly is sitting on the flower.)","","A butterfly is a little TITCH of color."],["معاف کیجیے","maaf KEE-ji-ye","phrase","Excuse me or forgive me, said politely.","Maaf kijiye, mujhe der ho gayi. (Forgive me, I am late.)","From Arabic.","“Moff” the mistake away: please forgive me."],["لحاظ","li-HAAZ","noun","Consideration and regard for others.","Doosron ke jazbaat ka lihaaz karo. (Be considerate of others’ feelings.)","From Arabic.",""],["خالہ","KHAA-la","noun","Aunt; your mother’s sister.","Khala ne biryani banaai. (Auntie made biryani.)","From Arabic.","Khala, the aunt who calls “Hello-la!” at the door."],["پیارا","PYAA-ra","adjective","Lovely, sweet and dear.","Kitna pyaara bachcha hai. (What a sweet child.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.",""],["سچائی","sach-CHAA-i","noun","Truth and truthfulness.","Sachaai hamesha saamne aati hai. (The truth always comes out.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","Sach sounds like “such” truth."],["چادر","CHAA-dar","noun","A sheet or a large shawl.","Bistar par saaf chaadar bichhaao. (Put a clean sheet on the bed.)","From Persian.","Chaadar covers you like a big tent."],["آنگن","AAN-gan","noun","An open courtyard in the middle of a home.","Bachay aangan mein khel rahe hain. (The children are playing in the courtyard.)","From Sanskrit.","The courtyard where the whole family “hangs” out."],["بازار","ba-ZAAR","noun","A market or bazaar.","Ammi ke saath bazaar chalo. (Come to the market with Mom.)","From Persian.","English got bazaar from this very word."],["بچپن","BACH-pan","noun","Childhood.","Mujhe apna bachpan yaad aata hai. (I miss my childhood.)","","A baby in a frying pan? Only in childhood stories."],["تھالی","THAA-li","noun","A large round plate or tray.","Thaali mein khana sajaaya. (The food was arranged on the platter.)","From Sanskrit.","Thaali: a “tally” of all the dishes on one plate."],["دوست","DOST","noun","A friend.","Woh mera sab se achha dost hai. (He is my best friend.)","From Persian.","A friend helps you “dust” off after a fall."],["دوپہر","do-PA-har","noun","Afternoon, around midday.","Dopahar ko bohat garmi hoti hai. (It gets very hot in the afternoon.)","","DOUGH PA-HAR: lunch at midday."],["کل","KAL","noun","Tomorrow or yesterday; the sentence tells you which.","Kal school ki chutti hai. (Tomorrow is a school holiday.)","From Sanskrit.","“Call” me kal: tomorrow, or was it yesterday."],["ناشتہ","NAASH-ta","noun","Breakfast.","Naashte mein paraathe hain. (There are parathas for breakfast.)","From Persian.","Naashta: “nosh” first thing in the morning."],["شفقت","SHAF-qat","noun","Tender, caring kindness, like an elder’s.","Ustaad ki shafqat hamesha yaad rahegi. (I will always remember my teacher’s kindness.)","From Arabic.",""],["چابی","CHAA-bi","noun","A key.","Ghar ki chaabi kahaan hai? (Where is the house key?)","From Portuguese.","Where is the key? Chaabi, chaabi, everyone asks."],["کاغذ","KAA-ghaz","noun","Paper.","Mujhe ek safaid kaaghaz do. (Give me a white sheet of paper.)","From Persian.","A paper CAR made with GAS? Only on paper."],["دھوپ","DHOOP","noun","Sunshine.","Bachche dhoop mein khel rahe hain. (The children are playing in the sunshine.)","","Sunshine DROPS warm light on you."],["اونٹ","OONT","noun","A camel.","Registaan mein oont chalte hain. (Camels walk in the desert.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","A camel goes OOH on the hump."],["تحفہ","TOH-fa","noun","A gift.","Yeh tohfa aap ke liye hai. (This gift is for you.)","From Arabic.","A gift wrapped with a “toffee” on top."],["دال","DAAL","noun","Lentils, or a lentil curry.","Daal chawal mera pasandeeda khana hai. (Lentils and rice is my favorite meal.)","From Sanskrit.","Daal, the “doll” of comfort food: small and loved."],["ممتا","MAM-ta","noun","A mother’s love.","Maa ki mamta ka koi mol nahin. (A mother’s love has no price.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","Mamta starts with “Mam”: a mother’s love."],["بلی","BIL-li","noun","A cat.","Billi dhoop mein so rahi hai. (The cat is sleeping in the sun.)","","BILLY the cat naps all day."],["دلکش","dil-KASH","adjective","Charming, pulling at the heart.","Uski awaaz bohat dilkash hai. (Her voice is very charming.)","From Persian.","Dil plus kash, meaning pull: it pulls the heart."],["گھاس","GHAAS","noun","Grass.","Bachche hari ghaas par daur rahe hain. (The children are running on the green grass.)","","Grass grows FAST, says GHAAS."],["بارش","BAA-rish","noun","Rain.","Aaj bohat baarish ho rahi hai. (It is raining a lot today.)","From Persian.","Rain makes everything BRISK and fresh."],["دوڑنا","DAUR-na","verb","To run.","Kutta ball ke peeche daur raha hai. (The dog is running after the ball.)","","Run out the DOOR."],["سمندر","sa-MUN-dar","noun","The sea or ocean.","Hum ne samundar ke kinaare seepiyan dhoondhin. (We looked for shells by the sea.)","From Sanskrit, through Hindi.","SOME UNDER the sea, some on the sand."],["دکان","du-KAAN","noun","A shop.","Kone wali dukaan se doodh le aao. (Bring milk from the corner shop.)","From Persian.","DO CAN you buy a can at the shop?"],["دلربا","dil-ru-BAA","adjective","Heart-stealing, enchanting.","Kya dilruba muskurahat hai. (What an enchanting smile.)","From Persian.","Dil plus ruba, “stealer”: it steals your heart."],["السلام علیکم","as-sa-LAAM-o-a-LAI-kum","phrase","Peace be upon you; the everyday hello.","Assalam-o-alaikum, aap kaise hain? (Peace be upon you, how are you?)","From Arabic.","Say salaam, share peace."],["مہنگا","MEH-nga","adjective","Expensive.","Yeh joote bohat mehnge hain. (These shoes are very expensive.)","","MEH, too expensive, I will skip it."]]};
